window.SEARCH_DATA = [{"nav":"essay/essay-frameworks/bullets","file":"essay-writing-points.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-frameworks/bullet-points/essay-writing-points.html","title":"Essay Writing — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Writing — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Topic families → ready frames"},{"level":2,"text":"Quotes w/ safe attribution"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden sentence trains"},{"level":2,"text":"Discipline bullets"}],"text":"Essay Writing — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap ESSAY PAPER (250 marks; 2 essays) ├── Examiner rewards: structure · content breadth · language clarity · originality · balance ├── 6-move framework: decode → thesis → 3×3 grid → evidence → counterpoint → forward conclusion ├── Intro methods: anecdote/stat · verified quote · definition turn · contrast pair · question (once) ├── Paragraph: topic → mechanism → evidence vs counter → mini-case → linkage ├── Dimensions playlist (choose 4–5): social·political·economic·cultural·ethical·environmental·technological·IR·psychological·gender·historical ├── Anchor pantry: PLI/GST2.0/DBT · 106th Amdt/bonds/quasicuster · MK Ranjitsinh/GBF/Nicobar · NISAR/SpaDeX/AI Mission · NFHS-5/Bihar survey/Census 2027 · Gaza/Ukraine/BRI ├── Pitfalls: drift · fact-dump · fabricated quotes · political bias · ornamental rhetoric └── Time: 15 min plan · 70 min ×2 · final 5–10 ","chars":2334},{"nav":"essay/essay-frameworks/notes","file":"essay-writing.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-frameworks/detailed-notes/essay-writing.html","title":"Essay Writing — Frameworks &amp; Method (Detailed Notes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Writing — Frameworks & Method (Detailed Notes)"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What the examiner rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The 6-move framework (use on any topic)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Intro methods (rotate, don't repeat)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Body architecture — dimension rotation"},{"level":3,"text":"Paragraph pattern (per dimension)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Recurring topic families & ready frames"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Examples & evidence pantry (rotation discipline)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Language discipline"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Common pitfalls (and their fixes)"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Practise regimens"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Essay Writing — Frameworks & Method (Detailed Notes) Syllabus slot: UPSC Mains Essay Paper (250 marks; two essays of ~1,000–1,200 words, Section A philosophical/abstract + Section B socio-economic/policy) Goal advantage: predictable 130+ scoring via framework discipline. This note is the engine; bullets and PYQs accentuate. 1. What the examiner rewards Structure : discernible intro → body arcs → conclusion; logical paragraph chains. Content : domain breadth (polity, economy, environment, technology, ethics, gender, IR, culture). Language : clarity, correct grammar, controlled rhetoric — no ornamental long sentences. Originality : lived-data anchors, fresh examples beyond trope cycles (CAA/Agnipath/COVID overuse). Balance : acknowledges counter-arguments without fence-sitting. 2. The 6-move framework (use on any topic) Decode the aphorism/statement : unpack the metaphor; identify the cent","chars":5498},{"nav":"essay/essay-frameworks/pyqs","file":"essay-frameworks-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-frameworks/pyqs/essay-frameworks-pyqs.html","title":"Essay Frameworks — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Frameworks — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Recent pattern signals (2019–2024 trend view)"},{"level":2,"text":"Model approaches to five archetypes"},{"level":2,"text":"Practise prompts (current-affairs-tilted, as of 2026)"},{"level":2,"text":"Self-scoring rubric (use during mock reviews)"}],"text":"Essay Frameworks — PYQs Pattern study of Section A (abstract/philosophical) vs Section B (socio-economic) from recent years + model approaches. Actual wording paraphrased from memory — verify via UPSC archives. Recent pattern signals (2019–2024 trend view) Section A flavours (abstract): \"Reality does not conform to the ideal, but confirms it.\" \"Truth is lived, not taught.\" \"Not all who wander are lost.\" \"The empires of the future will be the empires of the mind.\" \"Half knowledge is dangerous*\" style proverbs recur. Section B flavours (applied): Education-value pairing; women's workforce reality; technology vs jobs; climate-development equation; urbanisation vs liveability; media & trust; federalism & fiscal order; elections & reform. recurring frame: trade-off statements (growth vs ecology, freedom vs security, tech vs employment, tradition vs modernity). Model approaches to five archety","chars":3878},{"nav":"essay/essay-frameworks/short","file":"essay-writing-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-frameworks/short-notes/essay-writing-revision.html","title":"Essay Writing — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Writing — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"6-move framework (tattoo this)"},{"level":2,"text":"Intro methods (rotate)"},{"level":2,"text":"Dimension playlist (choose 4–5, never all)"},{"level":2,"text":"Paragraph pattern"},{"level":2,"text":"Anchor pantry (fresh CA)"},{"level":2,"text":"Language discipline"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfalls → fixes"},{"level":2,"text":"Time structure (3-hour exam)"},{"level":2,"text":"Sample thesis templates"}],"text":"Essay Writing — Revision Notes Exam-day sheet: framework, discipline, rapidfills. 6-move framework (tattoo this) Decode the aphorism → identify central tension. Thesis sentence → \"This essay argues that X is Y provided Z.\" 3×3 grid → 3 dimensions × (cause / consequence / corrective). Evidence anchors → 1–2 per dimension (dated, precise). Counterpoint → state strongest opposite fairly; resolve. Conclusion → thesis echo + forward frame (SDG/constitutional/future-facing). Intro methods (rotate) Anecdote/statistic (place + year). Verified quote (Gandhi talisman; Ambedkar fraternity; Tagore). Definition-turn (\"The Oxford dictionary defines X as … but in Indian context…\"). Contrast (\"Between 1996 and 2026 Kerala's literacy slope …\"). Question (ONE per essay max). Dimension playlist (choose 4–5, never all) Social · Political · Economic · Cultural · Ethical · Environmental · Technological · Geop","chars":2812},{"nav":"essay/essay-quotes/bullets","file":"essay-quotes-points.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-quotes/bullet-points/essay-quotes-points.html","title":"Essay Quotes — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Quotes — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"The placement map"},{"level":2,"text":"Selection rules"},{"level":2,"text":"Interpretation frames (ready-made)"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"Essay Quotes — Bullet Points Rules of engagement for quotations — the difference between an ornament and an argument. ASCII mindmap QUOTES IN ESSAYS ├── WHY ......... hook attention · signal reading · frame thesis ├── WHEN ........ intro (hook) or conclusion (seal) — ONE per essay ├── WHICH ....... verified attribution · theme-fit · short (<25 words) ├── HOW ......... quote → interpret → link to thesis (never leave it bare) └── RISKS ....... misattribution · quote-stuffing · decorative quote drift The placement map Introduction: quote → 1-line interpretation → thesis. The quote borrows authority; your line earns relevance. Conclusion: return to the intro's quote and flip it forward (hope/duty) — creates a loop examiners remember. Body: prefer thinkers as paraphrased lenses (Rawls' fairness, Gandhi's talisman) rather than more quotation marks. Selection rules Length: under 25 words; long ","chars":1773},{"nav":"essay/essay-quotes/notes","file":"quote-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-quotes/detailed-notes/quote-bank.html","title":"Essay Quote Bank (Verified Attributions Only)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Quote Bank (Verified Attributions Only)"},{"level":2,"text":"Indian canon"},{"level":2,"text":"Western canon"},{"level":2,"text":"Proverbs & stock lines (no attribution risk)"},{"level":2,"text":"Usage discipline"}],"text":"Essay Quote Bank (Verified Attributions Only) Rule: two quotes maximum per essay; attribute confidently. Quotes whose attribution is shaky are listed as \"as the saying goes…\" alternatives or omitted. Indian canon Mahatma Gandhi \"There is no path to peace; peace is the path.\" — widely verified. Talisman framing (paraphrase-safe): recall the face of the poorest and weakest person; ask whether your action helps them. (Paraphrase from the 1948 note — do not quote verbatim.) \"Earth provides enough to satisfy every man's needs, but not every man's greed.\" — attributed to Gandhi (popular attribution; context uncertain — safer as \"attributed to Gandhi\"). B. R. Ambedkar \"I measure the progress of a community by the degree of progress which women have achieved.\" — verified (writings). Constitutional morality framing: Ambedkar warned of \"a life of contradictions\" — political equality without social","chars":3658},{"nav":"essay/essay-quotes/pyqs","file":"essay-quotes-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-quotes/pyqs/essay-quotes-pyqs.html","title":"Essay Quotes — PYQs &amp; Drills","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Quotes — PYQs & Drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Attribution checks (MCQs)"},{"level":2,"text":"Timed drills (12 minutes each)"},{"level":2,"text":"How examiners read quotes"}],"text":"Essay Quotes — PYQs & Drills Quote-themed practice: MCQs on attribution, and timed drills that train quote placement. Attribution checks (MCQs) Q1. \"In the happiness of his subjects lies the king's happiness\" is from: (a) Manusmriti (b) Kautilya's Arthashastra (c) Shanti Parva (d) Ashokan edicts Model answer: (b). Arthashastra's rajadharma formulation — safe to cite in governance essays. Q2. \"Constitutional morality is not a natural sentiment\" was said by: (a) Nehru (b) Rajaji (c) Ambedkar (d) L.M. Singhvi Model answer: (c). Ambedkar, Constituent Assembly — pairs with polity and ethics essays alike. Q3. Which quote is attribution-RISKY and should be used with \"attributed to\"? (a) \"Unexamined life…\" — Socrates (b) \"Earth provides enough…\" — Gandhi (c) \"Injustice anywhere…\" — M.L. King (d) \"We are what we repeatedly do\" — Aristotle (as phrased by W. Durant) Model answer: (b) and (d) both n","chars":2522},{"nav":"essay/essay-quotes/short","file":"essay-quotes-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-quotes/short-notes/essay-quotes-revision.html","title":"Essay Quotes — Revision Cards","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Quotes — Revision Cards"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Development, means and ends"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Technology and change"},{"level":2,"text":"C. Environment and future generations"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Justice, equality, constitution"},{"level":2,"text":"E. Ethics, character, self"},{"level":2,"text":"F. Women, gender, empowerment"},{"level":2,"text":"Usage in ten seconds"}],"text":"Essay Quotes — Revision Cards Theme-tagged, attribution-safe quotes for the last 48 hours before the exam. Full bank in detailed-notes; rules in bullet-points. A. Development, means and ends Gandhi — \"The true source of rights is duty. If we all discharge our duties, rights will not be far to seek.\" (tags: duty, governance) Vivekananda — \"They alone live who live for others.\" (service) Ambedkar — \"However good a Constitution may be, if those who are implementing it are not good, it will prove to be bad.\" (administration, integrity) Tagore — \"The highest education is that which does not merely give us information but makes our life in harmony with all existence.\" (education, harmony) B. Technology and change Einstein — \"Concern for man himself and his fate must always form the chief interest of all technical endeavours.\" (tech + humanism) Marshall McLuhan — \"We shape our tools and thereaf","chars":3034},{"nav":"essay/essay-topics/bullets","file":"essay-topics-points.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-topics/bullet-points/essay-topics-points.html","title":"Essay Topics — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Topics — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Selection scoring card"},{"level":2,"text":"Handling absolute and paradox prompts"},{"level":2,"text":"Blueprint discipline"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"Essay Topics — Bullet Points Selection and handling rules — the decision layer before writing begins. ASCII mindmap ESSAY TOPIC DECISION (first 15 min) ├── READ ALL ...... under every option, note 2 sparks ├── SHORTLIST 2 ... dimensions? examples? balance? ├── SCORE 1-5 ..... familiarity · dimension-spread · example-supply │ (pick highest TOTAL, not highest familiarity) ├── THESIS ........ one sentence, balanced, yours └── BLUEPRINT ..... 5-7 sections + example slots before para 1 Selection scoring card Familiarity — can I define every term in the prompt? Dimension-spread — ≥4 lenses (social, political, economic, ethical, tech/environmental)? Example-supply — ≥6 examples across history/law/schemes/data/personal observation? Balance-potential — can I argue both sides without straw-manning? Trap-detector — absolute-word prompts (\"never\", \"only\") need a nuanced thesis, not acceptance. Handl","chars":1898},{"nav":"essay/essay-topics/notes","file":"topic-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-topics/detailed-notes/topic-bank.html","title":"Essay Topic Bank — 30 Prompts with Opening Angles","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Topic Bank — 30 Prompts with Opening Angles"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Technology & Society"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Gender & Society"},{"level":2,"text":"C. Environment & Climate"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Polity & Constitution"},{"level":2,"text":"E. Economy & Development"},{"level":2,"text":"F. Ethics, Culture & Philosophy"},{"level":2,"text":"G. Rurban & Urban Futures"},{"level":2,"text":"H. India & The World"},{"level":2,"text":"How to use this bank"}],"text":"Essay Topic Bank — 30 Prompts with Opening Angles Grouped practice bank for the Essay paper. For each topic: an angle line you can bend into a thesis. Rotate the 6-move framework (see essay-frameworks/detailed-notes/essay-writing.html ). A. Technology & Society \"Artificial intelligence will not replace humans, but humans who fail to govern AI will be replaced by those who do.\" — agency vs governance; compute equity (IndiaAI Mission), skill-transition gaps, algorithmic accountability. \"The smartphone made the world flat and the mind hollow.\" — attention economy, education shifts, political polarisation, digital public infrastructure as counterweight (DPI example). \"Data is the new commons.\" — DPDP 2023 tensions, platform power, open-standards diplomacy (MOSIP, UPI links). \"Space is no longer the final frontier; it is the next ledger.\" — commercial space (Space FDI 2024, IN-SPACe, Skyroot/","chars":5202},{"nav":"essay/essay-topics/pyqs","file":"essay-topics-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-topics/pyqs/essay-topics-pyqs.html","title":"Essay Topics — PYQ Patterns &amp; Practice","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Topics — PYQ Patterns & Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Observed patterns in the essay paper"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice prompts (pattern-style)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Self-review grid"}],"text":"Essay Topics — PYQ Patterns & Practice How UPSC's essay paper has behaved, and drills to internalise it. Prompts below are pattern-descriptions for practice, not verbatim reproductions. Observed patterns in the essay paper Structure : two sections, four topics each; one to be written; 125 marks ×2; 3 hours. Section 1 skews philosophical/abstract (\"…state of mind\", \"…journey\", paired concepts); Section 2 skews concrete-governance (tech, economy, environment, gender). Recurrence : technology-and-human (multiple years), women's empowerment as process, local-vs-global tension, \"hand-holding vs hand-out\" development debates, self-mastery themes. What changed recently : prompts got shorter and more paradoxical — a premium on definitional clarity in the introduction. Practice prompts (pattern-style) \"Contentment is the seed of ambition, not its enemy.\" — philosophical pair. \"A census is the aut","chars":2057},{"nav":"essay/essay-topics/short","file":"essay-topics-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-topics/short-notes/essay-topics-revision.html","title":"Essay Topics — Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Topics — Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"The seven prompt families UPSC cycles through"},{"level":2,"text":"Topic selection in 90 seconds"},{"level":2,"text":"Angle generators (when mind goes blank)"},{"level":2,"text":"Ten-second drill"}],"text":"Essay Topics — Revision Theme-wise prompt families and one-line angles. Use with the 30-prompt bank in detailed-notes. The seven prompt families UPSC cycles through Family Recent-style prompt patterns Angle one-liner Philosophy & self \"…is a state of mind\" / \"…begins with the self\" / paradox pairs Define in your terms → inner-outer dialectic → social consequence → balance Polity & constitution rights vs duties · institutions · federal trust · judiciary-society Constitutional morality as spine; institution-building as hero Society & gender empowerment as process · demography · urbanisation · media & self-image Structural view over sermon; data + lived reality pairs Economy & development growth vs equity · handouts vs hand-ups · work & meaning Growth is a means; the argument is distribution and dignity Science, tech & society AI & human worth · machines & minds · innovation & ethics Tool-s","chars":1947},{"nav":"essay/essay-toppers/bullets","file":"essay-toppers-points.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-toppers/bullet-points/essay-toppers-points.html","title":"Essay Toppers — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Toppers — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Intro archetypes that work"},{"level":2,"text":"Conclusion archetypes that work"},{"level":2,"text":"Score-band signatures"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"Essay Toppers — Bullet Points Aggregate observations, compressed. ASCII mindmap TOPPER PATTERN ├── ARCH ....... hook → defined terms → thesis → 5-7 signposted sections │ → counter-view → synthesis → full-circle conclusion ├── FLOW ....... thesis sentence ends each section; links bridge sections ├── MIX ........ India + world · data + narrative · policy + philosophy ├── VOICE ...... first-person judgement where apt (\"I would argue\") └── MECHANICS .. legible drift-proof handwriting · underlined keywords · clean margins · ~1000-1200 words Intro archetypes that work Anecdote (one paragraph, human face) → widen to the question. Paradox or paired terms unpacked → thesis. Image/metaphor (river, ledger, mirror) → carried through the essay. Quote (one only) → interpreted → thesis. Conclusion archetypes that work Return-to-image with the argument resolved. Duty-invitation (\"the answer is less a po","chars":1583},{"nav":"essay/essay-toppers/notes","file":"toppers-analysis.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-toppers/detailed-notes/toppers-analysis.html","title":"Toppers&#x27; Essay Patterns — Aggregate Analysis","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Toppers' Essay Patterns — Aggregate Analysis"},{"level":2,"text":"Repeated patterns across high-score write-ups"},{"level":2,"text":"Timing splits used by top scorers (aggregate)"},{"level":2,"text":"Introduction archetypes observed"},{"level":2,"text":"Conclusion archetypes observed"},{"level":2,"text":"Anti-patterns (what low scores share)"},{"level":2,"text":"Calibrating your mock scores"},{"level":2,"text":"Drills"}],"text":"Toppers' Essay Patterns — Aggregate Analysis Not a list of individuals' marks (unverifiable); a patterned digest of what high-scoring memoirs/test series consistently show. Use alongside the 6-move framework and rubric (see essay-frameworks/essay-frameworks/pyqs/essay-frameworks-pyqs.html ). Repeated patterns across high-score write-ups Theses are invisible to the author but visible to the examiner. High scripts state a claim within 120 words of the opening — usually tempered (\"…provided that…\", \"…except when…\"). Cross-domain evidence density. Each body paragraph carries at least one dated anchor (a year, a rate, a section number, a named mission). Most cited across topper scripts: schemes (MGNREGA/NFSA/GST missions), articles/amendments (via KM/103rd/106th), data (NFHS, PLFS), institutions (ISA, CDRI, NITI). Multi-dimensional without superficiality. 4–5 dimensions max; each dimension cl","chars":3851},{"nav":"essay/essay-toppers/pyqs","file":"essay-toppers-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-toppers/pyqs/essay-toppers-pyqs.html","title":"Essay Toppers — Graded Drills","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Toppers — Graded Drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercise 1 — The ten-second skim"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercise 2 — Anatomy transplant"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercise 3 — The example audit"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercise 4 — The counter-view court"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercise 5 — Mock calibration"}],"text":"Essay Toppers — Graded Drills Self-assessment exercises built on the aggregate patterns. Grade honestly against each rubric line. Exercise 1 — The ten-second skim Take your last full essay. Hand it to a study partner for ten seconds only , then ask: What was the thesis? How many dimensions? Was there a counter-view? If any answer fails, your signage (headings, lead sentences) is weak — the examiner's eye had the same ten seconds. Rubric: thesis visible (0/1) · dimensions countable (0/1) · counter-view spotted (0/1) · conclusion type identifiable (0/1). Target: 4/4. Exercise 2 — Anatomy transplant Rewrite your essay's introduction using a different archetype (if anecdotal, try paradox). Rewrite the conclusion as a return-to-image. Compare coherence: better or worse? Keep whichever serves the thesis — pattern awareness, not pattern worship. Rubric: new intro defines terms (0/1) · states th","chars":2062},{"nav":"essay/essay-toppers/short","file":"essay-toppers-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/essay-frameworks/essay-toppers/short-notes/essay-toppers-revision.html","title":"Toppers' Essay Patterns — Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Toppers' Essay Patterns — Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"The nine habits that separate 130+ from ~100"},{"level":2,"text":"Timing card"},{"level":2,"text":"Anti-pattern quick list"},{"level":2,"text":"Final 48 hours drills"}],"text":"Toppers' Essay Patterns — Revision The last-week card distilled from aggregate analysis in detailed-notes. The nine habits that separate 130+ from ~100 A spine, not a pile — one controlling thesis restated as each section's last line. Dimension signage — visible section headings/lead sentences: historical, social, economic, ethical… Example rotation — law, scheme, data, thinker, lived case; never three examples of the same species. The genuine counter-view — one section steel-manning the opposite case, then resolving it. Handwriting stability — pages decelerate; the last third matches the first in legibility. Time splits honoured — ~15 min selection/outline, ~150 writing, ~15 review; no section is abandoned. Definitional first para — key terms defined in your own words before any flourish. Closure engineering — conclusion echoes the introduction's image and ends on duty/hope, not summary","chars":1637},{"nav":"gs-1/geography-world-india/bullets","file":"geography-world-india-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/geography-world-india/bullet-points/geography-world-india-points.html","title":"Geography of India &amp; World — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography of India & World — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":3,"text":"India's physiography"},{"level":3,"text":"Rivers"},{"level":3,"text":"Resources (India match-ups)"},{"level":3,"text":"World resources"},{"level":3,"text":"Population & settlements"},{"level":3,"text":"Transport & chokepoints"},{"level":3,"text":"Current-development hooks"}],"text":"Geography of India & World — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap INDIA & WORLD GEOGRAPHY │ ┌─────────┬─────────┼─────────┬─────────┐ PHYSIO RIVERS RESOURCES SETTLEMENTS LINKAGE graphy (incl. world) & people (transport & straits) │ │ │ │ │ Himalayas Himalayan minerals India most GQ · Bharatmala Plains + Penin. (India) populous '23 ports · Gati Peninsula rivers oil · ore Census '27 Shakti · IMEC Coasts (deltas/ (world) urban ~35% chokepoints Islands estuaries) │ city count (2011: 7,935 towns) Bullet facts (prelims gold) India's physiography Himadri (Greater Himalaya, Everest 8,848 m) · Himachal (Lesser — hill stations) · Shiwaliks (youngest — duns : Dehradun, Kathmandu). Bhabar (stones, streams sink) → Terai (marshy, springs re-emerge) → alluvial plain (khadar floodplain vs bhangar upland) . Western Ghats (~1,600 km, continuous escarpment) vs Eastern Ghats (discontinuous) ; highest — Anamu","chars":3965},{"nav":"gs-1/geography-world-india/notes","file":"geography-of-india-and-world.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/geography-world-india/detailed-notes/geography-of-india-and-world.html","title":"Geography of India &amp; the World: Physiography, Resources, Distribution &amp; Mapping — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography of India & the World: Physiography, Resources, Distribution & Mapping — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. India's physiographic canvas (N → S)"},{"level":3,"text":"Drainage — the two systems"},{"level":3,"text":"Indian climate & water resources"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Resources — India & the world (the Mains \"distribution\" angle)"},{"level":3,"text":"India's mineral & energy map (state → resource)"},{"level":3,"text":"Industrial location logic (use in frame answers)"},{"level":3,"text":"World resource map (memorise the famous regions)"},{"level":3,"text":"World industrial regions"},{"level":2,"text":"3. India's agriculture geography"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Population & settlement"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Transport & connectivity geography"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Mapping & geography as an answer tool"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Quick PYQ angles"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Geography of India & the World: Physiography, Resources, Distribution & Mapping — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Geography of India & World — Resources, Distribution, Mapping Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Geography; GS 3 (resources & infrastructure links) 1. India's physiographic canvas (N → S) Region Extent Key facts The Himalayas Northern wall, ~2,400 km, ~250–400 km wide Three parallel ranges: Himadri (Greater Himalaya — granite core, permanent snow: Everest, Kanchenjunga, Nanda Devi) ; Himachal (Lesser — Pir Panjal, Dhauladhar; hill stations: Shimla, Mussoorie, Darjeeling) ; Shiwaliks (outermost, youngest — duns: Dehradun, Kathmandu valley) Indo-Gangetic-Brahmaputra Plains ~2,400 km north-south band The world's largest alluvial plain; khadar (floodplain) vs bhangar (older, higher) ; regional names: Bhabar (coarse, sub-surface flow) & Terai (marshy) in UP–Uttar","chars":11072},{"nav":"gs-1/geography-world-india/pyqs","file":"geography-world-india-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/geography-world-india/pyqs/geography-world-india-pyqs.html","title":"Geography of India &amp; World — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography of India & World — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Geography of India & World — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2016) Which of the following hills/mountain ranges run from the north-east to the south-west ? (a) Aravalli (b) Satpura (c) Vindhya (d) Western Ghats Model answer: (a) — the Aravallis run SW-from-Delhi toward Gujarat; Western Ghats run NNW–SSE; Vindhya–Satpura run broadly east–west. Q2. (2017) Which one of the following is the correct sequence of the given hills starting from the north and going toward the south? (a) Nallamalai → Nilgiri → Javadi → Anamalai (b) Anamalai → Nilgiri → Javadi → Nallamalai (c) Nallamalai → Javadi → Nilgiri → Anamalai (d) Anamalai → Javadi → Nilgiri → Nallamalai Model answer: (c) — N.→S.: Nallamalai (Eastern Ghats, AP) → Javadi → Nilgiri (the tri-junction) → Anamalai (south of Palakkad gap's line). Q3. (2019) Consider the following pairs: River ↔ Origin Brahmaputra — near Lake Mansarovar ","chars":6020},{"nav":"gs-1/geography-world-india/short","file":"geography-world-india-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/geography-world-india/short-notes/geography-world-india-revision.html","title":"Geography of India &amp; World — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography of India & World — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"India's physiography (snap memory: \"H-P-P-C-I\")"},{"level":2,"text":"Rivers — the pattern"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate & water (India)"},{"level":2,"text":"India's resources (match-region cheat table)"},{"level":2,"text":"World resource anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"People & settlements"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick PYQ answers"}],"text":"Geography of India & World — Short Revision Notes One-liner India's geography runs north-wall → alluvial plains → peninsular shield → twin coasts , with a monsoon-fed, west-flowing-exception river system (Narmada/Tapti = rift-valley estuaries) — whose resources (Chotanagpur minerals, Mumbai High oil, Brahmaputra water) and world chokepoints (Malacca–Hormuz–Suez) shape its economy. India's physiography (snap memory: \"H-P-P-C-I\") Himalayas (Himadri–Himachal–Shiwalik) → Plains (Bhabar–Terai; Khadar–Bhangar) → Peninsula (Western Ghats 1,600 km, Anamudi 2,695 m; Aravalli = oldest folds; Narmada's rift valley) → Coasts (east = deltas; west = estuaries) → Islands (A&N w/ Barren Island volcano; Lakshadweep atolls). Rivers — the pattern Himalayan (perennial) : Indus (Panchnad — Jhelum/Chenab/Ravi/Beas/Sutlej), Ganga (Gangotri + Alaknanda + Yamuna), Brahmaputra (Mansarovar origin, Namcha Barwa U-t","chars":2883},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-heritage-culture/bullets","file":"heritage-culture-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-heritage-culture/bullet-points/heritage-culture-points.html","title":"Indian Heritage &amp; Culture — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Exam bullets"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank (for Mains)"}],"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap HERITAGE & CULTURE ├── Architecture │ ├── Nagara (N): shikhara, amalaka, kalasha → Khajuraho, Puri, Konark │ ├── Dravida (S): vimana + gopuram → Thanjavur, Mahabalipuram, Meenakshi │ ├── Vesara (Deccan): hybrid/stellate → Aihole, Pattadakal, Belur–Halebidu │ ├── Rock-cut: Ajanta | Ellora (Kailasa) | Elephanta | Badami │ ├── Buddhist: Sanchi stupa, Bharhut, Amaravati, Sarnath capital │ └── Indo-Islamic: Qutb Minar, Humayun's Tomb (charbagh) → Taj, Gol Gumbaz, CSMT ├── Painting: Ajanta → Mughal (Mansur) → Rajput (Bani Thani-Kishangarh) → Deccan → Company → Bengal School (Bharat Mata) → modernists (PAG 1947, Sher-Gil, Jamini Roy) ├── Dance (8): Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Mohiniyattam, Odissi, Kuchipudi, Manipuri, Sattriya ├── Music: Hindustani (Dhrupad→Khayal, gharanas) vs Carnatic (Trinity: Tyagaraja–Dikshitar–Shyama Sastri) ├─","chars":2444},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-heritage-culture/notes","file":"indian-heritage-culture.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-heritage-culture/detailed-notes/indian-heritage-culture.html","title":"Indian Heritage &amp; Culture — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Architecture — the stone grammar of Indian history"},{"level":3,"text":"1.1 Temple styles (the prelims-dominant trio)"},{"level":3,"text":"1.2 Islamic & Indo-Islamic architecture"},{"level":3,"text":"1.3 UNESCO World Heritage (latest asks)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Painting schools"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Music & dance"},{"level":3,"text":"Music"},{"level":3,"text":"Eight classical dance forms (memorise region pairs)"},{"level":3,"text":"Theatre"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Literature, languages & scripts"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Fairs, festivals & crafts"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Heritage preservation machinery"},{"level":2,"text":"7. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Indian Heritage & Culture — art, architecture, literature, painting, music, dance, languages, fairs & festivals Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture (heavily weighted: 6–10 questions/yr) 1. Architecture — the stone grammar of Indian history 1.1 Temple styles (the prelims-dominant trio) Style Region Identifiers Signature examples Nagara (North) Gangetic belt & Deccan-north Curvilinear shikhara rising over the garbhagriha; amalaka (cogged stone wheel) on top; kalasha finial; generally no elaborate boundary walls Kandariya Mahadeva & Lakshmana (Khajuraho, Chandela) ; Jagannath, Puri ; Sun Temple, Konark (chariot-rath of Surya; UNESCO) Dravida (South) TN, parts of AP–Kerala–Karnataka Pyramidal vimana of receding storeys; gopurams as monumental gateways (later dominate); temple towns Brihadisv","chars":8001},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-heritage-culture/pyqs","file":"heritage-culture-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-heritage-culture/pyqs/heritage-culture-pyqs.html","title":"Indian Heritage &amp; Culture — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Prelims MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Mains questions"}],"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — PYQs Prelims-style MCQs with model answers + Mains questions with skeleton outlines. Questions that are exact PYQs are marked [PYQ] ; practice originals are explicitly hedged in the answer lines. A. Prelims MCQs Q1. The amalaka is a feature found on: (a) The gopuram of Dravida temples (b) The top of a Nagara temple's shikhara (c) The prayer hall of a vihara (d) The pillar base of Ashokan columns Model answer: (b). The amalaka is the cogged, ribbed stone wheel crowning the curvilinear shikhara of Nagara temples (e.g., Khajuraho, Konark). Dravida temples mark entrances with gopurams instead. Q2. Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched? Kailasa temple, Ellora — Pallava dynasty Lingaraja temple — Kalinga (Nagara) style Brihadisvara temple — Rajaraja I, 11th century (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 2 and 3 only (c) 1 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (b). S","chars":4139},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-heritage-culture/short","file":"heritage-culture-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-heritage-culture/short-notes/heritage-culture-revision.html","title":"Indian Heritage &amp; Culture — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Rapid-fire anchors"},{"level":3,"text":"Architecture"},{"level":3,"text":"UNESCO WH sites (India, ~44)"},{"level":3,"text":"Painting"},{"level":3,"text":"Classical dances (8) — state pairs"},{"level":3,"text":"Music"},{"level":3,"text":"Literature & languages"},{"level":3,"text":"Festivals / UNESCO intangible (India)"},{"level":3,"text":"Crafts / GI"},{"level":3,"text":"Heritage institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Indian Heritage & Culture — Revision Notes One-hour refresh for Prelims + Mains recall anchors. Rapid-fire anchors Architecture Nagara : curvilinear shikhara, amalaka, kalasha → Khajuraho (Chandela), Puri Jagannath, Konark Sun Temple. Dravida : pyramidal vimana, gateway gopurams → Brihadisvara (Rajaraja I, 1010), Shore temples (Pallava), Meenakshi. Vesara : Deccan hybrid, stellate plans → Aihole/Pattadakal (Chalukya), Belur–Halebidu (Hoysala, UNESCO 2023). Rock-cut: Ajanta (Buddhist), Ellora (34 caves, Kailasa monolithic Rashtrakuta), Elephanta (Trimurti). Stupas: Sanchi toranas (Shunga/Satavahana additions). Ashoka: Sarnath capital; Prinsep deciphered Brahmi 1837. Indo-Islamic: dome-arch-minaret; Qutb complex → Alai Darwaza; Humayun's Tomb (charbagh) → Taj; Gol Gumbaz (Bijapur); CSMT (Indo-Saracenic, UNESCO). UNESCO WH sites (India, ~44) Newest: Charaideo Moidams, Assam (2024) ; Hoysala","chars":3104},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-society/bullets","file":"indian-society-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-society/bullet-points/indian-society-points.html","title":"Indian Society — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Society — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank (enrich answers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Sticky facts (write under answers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer-skeleton shortcuts"}],"text":"Indian Society — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap INDIAN SOCIETY (GS-1) ├── Structure: unity-in-diversity + hierarchy/difference (Dumont/Betéille) ├── Caste │ ├── Varna vs jati; Art17 → PCR 1955 → SC/ST PoA 1989 (+2015/2018) │ ├── Quotas: SC15 · ST7.5 · OBC27 · EWS10 (103rd) · Women 33% (106th, 2023, post-delimitation) │ ├── Indra Sawhney 50% ceiling → Maratha 2021 reaffirm │ └── Caste census: Bihar 2023 → Census 2027 debate ├── Gender │ ├── NFHS-5: TFR 2.0; sex ratio ~1020 (methods caveat); FLFPR ~37–41% │ ├── Law: POCSO 2012 · POSH 2013 · DV 2005 · BNS 2023 │ └── SHGIF economy: ~9 crore women, Lakhpati Didi targets ├── Population & urbanisation │ ├── Demographic dividend window to ~2040; median age ~28; elderly 2050 gap │ ├── Urban share 15→31→35%+; 6 cities >10 mn; 74th Amdt fiscal weakness │ └── Programmes: Smart Cities · AMRUT 2.0 · PMAY-U 2.0 · HAPs 23+ states ├── Communalism │ ├── Arc:","chars":2764},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-society/notes","file":"indian-society.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-society/detailed-notes/indian-society.html","title":"Indian Society — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Society — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Structural features of Indian society"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Caste — structure and dynamics"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Classical frame"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Change vectors"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Women & gender"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Population & demographics"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Urbanisation"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Communalism, regionalism & secularism"},{"level":3,"text":"Communalism"},{"level":3,"text":"Regionalism"},{"level":3,"text":"Secularism"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Globalisation & Indian society"},{"level":2,"text":"8. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Indian Society — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Indian Society — salient features, diversity, women, population, urbanisation, communalism, regionalism, secularism, globalisation's effects 1. Structural features of Indian society Unity in diversity : single civilisational frame (shared epics, pilgrimage circuits) + layered plurality (language, religion, caste). Hierarchy (Dumont: pure/impure axis) braided with difference (Betéille: a society of unequal accommodations). Ascriptive stratification persists (caste, kinship); modern equality codes (constitutional + electoral) reshape it. Rural-urban continuum: village republics (Metcalfe's romanticism contested) → census 2027 shift will update urban share (~35% currently estimated). 2. Caste — structure and dynamics 2.1 Classical frame Varna (4-fold) vs jati (thousands of endogamous units); regional jati mapping differs sharply.","chars":6686},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-society/pyqs","file":"indian-society-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-society/pyqs/indian-society-pyqs.html","title":"Indian Society — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Society — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Prelims-style MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Mains questions with skeletons"}],"text":"Indian Society — PYQs Mains-heavy; society questions almost always appear in GS-1 (3–6 questions/yr approx). Outlines original; [PYQ-pattern] marks near-actual wording. A. Prelims-style MCQs Q1. Which constitutional article abolishes \"untouchability\" and forbids its practice? (a) Article 14 (b) Article 15 (c) Article 17 (d) Article 19 Model answer: (c). Article 17; enforcement via PCR Act 1955 → SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act 1989. Q2. The Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam corresponds to which constitutional amendment? (a) 102nd (b) 103rd (c) 105th (d) 106th Model answer: (d). The 106th Amendment (2023) institutionalises 33% women's reservation in Lok Sabha and state assemblies (post-delimitation). 103rd was EWS; 105th restored states' OBC list power. Q3. Which statements about NFHS-5 (2019–21) are correct? India's TFR fell to 2.0. All states are below replacement fertility. (a) 1 only ","chars":4135},{"nav":"gs-1/indian-society/short","file":"indian-society-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/indian-society/short-notes/indian-society-revision.html","title":"Indian Society — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Society — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Population numbers (NFHS-5 unless noted)"},{"level":2,"text":"Urban anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Gender scorecard"},{"level":2,"text":"Communalism toolkit"},{"level":2,"text":"Regionalism table"},{"level":2,"text":"Globalisation impacts (use as quotes-free anchors)"},{"level":2,"text":"Framing devices for answers"}],"text":"Indian Society — Revision Notes High-yield numbers + framing moves for GS-1 society answers. One-liner anchors Diversity framed by unity in diversity with hierarchy/difference twin reading (Dumont vs Betéille). Untouchability abolished : Art.17; poles of enforcement: PCR 1955 → SC/ST PoA 1989 (+2015/2018 amendments). Quota architecture: SC 15 · ST 7.5 · OBC 27 · EWS 10 (103rd Amdt 2019) ; women's reservation 33% (106th Amdt 2023, post-delimitation) . The 50% tableau: Indra Sawhney v UoI (1992) ceiling; Maratha judgment (2021) re-affirmed ceiling, states barred from breaching post-102nd Amdt. Population numbers (NFHS-5 unless noted) TFR 2.0 (below replacement 2.1) : Bihar 3.4 highest; Kerala TN ~1.8. Sex ratio: 943 (Census 2011); NFHS-5 ~1020 (methods caveat). Median age ~28; working-age bulge peak ~2040. Migration: ~450 mn internal migrators (2011 base); 2020 lockdown exodus. Urban ancho","chars":2622},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/constitutional-development/bullets","file":"constitutional-development-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/constitutional-development/bullet-points/constitutional-development-points.html","title":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Must-know labels (traps to avoid)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap CONSTITUTIONAL DEVELOPMENT 1773–1947 │ ┌──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┐ COMPANY RULE 1773–1858 CROWN RULE 1858–1947 TRANSFER 1946–47 │ │ │ Regulating Act 1773 Crown takeover 1858 Cabinet Mission 1946 Pitt's Act 1784 Councils Acts Direct Action Day Charter Acts 1861 · 1892 · 1909 Interim Government 1813 · 1833 · 1853 Montagu–Chelmsford 1919 Constituent Assembly GoI Act 1935 Independence Act 1947 Cabinet Mission Bullet facts (prelims gold) Regulating Act 1773 — Governor of Bengal becomes Governor-General of Bengal (first: Warren Hastings); Council of Four ; Supreme Court at Calcutta 1774 (Elijah Impey first Chief Justice). Pitt's India Act 1784 — the Board of Control ; Company's commercial vs political wings separated. Charter Act 1813 — ended trade monopoly (tea and China trade excepted); ₹ 1 lakh an","chars":3405},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/constitutional-development/notes","file":"constitutional-development.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/constitutional-development/detailed-notes/constitutional-development.html","title":"Constitutional Development &amp; Government of India Acts (1773–1947) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development & Government of India Acts (1773–1947) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Why Study This?"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Company Period Regulations (1773–1858)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Regulating Act, 1773"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Pitt's India Act, 1784"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Charter Acts (1793–1853)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Crown Takes Over (1858–1909)"},{"level":3,"text":"3.1 Government of India Act, 1858"},{"level":3,"text":"3.2 The Moderates' foothold — Indian Councils Act 1892"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Minto–Morley Reforms (Government of India Act 1909)"},{"level":3,"text":"Key provisions"},{"level":3,"text":"Evaluation"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Montagu–Chelmsford Reforms (Government of India Act 1919)"},{"level":3,"text":"Background"},{"level":3,"text":"Key provisions"},{"level":3,"text":"Why it failed"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Government of India Act 1935 — The Last Colonial Blueprint"},{"level":3,"text":"Background"},{"level":3,"text":"Key provisions (memorise the architecture)"},{"level":3,"text":"What independent India borrowed"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Final Acts (1946–47)"},{"level":3,"text":"Cabinet Mission Plan (June 1946)"},{"level":3,"text":"Indian Independence Act, 1947"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Gradations table — the twelve turning points"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Constitutional Development & Government of India Acts (1773–1947) — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History · Polity & Constitution Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture; GS Paper II — Polity (historical background) 1. Why Study This? The 174 years between Plassey (1757) and Independence (1947) produced a continuous line of constitutional reworking by the British that became the institutional scaffolding of independent India. Many features of the Constitution of India (1950) — federal division of powers, the office of Governor, the Public Service Commission, the Supreme Court's structural ancestry, the three legislative lists, the pattern of parliamentary procedure — were inherited, adapted or consciously rejected from the Government of India Acts of 1919 and 1935 . 2. Company Period Regulations (1773–1858) 2.1 Regulating Act, 1773 Fi","chars":12591},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/constitutional-development/pyqs","file":"constitutional-development-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/constitutional-development/pyqs/constitutional-development-pyqs.html","title":"Constitutional Development — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Constitutional Development — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2011) The Regulating Act of 1773 provided for which of the following? The establishment of a Supreme Court at Calcutta A Governor-General of Bengal A Board of Control in London (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1 and 3 only (c) 2 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a) — the Regulating Act created the Governor-General of Bengal (Warren Hastings) and the Supreme Court at Calcutta (1774); the Board of Control came with Pitt's India Act (1784) . Q2. (2015) Which of the following acts is associated with the separation of legislative and executive functions of the Governor-General's Council for the first time? (a) Charter Act 1813 (b) Charter Act 1833 (c) Charter Act 1853 (d) Government of India Act 1858 Model answer: (c) — The Charter Act 1853 introduced the separate legislative wing in the Governor-General's Council. Q3. (2017)","chars":6008},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/constitutional-development/short","file":"constitutional-development-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/constitutional-development/short-notes/constitutional-development-revision.html","title":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"The golden timeline (memorise in order)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick match-up (prelims favourites)"},{"level":2,"text":"What independent India borrowed from the 1935 Act"},{"level":2,"text":"Famous labels (traps)"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liners for the exam"}],"text":"Constitutional Development 1773–1947 — Short Revision Notes One-liner From the Regulating Act (1773) to the Indian Independence Act (1947), British constitutional reforms gradually created the federal, parliamentary and administrative architecture that independent India's Constitution inherited and adapted. The golden timeline (memorise in order) Act Year One-line significance Regulating Act 1773 First parliamentary control; Governor-General of Bengal; Supreme Court at Calcutta (1774) Pitt's India Act 1784 Board of Control; crown supervision; commercial vs political split Charter Act 1813 End of trade monopoly; ₹ 1 lakh for education ; Crown sovereignty asserted Charter Act 1833 Company purely administrative; first Governor-General of India (Bentinck) ; Law Commission (Macaulay → IPC) Charter Act 1853 Separate legislative wing; open competition for ICS Government of India Act 1858 Crown ","chars":3354},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/east-india-company/bullets","file":"advent-of-europeans-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/east-india-company/bullet-points/advent-of-europeans-points.html","title":"Advent of Europeans — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap ADVENT OF EUROPEANS 1757–1857 │ ┌─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐ EUROPEAN POWERS BENGAL BASE EXPANSION TOOLS │ │ │ Portuguese 1498 Plassey 1757 Subsidiary Alliance Dutch (Beder 1759) Buxar 1764 Doctrine of Lapse English 1600 Dual Govt 1765–72 \"Misgovernment\" → Awadh Danish (minor) Famine 1770 Battles & Treaties French (Paris 1763) Bullet facts Vasco da Gama — Calicut, 1498 — sea route to India. East India Company chartered — 1600 (reigned over British Indian trade from 1612). Job Charnock founded Calcutta — 1690 . Plassey — 23 June 1757 ; betrayed by Mir Jafar and Jagat Seths . Buxar — 22 October 1764 ; combined forces of Mir Qasim + Shuja-ud-Daula + Shah Alam II defeated. Treaty of Allahabad — 1765 ; Diwani of Bengal, Bihar, Orissa. Dual Government (Clive, 1765–72) ; ended by Warren Hastings. Bengal Famine, 1770 — es","chars":1944},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/east-india-company/notes","file":"advent-of-europeans.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/east-india-company/detailed-notes/advent-of-europeans.html","title":"Advent of Europeans &amp; East India Company (1757–1857) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company (1757–1857) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Why Europeans Came"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The East India Company's Rise in Bengal"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Why Bengal?"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Conflict with Siraj-ud-Daula"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Battle of Plassey (23 June 1757)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.4 Battle of Buxar (23 October 1764)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.5 Dual Government (1765–72)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The Anglo-Mysore Wars (1767–1799)"},{"level":3,"text":"Hyder Ali"},{"level":3,"text":"Tipu Sultan (\"Tiger of Mysore\")"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Subsidiary Alliance & Doctrine of Lapse — Tools of Expansion"},{"level":3,"text":"Subsidiary Alliance (systematised by Lord Wellesley , 1798–1805)"},{"level":3,"text":"Doctrine of Lapse (systematised under Lord Dalhousie , 1848–56)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Anglo-Maratha Wars (1775–1818)"},{"level":3,"text":"Background"},{"level":3,"text":"First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–82)"},{"level":3,"text":"Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–05) — under Lord Wellesley"},{"level":3,"text":"Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817–19) — under Lord Hastings"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Anglo-Sikh Wars (1845–46 and 1848–49)"},{"level":3,"text":"Background"},{"level":3,"text":"First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–46)"},{"level":3,"text":"Second Anglo-Sikh War (1848–49)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Land Revenue Systems (summary table)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Key Figures & Facts (one-liners for prelims)"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Timeline (1757–1849)"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Why the EIC Succeeded Where Rivals Failed (Mains-ready answer)"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Anti-company Resistance Before 1857 (context for \"culmination\" argument)"},{"level":2,"text":"12. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company (1757–1857) — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History (mid-18th century onward) Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture 1. Why Europeans Came The discovery of the sea route to India by Vasco da Gama (1498, landing at Calicut) opened direct European trade in spices, textiles and indigo without Arab or Venetian middlemen. Five European powers built trading settlements in India: Power Arrival Main settlements Fate Portuguese 1498 (da Gama); Estado da Índia under Albuquerque Goa (1510), Daman, Diu, Hooghly Declined after 17th c.; confined to Goa Dutch (VOC) 1602 company; Coromandel base Pulicat, Surat, Nagapatnam, Chinsurah Defeated by English at Beder (1759) ; withdrew to Indonesia English (EIC) 1600 charter; 1608 Surat Surat, Madras (1639), Bombay (1668), Calcutta (1690, Job Charnock) Became territ","chars":12988},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/east-india-company/pyqs","file":"advent-of-europeans-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/east-india-company/pyqs/advent-of-europeans-pyqs.html","title":"Advent of Europeans &amp; EIC — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans & EIC — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice queue"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans & EIC — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2005) Who among the following was the first Governor-General of Bengal? (a) Lord Cornwallis (b) Lord Wellesley (c) Warren Hastings (d) Lord Bentinck Model answer: (c) — Warren Hastings was the first Governor-General of Bengal (appointed in 1773 under the Regulating Act ). Q2. (2015) The Subsidiary Alliance was first accepted by the ruler of: (a) Mysore (b) Hyderabad (c) Awadh (d) Poona Model answer: (b) — The Nizam of Hyderabad was the first to accept the Subsidiary Alliance in 1798 under Lord Wellesley. Q3. (2018) Which one of the following statements is not correct about Lord Dalhousie's Doctrine of Lapse ? (a) It was used to annex Jhansi and Nagpur. (b) It was used against the state of Awadh. (c) It was used to annex Sambalpur and Baghat. (d) It led to protests that contributed to the 1857 uprising. Model answer: ","chars":4759},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/east-india-company/short","file":"advent-of-europeans-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/east-india-company/short-notes/advent-of-europeans-revision.html","title":"Advent of Europeans &amp; East India Company — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Europeans in order"},{"level":2,"text":"The two foundation battles"},{"level":2,"text":"Key dates & events"},{"level":2,"text":"Four expansion wars (chronological)"},{"level":2,"text":"Tools of annexation"},{"level":2,"text":"Land-revenue hook"},{"level":2,"text":"Must-know quotes"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company — Short Revision Notes One-liner European trading companies (Portuguese, Dutch, English, French) gradually gave way to East India Company dominance after the Battles of Plassey (1757) and Buxar (1764) , which converted trade privileges into territorial power between 1757 and 1857. Europeans in order Portuguese (1498, Vasco da Gama) → Dutch → English (1600) → Danish → French (1664) The two foundation battles Battle Year Between Consequence Plassey 1757 Robert Clive vs Siraj-ud-Daula (betrayed by Mir Jafar) Company controls Bengal politics Buxar 1764 Hector Munro vs combined armies (Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-Daula, Shah Alam II) Diwani of Bengal (1765) — legitimacy to collect revenue Buxar > Plassey as the real foundation of British rule in India. Key dates & events Battle of Wandiwash (1760) — French decisively defeated in Carnatic War. 1757 — Plassey → ","chars":2313},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/bullets","file":"gandhian-era-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/bullet-points/gandhian-era-points.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap GANDHIAN ERA 1919–1947 │ ┌──────┬───────────┼───────────┬───────────┐ NON-COOP. CIVIL DISO. QUIT INDIA CONSTITUTIONAL PARALLEL 1920–22 1930–34 1942 MILESTONES STREAMS │ │ │ │ │ Khilafat Dandi 1930 \"Do or Die\" GoI Act 1935 Revolutionaries Khadi Dharasana Parallel govs 1937 elections (Bhagat Singh) Chauri Peshawar Ballia 1942 Cripps INA / Azad Hind Chaura Chittagong Tamluk Cabinet Mission Peasant/Labour withdrawal Irwin Pact Satara Mountbatten movements Poona Pact Independence Bullet facts (prelims gold) Special session, Calcutta (Sept 1920) — non-cooperation resolution; Nagpur (Dec 1920) — new Congress constitution; \"attainment of swaraj by peaceful and legitimate means\". C. R. Das — first president of the Swaraj Party (with Motilal Nehru ); died 1925. Bardoli Satyagraha (1928) — Vallabhbhai Patel; title \"Sardar\" from the women ","chars":3440},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/notes","file":"gandhian-era-1919-1947.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/detailed-notes/gandhian-era-1919-1947.html","title":"Gandhian Era: Non-Cooperation to Quit India (1919–1947) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era: Non-Cooperation to Quit India (1919–1947) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The Gandhian Breakthrough (1919–1920)"},{"level":3,"text":"Context"},{"level":3,"text":"The Nagpur Session (December 1920)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–22)"},{"level":3,"text":"Launch"},{"level":3,"text":"Programme (constructive + coercive)"},{"level":3,"text":"Popular response (regional varieties)"},{"level":3,"text":"Chauri Chaura and withdrawal (February 1922)"},{"level":3,"text":"Evaluation"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The Swaraj Party (1923–27)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Simon Commission to the Civil Disobedience Movement (1927–1934)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.1 Simon Commission (1927)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.2 The Nehru Report (1928) and the Calcutta session (December 1928)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.3 Lahore session (December 1929) — Purna Swaraj"},{"level":3,"text":"4.4 The Salt Satyagraha (1930)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.5 Civil Disobedience — regional peaks"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Gandhi–Irwin Pact to the Government of India Act (1935)"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Government of India Act 1935"},{"level":3,"text":"Key provisions (memorise)"},{"level":3,"text":"Evaluation"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Congress Ministries, World War II and the Cripps Offer (1937–1942)"},{"level":3,"text":"The 1937–39 ministries"},{"level":3,"text":"Subhas Chandra Bose breaks with Congress (1939)"},{"level":3,"text":"The Cripps Mission (March–April 1942)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Quit India Movement (1942) — The August Revolution"},{"level":2,"text":"9. War's End — Wavell Plan and the Cabinet Mission (1945–46)"},{"level":3,"text":"Wavell Plan (June 1945)"},{"level":3,"text":"Cabinet Mission Plan (March–June 1946)"},{"level":3,"text":"Direct Action Day (16 August 1946)"},{"level":3,"text":"Interim Government (September 1946) and Constituent Assembly"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Mountbatten Plan and Independence (1947)"},{"level":3,"text":"Gandhiji's last months"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Parallel histories to remember"},{"level":2,"text":"12. The Gandhian synthesis (Mains-ready answer)"},{"level":2,"text":"13. Timeline (1919–1947)"},{"level":2,"text":"14. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Gandhian Era: Non-Cooperation to Quit India (1919–1947) — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History (mid-18th century onward) Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture 1. The Gandhian Breakthrough (1919–1920) Context Rowlatt Act (March 1919) — \"no vakil, no daleel, no appeal\" provoked the first nationwide satyagraha. Jallianwala Bagh (13 April 1919) — destroyed moderate faith in British justice. Khilafat question (1919–24) — the fate of the Ottoman Caliph after WWI; Gandhi supported the Khilafat movement to bind Hindus and Muslims. Government of India Act 1919 (dyarchy) — introduced ministerial responsibility in the provinces' \"transferred\" subjects, but the British held the vital portfolios. The Nagpur Session (December 1920) Congress adopted a new constitution : reorganised into linguistic provinces, a Working Committee of 15, and changed","chars":16995},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/pyqs","file":"gandhian-era-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/pyqs/gandhian-era-pyqs.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2014) The Swaraj Party was formed by: (a) Motilal Nehru and C. R. Das (b) Gandhi and Patel (c) Tilak and Besant (d) Jinnah and Liaquat Ali Khan Model answer: (a) — Formed in 1923 after the Gaya session deadlock, to contest council elections and wreck the reforms from within. Q2. (2015) Who founded the Forward Bloc ? (a) Jawaharlal Nehru (b) Subhas Chandra Bose (c) C. Rajagopalachari (d) M. N. Roy Model answer: (b) — Subhas Chandra Bose founded the Forward Bloc in May 1939 after resigning the Congress presidency at Tripuri. Q3. (2016) With reference to the Khilafat movement , consider the following statements: It aimed at restoring the Caliphate of Turkey. It was supported by the Congress under Gandhi's leadership. It merged with the Non-Cooperation movement in a joint struggle. Which of the statements given above is/are","chars":5899},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/short","file":"gandhian-era-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/short-notes/gandhian-era-revision.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Three mass movements (memorise dates & triggers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Milestones between movements"},{"level":2,"text":"Match: leaders to moments (prelims traps)"},{"level":2,"text":"One-line answers to famous quotations"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Short Revision Notes One-liner From Nagpur 1920 to the midnight of 15 August 1947 , Gandhi turned Congress into a mass movement through three great campaigns — Non-Cooperation (1920–22), Civil Disobedience (1930–34), Quit India (1942) — which broke the legitimacy of British rule. Three mass movements (memorise dates & triggers) Movement Launch Trigger Key symbol Ends Non-Cooperation 1 Aug 1920 (Calcutta special session) Rowlatt Act, Jallianwala Bagh, Khilafat Khadi & charkha Chauri Chaura (5 Feb 1922) — withdrawn at Bardoli Civil Disobedience 12 Mar 1930 (Dandi March) Simon Commission & Purna Swaraj resolution (Lahore 1929) Salt Gandhi–Irwin Pact (Mar 1931); Communal Award / Poona Pact (1932); withdrawal (May 1934) Quit India 8 Aug 1942 (Gowalia Tank) Cripps Mission failure \"Do or Die\" Crushed by 1943–44 Milestones between movements 1923: Swaraj Party (Das & Moti","chars":3229},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/bullets","file":"national-movement-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/bullet-points/national-movement-points.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap INDIAN NATIONAL MOVEMENT 1885–1919 │ ┌────────────┬──────────────┼──────────────┬────────────┐ BACKGROUND MODERATES EXTREMISTS THE MUSLIM GANDHIAN 1885–1905 1905–1919 LEAGUE DAWN │ │ │ (1906) 1915–19 A.O. Hume Naoroji Tilak Salimullah Champaran 1917 Ilbert Bill Mehta Pal Aga Khan Ahmedabad 1918 Poona Sabha Gokhale Lajpat Rai Lucknow Pact Kheda 1918 Indian Assoc. Banerjee Aurobindo (1916) Rowlatt 1919 Ranade │ Swadeshi & Boycott (partition of Bengal) Bullet facts (prelims gold) First Congress — Bombay, 28 December 1885 ; 72 delegates ; W. C. Bonnerjee first president. First Bengali president — Dadabhai Naoroji (Calcutta 1886). First Muslim president — Rahimtullah M. Sayani (Calcutta 1896); Vande Mataram sung for the first time. Moderate achievements — Indian Councils Act 1892; economic critique (drain theory); proving that ","chars":2716},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/notes","file":"national-movement-1885-1919.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/detailed-notes/national-movement-1885-1919.html","title":"National Movement: Moderates, Extremists &amp; Gandhian Dawn (1885–1919) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement: Moderates, Extremists & Gandhian Dawn (1885–1919) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Background — Why Congress Emerged (1885)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Moderate Era (1885–1905)"},{"level":3,"text":"Character"},{"level":3,"text":"Achievements"},{"level":3,"text":"Limitations"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The Extremist / Assertive Nationalist Era (1905–1919)"},{"level":3,"text":"Why the shift after 1905?"},{"level":3,"text":"The Partition & Swadeshi Movement (1905–08)"},{"level":3,"text":"Extremist leaders"},{"level":3,"text":"The Surat Split (December 1907)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Muslim League & the Khilafat Context (1906–1919)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Foreign Policy & International Environment"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Key Congress Sessions (1885–1919)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Gandhian Dawn (1915–1919)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Most-asked PYQ angles"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"National Movement: Moderates, Extremists & Gandhian Dawn (1885–1919) — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History (mid-18th century onward) Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture 1. Background — Why Congress Emerged (1885) By the 1880s three converging trends made a national political body inevitable: Factor Explanation Modern intelligentsia English-educated middle class (moderate lawyers, journalists, doctors, teachers) — fluent in a common political vocabulary of liberty, rights and representation. Economic critique Dadabhai Naoroji's drain theory ( Poverty and Un-British Rule in India , 1901); R. C. Dutt's Economic History of India — provided an economic case against colonialism. Political exclusion Ilbert Bill agitation (1883–84) — Europeans' privileged position in criminal trials exposed racial discrimination; educated Indians realis","chars":10473},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/pyqs","file":"national-movement-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/pyqs/national-movement-pyqs.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2014) The Partition of Bengal was abolished in the year: (a) 1905 (b) 1911 (c) 1919 (d) 1947 Model answer: (b) — King George V's Delhi Durbar (December 1911) announced the annulment of the partition; effective 1912. Q2. (2015) The Lucknow Pact (1916) resulted in which of the following? (a) Congress gave up its demand for Swaraj (b) Congress accepted separate electorates for Muslims (c) The Muslim League merged with the Congress (d) Provincial councils gained full autonomy Model answer: (b) — Under the Lucknow Pact the Congress accepted separate electorates for Muslims — a tactical compromise engineered by Tilak and Jinnah. Q3. (2016) With reference to the Surat Split (1907) , consider the following statements: The Extremists wanted the Calcutta (1906) resolutions on swadeshi and boycott confirmed. The Moderates wal","chars":5594},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/short","file":"national-movement-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/short-notes/national-movement-revision.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Three phases (memorise the years)"},{"level":2,"text":"Milestone timeline"},{"level":2,"text":"Two leader sets (match for prelims)"},{"level":2,"text":"Key Prelims particulars"},{"level":2,"text":"Most-asked answers (one-sentence versions)"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Short Revision Notes One-liner From the moderate petitions of 1885 to the boycott of 1905 and the reunion at Lucknow (1916), Indian nationalism evolved from prayer to mass action — and Gandhi's three campaigns (1917–18) readied the country for the 1920 mass movement. Three phases (memorise the years) Moderates (1885–1905) — petitions, prayer, English liberalism. Leaders: Naoroji, Mehta, Gokhale, Banerjee, Ranade. Extremists / Assertive (1905–1919) — partition of Bengal (1905) → Swadeshi, boycott, national education; leaders: Tilak, Pal, Lajpat Rai, Aurobindo. Gandhian dawn (1915–19) — return from South Africa (Jan 1915); Champaran (1917), Ahmedabad (1918), Kheda (1918); Rowlatt Satyagraha (1919). Milestone timeline Year Event 1885 (28 Dec) First Congress at Bombay — W. C. Bonnerjee presiding 1905 (16 Oct) Partition of Bengal effective — Curzon 1906 Muslim Le","chars":3011},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/revolt-1857/bullets","file":"revolt-1857-mindmap.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/revolt-1857/bullet-points/revolt-1857-mindmap.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap structure"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap structure REVOLT OF 1857 │ ┌──────────┬─────────┼─────────┬──────────┐ CAUSES LEADERS SPREAD FAILURE RESULTS │ │ │ │ │ P-E-S-M Delhi Meerut→ No unity GoI Act Kanpur Delhi No vision 1858 Lucknow Awadh Limited Crown rule Jhansi Rohilkhand spread Army Arrah Bundelkhand reorg. │ Divide & Bihar (Kunwar Rule Singh) Bullet facts 10 May 1857 — revolt begins at Meerut ; sepoys march to Delhi 12 May 1857 — Delhi captured; Bahadur Shah Zafar proclaimed Emperor 29 March 1857 — Mangal Pandey at Barrackpore (34th Native Infantry) 1856 — Awadh annexed; Religious Disabilities Act Doctrine of Lapse — Satara, Jhansi, Nagpur, Sambalpur, Jaitpur, Udaipur Enfield cartridge — greased with cow/pig fat (trigger) Kunwar Singh — the 80-year-old zamindar of Jagdishpur (Arrah) Begum Hazrat Mahal — led Awadh in the name of son Birjis Qadr Tantia Tope — Nana Sahib'","chars":1310},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/revolt-1857/notes","file":"revolt-1857.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/revolt-1857/detailed-notes/revolt-1857.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Nature of the Revolt"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Causes"},{"level":3,"text":"Political (Doctrine of Lapse + annexations)"},{"level":3,"text":"Economic"},{"level":3,"text":"Socio-Religious"},{"level":3,"text":"Military (immediate cause)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Beginning and Spread (Key Centres)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Why the Revolt Failed"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Consequences"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History (mid-18th century onward) Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture 1. Nature of the Revolt The Revolt of 1857 was the first major, large-scale uprising against British rule in India. Historians debate its character: Historian / School View V. D. Savarkar Called it the First War of Indian Independence British historians (Seeley, Kaye) Dismissed it as a mere sepoy mutiny R. C. Majumdar A sepoy mutiny that grew into a peasant revolt ; not a national war Marxist historians A feudal reaction — the ancien régime fighting to preserve its privileges S. N. Sen (official centenary history) It was not a national movement, but its leaders were not all reactionaries 2. Causes Political (Doctrine of Lapse + annexations) Doctrine of Lapse by Lord Dalhousie — adopted sons could not inherit; annexa","chars":5248},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/revolt-1857/pyqs","file":"revolt-1857-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/revolt-1857/pyqs/revolt-1857-pyqs.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQ"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice queue"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2014) Which of these statements about the Revolt of 1857 is/are correct? It was confined to the Bengal Army. It spread to the Bombay and Madras Armies. It was supported by most of the princely states. Select the answer: (a) 1 only (b) 1 and 2 (c) 2 and 3 (d) None of the above Model answer: (d) — The revolt was largely confined to the Bengal Army; the Bombay and Madras armies remained loyal, and only some princely states (Jhansi, Awadh) joined. Q2. (2018) Who among the following was associated with the suppression of the Revolt of 1857? (a) Lord Dalhousie (b) Lord Canning (c) Lord Mayo (d) Lord Ripon Model answer: (b) — Canning was Governor-General (1856–58) and first Viceroy (1858–62); Dalhousie had left in 1856. Q3. (2020) The Doctrine of Lapse was used to annex which of the following states? Satara 2. Jhansi 3. Nagpur 4. Myso","chars":2809},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/revolt-1857/short","file":"revolt-1857-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/revolt-1857/short-notes/revolt-1857-revision.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Causes (memory hook: P-E-S-M)"},{"level":2,"text":"Key figures (match)"},{"level":2,"text":"Why it failed"},{"level":2,"text":"Consequences (memory hook: C-A-R-P-P)"},{"level":2,"text":"Most-asked PYQ angles"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Short Revision Notes One-liner First major uprising against British rule (started 10 May 1857, Meerut) — ended Company rule; Crown took over in 1858. Causes (memory hook: P-E-S-M) P olitical — Doctrine of Lapse, Awadh annexation (1856) E conomic — ruin of artisans, heavy revenue, dispossession of talukdars S ocio-religious — greased cartridges, missionary fears, Sati abolition M ilitary — loss of batta, General Service Enlistment Act (1856), no promotions Key figures (match) Leader Centre Bahadur Shah Zafar Delhi Nana Sahib / Tantia Tope Kanpur Begum Hazrat Mahal Lucknow Rani Lakshmibai Jhansi (with Hugh Rose as suppressor) Kunwar Singh Arrah, Bihar Khan Bahadur Khan Bareilly Mangal Pandey Barrackpore (34th NI, 29 Mar 1857) Why it failed No all-India leadership · limited spread (Punjab/Bombay/Bengal calm) · modern intelligentsia opposed · feudal rivalries · British resou","chars":1526},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/bullets","file":"socio-religious-reforms-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/bullet-points/socio-religious-reforms-points.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims traps highlighted)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap SOCIO-RELIGIOUS REFORM MOVEMENTS │ ┌───────────────────┴───────────────────┐ REFORMIST REVIVALIST │ │ Brahmo Samaj 1828 (Roy) Arya Samaj 1875 (Dayananda) Young Bengal (Derozio) Deoband 1867 (Nanautavi) Prarthana Samaj (Pandurangs) Wahabi (Sayyid Ahmad Barelvi) Satyashodhak Samaj (Phule) Singh Sabha (Punjab) Aligarh Movement (Sir Syed) Akali movement Ramakrishna Mission (Vivekananda) Theosophical Society (Blavatsky/Olcott) │ LEGAL OUTPUT │ Sati abolition (1829) Widow Remarriage Act (1856) Civil Marriage Act (1872) Age of Consent Act (1891) Sarda Act (1929) Bullet facts (prelims traps highlighted) Atmiya Sabha (1815) → formalised as Brahmo Samaj (1828) — Raja Rammohan Roy . Roy's paper: Samvad Kaumudi (weekly Bengali newspaper, 1821). Adi Brahmo Samaj (Debendranath Tagore, 1839) — the original line after the 1866 split. Keshab C","chars":2518},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/notes","file":"socio-religious-reforms.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/detailed-notes/socio-religious-reforms.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements (19th–early 20th Century) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements (19th–early 20th Century) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Why Reform Movements Emerged"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Reformist Movements (chronological)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Brahmo Samaj (1828) — Raja Rammohan Roy"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Young Bengal Movement (late 1820s–1830s)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Prarthana Samaj (1863/1867) — Bombay"},{"level":3,"text":"2.4 Satyashodhak Samaj (1873) — Jyotiba Phule"},{"level":3,"text":"2.5 Theosophical Society"},{"level":3,"text":"2.6 Aligarh Movement — Sir Syed Ahmad Khan"},{"level":3,"text":"2.7 Ramakrishna Mission (1897) — Swami Vivekananda"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Revivalist Movements"},{"level":3,"text":"3.1 Arya Samaj (1875) — Swami Dayananda Saraswati"},{"level":3,"text":"3.2 Deoband Movement (1867)"},{"level":3,"text":"3.3 Wahabi / Walliullah Movement"},{"level":3,"text":"3.4 Singh Sabha & Akali movements (Punjab)"},{"level":3,"text":"3.5 Parsi & smaller movements"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Women and Social Legislation"},{"level":3,"text":"Key reformers"},{"level":3,"text":"Legislative milestones (memorise the years)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Why This Movement Matters (Mains-ready synthesis)"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Quick-memorise table (reformist vs revivalist)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements (19th–early 20th Century) — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Modern Indian History · Indian Heritage & Culture Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — History & Indian Culture 1. Why Reform Movements Emerged By the early 19th century Indian society faced a crisis of legitimacy: Colonial critique — missionaries (e.g., William Carey, Alexander Duff ) and British officials mocked caste, sati, female illiteracy and idolatry as signs of \"degradation,\" giving urgency to reform. Print and modern education — after 1835 (Macaulay's Minute) a new class of English-educated Indians engaged with European rationalism, liberalism and humanism. Economic and social distress — peasant dislocation, ruin of artisans and growing widow and child-marriage problems made old institutions appear oppressive. Rediscovery of texts — Western orientalists (William Jones, Co","chars":12636},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/pyqs","file":"socio-religious-reforms-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/pyqs/socio-religious-reforms-pyqs.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reforms — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice queue"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2016) Which one of the following books is not associated with the socio-religious reforms of the 19th century? (a) Satyarth Prakash (b) Gulamgiri (c) Hind Swaraj (d) Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq Model answer: (c) — Hind Swaraj (1909) is Gandhi's political text. Satyarth Prakash (Dayananda), Gulamgiri (Phule) and Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq (Sir Syed) are all reform-era works. Q2. (2020) Who among the following founded the Atmiya Sabha (1815) ? (a) Debendranath Tagore (b) Raja Rammohan Roy (c) Keshab Chandra Sen (d) Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar Model answer: (b) — Roy founded Atmiya Sabha in 1815 ; it evolved into the Brahmo Samaj in 1828 . Q3. (2018) Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched? Brahmo Samaj — 1828 Arya Samaj — 1875 Ramakrishna Mission — 1897 Theosophical Society — 1882 (India HQ) (a) 1 only (b) 1 and 2 only (c) 1, 2 a","chars":4648},{"nav":"gs-1/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/short","file":"socio-religious-reforms-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/short-notes/socio-religious-reforms-revision.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Two strands (memorise)"},{"level":2,"text":"Founders & dates (the essential table)"},{"level":2,"text":"Legal timeline (memorise years)"},{"level":2,"text":"Can't-miss one-liners"},{"level":2,"text":"For Mains answers (remember 3 bullet points)"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Short Revision Notes One-liner 19th–early 20th century Indian reformers responded to colonial critique by reinterpreting tradition ( reformist ) or defending it militantly ( revivalist ) — effectively creating the modern public sphere that birthed the national movement. Two strands (memorise) Strand Motto Example Reformist Critique & modernise Brahmo Samaj, Prarthana Samaj, Aligarh, Ramakrishna Mission Revivalist Assert & defend Arya Samaj, Deoband, Wahabi, Singh Sabha Founders & dates (the essential table) Movement Founder Year Location Brahmo Samaj Raja Rammohan Roy 1828 Calcutta Young Bengal Henry Vivian Derozio c. 1826 Calcutta Adi Brahmo Samaj Debendranath Tagore 1839 Calcutta Prarthana Samaj Dadoba / Atmaram Pandurang 1867 Bombay Satyashodhak Samaj Jyotiba Phule 1873 Pune Arya Samaj Swami Dayananda Saraswati 1875 Bombay → Lahore Theosophical Socie","chars":2145},{"nav":"gs-1/physical-geography/bullets","file":"physical-geography-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/physical-geography/bullet-points/physical-geography-points.html","title":"Physical Geography — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":3,"text":"Earth interior & tectonics"},{"level":3,"text":"Climatology"},{"level":3,"text":"Oceanography"},{"level":3,"text":"Biogeography"},{"level":2,"text":"Diagram recall for Mains answers"}],"text":"Physical Geography — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap PHYSICAL GEOGRAPHY │ ┌────────┬──────────┼──────────┬──────────┐ GEOMORPH. CLIMATOLOGY OCEANOGRAPHY BIOGEOGRAPHY │ │ │ │ interior atmosphere relief/salinity soils plates heat budget currents/tides vegetation landforms monsoon fisheries hotspots quakes cyclones El Niño/IOD Bullet facts (prelims gold) Earth interior & tectonics SIAL (continental crust) floats over SIMA (oceanic crust). Moho — crust/mantle; Gutenberg — mantle/core; Lehmann — outer/inner core. S-waves can't pass through liquid → outer core is liquid. P-waves travel through all media — fastest. Wegener (1912) — continental drift; mechanism came from Hess (1962) sea-floor spreading & the plate-tectonics formalism (1967). 7 major plates ; India on the Indo-Australian plate colliding with Eurasian → the Himalayas . Deccan Traps (~66 million years) — Réunion hotspot — basal","chars":3181},{"nav":"gs-1/physical-geography/notes","file":"physical-geography.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/physical-geography/detailed-notes/physical-geography.html","title":"Physical Geography: Geomorphology, Climatology, Oceanography &amp; Biogeography — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography: Geomorphology, Climatology, Oceanography & Biogeography — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"PART A — GEOMORPHOLOGY: Earth's Interior & Landforms"},{"level":3,"text":"1. Earth's interior (source → structure)"},{"level":3,"text":"2. Continental Drift → Plate Tectonics"},{"level":3,"text":"3. Plate boundaries — the classification every map answer needs"},{"level":3,"text":"4. Landforms & rock cycle"},{"level":3,"text":"5. Earthquakes & tsunamis (India-relevant)"},{"level":2,"text":"PART B — CLIMATOLOGY: Atmosphere, Monsoon & Cyclones"},{"level":3,"text":"1. Atmosphere — structure & composition"},{"level":3,"text":"2. Heat & pressure engine"},{"level":3,"text":"3. The Indian Monsoon — mechanism (Mains-worthy depth)"},{"level":3,"text":"4. Cyclones — the twin classes"},{"level":3,"text":"5. Climate classification (Köppen quick code)"},{"level":3,"text":"6. Climate-change signals in the physical system (gate way to GS-3 environment)"},{"level":2,"text":"PART C — OCEANOGRAPHY"},{"level":3,"text":"1. Ocean-floor relief (depth order, from the coast)"},{"level":3,"text":"2. Temperature & salinity"},{"level":3,"text":"3. Currents (memorise direction + temperature)"},{"level":3,"text":"4. Tides & India's coast"},{"level":2,"text":"PART D — BIOGEOGRAPHY (soils & natural vegetation)"},{"level":3,"text":"Soils"},{"level":3,"text":"Natural vegetation & biomes"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick reference — PYQ angles on this topic"},{"level":2,"text":"Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Physical Geography: Geomorphology, Climatology, Oceanography & Biogeography — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · Physical Geography: Geomorphology, Climatology, Oceanography Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Geography · Environment & Ecology PART A — GEOMORPHOLOGY: Earth's Interior & Landforms 1. Earth's interior (source → structure) Layer Depth Composition & facts Crust (Lithosphere's top) 0–~35 km (continental <70 km under mountains; oceanic crust ~5–10 km) SIAL (continental: silica + aluminium) floating over SIMA (silica + magnesium, the ocean-floor material) Mohorovičić discontinuity (Moho) crust–mantle boundary P-wave velocity jumps Mantle ~35–2900 km Upper mantle + Asthenosphere (the plastic \"soggy\" layer on which plates move, ~100–250 km) + lower mantle; Gutenberg discontinuity at the base (mantle–core) Outer core 2900–~5150 km Liquid iron-nickel (generates the Ear","chars":12633},{"nav":"gs-1/physical-geography/pyqs","file":"physical-geography-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/physical-geography/pyqs/physical-geography-pyqs.html","title":"Physical Geography — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Physical Geography — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2015) In the structure of planet Earth, below the mantle, the core is mainly made up of which one of the following? (a) Aluminium (b) Chromium (c) Iron (d) Silicon Model answer: (c) — the core is iron-nickel; the crustal rocks are dominated by silica, aluminium (SIAL) and magnesium (SIMA). Q2. (2016) With reference to the ocean currents , consider the following: The Gulf Stream is a warm current. The Oyashio (Kurile) is a cold current. The Benguela is a warm current. Which of the statements given above is/are correct? (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 2 and 3 only (c) 1 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a) — the Benguela is a cold current off SW Africa (the companion fact: it underlies the Namib's aridity). Q3. (2013) Which one of the following pairs correctly contains the phenomenon and the region of maximum impact? (a) El Niño ","chars":5724},{"nav":"gs-1/physical-geography","file":"README.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/physical-geography/README.html","title":"Physical Geography (GS 1)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography (GS 1)"}],"text":"Physical Geography (GS 1) Geomorphology · Climatology · Oceanography · Biogeography · World physical geography. Tip: keep map practice in diagrams/ and use pyqs/ for map-based questions.","chars":186},{"nav":"gs-1/physical-geography/short","file":"physical-geography-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/physical-geography/short-notes/physical-geography-revision.html","title":"Physical Geography — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Earth interior (memorise order & discontinuities)"},{"level":2,"text":"Plate boundaries (example table)"},{"level":2,"text":"Monsoon mechanism (5-step answer frame)"},{"level":2,"text":"Pattern facts to never miss"},{"level":2,"text":"Oceanography one-liners"},{"level":2,"text":"Biogeography quick palette"},{"level":2,"text":"Recent tools (show current awareness)"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Physical Geography — Short Revision Notes One-liner From the crust core-ward (crust → mantle → outer core → inner core) to the atmosphere skyward (troposphere → exosphere), physical geography explains landforms through plate tectonics , rainfall through the monsoon machine (ITCZ + jets + ENSO/IOD), and ocean behaviour through currents, salinity and tides . Earth interior (memorise order & discontinuities) Crust (SIAL over SIMA) → Moho → Mantle (asthenosphere ~100–250 km) → Gutenberg → liquid outer core (S-waves stop here — the proof) → Lehmann → solid inner core . Plate boundaries (example table) Boundary Process Example Divergent new crust at ridges/rifts Mid-Atlantic Ridge; East African Rift Convergent fold mtns / trenches / arcs Himalayas ; Andes; Japan Transform lateral slide San Andreas Fault Hotspot gift: Deccan Traps (~66 Ma, Réunion hotspot) — the world's largest basalt province ","chars":3188},{"nav":"gs-1","file":"README.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/README.html","title":"General Studies I — Heritage, Geography &amp; Society","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"General Studies I — Heritage, Geography & Society"}],"text":"General Studies I — Heritage, Geography & Society Six topic folders (Modern History carries six sub-chapters); every topic has the standard 5-section set ( detailed-notes/ · short-notes/ · bullet-points/ · diagrams/ · pyqs/ ) — 46 chapters and 11 SVG diagrams/maps in all: modern-history/ — sub-chapters: east-india-company , revolt-1857 , socio-religious-reforms , national-movement-1885-1919 , gandhian-era-1919-1947 , constitutional-development indian-heritage-culture/ — architecture, sculpture & painting, music/dance/theatre, literature, festivals & fairs, UNESCO lists world-history/ — industrial revolution, ideologies, world wars, redrawal of boundaries, decolonisation, cold war indian-society/ — diversity, caste, women, population, poverty, urbanisation, communalism, regionalism, globalisation physical-geography/ — geomorphology, climatology, oceanography, biogeography geography-world-","chars":1236},{"nav":"gs-1/world-history/bullets","file":"world-history-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/world-history/bullet-points/world-history-points.html","title":"World History — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World History — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner frames for Mains"},{"level":2,"text":"Date pattern drills"}],"text":"World History — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap WORLD HISTORY (Mains GS-1) ├── Industrial Revolution (Britain c.1760–1840) │ ├── Preconditions: enclosures, empire capital, coal+iron, patents │ ├── Tech: Jenny 1764 · water frame · mule 1779 · Watt 1769 · Rocket 1829 │ └── Effects: classes (bourgeois/proletariat), urbanisation, deindustrialised colonies ├── Ideologies │ ├── Liberalism (Locke/Mill) → 1688/1776/1789 │ ├── Socialism (Owen, Saint-Simon) → welfare states │ ├── Marxism-Leninism (1848 Manifesto → 1917) │ ├── Fascism/Nazism (1922 Rome; 1933 Berlin) → WWII + Holocaust │ └── Keynesian social democracy (1936 → Beveridge 1942) ├── Colonisation & Decolonisation │ ├── Scramble (Berlin 1884–85) → indirect vs direct rule │ ├── Asia: 1947 India | 1954 Dien Bien Phu | Bandung 1955 │ └── Africa: 1957 Ghana · 1960 Year of Africa · 1962 Algeria · 1975 Portuguese · 1980 Zimbabwe · 1990 Namibia · 19","chars":2500},{"nav":"gs-1/world-history/notes","file":"world-history.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/world-history/detailed-notes/world-history.html","title":"World History — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World History — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Industrial Revolution — the hinge of modernity"},{"level":3,"text":"1.1 Why Britain first (c. 1760–1840)"},{"level":3,"text":"1.2 Consequences"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Political philosophies — the ideological triad"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Colonisation & decolonisation"},{"level":3,"text":"3.1 Mechanisms of colonisation"},{"level":3,"text":"3.2 Decolonisation waves"},{"level":2,"text":"4. World War I & II — causes, course contours, consequences"},{"level":3,"text":"WWI (1914–18)"},{"level":3,"text":"Interwar capitalism crisis"},{"level":3,"text":"WWII (1939–45)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Cold War & its end"},{"level":2,"text":"6. 21st-century continuations"},{"level":2,"text":"7. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"World History — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 1 (Mains) · World History — Industrial Revolution, World Wars, colonisation & decolonisation, political philosophies (communism, capitalism, socialism) and their effects Note: World History is Mains-only (not in Prelims syllabus explicitly) — orient for quality, not trivia. 1. Industrial Revolution — the hinge of modernity 1.1 Why Britain first (c. 1760–1840) Agricultural revolution (enclosure, new crops) freed labour. Empire & mercantilist income : slave-trade & colonial revenue feeding capital accumulation. Institutions : property rights, patent system, joint-stock banking. Coal + iron geography ; island insulation from Continental wars. Tech cluster : Spinning Jenny (Hargreaves 1764) , water frame (Arkwright) , mule (Crompton 1779) , steam engine (Newcomen → Watt 1769/76) ; railways: Stephenson's Rocket (1829), Manchester-Liverpool (183","chars":5884},{"nav":"gs-1/world-history/pyqs","file":"world-history-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/world-history/pyqs/world-history-pyqs.html","title":"World History — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World History — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style MCQs (occasionally asked via IR/culture crossovers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains questions with skeletons"}],"text":"World History — PYQs Mains-oriented practice with skeletons. Exact-PYQ style; answer outlines are original work, hedged where memory-based. Prelims-style MCQs (occasionally asked via IR/culture crossovers) Q1. The \"Bandung Conference\" (1955) primarily led to: (a) Formation of the UN (b) The Afro-Asian solidarity network and a precursor to NAM (c) The SEATO defence pact (d) The Bandung bond market Model answer: (b). Bandung (Indonesia, April 1955) — 29 Asian-African states; later Belgrade 1961 formalised NAM. (Note: NAM founding leaders: Nehru, Tito, Nasser, Sukarno, Nkrumah.) [PYQ pattern] Q2. Operation Overlord refers to: (a) Battle of Stalingrad (b) Normandy landings (D-Day) (c) Attack on Pearl Harbor (d) Battle of Midway Model answer: (b). 6 June 1944 Allied invasion of Normandy. Q3. The phrase \"war-guilt clause\" is associated with which article of the Treaty of Versailles? (a) Articl","chars":3878},{"nav":"gs-1/world-history/short","file":"world-history-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/world-history/short-notes/world-history-revision.html","title":"World History — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World History — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Industrial Revolution chain"},{"level":2,"text":"Ideology quick-table"},{"level":2,"text":"Colonisation → decolonisation"},{"level":2,"text":"WWI skeleton (MANIA)"},{"level":2,"text":"WWII skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Cold War arc"},{"level":2,"text":"21st century echoes"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer frames"}],"text":"World History — Revision Notes Fast recall sheet for Mains answers — cause-effect chains, not dates. Industrial Revolution chain Agricultural revolution + empire revenue + property rights + coal/iron + banking → textile tech (Jenny 1764 → mule 1779 → Watt 1769) → factory system → urban labour class → imperial realignment (deindustrialising colonies) → second wave (steel/chemicals/electricity, 1870–1914) → Fordism/Taylorism → mass consumption states. Ideology quick-table Liberalism: rights, limited govt (Locke, Mill) — revolutions of 1688/1776/1789. Socialism: exploitation critique (Owen, Fourier) → welfare states. Marxism-Leninism: 1848 Manifesto → 1917 Bolshevik → planned economies. Fascism/Nazism: Mussolini (1922 march on Rome), Hitler (1933 enabling act). Keynes: 1936 General Theory → post-war welfare settlements (Beveridge 1942). Colonisation → decolonisation Berlin 1884–85 scramble ","chars":2559},{"nav":"gs-2/constitution-polity/bullets","file":"constitution-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/constitution-polity/bullet-points/constitution-points.html","title":"Indian Constitution — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Constitution — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Indian Constitution — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap CONSTITUTION OF INDIA │ ┌──────────┬──────────┼──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐ MAKING PREAMBLE PART III PART IV AMENDMENT FEDERAL 1946–49 JUSTICE FUND. DPSP Art 368 UNION/STATE LIBERTY RIGHTS (+FD) + BASIC (3 Lists) EQUALITY (Arts STRUCTURE FRATERNITY 12–35) Bullet facts (prelims gold) M. N. Roy (1934) first proposed a Constituent Assembly. First meeting — 9 December 1946 ; Muslim League boycotted initially. Sachchidananda Sinha — temporary chairman; Rajendra Prasad permanent. Objectives Resolution — Nehru, 13 Dec 1946 . Drafting Committee — 29 August 1947 ; B. R. Ambedkar chairman; B. N. Rau constitutional adviser. 2 years, 11 months, 18 days ; ~₹ 64 lakh; adopted 26 Nov 1949 , enforced 26 Jan 1950 . Original text: 395 articles, 22 parts, 8 schedules → now ~470 articles in 25 parts, 12 schedules. Preamble words added by 42nd (1","chars":2593},{"nav":"gs-2/constitution-polity/notes","file":"constitution-of-india.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/constitution-polity/detailed-notes/constitution-of-india.html","title":"Indian Constitution: Making, Features, Amendments &amp; Comparisons — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Constitution: Making, Features, Amendments & Comparisons — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Making of the Constitution (1934–1950)"},{"level":3,"text":"The idea and the demand"},{"level":3,"text":"Constituent Assembly — key dates & people"},{"level":3,"text":"Composition"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Sources of the Constitution (borrowed features)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Architecture of the Constitution"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Preamble"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Fundamental Rights (Part III, Articles 12–35)"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV, Arts 36–51)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Amendment of the Constitution (Art 368)"},{"level":3,"text":"Landmark amendment controversies — the \"Basic Structure\" doctrine"},{"level":3,"text":"Notable amendments (memorisation set)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Federalism & the Distribution of Powers"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Federalism — centres of contemporary debate (for Mains essays)"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Salient features — quick-comparison table (prelims favourites)"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Most-asked PYQ angles"},{"level":2,"text":"12. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Indian Constitution: Making, Features, Amendments & Comparisons — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 2 (Mains) · Constitution & Polity Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Polity & Governance; Essay (constitutional themes) 1. Making of the Constitution (1934–1950) The idea and the demand 1934 — M. N. Roy , a pioneer of the communist movement in India, first put forward the idea of a Constituent Assembly for India. 1935 — the Indian National Congress officially demanded a Constituent Assembly to frame the Constitution. 1938 — Jawaharlal Nehru declared that the Constitution of free India must be framed by a Constituent Assembly elected on the basis of adult franchise. 1940 — the demand was accepted in principle in the August Offer of the Viceroy Linlithgow . 1942 — the Cripps Proposals repeated the promise but were rejected. 1946 — the Cabinet Mission Plan finally produced the scheme for ","chars":18066},{"nav":"gs-2/constitution-polity/pyqs","file":"constitution-polity-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/constitution-polity/pyqs/constitution-polity-pyqs.html","title":"Indian Constitution — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Constitution — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Indian Constitution — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2015) The mind of the makers of the Constitution of India is reflected in which of the following? (a) The Preamble (b) The Fundamental Rights (c) The Directive Principles of State Policy (d) The Fundamental Duties Model answer: (a) — as stated in the Constituent Assembly debates, the Preamble encapsulates the philosophy and ideals of the Constitution-makers. Q2. (2016) Which one of the following statements is correct about the Objectives Resolution ? (a) It was moved by Sardar Patel. (b) It formed the basis of the Preamble. (c) It was adopted on 26 January 1950. (d) It declared India a socialist State. Model answer: (b) — moved by Jawaharlal Nehru on 13 December 1946 and adopted 22 January 1947 , it became the foundations of the Preamble. Q3. (2017) Which of the following features of the Constitution of India were borrowed","chars":7254},{"nav":"gs-2/constitution-polity/short","file":"constitution-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/constitution-polity/short-notes/constitution-revision.html","title":"Indian Constitution — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Constitution — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Making (memorise the dates)"},{"level":2,"text":"Borrowed features (prelims match-up)"},{"level":2,"text":"FRs (6) — after the 44th Amendment (1978) removed property rights"},{"level":2,"text":"Landmark cases"},{"level":2,"text":"Key amendments (don't miss the years)"},{"level":2,"text":"Federal design"},{"level":2,"text":"Current flashpoints (2025–26)"}],"text":"Indian Constitution — Short Revision Notes One-liner A borrowed-but-original synthesis adopted on 26 November 1949 , enforced on 26 January 1950 — the world's longest written constitution combining British parliamentary government, American judicial review, Irish DPSP, Canadian federalism and Indian innovations. Making (memorise the dates) M. N. Roy (1934) — first mooted a Constituent Assembly. Cabinet Mission Plan (1946) — Constituent Assembly elected by provincial assemblies. 9 December 1946 — first meeting; Sachchidananda Sinha temporary chairman; Rajendra Prasad the permanent president (11 Dec). Nehru, 13 December 1946 — Objectives Resolution (became the basis of the Preamble). B. R. Ambedkar — Chairman of the Drafting Committee (August 1947) . 26 November 1949 — adopted (Constitution Day from 2015); 26 January 1950 — enforced (commemorating Purna Swaraj Day, 26 January 1930 ). Borro","chars":3510},{"nav":"gs-2/governance-administration/bullets","file":"organs-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/governance-administration/bullet-points/organs-points.html","title":"Organs of Government — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Organs of Government — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":3,"text":"Executive"},{"level":3,"text":"Parliament"},{"level":3,"text":"Judiciary"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains one-liners"}],"text":"Organs of Government — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap THE UNION GOVERNMENT │ ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐ EXECUTIVE (52–78) PARLIAMENT (79–122) JUDICIARY (124–237) │ │ │ President Lok Sabha (543) Supreme Court VP (RS Chairman) Rajya Sabha (245) High Courts PM + CoM (≤15%) Money Bill (110) Subordinate AGI (76) Joint sitting (108) Writs: 32 (SC) | 226 (HC) Committees Collegium Bullet facts (prelims gold) Executive President elected by elected MPs + MLAs (incl. Delhi & Puducherry MLAs) — PR with single transferable vote. Value of MLA's vote = (State population ÷ total elected MLAs) ÷ 1000 (1971 census figures until the next delimitation). Art 74: President acts on advice — 44th Amendment allows returning advice once . Art 72 pardoning; Art 123 ordinances; Art 143 advisory references. VP elected by all MPs (elected + nominated); ex-officio Chairman of RS ; removal: RS resolut","chars":3002},{"nav":"gs-2/governance-administration/notes","file":"parliament-executive-judiciary.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/governance-administration/detailed-notes/parliament-executive-judiciary.html","title":"Governance &amp; Administration: Parliament, Executive, Judiciary — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance & Administration: Parliament, Executive, Judiciary — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Overview — India's Parliamentary Model"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Union Executive"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 President of India (Arts 52–62)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Vice-President (Arts 63–73)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Prime Minister & Council of Ministers (Arts 74–78)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Parliament (Arts 79–122)"},{"level":3,"text":"3.1 Structure"},{"level":3,"text":"3.2 Powers & functions"},{"level":3,"text":"3.3 Parliamentary sessions & motions"},{"level":3,"text":"3.4 Parliamentary committees (key ones)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Judiciary"},{"level":3,"text":"4.1 Supreme Court (Arts 124–147)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.2 High Courts (Arts 214–231)"},{"level":3,"text":"4.3 Subordinate courts"},{"level":3,"text":"4.4 Judicial controversies & reforms (Mains-critical)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Constitutional & statutory bodies supporting these organs"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Most-asked PYQ angles"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Governance & Administration: Parliament, Executive, Judiciary — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 2 (Mains) · Governance & Administration Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Polity & Governance 1. Overview — India's Parliamentary Model Westminster (parliamentary) model — executive responsible to the legislature; nominal + real executive distinction; majority-party rule; a civil service politically neutral in theory. Distribution : Union Executive (President + Vice-President + PM & Council of Ministers + Attorney-General: Arts 52–78 ); Parliament (President + two Houses: Arts 79–122 ); Judiciary (Supreme Court Arts 124–147 ; High Courts 214–231 ; subordinate courts 233–237 ). Checks built into design: bicameralism, federal division, independent judiciary, constitutional watchdogs — CAG (148–151), UPSC (315–323), Election Commission (324–329A), Finance Commission (280), GST Council (279","chars":11508},{"nav":"gs-2/governance-administration/pyqs","file":"governance-administration-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/governance-administration/pyqs/governance-administration-pyqs.html","title":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice queue"}],"text":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2015) Who/Which of the following is the custodian of the Constitution of India ? (a) The President of India (b) The Prime Minister of India (c) The Lok Sabha Secretariat (d) The Supreme Court of India Model answer: (d) — through Article 13 read with Articles 32 and 136, the Supreme Court interprets and enforces constitutional limits. Q2. (2016) What is the provision to safeguard the autonomy of the Supreme Court of India ? The judges are appointed by a non-political collegium procedure originating in judicial interpretation. Removal is by an address of Parliament with a special majority, on grounds of proved misbehaviour or incapacity. Salaries and allowances are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India. (a) 1 only (b) 2 and 3 only (c) 1 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (d) — all three protect judici","chars":7335},{"nav":"gs-2/governance-administration/short","file":"organs-of-government-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/governance-administration/short-notes/organs-of-government-revision.html","title":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"The three organs (snapshot)"},{"level":2,"text":"Match-the-fact (prelims traps)"},{"level":2,"text":"Landmark judgments (Mains)"},{"level":2,"text":"One-line Mains conclusions"}],"text":"Parliament · Executive · Judiciary — Short Revision Notes One-liner India has a Westminster-style parliamentary system where the President is the nominal head , the PM-headed Council of Ministers holds real power and is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha , and an integrated, independent judiciary with the Supreme Court at the apex acts as the Constitution's guardian. The three organs (snapshot) Organ Articles Key points Executive 52–78 President (electoral college; Art 74 — advice binding since the 42nd, one send-back by the 44th); Vice-President ex-officio chairman of RS ; PM & CoM (15% of LS limit — 91st Amendment, 2003) ; AGI (76) Parliament 79–122 LS (543) directly elected, 5 years; RS (245: 233 + 12 nominated) 6-year staggered, permanent; Money Bills only in LS (Art 110, Speaker's certificate); joint sitting (Art 108) thrice used Judiciary 124–237 SC (CJI + 33); writs 32 (SC)","chars":2759},{"nav":"gs-2/international-relations/bullets","file":"ir-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/international-relations/bullet-points/ir-points.html","title":"International Relations — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"International Relations — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"International Relations — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap INDIA'S FOREIGN POLICY │ ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐ DOCTRINES GREAT POWERS MULTILATERALISM │ │ │ Panchsheel 1954 US — iCET, LEMOA UN & UNSC reform (G4) NAM 1961 Russia — 1971 legacy G20 (India 2023, chair BRICS 2026) Look→Act East China — LAC, Galwan, SCO · BIMSTEC · QUAD Neighbourhood First Kazan '24 thaw I2U2 · IMEC · ISA SAGAR → MAHASAGAR Pk — Indus abeyance CDRI · GBA Bullet facts (prelims gold) Panchsheel — signed 29 April 1954 (India–China, Tibet trade). NAM — institutionalised Belgrade 1961 ; Bandung Conference 1955 the precursor. Gujral Doctrine — 1996–97; non-reciprocal generosity toward smaller neighbours. Look East 1992 → Act East 2014 (Myanmar, ASEAN, Indo-Pacific). Neighbourhood First (2014) ; SAGAR (2015) ; Indo-Pacific Oceans Initiative (2019) ; MAHASAGAR (2025) . Indo-US nuclear deal — 2005–08;","chars":3085},{"nav":"gs-2/international-relations/notes","file":"india-foreign-policy-ir.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/international-relations/detailed-notes/india-foreign-policy-ir.html","title":"International Relations: India&#x27;s Foreign Policy, Bilateral Ties &amp; Global Groupings — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"International Relations: India's Foreign Policy, Bilateral Ties & Global Groupings — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Foundations & vocabulary of Indian foreign policy"},{"level":3,"text":"Institutional set-up"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The great-power theatre"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 India–United States"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 India–Russia"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 India–China — the principal strategic challenge"},{"level":3,"text":"2.4 India–Pakistan"},{"level":3,"text":"2.5 Europe, Quad partners, the middle"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The neighbourhood (Neighbourhood First in practice)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Regional & mini-lateral groupings"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Global groupings & multilateral agenda"},{"level":3,"text":"United Nations"},{"level":3,"text":"G20"},{"level":3,"text":"Others"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Economic diplomacy & diaspora"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Current issues (Mains-ready, 2024–26)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Framework for IR mains answers"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"International Relations: India's Foreign Policy, Bilateral Ties & Global Groupings — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 2 (Mains) · International Relations: India & her neighbourhood; Bilateral, regional & global groupings; Indian diaspora Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Polity & Governance (IR questions); Essay (India's place in the world) Currency check: Updated to September 2026 . 1. Foundations & vocabulary of Indian foreign policy Concept Origin Content Panchsheel 29 April 1954 , India–China agreement on Tibet Five principles: mutual respect for sovereignty/territorial integrity; mutual non-aggression; non-interference; equality & mutual benefit; peaceful co-existence Non-Alignment (NAM) Bandung 1955 → Belgrade 1961 (Nehru, Tito, Nasser, Sukarno, Nkrumah) Strategic autonomy without military blocs; not neutrality — issue-based positions Gujral Doctrine 1996–97 (I. K. Gujral as ","chars":13264},{"nav":"gs-2/international-relations/pyqs","file":"international-relations-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/international-relations/pyqs/international-relations-pyqs.html","title":"International Relations — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"International Relations — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"International Relations — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2015) Consider the following pairs: Groupings in which India is a member — QUAD — India, US, Japan, Australia SCO — India joined in 2017 SAARC — headquartered in Kathmandu Which of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched? (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1 and 3 only (c) 2 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (d) — all three are correct (SCO accession Astana summit, June 2017; SAARC Secretariat is in Kathmandu). Q2. (2021) With reference to Panchsheel , consider the following statements: The five principles were first enumerated in the India–China agreement of 1954. They include \"mutual non-aggression\" and \"peaceful co-existence.\" They were declared by the UN General Assembly as the foundation of international law. (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1 and 3 only (c) 2 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a) — statement 3 is false;","chars":7004},{"nav":"gs-2/international-relations/short","file":"ir-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/international-relations/short-notes/ir-revision.html","title":"International Relations — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"International Relations — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Doctrines (memorise with years)"},{"level":2,"text":"Great-power snapshot"},{"level":2,"text":"Neighbourhood anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Groupings map"},{"level":2,"text":"UN & reform"},{"level":2,"text":"Diaspora"},{"level":2,"text":"Essay/Mantra one-liners"}],"text":"International Relations — Short Revision Notes One-liner India's foreign policy moved from Nehruvian non-alignment (Panchsheel 1954 → NAM) to multi-alignment with strategic autonomy — engaging QUAD, SCO, BRICS and the West simultaneously while keeping the Neighbourhood First (2014–) and Indo-Pacific (SAGAR 2015 → MAHASAGAR 2025) frames at the centre. Doctrines (memorise with years) Panchsheel (1954) · NAM (Belgrade 1961) (Bandung precursor 1955) · Gujral Doctrine (1996–97) · Look East 1992 → Act East 2014 · Neighbourhood First 2014 · SAGAR 2015 · Indo-Pacific Oceans Initiative 2019 · MAHASAGAR 2025 · Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam (G20 motto 2023) . Great-power snapshot USA — civil nuclear deal 2005–08 (NSG waiver 2008); foundational pacts LEMOA (2016), COMCASA (2018), BECA (2020) ; iCET 2023 ; tariff clash of 2025 → trade negotiations continue. Russia — 1971 Treaty; annual summits since 2000; S-","chars":2880},{"nav":"gs-2","file":"README.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/README.html","title":"General Studies II — Governance, Polity, Social Justice &amp; IR","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"General Studies II — Governance, Polity, Social Justice & IR"}],"text":"General Studies II — Governance, Polity, Social Justice & IR Four topic folders, each carrying the standard 5-section set ( detailed-notes/ · short-notes/ · bullet-points/ · diagrams/ · pyqs/ ): constitution-polity/ — Constitution: sources, FR/DPSP/FD, amendments, federal structure governance-administration/ — Parliament, executive, judiciary; bodies & accountability social-justice/ — health, education, welfare architecture, vulnerable sections international-relations/ — India's foreign policy, groupings, neighbourhood, global governance After adding/editing files: node cli/generate.mjs from upsc-portal/ re-indexes them.","chars":628},{"nav":"gs-2/social-justice/bullets","file":"social-justice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/social-justice/bullet-points/social-justice-points.html","title":"Social Justice — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Social Justice — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Social Justice — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap SOCIAL JUSTICE │ ┌────────┬──────────┼──────────┬──────────┐ FOOD EMPLOYMENT HEALTH EDUCATION VULNERABLE SECURITY & WORK & HRD SECTIONS │ │ │ │ │ NFSA'13 MGNREGA'05 NHM'13 RTE'09 PoA'89 PMGKAY →VB-G RAM PM-JAY'18 NEP'20 FRA'06 POSHAN G'25/26 ABDM'21 PM SHRI RPwD'16 PM AJAY 70+ '24 5+3+3+4 JANMAN'23 e-Shram NIPUN PoA'18 Bullet facts (prelims gold) MGNREGA (2005) — 100 days; demand-driven; ~100% central wage funding; repealed 1 July 2026 . VB-G RAM G Act (2025, assent 21 Dec 2025) — 125 days from 1 July 2026 ; 60:40 Centre-State split (general), 90:10 (NE/Himalayan); 4 work categories; 60-day agri-season pause ; Viksit Gram Panchayat Plans aligned with PM Gati Shakti ; normative allocations using the 16th Finance Commission formula . NFSA (2013) — 75% rural / 50% urban coverage; 5 kg/person/month at ₹3/2/1; Antyodaya 35 kg/family ; State","chars":2267},{"nav":"gs-2/social-justice/notes","file":"social-justice-welfare.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/social-justice/detailed-notes/social-justice-welfare.html","title":"Social Justice: Welfare Schemes, Health, Education &amp; HRD — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Social Justice: Welfare Schemes, Health, Education & HRD — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Constitutional foundation of social justice"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The welfare delivery architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Employment & livelihoods"},{"level":3,"text":"MGNREGA (2005) → VB-G RAM G Act (2025), in force from 1 July 2026"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Food, nutrition & the social safety net"},{"level":3,"text":"National Food Security Act (2013)"},{"level":3,"text":"PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (PMGKAY)"},{"level":3,"text":"Nutrition"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Health"},{"level":3,"text":"Architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Education & HRD"},{"level":3,"text":"Right to education"},{"level":3,"text":"National Education Policy 2020 — the current superstructure"},{"level":3,"text":"The HRD frame (for GS-2 answers)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Vulnerable sections — the statutory core"},{"level":3,"text":"Adivasis & tribal governance flashpoints"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Gender & the political-economy frame (Mains analytics)"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Recent developments to cite (2024–26)"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Model-answer sensory checklist"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Social Justice: Welfare Schemes, Health, Education & HRD — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 2 (Mains) · Social Justice: Welfare Schemes, Health, Education, Human Resource Development Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Polity & Governance; Essay (development & equity themes) Currency check: Updated to September 2026 — includes the VB-G RAM G Act, 2025 replacing MGNREGA from 1 July 2026 . 1. Constitutional foundation of social justice Provision Content Preamble Justice — social, economic, political; equality of status and opportunity FRs Arts 14–16 (equality & reservation), 17 (social justice against untouchability), 21 (life incl. livelihood, health, education — judicially read), 21A (elementary education) , 23–24 (exploitation) DPSP Arts 38, 39, 41 (work), 42 (just & humane work), 43 (living wage), 43A, 45 (early childhood care), 46 (promotion of SC/ST/OBC educational & economic int","chars":12439},{"nav":"gs-2/social-justice/pyqs","file":"social-justice-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/social-justice/pyqs/social-justice-pyqs.html","title":"Social Justice — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Social Justice — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Social Justice — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2017) With reference to the National Food Security Act (NFSA) 2013 , consider the following statements: It covers up to 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population. It provides 5 kg of foodgrains per person per month at ₹3/2/1 per kg. Which of the statements given above is/are correct? (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (c) — both statements are the familiar statutory entitlements (Priority Households; Antyodaya gets 35 kg/family). Q2. (2019) Which of the following is not an objective of the Right to Education Act, 2009 ? (a) Free and compulsory education to children between 6 and 14 years (b) 25% reservation for disadvantaged groups in private schools (c) Board examination after Class 10 (d) Norms for teacher-pupil ratios Model answer: (c) — RTE covers elementary education (Classes 1–8); the Class 10","chars":6484},{"nav":"gs-2/social-justice/short","file":"social-justice-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-2-polity-governance-ir/social-justice/short-notes/social-justice-revision.html","title":"Social Justice &amp; Welfare — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Social Justice & Welfare — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Constitutional spine"},{"level":2,"text":"The master table (schemes → what to remember)"},{"level":2,"text":"Vulnerable-section statute kit (memorise)"},{"level":2,"text":"Judgments to cite"},{"level":2,"text":"The evaluation template for any scheme answer"}],"text":"Social Justice & Welfare — Short Revision Notes One-liner India's welfare state is constitutionally grounded (Preamble, FRs 14–16, 21, 21A, DPSP 38–47) and delivered through a JAM-enabled, rights-instruments-and-missions architecture — whose rural-employment spine shifted from MGNREGA (100-day entitlement) to VB-G RAM G (125-day allocation-based guarantee) from 1 July 2026 . Constitutional spine Art 14–16 equality & reservations · Art 17 untouchability · Art 21 / 21A life & elementary education. DPSP 38–47 welfare state instructions; Art 46 SC/ST/OBC interests. The master table (schemes → what to remember) Scheme Year One-line fact MGNREGA 2005 100 days guaranteed rural wage work, demand-driven; repealed 1 July 2026 VB-G RAM G 2025 (force 2026) 125 days ; 60:40 funding (general states), 90:10 NE/Himalayan; 4 work categories (water security, rural infra, livelihoods, weather mitigation); ","chars":2604},{"nav":"gs-3/agriculture-food/bullets","file":"agriculture-food-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/agriculture-food/bullet-points/agriculture-food-points.html","title":"Agriculture &amp; Food Security — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap AGRICULTURE & FOOD SECURITY │ ┌─────────┬────────────┼────────────┬─────────┐ PRICE & WATER & MARKETING & RISK & FOOD SUPPORT IRRIGATION INFRA CREDIT SECURITY │ │ │ │ │ MSP (23 PMKSY '15 APMC reform PMFBY '16 NFSA '13 crops; Atal Bhujal e-NAM KCC '98 PMGKAY (free) 1.5×A2+FL) Ken-Betwa FPO 10K Crop debt ONORC CACP→ Groundwater Agri Inf. waiver PDS/FCI Cabinet (≈89% share) Fund ₹1L politics WTO Bali clause Bullet facts (prelims gold) CACP recommends; Cabinet (CCEA) decides — MSP announced each kharif/rabi season. Formula: MSP ≥ 1.5 × (A2+FL) — 2018-19 — A2 = paid-out costs; FL = imputed family labour ; farmers demand the C2 (comprehensive cost) base and a law. 23 crops : 14 kharif + 6 rabi + copra + toria + jute (raw). Procurement geography : Punjab & Haryana supply the lion's share of FCI rice/wheat. PM-AASHA (2018) — PSS (","chars":3149},{"nav":"gs-3/agriculture-food/notes","file":"agriculture-and-food-security.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/agriculture-food/detailed-notes/agriculture-and-food-security.html","title":"Agriculture &amp; Food Security: MSP, Irrigation, Marketing, Subsidies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture & Food Security: MSP, Irrigation, Marketing, Subsidies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Why agriculture matters for UPSC answers (anchors to cite)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Green Revolution & its afterlife"},{"level":2,"text":"3. MSP & procurement — the core exam topic"},{"level":3,"text":"Mechanism"},{"level":3,"text":"Debate (Mains-frame)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The 2020 farm laws episode (Mains essay fodder)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Marketing & infrastructure"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Irrigation & water"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Subsidies & fertilizer"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Crop insurance & risk"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Livestock, fisheries, horticulture, allieds"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Food security's legal & administrative spine"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Recent developments (2024–26 checklist)"},{"level":2,"text":"12. Quick PYQ anchors (Prelims)"},{"level":2,"text":"13. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Agriculture & Food Security: MSP, Irrigation, Marketing, Subsidies — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 3 (Mains) · Agriculture & Food Security — MSP, irrigation, marketing, subsidies Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Economy & Geography; Essay (farmer welfare themes) Currency check: Updated to September 2026 (includes VB-G RAM G rural-livelihoods context & 2025–26 agricultural cycle). 1. Why agriculture matters for UPSC answers (anchors to cite) Share of GVA : ~ 16–17% (FY25) — but employs ~46% of the workforce (PLFS) → the central structural mismatch: low productivity per worker. Land fragmentation : average operational holding ~1.08 ha (Agriculture Census 2015-16); ~86% of holdings are small/marginal (<2 ha) . Population fed : ~1.45 billion with net foodgrains availability assured through the PDS + NFSA safety net. Export footprint : among the world's largest exporters of rice (la","chars":10095},{"nav":"gs-3/agriculture-food/pyqs","file":"agriculture-food-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/agriculture-food/pyqs/agriculture-food-pyqs.html","title":"Agriculture &amp; Food Security — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2013) In India, which of the following can be considered as Government procurement at Minimum Support Price ? Procurement of foodgrains by the FCI Price deficiency payments to farmers Purchase of agricultural commodities at minimum notified price by designated agencies (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1 and 3 only (c) 2 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (b) — 2 (price deficiency payment) is a market-difference mechanism (under PDPS), not procurement. Procurement happens by FCI/designated state agencies at the notified MSP. Q2. (2015) Which one of the following is the largest producer of milk in the world ? (a) China (b) India (c) USA (d) Brazil Model answer: (b) — India (Operation Flood legacy; ~230+ million tonnes). Q3. (2016) With reference to MSP for foodgrains , consider the following: MSP is announced by the Departm","chars":5852},{"nav":"gs-3/agriculture-food/short","file":"agriculture-food-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/agriculture-food/short-notes/agriculture-food-revision.html","title":"Agriculture &amp; Food Security — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Anchors (numbers to quote in any answer)"},{"level":2,"text":"MSP & procurement"},{"level":2,"text":"Farm laws circa 2020"},{"level":2,"text":"Water & soil"},{"level":2,"text":"Insurance & credit"},{"level":2,"text":"Allied sectors"},{"level":2,"text":"Food security spine"},{"level":2,"text":"2025–26 hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Agriculture & Food Security — Short Revision Notes One-liner Agriculture feeds 1.45 billion from farms averaging ~1.1 ha (86% small/marginal) — its policy spine is MSP + procurement + NFSA , and its water spine is groundwater (≈89% of irrigation) under stress . Anchors (numbers to quote in any answer) GVA share ~ 16–17% ; employment share ~ 46% . Average holding ~1.08 ha ; small & marginal ≈ 86% . Largest exporter of rice ; biggest producer of milk (~230+ mt). Import dependence: edible oils ~55–60% , pulses significant. Buffer godowns (FCI) vs buffer norms — monthly points (Jan/Apr/Jul/Oct). MSP & procurement Recommended by CACP ; approved by Cabinet. 23 crops ; formula since 2018-19: MSP ≥ 1.5 × (A2 + FL) (farmers demand the C2 base and a statutory guarantee). Procurement concentrated: Punjab/Haryana/West UP (rice-wheat); PM-AASHA for other crops (PSS, PSF, PDPS). Farm laws circa 2020 3","chars":2784},{"nav":"gs-3/environment-biodiversity/bullets","file":"environment-biodiversity-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/environment-biodiversity/bullet-points/environment-biodiversity-points.html","title":"Environment &amp; Biodiversity — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Species ↔ location (prelims pairing drill)"},{"level":2,"text":"Convention beats"},{"level":2,"text":"One-line framers"},{"level":2,"text":"Number drills"}],"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap ENVIRONMENT (GS-3) ├── Law: EPA'86 · WPA'72 · FCA'80(+23) · Water'74/Air'81 · Biodiversity Act'02(+23) · NGT'10 ├── Protected areas │ ├── 57+ Tiger reserves (Veerangana Durgavati 2023) · Tiger census 2022 = 3,682 (3,925 band) │ ├── Project Elephant 1992 · synchronised elephant census (band ~27–30k) · cheetah (Kuno '22 → SA '23) │ ├── Ramsar 80+ · Biosphere reserves 18 (Nilgiri first) · WH natural: Khangchendzonga 2016 │ └── Snow leopard ~718 (2024, first estimate) · IBCA 2023→HQ 2024 ├── Pollution: NCAP 2019 (40% PM by '26) · CAQM+GRAP NCR · EPR plastics/e-waste/battery · sup ban July '22 · Namami Gange 2015 · Green Credit 2023·24 debate ├── Climate │ ├── NDC: net-zero 2070 · 500GW · 50% RE · 45% EI · 1 bn t CO2 │ ├── CCTS 2023 · 8 NAPCC missions + LiFE '22 · HAPs 23+ · Mission Mausam ₹2k cr │ ├── COP26(21)→27(22 L&D)→28(23 GST)→29","chars":2802},{"nav":"gs-3/environment-biodiversity/notes","file":"environment-biodiversity.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/environment-biodiversity/detailed-notes/environment-biodiversity.html","title":"Environment &amp; Biodiversity — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Constitutional & legal architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Protected-area system"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Pollution regimes"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Climate change"},{"level":3,"text":"4.1 Global architecture"},{"level":3,"text":"4.2 IPCC + COP beats"},{"level":3,"text":"4.3 Domestic adaptation"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Biodiversity governance"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Key litigation & institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"7. CA-tied facts (2024–26)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 3 (Mains) · Conservation, EIA, Disaster-linked ecology, climate change, protected areas, pollution Also: Prelims GS (env questions ~12–18/yr — the heaviest non-core segment) 1. Constitutional & legal architecture Art. 21 → right to clean environment (Maneka→M C Mehta line of cases); Art. 51A(g) (duty); Art. 48A (DPSP). Framework laws: EPA 1986 (post-Bhopal umbrella), WPA 1972 , F(Conservation)A 1980 (+2023 Amendment) , Water Act 1974 → CPCB , Air Act 1981 , Biodiversity Act 2002 (NBA + SBBs; amendments 2023 eased benefit-sharing), NGT Act 2010 . 2. Protected-area system NPs & WLSs (WPA schedules); Conservation Reserves ; Community Reserves ; Tiger Reserves (57+; latest Veerangana Durgavati TR Madhya Pradesh 2023 → corridor question) under Project Tiger 1973 . Project Elephant (1992) — Elephants in WPA Schedule I. Cheetah reint","chars":5098},{"nav":"gs-3/environment-biodiversity/pyqs","file":"environment-biodiversity-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/environment-biodiversity/pyqs/environment-biodiversity-pyqs.html","title":"Environment &amp; Biodiversity — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Prelims MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Mains questions with skeletons"}],"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — PYQs Emphasis on ecosystem conventions + protected-area pairings (prelims) with institution-heavy Mains skeletons. A. Prelims MCQs Q1. [PYQ-pattern] Which of the following are among the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework? 30×30 conservation coverage Halting human-induced extinction of known threatened species Expanding carbon markets for offset-based biodiversity credits as a mandatory mechanism (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 2 and 3 only (c) 1 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a). 1 & 2 are GBF targets; biodiversity credit markets are debated but are not a mandated mechanism of the GBF text. Q2. The first-ever national population estimate of snow leopards in India (2024) reported approximately: (a) 200 (b) 718 (c) 1,400 (d) 5,800 Model answer: (b). ~718 individuals (SPA–WWF-style collaboration; Ladakh + Himachal + Uttarakhand + Sikkim","chars":4755},{"nav":"gs-3/environment-biodiversity/short","file":"environment-biodiversity-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/environment-biodiversity/short-notes/environment-biodiversity-revision.html","title":"Environment &amp; Biodiversity — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Legal spine"},{"level":2,"text":"Protected areas"},{"level":2,"text":"Pollution programmes"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate stack"},{"level":2,"text":"Global beats"},{"level":2,"text":"Landmark judgments"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims quick pairs"}],"text":"Environment & Biodiversity — Revision Notes Numbers-first card; species+convention pairings are prelims gold. Legal spine EPA 1986 (umbrella) · WPA 1972 · F(Conservation)A 1980 + 2023 Amdt · Water Act 1974 (CPCB) · Air Act 1981 · Biodiversity Act 2002 (+ 2023 Amdt) · NGT 2010 . Constitutional: Art.21 (MC Mehta line) · 48A · 51A(g). Protected areas 57+ Tiger Reserves (latest: Veerangana Durgavati, MP 2023) — Project Tiger 1973. Tiger count 2022: mean 3,682 (band 3,925) . Project Elephant 1992; elephant census (synchronised) rolling 2023–25 → ~27–30k estimate band. Cheetah: Namibia batch Sep 2022 + SA batch 2023 → Kuno NP. Ramsar sites 80+ (2025 band) — count rises around 2 February each year; use the latest notified figure. Snow leopard first national estimate ~718 (2024) . IBCA (International Big Cat Alliance) operationalised 2023→HQ 2024. Pollution programmes NCAP 2019 → 40% PM reductio","chars":2570},{"nav":"gs-3/indian-economy/bullets","file":"indian-economy-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/indian-economy/bullet-points/indian-economy-points.html","title":"Indian Economy — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Bullet facts (prelims gold)"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote bank"}],"text":"Indian Economy — Key Bullet Points / Mindmap Mindmap INDIAN ECONOMY │ ┌──────────┬─────────┼─────────┬──────────┐ PLANNING FISCAL MONETARY EXTERNAL STRUCTURAL 1947–2014 & TAX & BANKING SECTOR ISSUES │ │ │ │ │ 5-year FRBM '03 RBI '34 balance jobs-switch plans GST '17 MPC '16 of pays PLI sectors NITI '15 FD ~4.4% repo/ UPI informality GST-2.0 '25 SDF/MSF FDI/FDI formalisation IBC '16 FPI/INR Bullet facts (prelims gold) Planning Commission — set up 25 March 1950 (Cabinet resolution); PM = ex-officio chairman; D. R. Gadgil framed early plan frameworks. NITI Aayog — 1 January 2015 ; PM chair, VC + CEO-led; Governing Council = PM + CMs + LGs. 1991 reforms — PM P. V. Narasimha Rao + Finance Minister Manmohan Singh ; BoP reserves crisis (2–3 weeks of imports); gold pledged (Bank of England) July 1991. LPG tenets — de-licensing (most industries), trade liberalisation, FDI norms, PSU disinvestment","chars":2817},{"nav":"gs-3/indian-economy/notes","file":"indian-economy.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/indian-economy/detailed-notes/indian-economy.html","title":"Indian Economy: Growth, Planning, Resources &amp; Liberalisation — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy: Growth, Planning, Resources & Liberalisation — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The arc of Indian economic policy (1947 → today)"},{"level":3,"text":"Where India stands (for facts-based openers)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Planning — from Yojana to NITI"},{"level":2,"text":"3. National income & data architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Fiscal policy"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Monetary policy & the financial system"},{"level":2,"text":"6. External sector"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Industry & infrastructure policy"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Inclusion, employment & social measures (economy-side)"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Structural questions for the 2020s (Mains-ready synthesis)"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Quick PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Indian Economy: Growth, Planning, Resources & Liberalisation — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 3 (Mains) · Indian Economy — growth, planning, resources, liberalisation Also relevant for: Prelims GS Paper I — Economy; Essay (development themes) Currency check: Updated to September 2026 . 1. The arc of Indian economic policy (1947 → today) Era Character Key markers 1947–1967 — Planning & the public sector Mixed economy; heavy-industries strategy (Mahalanobis model, Plan 2) RBI nationalised (1949); Planning Commission (1950); Industrial Policy Resolutions (1948, 1956 — the commanding-heights resolution); First IITs/IIMs; land reforms (zamindari abolition unevenly) 1967–1991 — \"Green Revolution + controls\" Nationalisation wave; import substitution; MRTP & the licence raj Bank nationalisation (1969, 1980); Green Revolution (1966 onwards); MRTP Act (1969) ; FERA (1973); Janata industrial stat","chars":11473},{"nav":"gs-3/indian-economy/pyqs","file":"indian-economy-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/indian-economy/pyqs/indian-economy-pyqs.html","title":"Indian Economy — PYQs &amp; Model Answers","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy — PYQs & Model Answers"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Prelims PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC Mains PYQs"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"},{"level":3,"text":"Model answer skeleton"}],"text":"Indian Economy — PYQs & Model Answers UPSC Prelims PYQs Q1. (2015) In India, which one of the following is responsible for maintaining the supply of money by controlling interest rates? (a) The Ministry of Finance (b) The Reserve Bank of India (c) The NITI Aayog (d) The Monetary Policy Committee of the Bank of International Settlements Model answer: (b) — the RBI's Monetary Policy Committee (created by the 2016 amendment to the RBI Act) sets the policy repo rate; the Ministry of Finance handles fiscal policy. Q2. (2016) With reference to the New Monetary Policy Framework , consider the following statements: It mandates CPI-based inflation targeting. The target is a band rather than a point estimate. The band's midpoint is 2%. (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1 and 3 only (c) 2 and 3 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a) — the framework targets 4% ± 2% CPI (the midpoint is 4%, not 2%). Q3. (2017) The","chars":6524},{"nav":"gs-3/indian-economy/short","file":"indian-economy-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/indian-economy/short-notes/indian-economy-revision.html","title":"Indian Economy — Short Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy — Short Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liner"},{"level":2,"text":"Policy eras (one line each)"},{"level":2,"text":"Macro numbers to quote"},{"level":2,"text":"Monetary & banking"},{"level":2,"text":"Fiscal"},{"level":2,"text":"Manufacturing & infra"},{"level":2,"text":"Inclusion"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Indian Economy — Short Revision Notes One-liner India's economy — ~ 4th-largest ($4.2 tn) — moved from the planning era (1947–91) through the 1991 LPG reforms into today's mission-mode formalisation (GST, PLI, DPI/UPI), with an inflation-targeting RBI alongside a capex-led fiscal strategy. Policy eras (one line each) Era Signal 1947–67 Planning Commission + public sector (IPR 1956, Mahalanobis model) 1967–91 Bank nationalisation (1969/80), Green Revolution, MRTP licence raj 1991 LPG reforms — delicensing, BoP-crisis-triggered 2003–2014 FRBM (2003), VAT (2005), UIDAI (2009), MGNREGA (2005) 2014– Demonetisation (2016), IBC (2016), GST (2017) , PLI (2020), Gati Shakti (2021), GST 2.0 (Sep 2025) Macro numbers to quote GDP ~ $4.2 tn (crossed Japan, ~2025; next: Germany). Growth: FY24 ~8%+; FY25 ~6.5%; FY26 ~6.5% band projected. Structure (GVA): services ~55% · industry ~27–28% · agriculture ~","chars":2158},{"nav":"gs-3","file":"README.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/README.html","title":"General Studies III — Economy, S&amp;T, Environment, Security","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"General Studies III — Economy, S&T, Environment, Security"}],"text":"General Studies III — Economy, S&T, Environment, Security Five topic folders, each with the standard 5-section set ( detailed-notes/ · short-notes/ · bullet-points/ · diagrams/ · pyqs/ ): indian-economy/ — growth, inflation, fiscal/monetary, trade, reforms, GST 2.0 agriculture-food/ — farm economy, MSP, food security (NFSA), irrigation, agri-markets science-technology/ — space (ISRO), nuclear, IT/AI/quantum missions, biotech, defence tech environment-biodiversity/ — laws, protected areas, climate stack, COP beats, litigation security-disaster/ — internal security (LWE/NE/Kashmir), borders, cyber, disaster management After adding/editing files: node cli/generate.mjs from upsc-portal/ re-indexes them.","chars":708},{"nav":"gs-3/science-technology/bullets","file":"science-tech-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/science-technology/bullet-points/science-tech-points.html","title":"Science &amp; Technology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Technology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Seconds-ready answer frames"},{"level":2,"text":"Factoids that win marks"}],"text":"Science & Technology — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap SCIENCE & TECH (GS-3) ├── Institutions: DST | DBT | MoES | DAE | DoS/ISRO | DRDO | MeitY | CSIR | ICMR ├── Space │ ├── Vehicles: SLV→ASLV→PSLV→GSLV(Mk-II)→LVM3 → NGLV(\"Soorya\"); SSLV; private Skyroot (Vikram-S '22) · Agnikul (SorTeD '24) │ ├── Recent: CH-3 landed 23-Aug-23 (Shiv Shakti Pt) · Aditya-L1 Sep-23 · Gaganyaan TV-D1 Oct-23 · SpaDeX Dec-24 docked Jan-25 · NISAR Jul-25 · XPoSat Jan-24 │ ├── Stations & growth: Bharatiya Antariksh Station ~2035 · Space FDI 2024 (100% components) · IN-SPACe + NSIL │ └── NavIC 7-sat (L5/S + L1 civil) vs GPS MEO ├── Nuclear │ ├── 3-stage: PHWR → FBR (PFBR 500MWe Kalpakkam) → thorium AHWR-300 │ ├── Fleet ~7-8 GWe; fleet-mode PHWR (Kaiga 5/6); Budget 2025 Bharat SMR 220 MWe + private-open door (SHANTI-style bill Dec-25 — verify final form) │ └── Fusion: ITER partner; IPR SST-1/2 ├── IT / AI │ ├── IndiaA","chars":2660},{"nav":"gs-3/science-technology/notes","file":"science-technology.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/science-technology/detailed-notes/science-technology.html","title":"Science &amp; Technology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Technology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. India's S&T architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Space programme (DoS/ISRO)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Launch vehicles"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Missions timeline (recent)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Navigation & applications"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Nuclear & energy S&T"},{"level":2,"text":"4. IT, AI, quantum & computation"},{"level":3,"text":"AI"},{"level":3,"text":"Quantum"},{"level":3,"text":"Supercomputing"},{"level":3,"text":"Biotechnology"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Defence & frontier"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Colour ethics & governance (bridges to GS-IV)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Science & Technology — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 3 (Mains) · S&T developments & everyday applications; indigenisation; IT/Space/computers/robotics/nano/biotech/IPR Cross-links: Prelims tech questions (6–12/yr), essay hooks, ethics (dual-use discussions) 1. India's S&T architecture Departments : DST, DBT (biotech), MoES (earth sciences), DAE (atomic), DoS/ISRO, MoD DRDO, MeitY (IT), CSIR labs (37+), ICMR. Mission-style programmes: National Quantum Mission (2023, ₹6,003 cr) , National Research Foundation (ANRF Act 2023, ₹50k cr over 5 yrs) , IndiaAI Mission (₹10,371 cr, 2024) , National Supercomputing Mission (PARAM/Rudra families), Semiconductor Mission (ISM ₹76k cr) . Innovation bodies: Atal Innovation Mission (NITI), Startup India; patent ecology via IPR Policy 2016. 2. Space programme (DoS/ISRO) 2.1 Launch vehicles Sounding rockets → SLV (1980) → ASLV → PSLV (workhorse, 4-stage ","chars":6916},{"nav":"gs-3/science-technology/pyqs","file":"science-tech-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/science-technology/pyqs/science-tech-pyqs.html","title":"Science &amp; Technology — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Technology — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Prelims MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Mains questions with skeletons"}],"text":"Science & Technology — PYQs Space/AI/quantum heaviness mirrors recent UPSC drift. Exact-PYQ nature hedged where paraphrased from memory. A. Prelims MCQs Q1. [PYQ-pattern] With reference to India's satellite navigation systems: NavIC uses a mix of geostationary and geosynchronous orbits. GPS satellites are in medium earth orbit. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (c). NavIC has 7 satellites in GEO + GSO (added L1 civil band); GPS uses MEO (~20,200 km), 24+ sats. Q2. Mission Divyastra (2024) demonstrated: (a) ASAT interception (b) Scramjet cruise (c) MIRV capability on Agni-V (d) Cryogenic stage restart Model answer: (c). Multiple Independently Targetable Re-entry Vehicle test of Agni-V (11 March 2024). Q3. Which correctly orders Indian launch vehicles by payload-class ascending? (a) SLV → ASLV → PSLV → GSLV Mk-II → LVM3 (b) SLV → PSLV → ASLV → GSLV (c) PSLV → SLV → G","chars":3943},{"nav":"gs-3/science-technology/short","file":"science-tech-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/science-technology/short-notes/science-tech-revision.html","title":"Science &amp; Technology — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Technology — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Missions at a glance"},{"level":2,"text":"Launch-pad quick facts"},{"level":2,"text":"NavIC vs GPS"},{"level":2,"text":"Nuclear stack"},{"level":2,"text":"IT/AI quick grid"},{"level":2,"text":"Quantum facts"},{"level":2,"text":"Biotech"},{"level":2,"text":"Defence S&T"},{"level":2,"text":"Repeatable framers for Mains"}],"text":"Science & Technology — Revision Notes Mission-wise cheat sheet; recent-years missions weighted highest. Missions at a glance Chandrayaan-3 (2023) : soft-landed 23 Aug 2023 south-polar region (Shiv Shakti Point); propulsion module return tech demonstrated 2024. Aditya-L1 (2023) : first Indian solar observatory at L1 halo orbit. Gaganyaan : TV-D1 (Oct 2023) pad abort+crew escape success; crewed flight pipeline after G1/G2. SpaDeX (Dec 2024) : docking tech demo — Jan 2025 docking & undocking (autonomous docking milestone for Bharatiya Antariksh Station, target ~2035). NISAR (launched July 2025) : ISRO-NASA dual-frequency SAR for disaster/agri/climate mapping. National missions: Quantum (2023, ₹6,003 cr) · IndiaAI (2024, ₹10,371 cr) · ANRF (2023, ₹50,000 cr/5yr) · Semiconductor ISM (₹76,000 cr) · Green Hydrogen (₹19,744 cr) . Launch-pad quick facts PSLV: 4-stage (solid-liquid-solid-liquid), ","chars":3316},{"nav":"gs-3/security-disaster/bullets","file":"security-disaster-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/security-disaster/bullet-points/security-disaster-points.html","title":"Security &amp; Disaster — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Security & Disaster — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer-builders (three-line formulae)"},{"level":2,"text":"Numbers to seed"},{"level":2,"text":"Counter-points for \"critically examine\""}],"text":"Security & Disaster — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap SECURITY & DM (GS-3) ├── Law: List II (public order/police) + Entry 2A · NIA 2008(+19) · UAPA (+19) · Prakash Singh '06 directives ├── Theatres │ ├── LWE: Naxalbari 1967 → 200+dist → ~38 · \"Mar 2026 elimination\" goal · SIS scheme · 5G/fibre in Bastar · '24-25 leadership kills │ ├── NE: Bodo '20 · Bru-Reang '20 · Karbi '21 · ULFA talks '23 · Manipur '23 → PR Feb '25 · FMR suspended Dec '24 (Myanmar) │ ├── Kashmir: 370 dilution '19 → UT + delimitation '22 → Assembly '24 (NC govt) → Pahalgam Apr-25 → Op Sindoor May-25 → IWT abeyance │ ├── Narco: Golden Crescent sea-route (Gujarat busts '24-25) · Golden Triangle via Myanmar/Manipur · NCORD · MANAS │ └── Organised: drone-drops · crypto-hawala · wildlife corridor siliguri ├── Borders grid │ ├── Pak 3323km BSF drones/LC | China 3488km ITBP+Army (Galwan→buffer→Oct24 patrolling) | Nepal 1751km SSB","chars":2786},{"nav":"gs-3/security-disaster/notes","file":"security-disaster.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/security-disaster/detailed-notes/security-disaster.html","title":"Security &amp; Disaster Management — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Security & Disaster Management — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Internal security architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Theatre-wise threats"},{"level":3,"text":"2.1 Left-Wing Extremism (LWE/Maoism)"},{"level":3,"text":"2.2 Northeast insurgency & ethnic conflict"},{"level":3,"text":"2.3 Kashmir"},{"level":3,"text":"2.4 Terror financing & transnational links"},{"level":3,"text":"2.5 Organised crime & narcotics"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Border management"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Coastal & maritime extension"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Cybersecurity & new domains (bridge)"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Disaster management — the statutory frame"},{"level":3,"text":"Hazard archetypes (for Mains answers)"},{"level":3,"text":"Tools & tech"},{"level":2,"text":"7. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Security & Disaster Management — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 3 (Mains) · Internal security challenges, extremism, terrorism, organised crime, security forces, border management + Disaster & disaster management Note: One pair of linked domains — UPSC tends to draw 2–4 questions across this combined envelope yearly. 1. Internal security architecture Constitutional basis: Entry 1–2 (Public Order, Police) are STATE subjects (List II) ; Union retains Entry 2A (deployment of armed forces in aid of civil power), CISF/industrial security, intelligence coordination. Structures: MHA (with internal-security divisions), NIA (2008 Act), NATGRID, MAC/sMAC intelligence grid, NSG (post-26/11 hubs), CAPFs: BSF (Pakistan & Bangladesh borders), CRPF (largest), CISF, ITBP (China border), SSB (Nepal-Bhutan), Assam Rifles dual-control (MoD ops/MHA admin). Reforms lineage: police commissions (Gore 1971, R","chars":7903},{"nav":"gs-3/security-disaster/pyqs","file":"security-disaster-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/security-disaster/pyqs/security-disaster-pyqs.html","title":"Security &amp; Disaster — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Security & Disaster — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Prelims-style MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Mains questions with skeletons"}],"text":"Security & Disaster — PYQs Prelims occasional; Mains mainstay. Outlines original; verify current-affairs specifics before quoting live operations. A. Prelims-style MCQs Q1. With reference to India's CAPFs: ITBP guards the India-China border. Assam Rifles is administratively under the Ministry of Defence. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (a). Assam Rifles is operationally under Army (MoD) but administratively under MHA — hence statement 2 as phrased is wrong. Q2. The Sendai Framework lists how many priorities for action? (a) 2 (b) 4 (c) 7 (d) 10 Model answer: (b). Four priorities: understand risk; strengthen risk governance; invest in resilience; build back better. Q3. Mission Mausam (2024) primarily concerns: (a) Monsoon finance devolution (b) Weather forecasting infrastructure and early warning (c) Drought crop insurance (d) Cloud-seeding research only Model answ","chars":4360},{"nav":"gs-3/security-disaster/short","file":"security-disaster-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-3-economy-tech-environment/security-disaster/short-notes/security-disaster-revision.html","title":"Security &amp; Disaster — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Security & Disaster — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Architecture spine"},{"level":2,"text":"Theatres in one line each"},{"level":2,"text":"Border grid"},{"level":2,"text":"Cyber & new domains"},{"level":2,"text":"Disaster management"},{"level":2,"text":"Reusable quotes/stats"}],"text":"Security & Disaster — Revision Notes Grid-style recall; combine one \"architecture\" line + one \"trend\" line + one \"case\" line per answer. Architecture spine Public Order + Police = STATE subjects (List II) ; Union keeps armed-forces-aid (Entry 2A), central forces, IB/NIA/NATGRID/NCORD. CAPFs: BSF · CRPF · CISF · ITBP · SSB · Assam Rifles (dual control). Prakash Singh 2006 — 7 directives; least-implemented = separation of investigation; Model Police Act 2015 draft outstanding. NIA Act 2008 (+2019 amendment). Theatres in one line each LWE : Naxalbari 1967; contraction claim: from ~200 districts to ~38; govt deadline \"liberate by 31 Mar 2026\"; Bastar ops 2024–25 neutralised several senior commanders (verify names against latest MHA briefings); strategy = security + development + connectivity (5G/fibre in Dandakaranya). Northeast : accords beat — Bodo 2020 · Bru-Reang 2020 · Karbi 2021 · ULFA","chars":3093},{"nav":"gs-4/aptitude-foundations/bullets","file":"aptitude-foundations-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/aptitude-foundations/bullet-points/aptitude-foundations-points.html","title":"Aptitude &amp; Foundational Values — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"One-line differentiators (examiners love these)"},{"level":2,"text":"Behavioural indicators (quote these in answers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Institutional anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Bullet Points 60-second revision card for the six foundational values. ASCII mindmap APTITUDE × ATTITUDE = PERFORMANCE ├── Integrity ............ wholeness; same core public+private ├── Impartiality ......... merit+law, no regard of persons ├── Non-partisanship ..... serve the day's govt, no party colour ├── Objectivity .......... evidence + recorded reasons ├── Dedication ........... office as trust; follow-through └── Empathy→Compassion ... feel with → act for the weak └── Tolerance ......... hear all sides; plural civility One-line differentiators (examiners love these) Honesty = truth-telling; Integrity = consistency of values-speech-action; Probity = integrity made auditable in office. Sympathy = feeling for (can humiliate); Empathy = feeling with; Compassion = empathy mobilised into action. Neutrality ≠ indifference; impartial process can still carr","chars":2074},{"nav":"gs-4/aptitude-foundations/notes","file":"aptitude-foundations.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/aptitude-foundations/detailed-notes/aptitude-foundations.html","title":"Aptitude &amp; Foundational Values — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Aptitude — Meaning and Relevance"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Integrity"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Impartiality and Non-partisanship"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Objectivity"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Dedication to Public Service"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Empathy, Tolerance and Compassion towards Weaker Sections"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Values in Conflict — Holding Them Together"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Aptitude and foundational values for Civil Service: integrity, impartiality and non-partisanship, objectivity, dedication to public service, empathy, tolerance and compassion towards the weaker-sections.\" 1. Aptitude — Meaning and Relevance Aptitude is a natural or acquired potential to acquire a specific skill with training. Unlike attitude (a disposition to evaluate), aptitude is a capacity to perform. Aptitude × Attitude = Performance — capacity multiplied by willingness. Types: cognitive (analysis, judgment), psychomotor (drill, handling), social/emotional (dealing with people). For civil services the critical aptitudes are administrative (problem decomposition, drafting, numerical reasoning), emotional (composure under provocation) and moral (holding procedure and compassion together). Selection boards (ps","chars":5115},{"nav":"gs-4/aptitude-foundations/pyqs","file":"aptitude-foundations-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/aptitude-foundations/pyqs/aptitude-foundations-pyqs.html","title":"Aptitude &amp; Foundational Values — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Part-A theory style. Prelims-style checks Q1. Which pairing is correct? (a) Aptitude — capacity; Attitude — disposition (b) Aptitude — disposition; Attitude — capacity (c) Both are capacities (d) Both are dispositions Model answer: (a). Aptitude is trainability for a skill; attitude is an evaluative predisposition. Q2. \"Feeling with\" rather than \"feeling for\" describes: (a) sympathy (b) empathy (c) pity (d) charity Model answer: (b). Empathy is perspective-taking; sympathy remains observational. Q3. Probity is best described as: (a) private virtue (b) integrity demonstrated in public office (c) procedural delay (d) fiscal austerity Model answer: (b). Probity makes integrity auditable. Mains theory questions with model skeletons Q4. \"Impartiality is not indifference; objectivity is not coldness","chars":3254},{"nav":"gs-4/aptitude-foundations/short","file":"aptitude-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/aptitude-foundations/short-notes/aptitude-pack.html","title":"Aptitude &amp; Foundational Values — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Aptitude framing"},{"level":2,"text":"Value definitions for answers"},{"level":2,"text":"Where do they get shaped? (determinants)"},{"level":2,"text":"Conflicts observed in exams"},{"level":2,"text":"Drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer scaffold"}],"text":"Aptitude & Foundational Values — Leaf Pack Aptitude framing Aptitude = potential to acquire proficiency (distinct from intelligence and from skill). For civil service: cognitive + administrative temperament (frustration tolerance, ambiguity comfort, responsibility appetite) + values. Foundational values codified by UPSC/exam tradition: integrity, impartiality, non-partisanship, objectivity, dedication to public service, empathy, tolerance, compassion toward weaker sections . Value definitions for answers Integrity — coherence of word, value and action; financial+intellectual honesty; behavioural consistency under low-observability. Impartiality — equal treatment irrespective of personal affiliations; decision on merit documented (file-note logic). Non-partisanship — serve constitutional government of the day without political-indenture; advise fearlessly, implement lawful-political decis","chars":2922},{"nav":"gs-4/attitude/bullets","file":"attitude-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/attitude/bullet-points/attitude-points.html","title":"Attitude — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Attitude — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Definition one-liners"},{"level":2,"text":"Formation and change in ten seconds"},{"level":2,"text":"Administrative examples (cheat row)"},{"level":2,"text":"Moral vs political attitudes"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Attitude — Bullet Points 60-second revision card. Full detail in detailed-notes; applications in PYQ pack. ASCII mindmap ATTITUDE ├── Structure (ABC) ......... Affect · Behaviour · Cognition ├── Functions (Katz) ........ Knowledge · Ego-defence · Value-expression · Adjustive ├── Formation ............... Family → Peers → Experience → Media → Conditioning ├── Change .................. ELM (central/peripheral) · Dissonance · Nudges ├── Kinds ................... Moral (guilt-backed) · Political (power-oriented) └── Officer lens ............ Courtesy · Problem-solving · Role-model cascade Definition one-liners Attitude — learned, stable predisposition to evaluate and respond to an object. Values → Attitudes → Opinions — deep → mid → surface; few → many; frozen → volatile. Cognitive dissonance (Festinger) — belief–behaviour clash; the self-correction engine. Persuasion — attitude change by co","chars":2259},{"nav":"gs-4/attitude/notes","file":"attitude.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/attitude/detailed-notes/attitude.html","title":"Attitude — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Attitude — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What is Attitude?"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Structure — the ABC Model"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Functions of Attitude (Daniel Katz)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. How Attitudes are Formed"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Attitude, Thought and Behaviour"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Attitude Change, Persuasion and Social Influence"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Moral and Political Attitudes"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Attitude in Administration — Why It Matters"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Attitude — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · Aptitude & foundational values — \"Attitude: content, structure, function; its influence and relation with thought and behaviour; moral and political attitudes; social influence and persuasion.\" 1. What is Attitude? An attitude is a learned, relatively stable predisposition to respond favourably or unfavourably to a person, object, idea or situation. It sits between values (deep, abstract standards — few in number) and opinions (surface, verbal expressions — many and changeable). G. W. Allport called attitude \"the most distinctive and indispensable concept in social psychology.\" Construct Abstraction Number Stability Example Value Highest Few (a dozen core) Life-long, near-immutable Honesty, equality Attitude Middle Hundreds Stable but changeable Attitude toward transfers, toward RTI queries Opinion / belief Lowest Thousands Highly vo","chars":6206},{"nav":"gs-4/attitude/pyqs","file":"attitude-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/attitude/pyqs/attitude-pyqs.html","title":"Attitude — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Attitude — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Attitude — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Part-A theory style; use with the detailed notes. Prelims-style checks Q1. The A-B-C components of attitude stand for: (a) Attitude, Belief, Character (b) Affect, Behaviour, Cognition (c) Action, Bias, Choice (d) Awareness, Belief, Conduct Model answer: (b). Feelings, action-tendencies and beliefs constitute attitude structure. Q2. Katz's \"value-expressive\" function of attitude is best evoked by a campaign that appeals to: (a) monetary penalty (b) fear of disease (c) dignity and identity (d) procedural convenience Model answer: (c). Value-expressive attitudes signal who we are; dignity frames outperformed shame frames. Q3. Cognitive dissonance theory was proposed by: (a) Festinger (b) Asch (c) Milgram (d) Katz Model answer: (a). Leon Festinger, 1957. Q4. In the Elaboration-Likelihood Model, attitude change produced","chars":3798},{"nav":"gs-4/attitude/short","file":"attitude-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/attitude/short-notes/attitude-pack.html","title":"Attitude — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Attitude — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Structure (ABC)"},{"level":2,"text":"Functions (Katz)"},{"level":2,"text":"Formation & change"},{"level":2,"text":"Moral & political attitudes"},{"level":2,"text":"Measurement practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Civil-service applications"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liners"}],"text":"Attitude — Leaf Pack Structure (ABC) A ffective (feelings), B ehavioural (actions/tendencies), C ognitive (beliefs) components. Function-fit measures: self-report scales (Likert/semantic differential), behavioural observation. Functions (Katz) Knowledge (understanding world) · Ego-defensive (self-image protection) · Value-expressive (identity signalling) · Instrumental/adjustive (reward-pursuit). Administrative relevance: campaigns fail if aimed at the wrong function — anti-open-defecation messaging initially misfired on identity-ego channels in some belts; later dignity/identity reframing (women-led messaging) landed better. Formation & change Formation agents: family, peers, school, media, direct experience, classical/operant conditioning, social learning (Bandura modelling). Change levers: persuasive communication — Elaboration Likelihood Model : central route (argument quality — info","chars":2852},{"nav":"gs-4/case-studies/bullets","file":"case-studies-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/case-studies/bullet-points/case-studies-points.html","title":"Case Studies — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Case Studies — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Ethical quick tests"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring moves"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"Case Studies — Bullet Points 60-second revision card — the answering machine. ASCII mindmap CASE ANSWER (20 marks, ~15 min) ├── Facts (3 lines + what to verify) ├── Ethical issues/dilemma (name the clash) ├── Stakeholders (incl. silent: poor, future) ├── Options (≥3 + reject \"do nothing\") ├── Evaluation (legality · public interest · proportionality │ precedent · sustainability · conscience) ├── Decision + implementation steps └── Defence + safeguards (audit, sunset, review) Ethical quick tests Legality — is it within the Act/Rules/oath? Front-page test — would I defend it in print? Reversibility — can the affected party undo the harm? Universality — if every officer did this, what happens? Weakest-party test — who is worst hit and can they be heard? Scoring moves Name the dilemma in the language of values (integrity vs loyalty), not drama. Record, document, audit — the three institutiona","chars":1525},{"nav":"gs-4/case-studies/notes","file":"case-studies.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/case-studies/detailed-notes/case-studies.html","title":"Case Studies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Case Studies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What the Examiner Rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The 7-Step Framework (write these as sub-headings)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Worked Case 1 — The Whistle or the File"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Worked Case 2 — Flood Prioritisation"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Common Pitfalls"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Case Studies — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · Part B (125 marks) — case studies on ethical dilemma, governance, probity, conflict of interest, crisis ethics. Method here: framework + two fully worked cases. 1. What the Examiner Rewards Structure over sermonising — a visible decision process. Stakeholder completeness — who gains, who pays, who decides. Option analysis — at least three real options, honestly weighed. Constitutional anchoring — law and constitutional morality as the floor. Practicality — a course of action an officer can actually execute, with safeguards. 2. The 7-Step Framework (write these as sub-headings) Facts — restate in three lines; flag what is unknown (say what you would verify). Ethical issues & dilemmas — name the value clash (integrity vs loyalty; transparency vs privacy). Stakeholders — affected parties with their stakes; include the silent ones (f","chars":4299},{"nav":"gs-4/case-studies/pyqs","file":"case-studies-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/case-studies/pyqs/case-studies-pyqs.html","title":"Case Studies — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Case Studies — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Case 1 — The Friendly Contractor"},{"level":2,"text":"Case 2 — The Underperforming Block"},{"level":2,"text":"Case 3 — Hunger Strike of a Colleague"},{"level":2,"text":"Case 4 — The Data Release"},{"level":2,"text":"Case 5 — Environment vs Jobs"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Case Studies — PYQs & Model Approaches Five practice cases in UPSC Part-B style with approach notes (write full 250-word answers under timed conditions). Case 1 — The Friendly Contractor A contractor executing your office renovation offers festival sweets with a ₹50,000 gift voucher \"for the staff.\" He is also bidding for a municipal contract you will evaluate next week. Approach: gift rules and conflict of interest → return voucher, log the incident, disclose to superior, recuse from the bid or ensure collective evaluation with recorded reasons; institutional fix: gift registry + rotation of evaluators. Case 2 — The Underperforming Block Your district tops immunisation on paper, but a ASHA worker privately tells you sessions are \"adjusted\" in two blocks. Media is about to felicitate the district. Approach: integrity vs institutional reputation → verify quietly (field audit, independent ","chars":2760},{"nav":"gs-4/case-studies/short","file":"case-studies-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/case-studies/short-notes/case-studies-pack.html","title":"Case Studies — Method Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Case Studies — Method Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"The template (every case uses this)"},{"level":2,"text":"High-yield case archetypes with model hinges"},{"level":3,"text":"Illegal orders"},{"level":3,"text":"Conflict of interest (family member bidder)"},{"level":3,"text":"Service-delivery triage under scarcity (oxygen/bed/drug)"},{"level":3,"text":"Land acquisition resistance (tribal/forest zones)"},{"level":3,"text":"Whistleblower asymmetry (junior spots fraud)"},{"level":3,"text":"Grievance overload (municipality under flood of complaints)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer-form steps for paper"},{"level":2,"text":"Common fallacies to dodge"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice cadence"}],"text":"Case Studies — Method Pack The template (every case uses this) Dilemma wording — name the tension (legality vs compassion; loyalty vs conscience; efficiency vs equity). Stakeholder map — list who bears costs/benefits; mark the least-advantaged. Option set — realistic 3–4 options, avoid strawman extremes (\"do nothing\"/\"resign on the spot\"). Filter stack — legality → deontological (duty/norm) → consequentialist (outcome) → virtue (character) → care (relationships). Decision + principle statement — pick, and say the principle honoured. Documentation & escalation — written orders, file notes, vigilance/audit escalation paths. Systemic fix — SOP/code change, training, transparency dashboard — show the \"institutional-learning\" move. Message to institution — one-line closure: what your action signals about integrity standards. High-yield case archetypes with model hinges Illegal orders Ask for ","chars":3041},{"nav":"gs-4/emotional-intelligence/bullets","file":"emotional-intelligence-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/emotional-intelligence/bullet-points/emotional-intelligence-points.html","title":"Emotional Intelligence — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liners"},{"level":2,"text":"Administrative applications (cheat row)"},{"level":2,"text":"Development ideas"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Bullet Points 60-second revision card. ASCII mindmap EMOTIONAL INTELLIGENCE ├── Goleman-5 ... self-awareness · self-regulation · motivation │ empathy · social skill ├── Mayer–Salovey perceive → use → understand → manage ├── EI vs IQ ....... implementation skill vs design skill ├── Applications ... crowd · grievance · negotiation · teams └── Ethical bound .. empathy without capture; regulation without coldness One-liners EI = perceive, understand, use, regulate emotions (self & others) for effective action. Lineage: Thorndike (social intelligence) → Gardner (multiple) → Salovey–Mayer 1990 → Goleman 1995. Empathy = perspective-taking; sympathy = observational; EI includes both plus regulation. Emotional labour (Hochschild) = performing felt order for service roles — the front-desk reality. Administrative applications (cheat row) Protest policing: de-escalation tone","chars":1638},{"nav":"gs-4/emotional-intelligence/notes","file":"emotional-intelligence.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/emotional-intelligence/detailed-notes/emotional-intelligence.html","title":"Emotional Intelligence — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Concept and Lineage"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Two Models You Must Carry"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Why EI Matters in Administration"},{"level":2,"text":"4. EI vs IQ — and the Ethical Bridge"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Developing EI in Service"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Contributions of moral thinkers… emotional intelligence-concepts, and their utilities and application in administration and governance.\" 1. Concept and Lineage Emotional Intelligence (EI) is the ability to perceive, understand, use and regulate emotions — one's own and others' — to think clearly and act effectively. Lineage: Thorndike's social intelligence (1920) → Gardner's intrapersonal/interpersonal intelligences (1983) → Salovey & Mayer's formal EI construct (1990) → Goleman's popularisation (1995, Emotional Intelligence ), which claimed EI matters more than IQ for leadership success. 2. The Two Models You Must Carry Model Nature Components Goleman's competency model Trait/mixed; workplace-oriented Self-awareness · Self-regulation · Motivation (intrinsic) · Empathy · Social skill Mayer–Salovey four-branch Ability m","chars":3216},{"nav":"gs-4/emotional-intelligence/pyqs","file":"emotional-intelligence-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/emotional-intelligence/pyqs/emotional-intelligence-pyqs.html","title":"Emotional Intelligence — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Emotional Intelligence — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Emotional Intelligence — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Prelims-style checks Q1. The four-branch ability model of EI belongs to: (a) Goleman (b) Salovey & Mayer (c) Bar-On (d) Thorndike Model answer: (b). Perceive → use → understand → manage. Q2. Which Goleman component most directly prevents impulsive orders in a riot situation? (a) motivation (b) self-regulation (c) social skill (d) empathy Model answer: (b). Self-regulation manages impulse under provocation. Q3. \"Emotional labour\" as a service-work concept was coined by: (a) Hochschild (b) Goleman (c) Festinger (d) Katz Model answer: (a). Arlie Hochschild — performing emotional order as part of the job. Mains theory questions with model skeletons Q4. \"The difference between a lathi-charge and a dispersal is often one officer's self-regulation.\" Examine the role of EI in public-order management. (150 wor","chars":2694},{"nav":"gs-4/emotional-intelligence/short","file":"ei-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/emotional-intelligence/short-notes/ei-pack.html","title":"Emotional Intelligence — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Models"},{"level":2,"text":"Civil-services applications"},{"level":2,"text":"Empathy — design usage"},{"level":2,"text":"Dark-side sophistication (use in answers for balance)"},{"level":2,"text":"Building EI institutionally"},{"level":2,"text":"One-active-method nugget for answers"},{"level":2,"text":"Quote-safe anchors"}],"text":"Emotional Intelligence — Leaf Pack Models Goleman 5 : self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation (intrinsic), empathy, social skill. Mayer–Salovey four-branch ability model : perceive emotions → use emotions→ integrate thought → understand emotions → manage emotions. Related constructs: EQ/EI vs IQ ; social intelligence (Thorndike line); emotional labour (service work performance discipline — Arlie Hochschild). Civil-services applications Policing crowd situations: de-escalation protocols; riot-control tone-setter stories from 2020s public-order events. Grievance arena: front-desk empathy scripts + expectation-management; CPGRAMS dashboards (state-quality-ranked). Negotiation rooms: land acquisition talks, labour-strike conversations, hostage/release mediations — calibrated empathy channel. Crisis narrative: disaster briefings (Kerala floods, Wayanad landslides) — authority + composure ","chars":2503},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-foundations/bullets","file":"ethics-foundations-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-foundations/bullet-points/ethics-foundations-points.html","title":"Ethics Foundations — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics Foundations — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Thinker-citation pairings (for question triggers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase-bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Ethics Foundations — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap ETHICS GS-4 (FOUNDATIONS) ├── Frame: ethics (principles) vs morals (community) vs values (endstates) vs norms (rules); meta/normative/applied ├── Attitude: ABC (affect·behaviour·cognition) · Katz functions · ELM persuasion (central vs peripheral); applications — polio/sanitation ├── Aptitude & values for CS: integrity · impartiality · objectivity · dedication · empathy · compassion (UPSC phrase-list) ├── EI: Salovey-Mayer 4 branches · Goleman 5 · admin apps — de-escalation, grievances, stress; danger — manipulative capture ├── Thinkers │ ├── Western: Socrates (examined life) · Plato (justice=harmony) · Aristotle (virtue-mean·phronesis) · Kant (CI, ends-not-means) │ │ · Bentham/Mill (max happiness · harm) · Rawls (veil-of-ignorance) · Arendt (banality of evil) · Kohlberg (3-level) │ │ · Gilligan (care) · Thoreau (disobedience) │ └── Indian:","chars":2821},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-foundations/notes","file":"ethics-foundations.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-foundations/detailed-notes/ethics-foundations.html","title":"Ethics, Integrity &amp; Aptitude — Detailed Notes (Foundations)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics, Integrity & Aptitude — Detailed Notes (Foundations)"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Ethics & human interface — the ground frame"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Attitude — structure, function & formation"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Aptitude for civil services"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Emotional intelligence (EI)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Moral thinkers — compact canon for citation"},{"level":3,"text":"Western"},{"level":3,"text":"Indian traditions"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Conscience, accountability & work culture"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Probity & governance bridges"},{"level":2,"text":"8. PYQ anchors (foundation-side)"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Ethics, Integrity & Aptitude — Detailed Notes (Foundations) Syllabus slot: GS 4 (Mains) · Ethics & human interface, attitude, aptitude, emotional intelligence, moral thinkers (Socrates → Gandhi → moderns), conscience, accountability, codes of ethics, work culture Marking logic: high abstract-to-application conversion. Every topic below closes with a civil-service application line — use that pattern. 1. Ethics & human interface — the ground frame Ethics : system of moral principles informing conduct; morals = actual accepted standards of a community; values = prized end-states (integrity, compassion); norms = behavioural rules. Dimensions: meta-ethics (what IS good?), normative ethics (what should guide?), applied ethics (how does this play out in context: medical/AI/media ethics). Culture & institutions: family-school-workplace-socio-political channels of learning/constraints; \"consequen","chars":7891},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-foundations/pyqs","file":"ethics-foundations-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-foundations/pyqs/ethics-foundations-pyqs.html","title":"Ethics Foundations — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics Foundations — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks (concept positioning)"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model-skeletons"}],"text":"Ethics Foundations — PYQs GS-4 pattern: Part A ~6 mains theory Qs; Part B case studies. These originals mirror UPSC style with model approaches. Prelims-style checks (concept positioning) Q1. \"Ethics of care\" as a critique of dominant moral-development theory is linked to: (a) Carol Gilligan (b) Lawrence Kohlberg (c) John Rawls (d) Hannah Arendt Model answer: (a). Gilligan (1982, In a Different Voice ) critiqued Kohlberg's justice-bias stages and foregrounded relational care. Q2. The \"veil of ignorance\" is an instrument for arriving at principles of: (a) Virtue, in Aristotle (b) Justice, in Rawls (c) Rights, in Nozick (d) Duty, in Kant Model answer: (b). Rawls' original-position construct yields the two principles of justice. Q3. \"Banality of evil\" emerged from Arendt's reporting on: (a) Stalin's show trials (b) Adolf Eichmann's Jerusalem trial (c) the Nuremberg prosecutor general (d) Mi","chars":4431},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-foundations/short","file":"ethics-foundations-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-foundations/short-notes/ethics-foundations-revision.html","title":"Ethics Foundations — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics Foundations — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Foundational values (memorise the UPSC phrase-list)"},{"level":2,"text":"Attitude card"},{"level":2,"text":"EI card"},{"level":2,"text":"Thinkers — one-liner citation pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Conscience × Accountability × Work culture"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick argument scaffolds (for any ethics question)"}],"text":"Ethics Foundations — Revision Notes Thinker-quotables + framework cards; this is the \"citation engine\" for GS-4. Foundational values (memorise the UPSC phrase-list) Integrity · Impartiality · Non-partisanship · Objectivity · Dedication to public service · Empathy · Tolerance · Compassion toward weaker sections . Attitude card ABC = Affect, Behaviour, Cognition. Katz functions: knowledge · ego-defensive · value-expressive · instrumental. Persuasion: ELM central (logic) vs peripheral (cues); source credibility × framing × audience. Applications: polio vaccine campaigns (celebrity cues + mosque trust signals) vs sanitation behaviour-change failures without identity alignment. EI card Goleman 5 : self-awareness · self-regulation · motivation · empathy · social skill. Mayer-Salovey 4 branches: perceive → use → understand → manage. Admin uses: de-escalation, grievance-redress empathy maps, neg","chars":3487},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-human-interface/bullets","file":"ethics-interface-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-human-interface/bullet-points/ethics-interface-points.html","title":"Ethics &amp; Human Interface — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Core distinctions"},{"level":2,"text":"Determinants cheat row"},{"level":2,"text":"Private vs public conflicts"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — Bullet Points 60-second revision card. ASCII mindmap ETHICS & HUMAN INTERFACE ├── Essence ......... how humans ought to act; ≥ law, ≥ custom ├── Dimensions ...... meta · normative (consq/deont/virtue) · applied ├── Determinants .... individual · situational · systemic ├── Consequences .... micro (character) → meso (institution) │ → macro (State legitimacy) └── Private vs Public ... two grammars; recusal where they collide Core distinctions Law = minimum & enforceable; Ethics = optimum & self-binding. Consequentialism judges outcomes; deontology judges maxims; virtue ethics judges character. Milgram lesson: bad situations out-produce bad people — design situations. Constitutional morality (Ambedkar) anchors Indian public ethics above social morality. Determinants cheat row Individual: conscience, upbringing, self-interest. Situational: peers, incentives, hiera","chars":1720},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-human-interface/notes","file":"ethics-human-interface.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-human-interface/detailed-notes/ethics-human-interface.html","title":"Ethics &amp; Human Interface — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The Essence of Ethics"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Dimensions of Ethics"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Determinants of Ethics in Human Actions"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Consequences of Ethics in Human Actions"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Ethics in Private and Public Relationships"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Ethical Compass for Answers"}],"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Ethics and Human Interface: Essence, determinants and consequences of Ethics in human actions; dimensions of ethics; ethics in private and public relationships.\" 1. The Essence of Ethics Ethics is the systematic inquiry into how humans ought to live and act — the discipline that converts moral feeling into reasoned judgement. It answers three questions: What is good? (value theory) What is right? (normative theory) What is this situation demanding of me? (applied ethics). Ethics is not law (law = minimum, enforceable; ethics = optimum, self-binding) and not morality-in-the-colloquial-sense (custom); it includes the critique of custom. 2. Dimensions of Ethics Dimension Question Test / Thinker Meta-ethics What do \"good/right\" even mean? Naturalistic fallacy (Moore) Normative — consequentialism Judge by outcomes Utili","chars":3399},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-human-interface/pyqs","file":"ethics-interface-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-human-interface/pyqs/ethics-interface-pyqs.html","title":"Ethics &amp; Human Interface — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Ethics and Human Interface — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Prelims-style checks Q1. \"Charging prices what the market bears\" vs \"charging what is fair\" best illustrates: (a) law vs morality (b) legality vs legitimacy of action (c) both (a) and (b) (d) neither Model answer: (c). Legal-but-questionable actions map the law–ethics gap. Q2. The \"naturalistic fallacy\" (defining 'good' in terms of natural properties) is associated with: (a) G.E. Moore (b) Kant (c) Bentham (d) Rawls Model answer: (a). Moore, Principia Ethica — a meta-ethics landmark. Q3. Milgram's experiments are best read as evidence for the power of: (a) character (b) situation and authority (c) genes (d) markets Model answer: (b). Situational determinants dominate individual disposition. Mains theory questions with model skeletons Q4. \"Ethics is law's elder sibling: it begins where the rule-boo","chars":2551},{"nav":"gs-4/ethics-human-interface/short","file":"ethics-interface-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-human-interface/short-notes/ethics-interface-pack.html","title":"Ethics &amp; Human Interface — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ethics & Human Interface — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Essence"},{"level":2,"text":"Determinants of ethical action"},{"level":2,"text":"Dimensions & consequences"},{"level":2,"text":"Approaches to resolve dilemmas"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick-cite thinkers"},{"level":2,"text":"FAQs (mini-skeletons)"}],"text":"Ethics & Human Interface — Leaf Pack Deep material lives in mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/ethics-foundations/detailed-notes/ethics-foundations.html . This is the leaf-level drill for the syllabus facet. Essence Ethics = the systematic study of right/wrong conduct standards; morality = the actual norms a community lives by; values = prized end-states (integrity, compassion); norms = enforceable behavioural rules. Meta-ethics (what \"good\" means) / normative ethics (deontology — Kant; consequentialism — Bentham/Mill; virtue — Aristotle) / applied ethics (medical, bioethics, AI, media, corporate governance). Ethical prism test: Is it legal? Is it fair to all stakeholders? Would it withstand publicity (front-page test)? Does it protect the weakest affected? Determinants of ethical action Personal: conscience formation, self-esteem security, cognitive moral development stage (Kohlberg),","chars":3182},{"nav":"gs-4/governance-probity-case-studies/bullets","file":"governance-probity-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/governance-probity-case-studies/bullet-points/governance-probity-points.html","title":"Governance, Probity &amp; Case Studies — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring moves in case answers"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrases"},{"level":2,"text":"Avoid / flag"}],"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Bullet Points ASCII mindmap PROBITY & GOVERNANCE ├── Anti-corruption stack │ ├── PC Act 1988 (+2018) · PMLA 2002 · BNS 2023 offences │ ├── Watchdogs: CVC '03 · CBI (DSPE + Vineet Narain) · ED · Lokpal '13 · ICs under RTI │ └── Metrics: TI CPI 2024 (India ~93/180, score 38) · WGI control-of-corruption · barometer surveys ├── Transparency │ ├── RTI 2005 (+2019 tenure/salary amends) · Sec 4 proactive disclosures · social audits · open-data portals │ ├── Citizen charters → Sevottam → MP Lok Sewa Guarantee Act · state variants (Karnataka Sakala, Bihar etc.) │ └── CPGRAMS (30+ lakh/yr) · MyGov interactions ├── Public funds & procurement │ ├── GFR 2017 · GeM platform · CPPP e-tendering · Integrity Pacts (IEM) │ ├── DBT pipe: ₹2.7+ L cr savings claim (2014–23) · faceless IT assessment · shell-company strike-offs │ └── CAG constitutional role; PAC review chain","chars":2808},{"nav":"gs-4/governance-probity-case-studies/notes","file":"governance-probity-case-studies.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/governance-probity-case-studies/detailed-notes/governance-probity-case-studies.html","title":"Governance, Probity &amp; Case Studies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Probity in governance — the architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Conflict of interest & gift rules"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Corruption typologies, drivers & metrics"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Quality-of-service-delivery & citizen charters"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Public fund utilisation & integrity tools"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Case-study method (repeatable template)"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Contemporary reference anchors (2024–26)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. PYQ anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sources & Further Reading"}],"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS 4 (Mains) · Probity in governance, transparency & information-sharing, RTI, codes of conduct, citizen's charters, work culture, quality of service delivery, utilisation of public funds, corruption + case studies Structure guidance: this set leans on the decision-wheel from the Foundations companion folder — link them in answers. 1. Probity in governance — the architecture Probity = confirmed integrity in public life: legal + moral + procedural correctness across decision-making. Pillars: Anti-corruption stack : Prevention of Corruption Act 1988 (2018 amendment: bribe-giver offence, prior-approval for investigation (s17A) and sanction (s19)), BNS 2023 (criminal code successor), PMLA 2002 (money laundering tail; ED investigations). Watchdogs : CVC (statutory since 2003), CBI (DSPE Act derived — Vineet Narain fixed-tenure","chars":6191},{"nav":"gs-4/governance-probity-case-studies/pyqs","file":"governance-probity-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/governance-probity-case-studies/pyqs/governance-probity-pyqs.html","title":"Governance, Probity &amp; Case Studies — PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — PYQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Conceptual MCQ checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory Qs"},{"level":2,"text":"Case-study questions with structured model answers"}],"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — PYQs Part-A conceptual Qs + Part-B style case studies below. Answers are original model approaches. Conceptual MCQ checks Q1. The \"Independent External Monitors\" in public procurement are tied to: (a) CVC-supervised Integrity Pacts (b) Lokpal probes (c) RTI second appeals (d) CAG compliance audits Model answer: (a). IEMs monitor Integrity Pacts between procurers and bidders in major contracts. Q2. Which section of the RTI Act mandates proactive disclosure? (a) Section 4 (b) Section 6 (c) Section 8 (d) Section 18 Model answer: (a). Section 4 lists proactive disclosures; Section 8 lists exemptions; Section 6 is request-filing. Q3. Prior-approval for investigating certain government servants under the PC Act comes via: (a) Section 17A (b) Section 19 (c) Section 13 (d) Section 7 Model answer: (a). Section 17A (2018 amendment) covers approval to investigat","chars":6429},{"nav":"gs-4/governance-probity-case-studies/short","file":"governance-probity-revision.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/governance-probity-case-studies/short-notes/governance-probity-revision.html","title":"Governance, Probity &amp; Case Studies — Revision Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Revision Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Probity instrument grid"},{"level":2,"text":"COI & rules"},{"level":2,"text":"Corruption metrics (bracket-check at exam)"},{"level":2,"text":"Service-quality anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Fund-integrity mechanisms"},{"level":2,"text":"Contemporary anchors (2024–26)"},{"level":2,"text":"GS-4 case archetype table (memorise)"},{"level":2,"text":"Persuasive one-liners"}],"text":"Governance, Probity & Case Studies — Revision Notes Quick-scan: what each tool is, where it bites, what the the counterweight is. Probity instrument grid Instrument What it is Where it bites Watch-out PC Act 1988 (+2018 Amdt) corruption offences; s17A prior approval; bribe-giver now offence service counts, procurement investigation chill if approvals impede PMLA 2002 money-laundering tail to predicate offences asset trail enforcement bail-strictness & proportionality debates CVC (statutory 2003) supervisory integrity commission tender & service cases advisory limits (can't prosecute directly) CBI (DSPE) central criminal investigator; Vineet Narain fixed tenure PC offences off central servants \"caged parrot\" politics narrative Lokpal & Lokayuktas Act 2013 anti-corruption ombudsman for PM-CM-officials complaints since bench 2023-24 offices under-utilised so far RTI 2005 (+2019 tenure/salar","chars":3638},{"nav":"gs-4/moral-thinkers/bullets","file":"moral-thinkers-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/moral-thinkers/bullet-points/moral-thinkers-points.html","title":"Moral Thinkers — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Moral Thinkers — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Thinker-citation pairings for triggers"},{"level":2,"text":"Ten-second quotes"},{"level":2,"text":"Application habits"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase"}],"text":"Moral Thinkers — Bullet Points Flash-card list: one line each — idea, quote, application. ASCII mindmap MORAL THINKERS ├── WEST: Socrates(conscience) Plato(justice) Aristotle(virtue/mean) │ Kant(duty) Bentham(calculus) Mill(liberty) │ Rawls(fairness) Arendt(banality of evil) └── INDIA: Kautilya(statecraft+audit) Gandhi(talisman/means) Vivekananda(service) Ambedkar(constitutional morality) Aurobindo(integral) Radhakrishnan(ethics as religion) Ashoka(dhamma) Thinker-citation pairings for triggers Question says \"least advantaged\" → Rawls' difference principle + Gandhi's talisman. \"Just following orders\" → Arendt's banality of evil + Kant's autonomy. \"Means and ends\" → Gandhi: means are ends in the making. \"Corruption controls\" → Kautilya's audit machinery + Bentham's calculus of detection. \"Empathy in service\" → Vivekananda's service-as-worship + Aristotle's phronesis. Ten-second quotes \"Un","chars":1636},{"nav":"gs-4/moral-thinkers/notes","file":"moral-thinkers.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/moral-thinkers/detailed-notes/moral-thinkers.html","title":"Moral Thinkers &amp; Philosophers — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Moral Thinkers and Philosophers — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Western Canon"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Indian Traditions"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Holding the Map Together"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Moral Thinkers and Philosophers — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Contributions of moral thinkers and philosophers from India and the world.\" Learn each as: core idea → quotable line → administrative application. 1. Western Canon Thinker Core contribution Quotable Administrative application Socrates Examined life; conscience over majority; irony as method \"The unexamined life is not worth living.\" Self-scrutiny in file notes; conscience against groupthink Plato Justice as harmony; philosopher-king; cave allegory \"Until philosophers rule…\" Merit and knowledge in office; freeing citizens from \"shadow\" misinformation Aristotle Virtue as habituated mean; phronesis (practical wisdom); eudaimonia \"We are what we repeatedly do.\" Practical wisdom = the administrator's gold standard; SOPs as habituation of virtue Kant Categorical imperative (universalisability; humanity as end); good","chars":3713},{"nav":"gs-4/moral-thinkers/pyqs","file":"moral-thinkers-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/moral-thinkers/pyqs/moral-thinkers-pyqs.html","title":"Moral Thinkers — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Moral Thinkers — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Moral Thinkers — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Prelims-style checks Q1. The \"banality of evil\" concept is associated with: (a) Nietzsche (b) Arendt (c) Camus (d) Sartre Model answer: (b). Routinised bureaucratic evil — Eichmann trial reportage. Q2. The \"talisman\" of recalling the poorest man's face belongs to: (a) Vivekananda (b) Gandhi (c) Ambedkar (d) Aurobindo Model answer: (b). A decision test before any public act. Q3. Phronesis in Aristotle denotes: (a) theoretical wisdom (b) practical wisdom (c) craft skill (d) scientific knowledge Model answer: (b). Judgment in particulars — the administrator's virtue. Q4. \"Constitutional morality is not a natural sentiment; it has to be cultivated\" — who said this? (a) Nehru (b) Ambedkar (c) Patel (d) Rajaji Model answer: (b). Ambedkar, defending constitutionalism in the Constituent Assembly. Mains theory questio","chars":2580},{"nav":"gs-4/moral-thinkers/short","file":"moral-thinkers-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/moral-thinkers/short-notes/moral-thinkers-pack.html","title":"Moral Thinkers &amp; Philosophers — Leaf Pack (Rapid Cards)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Moral Thinkers & Philosophers — Leaf Pack (Rapid Cards)"},{"level":2,"text":"Western canon cards"},{"level":2,"text":"Indian canon cards"},{"level":2,"text":"Map recall table"}],"text":"Moral Thinkers & Philosophers — Leaf Pack (Rapid Cards) Western canon cards Socrates : \"unexamined life unworthy\"; conscience over groupthink — urban-hubris yokels trial moral teaching. Plato : philosopher-king; justice as harmony; cave allegory = knowledge liberation vs shadows. Aristotle : virtue = habituated mean between extremes; phronesis = practical wisdom (administrative judgment gold-standard concept); eudaimonia = flourishing end-state. Kant : categorical imperative (universalise / humanity-as-end); autonomy; \"good will\" as motivation anchor. Bentham : utility calculus intro (quantitative); J.S. Mill : qualitative pleasures refinement + harm principle (On Liberty) + representative government insights. Rawls : original position → veil of ignorance → two principles (equal liberty; difference principle for inequalities helping least-advantaged) → welfare-state ethic backbone. Nozic","chars":3533},{"nav":"gs-4/probity-governance/bullets","file":"probity-governance-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/probity-governance/bullet-points/probity-governance-points.html","title":"Probity in Governance — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Probity in Governance — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Precision points (years and functions)"},{"level":2,"text":"Code of conduct vs code of ethics"},{"level":2,"text":"Funds and work culture"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Probity in Governance — Bullet Points 60-second revision card. ASCII mindmap PROBITY IN GOVERNANCE ├── Concept .......... auditable integrity; office as trust ├── Instruments ...... RTI'05 · Lokpal'13 · CVC'03 · WBP'14 │ PCA'88/'18 · Charters+SEVOTTAM · e-gov ├── Accountability ... answerability + consequence + remediation │ (political · admin · legal · social) ├── Funds ............ legality-economy-efficiency-effectiveness-equity │ CAG · PFMS · GeM · social audit └── Corruption ....... Klitgaard: Monopoly + Discretion − Accountability Precision points (years and functions) RTI 2005 — citizen's right to information; PIO timelines (30 days / 48h life-liberty). Lokpal 2013 — apex ombudsman; Lokayuktas in states. CVC statutory 2003 — central vigilance oversight. Whistle Blowers Protection 2014 — disclosure channels (full operationalisation pending). SEVOTTAM 2009 — charter + grievance + ca","chars":1859},{"nav":"gs-4/probity-governance/notes","file":"probity-governance.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/probity-governance/detailed-notes/probity-governance.html","title":"Probity in Governance — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Probity in Governance — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Concept and Philosophical Basis"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Probity in Public Service"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The Ethical Infrastructure"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Accountability and Ethical Governance"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Work Culture and Quality of Service Delivery"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Utilisation of Public Funds"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Challenges of Corruption"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Probity in Governance — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Probity in Governance: concept; philosophical basis; public service; ethical infrastructure; accountability & ethical governance; RTI; citizen's charters; work culture; quality of service delivery; utilisation of public funds; challenges of corruption.\" 1. Concept and Philosophical Basis Probity is integrity demonstrated and auditable in public office — the visible, verifiable side of private virtue. Its philosophical roots: social-contract theory (office is a trust, power is delegated), Kautilyan rajadharma (the ruler's welfare-duty and audit), Gandhi's means-ends discipline, and the constitutional promise of equality (Article 14) which corruption quietly defeats. Probity asks not \"did I break a rule?\" but \"could this act survive sunlight, audit and the weakest citizen's gaze?\" 2. Probity in Public Service Conflict of ","chars":4314},{"nav":"gs-4/probity-governance/pyqs","file":"probity-governance-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/probity-governance/pyqs/probity-governance-pyqs.html","title":"Probity in Governance — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Probity in Governance — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Probity in Governance — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Prelims-style checks Q1. SEVOTTAM model relates to: (a) police reform (b) benchmarking citizen charters and grievance redress (c) electoral funding (d) judicial standards Model answer: (b). Charter compliance + grievance system + capability building. Q2. The Whistle Blowers Protection Act was enacted in: (a) 2004 (b) 2010 (c) 2014 (d) 2019 Model answer: (c). 2014 — following the 2004Resolution lineage (CVC resolution) and 2011 Bill. Q3. \"March rush\" in expenditure primarily indicates failure of: (a) accounting standards (b) planning and budget discipline (c) procurement law (d) audit Model answer: (b). Year-end surge reflects poor plan execution — a probity-of-planning issue. Mains theory questions with model skeletons Q4. \"Integrity is a virtue; probity is its audit trail.\" Discuss the concept of prob","chars":2727},{"nav":"gs-4/probity-governance/short","file":"probity-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/probity-governance/short-notes/probity-pack.html","title":"Probity in Governance — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Probity in Governance — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Working definition"},{"level":2,"text":"Instrument lattice"},{"level":2,"text":"Information-sharing & transparency debates"},{"level":2,"text":"Corruption phenomenology (PYQ word-bank)"},{"level":2,"text":"Ethical leadership in organisations"},{"level":2,"text":"Case-chunks for exams"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liners"}],"text":"Probity in Governance — Leaf Pack Working definition Probity = confirmed integrity in public life: compliance with law + adherence to moral standards + procedural correctness in decision-making. Adjacent to: rectitude (uprightness in complex pressures), propriety (process-legitimacy). Instrument lattice Transparency: RTI 2005 (Sec 4 proactive disclosure; ICs; 2019 amendments debates on tenure), audit trails, public dashboards (GeM statistics, CPGRAMS), electoral-funding transparency (post-Electoral-Bonds-struck Feb 2024 — disclosure vacuum = live question). Accountability stacks: hierarchical (departmental enquiries), legal (courts, PC Act), professional (CVC/ICAD style reviews), political (parliamentary committees, question hour), social (social audits, gram sabhas, media). Anti-corruption: PC Act 1988 (+2018), PMLA 2002, CBI under DSPE frame + CVC supervision; ED actor footprint; annua","chars":3111},{"nav":"gs-4/public-service-values/bullets","file":"public-service-values-points.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/public-service-values/bullet-points/public-service-values-points.html","title":"Public / Civil Service Values — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Government vs private cheat row"},{"level":2,"text":"Law vs conscience ladder"},{"level":2,"text":"Moral judgment quickies"},{"level":2,"text":"Strengthening ethics — five Rs"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Pitfall flags"}],"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — Bullet Points 60-second revision card. ASCII mindmap PUBLIC SERVICE VALUES ├── Core-4 ........ neutrality · anonymity · impartiality · integrity ├── Dilemmas ...... govt: discretion/secrecy/pressure │ private: profit vs safety, data, greenwash ├── Sources ....... law → hierarchy → constitutional morality → conscience ├── Judgment ...... Kohlberg: aim post-conventional; write rule-cited orders └── Strengthening . select · train · incentivise · institutionalise · culture Government vs private cheat row Duty: public interest vs shareholder value (stakeholder theory contests). Accountability: legislature/RTI/audit vs board/regulator/market. Bridge: CSR S.135 Companies Act 2013; both face conflicts & whistle-blowing. Law vs conscience ladder 1 Law/rules (certainty, equality) → 2 Advice through hierarchy → 3 Constitutional morality → 4 Conscience as alarm → reco","chars":1875},{"nav":"gs-4/public-service-values/notes","file":"public-service-values.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/public-service-values/detailed-notes/public-service-values.html","title":"Public / Civil Service Values — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Values of the Civil Service"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Ethical Issues and Dilemmas in Government vs Private Institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Laws, Rules, Regulations and Conscience as Sources of Ethical Guidance"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Ethical Reasoning and Moral Judgment"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Strengthening Ethical and Moral Values in Governance"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Answer-writing Pointers"}],"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — Detailed Notes Syllabus slot: GS Paper IV · \"Ethical issues and dilemmas in government and private institutions; accountability and ethical governance; laws, rules, regulations and conscience as sources of ethical guidance; ethical reasoning & moral judgment; strengthening of ethical and moral values in governance.\" 1. Values of the Civil Service Core quartet: neutrality (serve the government of the day, not a party), anonymity (advise in private, the minister answers in public), impartiality (merit and law) and integrity . Supporting values: objectivity, dedication, empathy, accountability, responsiveness. Northcote–Trevelyan (1854) planted merit as the founding value of modern services; Sardar Patel defended the inherited steel frame as a servant of the Constitution, not of any dynasty or party . The Second ARC (Ethics in Governance, 2007) reframed value","chars":3760},{"nav":"gs-4/public-service-values/pyqs","file":"public-service-values-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/public-service-values/pyqs/public-service-values-pyqs.html","title":"Public / Civil Service Values — PYQs &amp; Model Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — PYQs & Model Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims-style checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mains theory questions with model skeletons"}],"text":"Public / Civil Service Values — PYQs & Model Approaches UPSC-pattern questions with skeletons. Prelims-style checks Q1. The doctrine that civil servants advise privately while ministers answer publicly is: (a) neutrality (b) anonymity (c) impartiality (d) probity Model answer: (b). Anonymity of advice, accountability of the minister. Q2. Kohlberg's \"post-conventional\" level is characterised by reasoning from: (a) punishment avoidance (b) peer approval (c) self-chosen principles (d) legal literalism Model answer: (c). Principled, autonomous standards above rule worship. Q3. Corporate Social Responsibility under Section 135 applies to companies above thresholds in the: (a) Companies Act 1956 (b) Companies Act 2013 (c) SEBI LODR (d) FEMA Model answer: (b). S.135, Companies Act 2013 — the statutory public-private ethics bridge. Mains theory questions with model skeletons Q4. \"Conscience is a","chars":2803},{"nav":"gs-4/public-service-values/short","file":"public-service-values-pack.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/public-service-values/short-notes/public-service-values-pack.html","title":"Public Service Values &amp; Ethics in Administration — Leaf Pack","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public Service Values & Ethics in Administration — Leaf Pack"},{"level":2,"text":"The spine"},{"level":2,"text":"Constitutional-legal frame"},{"level":2,"text":"Civil-service neutrality: debates"},{"level":2,"text":"Work culture & quality of service delivery"},{"level":2,"text":"Utilisation of public funds (integrity lens)"},{"level":2,"text":"Case-triggers in exam"},{"level":2,"text":"One-liners"}],"text":"Public Service Values & Ethics in Administration — Leaf Pack The spine Civil service distinctness: permanent, professional, politically-neutral agent of constitutional order — serves governments, not parties (\"serve fearlessly, advise honestly, comply lawfully\"). Values catalogue drill: integrity · impartiality · non-partisanship · objectivity · dedication · empathy + accountability + transparency + rule-of-law + equity-first + efficiency + resilience. Constitutional-legal frame Art 311 dismissal-protection shield (inquiry requirement) — service-security logic for honest advice. All India Services (Conduct) Rules 1968 + CCS Conduct Rules 1964: integrity clause, gift ceilings, dowry bar, sexual-harassment prohibitions, bigamy and joint-family property declarations, political-neutrality expectations, private-trade/employment restrictions. Prevention of Corruption Act 1988 (+2018 amendment:","chars":3559},{"nav":"gs-4","file":"README.html","rel":"content/mains/gs-4-ethics-integrity-aptitude/README.html","title":"General Studies IV — Ethics, Integrity &amp; Aptitude","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"General Studies IV — Ethics, Integrity & Aptitude"}],"text":"General Studies IV — Ethics, Integrity & Aptitude Two topic folders, each with the standard 5-section set ( detailed-notes/ · short-notes/ · bullet-points/ · diagrams/ · pyqs/ ): ethics-foundations/ — ethics & human interface, attitude, aptitude, EI, thinkers, conscience governance-probity-case-studies/ — probity, transparency/RTI, corruption, service delivery, case-study method Companion: ../essay-frameworks/ covers the Mains essay paper (not a GS folder). After adding/editing files: node cli/generate.mjs from upsc-portal/ re-indexes them.","chars":546},{"nav":"optional-subjects/bullets","file":"optional-comparison.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/bullet-points/optional-comparison.html","title":"Optional Subjects — Quick Comparison Table","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Optional Subjects — Quick Comparison Table"},{"level":2,"text":"Underrated heuristics"}],"text":"Optional Subjects — Quick Comparison Table Companion to detailed-notes/choosing-an-optional.html . Scoring-history cells are directional, not certified — verify against the latest UPSC annual-report tables. Optional GS overlap Syllabus size Material ecosystem Answer texture Typical candidate fit Sociology High (GS-1 society, essays) Medium Large Analytical + case-study comfortable Humanities+sciences both Anthropology Medium (tribal welfare, science bits) Compact Good Diagram/flow-friendly Precise-writers; compactness seekers Geography High (GS-1, prelims maps) Large Large Map + concept dual format Spatial thinkers History High (GS-1 modern/world/art) Very large Large Narrative-analytic essays Substance stamina readers PSIR High (GS-2 both halves) Medium Large Theory + IR current fusion Polity/IR-strong candidates Public Administration High (GS-2 governance) Medium Medium (cohort thinned","chars":2496},{"nav":"optional-subjects/notes","file":"choosing-an-optional.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/detailed-notes/choosing-an-optional.html","title":"Choosing an Optional Subject — Decision Guide","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Choosing an Optional Subject — Decision Guide"},{"level":2,"text":"The selection stakes"},{"level":2,"text":"The 7-factor selection matrix"},{"level":2,"text":"The practical shortlist method"},{"level":2,"text":"Signals that you picked right (check after 2 months)"},{"level":2,"text":"Signals you may need to switch (check honestly, once)"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap quick-map"},{"level":2,"text":"Timeline archetype"},{"level":2,"text":"Sources"}],"text":"Choosing an Optional Subject — Decision Guide The optional (2 papers × 250 = 500 marks) is the single largest scoring block in Mains after GS. This note is the selection playbook; per-subject deep notes are a separate expansion track. The selection stakes 500/1750 Mains-written marks ≈ 29% of merit weight. Optional-score spread is wider than GS-spread in most years — a strong optional choice frequently swings final rank lists more than incremental GS effort. Time budget reality: optionals absorb 8–10 hours/week of a serious attempt for 6–9 months. The 7-factor selection matrix Graduation overlap — reduces marginal cost of basics; strongest predictor of sustained comfort. Interest stamina — you will re-read the syllabus 3–4 times; fatigue risk is real. Material ecosystem — standard books, coaching handouts, evaluated test-series availability, peer community size. GS overlap — cheaper prep","chars":4164},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/bullets","file":"anthropology-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/bullet-points/anthropology-points.html","title":"Anthropology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Anthropology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Anthropology — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation (why-pick) lives in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap ANTHROPOLOGY OPTIONAL ├── P1 = SCIENCE .... evolution · kinship/economy/polity/religion │ · theories (8 schools) · methods · genetics ├── P2 = INDIA ...... prehistoric → caste/village/tribe → │ land-alienation/PVTG/safeguards (5th/6th Sch) ├── STYLE ........... define first · diagram per answer · named │ tribal case + scheme + datum └── FIT ............. science-comfort · closed syllabus · memory discipline for fossils/genetics Scoring levers Definitional exactness (Tylor's culture, Morgan's periods, IPL of kinship terms). Diagrams: lineage trees, kinship triangles, theory tables — cheap marks, visible rigour. Tribal case notebook: 12 communities × (issue + constitutional hook + scheme + datum). Applications block (forensic/medical/public-health) ","chars":1550},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/notes","file":"anthropology-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/detailed-notes/anthropology-syllabus-map.html","title":"Anthropology — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Anthropology — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Indian Anthropology)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Anthropology — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1 builds the science (evolution, society, theory, methods); Paper 2 applies it to India (caste, tribe, nation). The optional rewards definite answers, diagrams and tribal case studies — and punishes vagueness least among humanities optionals. Paper 1 — Unit Map Block Units Core content Foundations 1.1–1.5 Meaning, scope, development; relations with sociology, history, biology; main branches (physical, socio-cultural, archaeological, linguistic); applied dimension Human evolution 2.1–2.5 Man's place in animal kingdom; primate evolution; fossil hominids (Australopithecus → Homo habilis → erectus → Neanderthal → Cro-Magnon); biological & cultural evolution; domestication Social organisation 3.1–3.4 & 4.x Marriage, family, kinship (types, terminology, usages); economic organisation (foraging to industrial); political organisation (band → tribe → c","chars":3728},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/pyqs","file":"anthropology-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/pyqs/anthropology-pyqs.html","title":"Anthropology — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Anthropology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Anthropology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise question patterns framed on recurring themes (see the official UPSC paper for verbatim texts). Each carries a model approach. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. Distinguish between emic and etic approaches with one ethnographic example each. (10 marks) Approach: define (insider-view vs observer-view; Harris lineage) → example: ritual cattle protection read emically as dharma, etically as agro-economy → evaluate complementarity; conclude method-pluralism. Q2. Trace the stages of human evolution from Australopithecus to Homo sapiens with fossil evidence. (15 marks) Approach: lineage table (species · region · cranial capacity · tools) → key fossils (Laetoli, Java, Zhoukoudian, Neander valley, Cro-Magnon) → trends (bipedalism → encephalisation → culture) → diagram essential. Q3. Critically examine Malinowski's functionalism with reference to t","chars":3201},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/short","file":"anthropology-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-anthropology/short-notes/anthropology-orientation.html","title":"Anthropology (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Anthropology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Core framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap & answer texture"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"First-90-days plan"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Anthropology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it Compact syllabus among humanities subjects; diagram-friendly answers; GS overlap in tribal affairs, common lessons with sociology; scoring historically kind to disciplined writers. Syllabus map Paper-I : meaning/scope of anthropology (physical/social-cultural/archaeological/linguistic threads); human evolution (fossil sequence — Australopithecus → Homo habilis → erectus → sapiens; tool-tech eras); primates; marriage-family-kinship frameworks; economic/political organisations (band-tribe-chiefdom-state); religion (animism, animatism, totemism), anthropological theories (classical evolutionism → diffusionism → functionalism (Malinowski, Radcliffe-Brown) → structuralism (Levi-Strauss) → cultural materialism (Harris) → postmodern turns); research methods (fieldwork, participant observation tradition from Malinow","chars":3128},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-economics/bullets","file":"economics-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-economics/bullet-points/economics-points.html","title":"Economics — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Economics — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Economics — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap ECONOMICS OPTIONAL ├── P1 MICRO ...... Slutsky · market structures (Cournot-Stackelberg) │ · game basics · market failures · welfare (Pareto) ├── P1 MACRO ...... accounting debates · IS-LM → RBC vs NK │ · Mundell-Fleming · expectations ├── P1 GROWTH ..... Solow vs Romer · HDI/MDP · Lewis dualism │ · balanced vs unbalanced · env-development ├── P1 PF+INTL .... Musgrave · Ramsey/incidence · Oates federalism │ · debt dynamics · Ricardian→HO→Krugman · WTO ├── P2 INDIA ...... 1991 hinge · LPG sequence · GST/IBC/MPC │ · agri-industry-services puzzles · Survey chapters └── STYLE ......... graph-first · 6 derivations drilled · Survey figure as data anchor · debate bank ×4 Scoring levers Graph shelf: indifference+Slutsky, IS-LM shifts, MF triangle (impossible trinity), tariff","chars":2027},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-economics/notes","file":"economics-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-economics/detailed-notes/economics-syllabus-map.html","title":"Economics — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Economics — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map (Theory)"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Indian Economy)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Economics — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: theory — micro, macro, growth, public finance, international economics. Paper 2: the Indian economy — reform lineage to present-day policy debates. GS-3's economy heartland overlaps; Paper 1's analytics shelter precise writers while penalising the under-prepared. Paper 1 — Unit Map (Theory) Block Units Anchors Microeconomics 1.x–3.x Consumer theory (utility → indifference → revealed preference, Slutsky), production-costs, market structures (perfect → monopoly → oligopoly models: Cournot, Stackelberg; game theory basics), welfare economics (Pareto, social-welfare functions, market failures — externalities, public goods, information asymmetry) Macroeconomics 4.x–6.x National-income accounting debates; money & banking (money multiplier, central-bank toolkit); inflation theories; IS-LM synthesis and beyond (new-classical RBC vs new-Keynesian micro","chars":4234},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-economics/pyqs","file":"economics-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-economics/pyqs/economics-pyqs.html","title":"Economics — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Economics — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Economics — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. Decompose the price effect into income and substitution effects (Hicksian or Slutskian — state your choice). (10 marks) Approach: compensated-variation construction → Slutsky equation sign logic (normal vs inferior vs Giffen) → labelled indifference-map with auxiliary budget line → one-line relevance: Giffen evidence debates. Q2. Compare Cournot and Stackelberg duopoly outcomes. (10 marks) Approach: simultaneous-quantity vs first-mover games → reaction-function diagram → outputs/prices ranking (Stackelberg leader gains, industry output higher than Cournot, lower than competition) → welfare note. Q3. \"An economy cannot simultaneously fix the exchange rate, allow free capital flows and run independent monetar","chars":3637},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-economics/short","file":"economics-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-economics/short-notes/economics-orientation.html","title":"Economics (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Economics (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Economics (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it GS-3 economy runs concurrent prep dividends; analytical derivation-culture gives precision-marking shelter; formal-answer format advantages the mathematically tidy. Needs genuine quantitative comfort. Syllabus map Paper-I (Micro/Macro/Monetary/Public/Intl) : micro theory — consumer & producer theory refinements (Slutsky vs Hicksian decompositions), market structures, welfare-economics box (Pareto-optimality, social-welfare functions, Arrow impossibility), asymmetric information (Akerlof-Spence-Stiglitz trinity); general equilibrium trace (Edgeworth); macro enumeration — income-determination (Keynesian cross → IS-LM → AD-AS), consumption hypotheses (Keynes-Friedman-Modigliani-Fisher traces), investment accelerator, demand-for-money theories (Fisher-Baumol-Tobin/Keynes), money-supply mechanics (H-theory credit-mu","chars":3831},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-geography/bullets","file":"geography-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-geography/bullet-points/geography-points.html","title":"Geography — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Geography — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap GEOGRAPHY OPTIONAL ├── P1 PHYSICAL ... geomorph (plates, landforms) · climatology (monsoon │ lineage, jets, fronts) · oceanography (currents, ENSO) │ · soils · biomes ├── P1 HUMAN ...... determinism→possibilism · Malthus/Boserup · DT │ · von Thünen · Weber/Losch · Christaller/Zipf ├── P2 INDIA ...... relief-drainage-climate · population/migration │ · agro-industrial belts · corridors · planning │ · reorganisation · contemporary (floods, sprawl) ├── STYLE ......... map in every answer · process→landform→example │ · model → assumptions → Indian verification └── FIT ........... systems-thinkers · drawing-comfort · biggest syllabus — volume discipline decides Scoring levers Pre-rehearsed diagram shelf: plate margins, monsoon ITCZ shift, ocean conveyor, von Thünen rings, ","chars":1925},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-geography/notes","file":"geography-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-geography/detailed-notes/geography-syllabus-map.html","title":"Geography — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (India)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Geography — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: physical + human geography and the model bank. Paper 2: India end-to-end plus planning and contemporary issues. The diagram-and-map craft decides bands — and the GS-1/Prelims overlap is the strongest of all optionals. Paper 1 — Unit Map Block Units Anchors Physical (Geomorphology) 1.x Earth interior, isostasy (Airy/Pratt), continental drift → plate tectonics, earthquakes/volcanoes, mountain building, fluvial/arid/glacial/karst/coastal landforms, applied geomorphology Climatology & oceanography 2.x–3.x Atmosphere composition/structure, insolation & heat budget, winds, jet streams, air masses/fronts/cyclones, monsoon mechanism lineage (Halley → Hadley → ITCZ dynamics), ocean relief, temperature/salinity, currents (El Niño/La Niña), marine resources, coral bleaching, Law of the Sea basics Bio & environmental 4.x Soil profile & classification, bio","chars":3722},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-geography/pyqs","file":"geography-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-geography/pyqs/geography-pyqs.html","title":"Geography — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Geography — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. \"Plate tectonics unified what isostasy could only explain.\" Discuss. (10 marks) Approach: isostasy's static balance (Airy roots vs Pratt density) → drift evidence (Wegener) → seafloor spreading → unification via plate margins (constructive/destructive/conservative) → Himalayan example → diagram: three margin types. Q2. Explain the monsoon mechanism; assess the role of the ITCZ and jet streams. (15 marks) Approach: lineage — Halley's thermal (differential heating) → Hadley cell/ITCZ seasonal migration → Tibetan-plateau heating, tropical easterly jet → ENSO/IOD modulation → forecasting value → India-map diagram: January vs July ITCZ positions. Q3. Critically examine von Thünen's agricultural location model fo","chars":3380},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-geography/short","file":"geography-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-geography/short-notes/geography-orientation.html","title":"Geography (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap & answer texture"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"First-90-days plan"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Geography (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it GS-1 geography + prelims maps overlap is genuinely strong; universal-manageable material; visual answers (maps/diagrams) reward accuracy; but syllabus is on the larger side. Syllabus map Paper-I (Principles) : geomorphology (endogenetic/exogenetic processes, plate tectonics, slope development, channel morphology); climatology (composition/instability/cyclones/jet streams/monsoon+ENSO/NAM etc dynamics); oceanography (T-S properties, currents, tides, coral reefs, marine resources, sea-level-change debate); biogeography (soil genesis, ecosystems, biomes, biogeochemical cycles, deforestation debate, social forestry); environmental geography (hazard management, EIA principles, environmentalism phases); perspectives (determinism-possibilism; regional/methods sequence; quantification turn and behavioural turn); econom","chars":3924},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-history/bullets","file":"history-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-history/bullet-points/history-points.html","title":"History — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"History — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"History — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap HISTORY OPTIONAL ├── P1 ANCIENT .... sources · Harappan debates · Vedic→janapada │ · Maurya (dhamma/kautilya) · Gupta debates ├── P1 MEDIEVAL ... feudalism debate (Sharma vs Mukhia) · Chola │ local govt · Sultanate iqta · Vijayanagara systems │ · Mughal mansab-jagir · Maratha · 18th-c debate ├── P2 MODERN ..... settlements & drain theory · 1857 · national │ movement (Cambridge/nationalist/subaltern lens) │ · Partition · 1947-64 consensus ├── P2 WORLD ...... revolutions · industrialisation · imperialism │ theories · wars · decolonisation · Cold War end └── STYLE ......... historiography per answer · theme-inside-chrono · map questions = free marks Scoring levers Debate dossier: feudalism, 18th-century, deindustrialisation, Partition causes — one card each (claims ×3 sch","chars":1785},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-history/notes","file":"history-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-history/detailed-notes/history-syllabus-map.html","title":"History — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"History — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map (Ancient + Medieval)"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Modern + World)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"History — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: ancient + medieval India. Paper 2: modern India, British rule, freedom struggle, early post-independence + world history. Volume discipline, historiographical framing and map questions decide the outcome; GS-1 overlap on modern history is heavy. Paper 1 — Unit Map (Ancient + Medieval) Block Units Anchors Sources & prehistory 1.x–2.x Archaeology/epigraphy/numismatics/literature as source classes; prehistoric cultures (palaeolithic → neolithic); Harappan civilisation debates (town planning, script, decline theories) Early states 3.x–5.x Vedic society (sacrificial polity to janapada); mahajanapadas, Magadhan rise; Mauryan state (kautilyan administration, Ashokan dhamma); post-Mauryan contacts (Indo-Greeks, Kushanas, Satavahanas); Gupta \"classical\" debates + Harsha Early medieval 6.x–9.x Regional polities (Pallavas-Cholas' local self-govt debates; R","chars":4114},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-history/pyqs","file":"history-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-history/pyqs/history-pyqs.html","title":"History — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"History — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"History — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. \"The Harappan decline is a palimpsest of environmental, hydrological and social readings.\" Discuss. (15 marks) Approach: decline-theory ledger — Ghaggar-Hakra desiccation, monsoon weakening (palaeoclimate proxies), river shifts, trade-network disruption, elite-crisis readings → caution: no single-cause verdict → de-urbanisation into late-Harappan cultures → conclude: layered transformation, not annihilation. Q2. Evaluate the Mauryan state through Kautilyan and Ashokan lenses. (15 marks) Approach: Kautilya — centralised treasury-army-spy architecture (saptanga frame) vs Ashokan inscriptions — dhamma as welfare-morality state, provincial viceroys → tensions: coercive-administrative vs ethical-integrative → lega","chars":3890},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-history/short","file":"history-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-history/short-notes/history-orientation.html","title":"History (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"History (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Follow-on skills"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"History (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it Deep GS-1 modern+world+heritage overlap; map-questions add scorable precision; but syllabus breadth is famously heavy — volume discipline decides viability. Syllabus map Paper-I (Ancient + Medieval India) : prehistory & proto-history (Indus civilisation); Vedic corpus & pastoral-agri transition; mahajanapadas→Mauryan state formation (megasthenes/Indica + edicts archaeology); post-Mauryan trade-urban explosion; Sangam social life; Gupta state & cultural fluorescence; regional kingdoms (Cholas admin/irrigation, Pallavas, Rashtrakutas, Chalukyas, Pratiharas + Arabs contact); post-750 socio-cultural trends (shramanic residue→bhakti consolidation). Medieval: early-medieval state debates (feudalism vs integrative polity conversation: R S Sharma vs B D Chattopadhyaya etc); Delhi sultanate (politics, iqta economy, Mongol","chars":3857},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/bullets","file":"philosophy-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/bullet-points/philosophy-points.html","title":"Philosophy — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Philosophy — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Philosophy — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap PHILOSOPHY OPTIONAL ├── P1 INDIAN ..... Carvaka · Bauddha (sunyata) · Jaina (anekanta) │ · Nyaya pramanas · Vaisesika padarthas · Samkhya │ · Yoga ashtanga · Mimamsa · Advaita maya (+rivals) ├── P1 WESTERN .... Plato/Aristotle → Descartes-Spinoza-Leibniz │ → Locke-Berkeley-Hume → Kant-Hegel · logic block ├── P2 SOCIO-POL .. ideologies · sovereignty · rights · punishment │ · gender · environmental ethics ├── P2 RELIGION ... ontological/cosmological/teleological proofs │ · problem of evil · religious experience/language └── STYLE ......... reconstruction format: thesis→args→objections→ reply→verdict · Sanskrit term + plain English Scoring levers Comparison tables: pramana counts across schools; satkarya debates; God-proof pairs with named objections. One-line objectio","chars":1807},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/notes","file":"philosophy-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/detailed-notes/philosophy-syllabus-map.html","title":"Philosophy — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Philosophy — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Philosophy — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: Indian + Western metaphysics/epistemology and logic. Paper 2: socio-political philosophy + philosophy of religion. The smallest syllabus among major optionals — but it pays only for clarity, structure and fair reconstruction of arguments. Paper 1 — Unit Map Block Units Anchors Indian systems orthodox + heterodox Carvaka (materialism, epistemic anti-extravagance); Bauddha (kshanikavada, pratityasamutpada, Madhyamaka's sunyata, Yogacara); Jaina (anekantavada, syadvada); Nyaya (pramanas, inference anatomy); Vaisesika (padarthas); Samkhya (purusa-prakrti, satkaryavada); Yoga (ashtanga, citta-vrtti); Mimamsa (svatah-pramanya, karma-kanda); Vedanta (Advaita's maya + anuvada, Visistadvaita, Dvaita contrasts) Western lineage ancient → modern Plato (theory of forms, soul), Aristotle (four causes, substance); Descartes (methodic doubt, mind-body proble","chars":3792},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/pyqs","file":"philosophy-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/pyqs/philosophy-pyqs.html","title":"Philosophy — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Philosophy — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Philosophy — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. Explain anekantavada and syadvada; how do they answer the charge of self-contradiction? (10 marks) Approach: define manifold-predication (reality's many-sidedness) → seven-fold modal scheme (syat-astina...) → objection: contradiction? → reply: conditional predication from standpoints (naya) → example: vase's material/persistent/temporal predicates → epistemic humility conclusion. Q2. Compare Carvaka and Nyaya on the number and validity of pramanas. (10 marks) Approach: Carvaka — perception alone; inference distrusted (observed-conjunction critique) → Nyaya — four pramanas with inference's anatomy (vyapti, paksata) → counter: Carvaka's own universal claims need inference → verdict: Nyaya's machinery survive","chars":3727},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/short","file":"philosophy-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-philosophy/short-notes/philosophy-orientation.html","title":"Philosophy (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Philosophy (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Philosophy (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it Compact syllabus (among the smallest), essay + GS-4 ethic bridges, pleasing logical answer formats; requires comfort with abstraction — strong verbal/written discipline pays. Syllabus map Paper-I (History & Problems of Philosophy) : Plato-Aristotle (forms/substance); rationalists (Descartes-Spinoza-Leibniz); empiricists (Locke-Berkeley-Hume); Kant (Copernican turn, categorical imperatives); Hegel (dialectic); Moore-Russell (analytic shift, logical atomism); Logical Positivism (verification theory → falsifiability Popper); Wittgenstein (language games, meaning-as-use); Phenomenology (Husserl); Existentialism (Sartre/Kierkegaard); Quine & Strawson (analytic-continental bridges contemp); applied problems units. Contemporary units recurring in PYQs: theory of meaning; truth-correspondence-coherence; realism-ideali","chars":3010},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-polity/bullets","file":"psir-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-polity/bullet-points/psir-points.html","title":"PSIR — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"PSIR — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"PSIR — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap PSIR OPTIONAL ├── P1A THEORY .... approaches · state theories · justice (Rawls-Sen-Nozick) │ · liberty/rights · democracy · power (Lukes) · ideologies ├── P1B INDIA ..... national movement streams · party system · federalism │ · judiciary/PIL · local govt · movements · identity politics ├── P2A COMP+IR ... realism (Morgenthau-Waltz) · liberal interdependence │ · constructivism (Wendt) · English school · IPE · deterrence ├── P2B INDIA-WORLD ... doctrine lineage · neighbours ×6 · powers · BIMSTEC/ │ SCO/QUAD/G20 · UN reform · climate diplomacy └── STYLE ......... scholar-density 2-3/answer · substance+structure in IR · current + durable-principle pairing Scoring levers Quotable one-liners: Rawls' basic structure, Waltz's anarchy, Wendt's \"anarchy is what states make of it\".","chars":1808},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-polity/notes","file":"psir-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-polity/detailed-notes/psir-syllabus-map.html","title":"PSIR — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Political Science & International Relations (PSIR) — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Political Science & International Relations (PSIR) — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: political theory + Indian government & politics. Paper 2: comparative politics + IR theory + India and the world. The GS-2 overlap is near-total — PSIR is the highest-compounding optional for aspirants targeting polity/IR depth. Paper 1 — Unit Map Block Units Anchors Political theory 1.x–4.x Approaches (normative, empirical, feminist, post-colonial); theories of state (liberal, neo-liberal, Marxist, Gandhian, pluralist); justice (Rawls, Sen, Nozick); equality, liberty, rights; democracy (procedural → deliberative); power (Lukes' faces); ideology (liberalism, socialism, Marxism, nationalism, feminism, multiculturalism) Indian politics 5.x–8.x National movement & constitutionalism (Gandhi-Ambedkar-Periyar-Lohia streams); party system evolution; federalism (commission-era → GST-era frictions); judiciary & ","chars":3447},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-polity/pyqs","file":"psir-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-polity/pyqs/psir-pyqs.html","title":"PSIR — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"PSIR — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"PSIR — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. \"Rawls, Sen and Nozick offer three incommensurable grammars of justice.\" Compare. (15 marks) Approach: Rawls — basic-structure fairness (difference principle); Sen — capability + comparative justice (nitya vs transcendental institutional); Nozick — entitlement, minimal state → Indian resonance (reservations as Rawlsian, DBT as capability, tax-debate as Nozickian) → conclude: policy mixes all three. Q2. Examine Ambedkar's and Gandhi's differing routes to social transformation. (15 marks) Approach: constitutional-modernity + state-led annihilation of caste vs moral-spiritual self-reform + village reconstruction → convergences (dignity, anti-untouchability) → post-47 synthesis in constitutional design → contemporar","chars":3601},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-polity/short","file":"psir-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-polity/short-notes/psir-orientation.html","title":"Political Science &amp; International Relations (PSIR) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Political Science & International Relations (PSIR) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap & answer texture"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Political Science & International Relations (PSIR) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it GS-2 polity & IR halves sync up; IR-literate aspirants gain essay+interview compounds; answer style mirrors GS writing with theory premium. Syllabus map Paper-I : Part A political theory (theories of state — liberal/neo-liberal/Marxist/Gramscian/feminist; justice & Rawls-vs-communitarian; equality debates; rights (Human-rights three-generation discourse); democracy (procedural-deliberative); power (Foucault-Weber scales); ideology tracks (liberalism, conservatism, socialism.. incl. neo-lines); debates in western canon (Plato→Machiavelli→Hobbes…); political ideologies stack; Part B: Indian political thought (Vedic-dharmashastra → Buddhist-arthashastra heritage → Renaissance blend (Rammohan) → Moderate-Extremist → Gandhian-Nehruvian-socialist/Ambedkarite streams → modern-indian think","chars":3896},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-psychology/bullets","file":"psychology-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-psychology/bullet-points/psychology-points.html","title":"Psychology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Psychology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Psychology — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap PSYCHOLOGY OPTIONAL ├── P1 SCIENCE .... methods · brain bases · perception (Gestalt) │ · learning (Pavlov-Skinner-Bandura) · memory │ (Baddeley) · thinking (Kahneman) · emotion theories │ · personality (Big Five vs Freud) · intelligence │ · attitudes (dissonance) · groups (Asch-Milgram) ├── P2 APPLIED .... stress-coping (Lazarus) · disorders + CBT ladder │ · work-IO · educational · community · environment │ · sports/terrorism edges ├── STYLE ......... def → model → named experiment → Indian │ programme (NMHS/Manodarpan) → critique + diagram └── FIT ........... GS-4 bridge · compact syllabus · precision marking — never invent studies Scoring levers Experiment bank: 20 classics with design+finding in two lines each (Milgram, Asch, Schachter-Singer, Seligman's dogs...)","chars":1834},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-psychology/notes","file":"psychology-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-psychology/detailed-notes/psychology-syllabus-map.html","title":"Psychology — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Psychology — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Psychology in Action)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Psychology — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: the science core (foundations, cognition, personality, social psychology). Paper 2: applications (well-being, disorders, organisational, educational, community). Compact syllabus, experiment-friendly examples, and a direct GS-4 bridge — but the marking rewards textbook precision plus Indian case application. Paper 1 — Unit Map Block Units Anchors Foundations 1.x–4.x Introduction & history (structuralism → behaviourism → cognitive turn); research methods (experiment, correlation, observation, ethics); biological bases (neurons, brain areas, endocrine links); sensation-attention-perception (thresholds, Gestalt laws, attention bottleneck models) Cognition 5.x–7.x Learning (classical/operant, observational — Bandura); memory (STM/LTM, Baddeley's working model, forgetting theories, mnemonics); thinking-problem-solving (heuristics — Kahneman lineag","chars":4042},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-psychology/pyqs","file":"psychology-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-psychology/pyqs/psychology-pyqs.html","title":"Psychology — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Psychology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Psychology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. Distinguish top-down and bottom-up processing with perceptual examples. (10 marks) Approach: define data-driven vs concept-driven → examples: reading degraded text (context fill — top-down) vs feature detection in novel stimuli (bottom-up) → interactionist evidence (word-superiority effect) → diagram the dual arrows. Q2. Explain Baddeley's working-memory model and its evidence. (15 marks) Approach: components — central executive, phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, episodic buffer → evidence: dual-task interference, phonological-similarity effect, word-length effect → upgrade from multistore account → applications: arithmetic, comprehension. Q3. Compare James-Lange, Cannon-Bard and Schachter-Singer ","chars":3467},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-psychology/short","file":"psychology-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-psychology/short-notes/psychology-orientation.html","title":"Psychology (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Psychology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Psychology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it GS-4 overlap in EI/attitude; tight syllabus; scoring history experiences steady median performance; useful for candidates with inclination toward human-behavior science. Syllabus map Paper-I (Foundations) : scope-methods (experimental, correlational, field/biological bases — nervous-system/brain localisation + sensors-perception modules incl. signal-detection theory; learning theories (classical/operant/observational — Bandura social-learning; conditioning applications); memory models (multi-store → working-memory Baddeley; forgetting curves-Ebbinghaus); thinking-problem solving & intelligence (psychometrics — Spearman/Thurstone/Cattell-Horn-fluid-crystallised, Gardner multiple-intelligences, Sternberg triarchic, emotional-intelligence stream); motivation & emotion (Maslow, intrinsic-extrinsic debates, cogniti","chars":3208},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/bullets","file":"pub-ad-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/bullet-points/pub-ad-points.html","title":"Public Administration — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public Administration — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Public Administration — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap PUB-AD OPTIONAL ├── PARADIGMS ..... politics-admin dichotomy → NPA → NPM → NPS │ → post-NPM (whole-of-govt, digital-era governance) ├── THINKERS ...... Wilson · Taylor · Fayol · Weber · Simon · Barnard │ · Waldo · Merton · Riggs · Ostrom (+ motivation trio) ├── MACHINERY ..... accountability web · personnel · budget (ZBB/PPBS) │ · admin law · OTs/policy · comparative (prismatic) ├── INDIA ......... Cabinet→NITI · CM secretariat · DM debate · PESA/ │ 74th devolution · 2nd ARC · Prakash Singh · CPGRAMS └── STYLE ......... thinker→claim→Indian echo (P1) · constitutional hook→map→record→ARC-referenced reform (P2) Scoring levers Thinker application cards: each canon thinker ↔ one current scheme/court line (Weber ↔ e-governance; Barnard ↔ acceptance-zone in tra","chars":1804},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/notes","file":"pub-ad-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/detailed-notes/pub-ad-syllabus-map.html","title":"Public Administration — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public Administration — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map (Theory)"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Indian Administration)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Public Administration — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1: administrative theory — thinkers, structures, processes. Paper 2: Indian administration — union to panchayat, welfare machinery and reforms. Heavy GS-2/GS-4 resonance, a thinker-canon to memorise, and a governance-currents coupling that rewards daily newspaper reading with an administrator's eye. Paper 1 — Unit Map (Theory) Block Units Anchors Introduction 1.x–2.x Meaning/scope/significance lineages (politics-administration dichotomy — Wilson, Goodnow); New Public Administration (Minnowbrook I/II/III), New Public Management, New Public Service, post-NPM talk Thinkers 3.x Wilson (dichotomy), Taylor (scientific management), Fayol (14 principles), Weber (bureaucracy/legal-rational), Simon (bounded rationality, proverbs critique), Barnard (cooperative systems, acceptance zone), Waldo (administration as moral science), Merton (goal disp","chars":4260},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/pyqs","file":"pub-ad-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/pyqs/pub-ad-pyqs.html","title":"Public Administration — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public Administration — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Public Administration — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Model approaches included. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. \"Simon buried the proverbs; NPM dug them up.\" Comment. (10 marks) Approach: Simon's critique — principles as contradictory proverbs (span vs flatness) → NPM's reinvention of techniques (targets, decentralisation) → post-NPM re-centralisation (whole-of-government) → verdict: pendulum with learning, not progress-by-refutation alone. Q2. Compare Weber's bureaucracy with Barnard's cooperative system on authority. (15 marks) Approach: Weber — legal-rational hierarchy, authority from office, obedience as duty vs Barnard — acceptance zone, authority from below-in-effect, communication-efficiency conditions → Indian bridge: transfer-policy cynicism vs team-based mission modes → assessment: hybrid realit","chars":3920},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/short","file":"pub-admin-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-public-administration/short-notes/pub-admin-orientation.html","title":"Public Administration (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Public Administration (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why candidates pick it"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map"},{"level":2,"text":"Framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Public Administration (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Why candidates pick it GS-2 governance heartland sync + GS-4 ethics resonance; small-to-medium ecosystem (coaching footprint lighter than sociology/thinner than 2010s peak but sufficient). Syllabus map Paper-I : administrative theory lineage (Classical/Wilson-Taylor-Fayol-Gulick, Human Relations/ Mayo-McGregor, Behavioural/Simon-Barnard, Systems/Open-system; Public-Choice (Buchanan), NPM (self-institution logic), Governance-buzz (network governance), post-NPM (Neo-Weberian state)); theories/meanings on organisation, bureaucracy (Weber-type); accountability & control (executive-legislative-judicial triads + citizen-centricry right-to-information); administrative behaviour (decision making: Herbert Simon's bounded rationality; communication, morale, motivation (Maslow/Herzberg/McGregor), leadership (LIKERT-style systems)); ","chars":3936},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-sociology/bullets","file":"sociology-points.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-sociology/bullet-points/sociology-points.html","title":"Sociology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sociology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Common mistakes"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap check"},{"level":2,"text":"Twin questions before committing"}],"text":"Sociology — Bullet Points Decision + execution card. Orientation in short-notes; full map in detailed-notes. ASCII mindmap SOCIOLOGY OPTIONAL ├── P1 TOOLKIT ..... science debate · methods · thinkers (Marx · Durkheim │ · Weber · Parsons · Merton · Mead) · stratification │ · religion · kinship · change theories ├── P2 INDIA ...... indological/S-F/Marxist lenses · caste-class-tribe │ · village canon (Srinivas/Beteille/Dube) │ · sanskritization → modernization · movements ├── FORMULA ....... thinker → concept → Indian study → current datum → critique └── FIT ........... any background · deepest GS overlap · craft decides margins (big pool) Scoring levers One ethnographic study per Paper-2 answer (Srinivas' Rampura, Beteille's Tanjore, Dube's Shamirpet). Thinker passages quoted in one line, then applied — not biographies. Data sheet: NFHS-5 · PLFS · SECC · Census SC/ST shares — refreshed mont","chars":1686},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-sociology/notes","file":"sociology-syllabus-map.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-sociology/detailed-notes/sociology-syllabus-map.html","title":"Sociology — Syllabus Map &amp; Strategy","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sociology — Syllabus Map & Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — Unit Map (Fundamentals)"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — Unit Map (Indian Society)"},{"level":2,"text":"Booklist (lean and sufficient)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"Scoring Profile and Fit"},{"level":2,"text":"Twelve-Week First Pass"}],"text":"Sociology — Syllabus Map & Strategy Paper 1 gives the discipline's toolkit (thinkers, concepts, methods); Paper 2 runs it on India (caste, village, movements, change). Its GS-1/GS-2/Essay overlap is the deepest of any optional — prep compounds instead of competing. Paper 1 — Unit Map (Fundamentals) Block Units Anchors Sociology & science 1.x–2.x Sociology as science debate, scope, relations with other sciences; research methods (survey, participant obs, comparative); techniques (sampling, questionnaire) Thinkers 3.x Marx (class, alienation, mode of production) · Durkheim (division of labour, suicide, social facts) · Weber (action types, bureaucracy, Protestant ethic, authority) · Parsons (systems, pattern variables) · Merton (manifest/latent, anomie, reference groups) · Mead (self, I–Me) Structures 4.x–7.x Stratification (class, status, power; caste-class debate); work & economic life; r","chars":3460},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-sociology/pyqs","file":"sociology-pyqs.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-sociology/pyqs/sociology-pyqs.html","title":"Sociology — PYQ Patterns &amp; Approaches","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sociology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 1 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper 2 — pattern questions"},{"level":2,"text":"How to practise"}],"text":"Sociology — PYQ Patterns & Approaches Paper-wise patterns framed on recurring themes (official papers carry verbatim texts). Each with a model approach. Paper 1 — pattern questions Q1. \"Science is a social institution.\" Examine with reference to the sociology-of-science debate. (10 marks) Approach: define scientific ethos (Merton's CUDOS norms) → counter-readings (Kuhn's paradigms, social construction) → example: funding & priority disputes → conclude: science is socially embedded, not socially determined. Q2. Compare Durkheim and Weber on the nature of social facts and social action. (15 marks) Approach: Durkheim — external, coercive, sui generis (suicide rates as evidence) vs Weber — meaningful action, verstehen, ideal types → methodological implications → bridge via contemporary mixed methods. Q3. Explain Merton's modes of adaptation with Indian illustrations. (10 marks) Approach: ano","chars":3440},{"nav":"optional-subjects/optional-sociology/short","file":"sociology-orientation.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/optional-sociology/short-notes/sociology-orientation.html","title":"Sociology (Optional) — Orientation &amp; Starter Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sociology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Why it attracts candidates"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllabus map (2 papers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Core framework skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Standard spine books"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlap & answer texture"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ pattern notes"},{"level":2,"text":"First-90-days plan"},{"level":2,"text":"Caution"}],"text":"Sociology (Optional) — Orientation & Starter Framework Starter framework pack. Deep per-unit notes are an expansion track; use this to decide and to structure first-pass study. Why it attracts candidates Compact-ish syllabus, GS-1 society overlap, essay resonance (poverty/social-change/gender prompts), strong material ecosystem (textbooks, test banks, toppers copies). Syllabus map (2 papers) Paper-I (Fundamentals) : discipline origins (Comte, Spencer); methods (positivism, hermeneutics, fieldwork logic); sociological thinkers (Marx, Weber, Durkheim, Mead, Merton); stratification (class, status, power); family-marriage-kinship; religion; social change + development frames. Paper-II (Indian society) : Indological/textual (Ghurye) vs field views (Marriott, Srinivas); caste system theories; tribal communities (integration vs isolation debates); rural social organisation; social classes in In","chars":2950},{"nav":"optional-subjects/pyqs","file":"optional-subjects-faq.html","rel":"content/mains/optional-subjects/pyqs/optional-subjects-faq.html","title":"Optional Subjects — FAQ (PYQ-shaped)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Optional Subjects — FAQ (PYQ-shaped)"}],"text":"Optional Subjects — FAQ (PYQ-shaped) FAQ-style answers to the recurring optional-selection questions; answer-writing textures mirror PYQ expectations. Q1. Does the choice of optional really decide rank variation? Correlational evidence says optionals have the widest spread of written scores among the Mains papers in most years — a mechanically true statement about variance, not destiny. Practical answer: pick by matrix fit (interest + material + texture), not by folklore leagues. Q2. Are technical/science/engineering optionals \"safer\" because marking is objective? Objectivity reduces randomness but raises preparation intensity; also reduces writing-ambiguity upside. For a working engineer mid-career switcher, engineering optionals can be high-value; for pure graduates three years out, the equation is weaker. Q3. Which optionals most reliably finish on time in the exam hall? Compact-sylla","chars":3111},{"nav":"mains-practice/essay-practice/bullets","file":"essay-practice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/essay-practice/bullet-points/essay-practice-points.html","title":"Essay Practice — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Practice — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Weekly quotas"},{"level":2,"text":"Quality checks"},{"level":2,"text":"Mistakes to avoid"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"Essay Practice — Bullet Points The training loop, compressed. ASCII mindmap ESSAY PRACTICE LOOP ├── SELECT ....... score prompts: familiarity × dimensions × examples ├── BLUEPRINT .... thesis + 5-7 signposts + 6 example slots ├── WRITE ........ checkpoints 80/140 min; counter-view mandatory ├── POLISH ....... title · underline keywords · no new arguments └── LEARN ........ self-grade /125 · rewrite worst section · notebook refill Weekly quotas 3 outline sprints · 1 full essay (clocked in execution phase) · 10 example sentences · 1 rewrite from memory. Quality checks Ten-second skim: thesis, dimensions, counter-view, closure — all findable? Example audit: six species represented, none dominating? Intro defines terms before flourish; conclusion resolves, never repeats. Mistakes to avoid Essay-favourite-theme bias · quote hoarding · ignoring longhand stamina · polishing prose before archite","chars":1031},{"nav":"mains-practice/essay-practice/notes","file":"essay-practice-system.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/essay-practice/detailed-notes/essay-practice-system.html","title":"Essay Practice — System","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Practice — System"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Why a System, Not Random Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Twelve-Week Programme"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The Outline Sprint (15 minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Full-Essay Protocol (3 hours)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. The Example Notebook"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Feedback Loops That Actually Work"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Common Practice Mistakes"}],"text":"Essay Practice — System A twelve-week training programme that turns the pyqs/ topic-outlines into a 130+ essay habit. 1. Why a System, Not Random Practice Essays are trained in three separable skills — selection , architecture , and execution — and random weekly essays train none deliberately. The system below isolates each skill, then combines them under exam conditions. 2. The Twelve-Week Programme Weeks Focus Drills (per week) 1–4 Architecture Skeleton before prose 3 outline sprints (15 min) · 1 full essay · anatomy study of one model essay 5–8 Dimensions Breadth of lenses and examples 5-lens sweep on 5 prompts · example-audit of 3 old essays · 1 full essay with a mandatory counter-view 9–11 Execution Clock, legibility, closure 1 full-length weekly under 3-hour clock · 10-second skim test · intro/conclusion rewrites ×2 12 Calibration Band-confirmation 2 full mocks (different families)","chars":2642},{"nav":"mains-practice/essay-practice/pyqs","file":"essay-topics-outlines.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/essay-practice/pyqs/essay-topics-outlines.html","title":"Essay Practice — 24 Topics with Outlines","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Practice — 24 Topics with Outlines"},{"level":2,"text":"How to structure any essay"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Philosophical / Abstract"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Society, Culture & Gender"},{"level":2,"text":"C. Economy & Polity"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Science, Ethics & Governance"},{"level":3,"text":"Evaluation checklist for self-marking (out of 125)"}],"text":"Essay Practice — 24 Topics with Outlines Two essays of 1,000–1,200 words each in three hours; each section offers four topics. Below are 24 topics across the philosophical, socio-economic, polity and science–society families UPSC favours, each with a thesis, a five-part structure, and the anchors (thinkers, examples, data) that lift an essay from 'average' to 'above 130'. How to structure any essay Hook (anecdote, quotation, paradox) → 2. Thesis in one sentence → 3. Dimensions (historical, social, economic, political, ethical, technological, ecological, personal) chosen for the topic → 4. Counter-view and reconciliation → 5. Forward-looking conclusion that returns to the hook. A. Philosophical / Abstract 1. 'Thinking is like a game; it does not begin unless there is an opposite team.' Thesis: dialectic is the engine of knowledge and democracy. Anchors: Socratic method, Buddhist councils,","chars":8267},{"nav":"mains-practice/essay-practice/short","file":"essay-practice-quick.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/essay-practice/short-notes/essay-practice-quick.html","title":"Essay Practice — Quick Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Essay Practice — Quick Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Phases"},{"level":2,"text":"Outline sprint (15 min)"},{"level":2,"text":"Full essay (3 hours)"},{"level":2,"text":"Notebook"},{"level":2,"text":"Feedback"},{"level":2,"text":"Reminders"}],"text":"Essay Practice — Quick Card Twelve-week programme compressed; full detail in detailed-notes. Phases Wk 1-4 architecture (outline sprints ×3 + 1 essay) → Wk 5-8 dimensions (5-lens sweeps + counter-view essay) → Wk 9-11 execution (clocked full essays + skim test) → Wk 12 calibration (2 mocks, band-graded). Outline sprint (15 min) 4 min score all prompts → 5 min blueprint (thesis + 5-7 sections + example slots) → 3 min intro draft + conclusion line → 3 min coherence check on titles. Full essay (3 hours) 15 select/blueprint · 150 write (checkpoints 80/140) · 15 polish, no new arguments · same-day self-grade /125. Notebook Six species × two entries weekly: legal anchor · scheme+mechanism · approximate data · thinker+quote · history · lived case. Feedback Peer 10-second skim (monthly) · mentor one-suggestion deep-read (monthly) · next-morning rewrite of weakest section (weekly — the highest-le","chars":1051},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-1-practice/bullets","file":"gs-1-practice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-1-practice/bullet-points/gs-1-practice-points.html","title":"GS I Practice — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Examiner rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"Common leaks"},{"level":2,"text":"Resource mapping for gaps"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Bullet Points What earns marks in GS-1 — compressed. ASCII mindmap GS-1 ANSWER MACHINE ├── DECODE ....... directive verb + scope + marks/word budget ├── INTRO ........ definition / period-set (dates, dynasty, Article, datum) ├── BODY ......... labelled dimensions; chrono (history) · thematic (society) │ · spatial (geography); evidence per dimension ├── VISUAL ....... map/timeline/flow — 30-sec clean sketch └── CLOSE ........ significance / legacy in 2 lines Examiner rewards Specific memory: names, years, texts, regions, schemes' data. Dimension labels that mirror the question's parts. A visual where the content is inherently spatial/temporal. Word-limit discipline — density over sprawl. Common leaks Generic intro that could open any answer. Examples listed without linking to the dimension. Diagrams drawn late and abandoned mid-way. No conclusion — the answer stops w","chars":1201},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-1-practice/notes","file":"gs-1-practice-system.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-1-practice/detailed-notes/gs-1-practice-system.html","title":"GS I — Practice System","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper I — Practice System"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What GS-1 Demands"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Time Protocol"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Answer Anatomy for GS-1"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Self-evaluation Rubric (score /10 per answer)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. The Weekly Cycle"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Error Log"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Pointers"}],"text":"GS Paper I — Practice System How to convert the GS-1 question bank (see pyqs/) into marks: syllabus mapping, time protocol, rubric, weekly cycle. 1. What GS-1 Demands GS-1 rewards substantive memory organised analytically : culture (art, architecture, personalities), modern history (1857–1947), post-independence consolidation, world history (revolutions, redrawing boundaries), society (urbanisation, globalisation, communalism, women), geography (physical, Indian, world) and geophysical phenomena (earthquakes, cyclones, tsunamis). The examiner's mix is roughly: recall-heavy (culture/history) + analytical (society) + applied-conceptual (geography). 2. The Time Protocol 10 markers (150 words): 7 minutes — 30 sec decode + 30 sec micro-outline + 5:30 write + 30 sec underline. 15 markers (250 words): 11 minutes — with a diagram/map slot wherever a spatial or temporal spread exists. Full mock (","chars":2743},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-1-practice/pyqs","file":"gs-1-question-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-1-practice/pyqs/gs-1-question-bank.html","title":"GS Paper I — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Indian Heritage & Culture"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Modern Indian History"},{"level":2,"text":"C. World History"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Indian Society"},{"level":2,"text":"E. Geography"},{"level":3,"text":"How to use this bank"}],"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks 30 original questions in the UPSC pattern (10-markers ≈ 150 words, 15-markers ≈ 250 words). Each carries a compact answer framework — the introduction hook, the body skeleton with the examples an examiner expects, and a forward-looking conclusion. Write full answers against a clock (7 minutes for 10 marks, 11 minutes for 15) and compare structure, not wording. A. Indian Heritage & Culture Q1. (10 marks) Discuss how the Chola temple architecture reflects the political and economic power of the Chola state. Intro: Brihadeeswara (1010 CE) as 'imperial temple' — Rajaraja I's inscription listing endowments. Body: vimana height as royal statement; temple as landlord, banker and employer (devadana grants, 400 dancers, 600 staff); bronze casting (Nataraja) linked to guild patronage; temple-town urbanisation (Thanjavur, Gangaikondacholapu","chars":15701},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-1-practice/short","file":"gs-1-practice-quick.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-1-practice/short-notes/gs-1-practice-quick.html","title":"GS I Practice — Quick Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Quick Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Timing"},{"level":2,"text":"Anatomy (60 seconds)"},{"level":2,"text":"Rubric /10"},{"level":2,"text":"Weekly rhythm"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper-specific reminders"},{"level":2,"text":"Score tracking"}],"text":"GS Paper I — Practice Quick Card One-page drill card. Full system in detailed-notes; question bank in pyqs/. Timing 10-marker: 7 min · 15-marker: 11 min · full mock: 20 Q / 180 min — abandon at the bell. Anatomy (60 seconds) Intro: define/period-set (dates!) → Body: 2-4 labelled dimensions + examples → Visual in geography & timeline questions → Close with significance. Rubric /10 Decode 2 · Substance 3 · Structure 3 · Closure 2. Score every practice answer; log the gap. Weekly rhythm M culture · T modern history · W society+data · Th geography+maps · F world/post-47 · Sa half-mock · Su error-log + rewrites. Paper-specific reminders Directive verbs: \"discuss\" = both sides + view; \"critically analyse\" = verdict required. Never exceed the word limit — GS-1 rewards density, not length. Maps: one clean India/world outline per answer; label 5-7 items max. Score tracking Track per-section avera","chars":995},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-2-practice/bullets","file":"gs-2-practice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-2-practice/bullet-points/gs-2-practice-points.html","title":"GS II Practice — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"Leaks"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice modes"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Bullet Points Compressed examiner-psychology for GS-2. ASCII mindmap GS-2 JUDGMENT MACHINE ├── DECODE ....... critically examine? assess? suggest? (verdict / balance / options) ├── ANCHOR ....... Article · case · Act · committee · index — named, dated ├── TWO RECORDS .. design vs delivery · intent vs implementation ├── CAUSES ....... structural > personal; incentives, capacity, federal frictions └── PROPOSE ...... sourced reform: committee line + feasibility note Rewards A stance in the first five lines for examine/assess questions. Citations that are checkable — wrong ones cost more than none. Design-vs-delivery separation — the mature analyst's reflex. Reforms that name mechanism + source (\"as 2nd ARC recommended…\"). Leaks Newspaper-recap IR answers without structure. Moralising in place of analysis; slogans for reforms. Ignoring the question's second clause (\"…a","chars":1245},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-2-practice/notes","file":"gs-2-practice-system.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-2-practice/detailed-notes/gs-2-practice-system.html","title":"GS II — Practice System","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper II — Practice System"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What GS-2 Demands"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Authority Bank (build before practising)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Answer Anatomy for GS-2"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Judgment Phrases That Signal Maturity"},{"level":2,"text":"5. The Weekly Cycle"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Rubric /10"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Pointers"}],"text":"GS Paper II — Practice System Governance, constitution, polity, social justice and international relations — converting the pyqs/ question bank into marks. 1. What GS-2 Demands GS-2 is the judgment paper : it asks you to take positions on constitutional design (\"critically examine\"), institutional performance (\"assess\"), and diplomacy (\"discuss the significance\"). It rewards authority-citation (Articles, cases, committees, Acts, indices) more than any other paper — every answer should carry at least one named anchor. 2. The Authority Bank (build before practising) Constitutional : Articles (14, 21, 32, 226, 243, 356, 368…), landmark cases (Kesavananda, Maneka, Puttaswamy, SR Bommai, Vishaka). Institutional : CAG, ECI, UPSC, Finance Commission, NHRC, NCW — one line each on mandate + one recent controversy. Committees/Commissions : Punchhi, Sarkaria, ARC (2nd), Rajamannar — for federalism ","chars":2746},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-2-practice/pyqs","file":"gs-2-question-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-2-practice/pyqs/gs-2-question-bank.html","title":"GS Paper II — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Constitution & Polity"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Governance & Social Justice"},{"level":2,"text":"C. International Relations"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Governance Institutions & Miscellaneous"},{"level":3,"text":"Using this bank"}],"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks 30 original questions (polity, governance, social justice, international relations) with compact answer frameworks. 10-markers ≈ 150 words; 15-markers ≈ 250 words. Frameworks list the constitutional provisions, judgments, committee reports and data an examiner expects — write the full answer yourself. A. Constitution & Polity Q1. (10 marks) 'The basic structure doctrine is a judicial innovation that has strengthened, not weakened, parliamentary democracy.' Discuss. Body: Kesavananda (1973) as a response to Golaknath/24th Amendment; elements — supremacy of Constitution, judicial review, federalism, secularism, free and fair elections (Indira Gandhi case), rule of law; applications — Minerva Mills, NJAC (2015); critique — undefined, counter-majoritarian; defence — prevents 'constitutional dictatorship', used sparingly; 2023 debate","chars":15857},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-2-practice/short","file":"gs-2-practice-quick.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-2-practice/short-notes/gs-2-practice-quick.html","title":"GS II Practice — Quick Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Quick Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Timing"},{"level":2,"text":"Anatomy"},{"level":2,"text":"Authority deposit — withdraw daily"},{"level":2,"text":"Rubric /10"},{"level":2,"text":"Judgment signals"},{"level":2,"text":"Weekly rhythm"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper-specific reminders"}],"text":"GS Paper II — Practice Quick Card One-page drill card for the judgment paper. Timing Same clock as GS-1: 7/11 minutes; but GS-2 outlines are 20 seconds longer — always draft the conclusion line first. Anatomy Intro: Article/case/Act context → Body: mandate → performance (both) → issues (structural) → comparison → Conclusion: sourced reform proposal. Authority deposit — withdraw daily Articles: 14 · 21 · 32 · 226 · 243 · 356 · 368 · Cases: Kesavananda · Maneka · Puttaswamy · Bommai · Vishaka. Committees: Punchhi · Sarkaria · 2nd ARC · Indices: NFHS-5 · ASER · WGI · IR: BIMSTEC · QUAD · SCO mandates. Rubric /10 Position 2 · Authority 3 · Balance 3 · Reform 2. Judgment signals \"Design is sound; delivery leaks.\" · \"Objection one survives, objection three does not.\" · \"The committee treats the symptom; the leak is…\" Weekly rhythm M polity · T governance/SJ · W IR · Th democracies+elections · ","chars":1139},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-3-practice/bullets","file":"gs-3-practice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-3-practice/bullet-points/gs-3-practice-points.html","title":"GS III Practice — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"Leaks"},{"level":2,"text":"Practice drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Bullet Points Compressed examiner-psychology for GS-3. ASCII mindmap GS-3 SOLUTION MACHINE ├── FRAME ........ magnitude datum (\"about X%\") ├── ANALYSE ...... structural causes; distribution of impacts ├── PRESCRIBE .... agent + incentive + constraint + sequence ├── DRAW ......... flow/graph/ring — labelled, value-adding └── LINK ......... national mission / SDG, without slogans Rewards Mechanism answers (\"PMO-to-panchayat: who does what, funded how\") over principle lists. Honest approximation of data; wrong precision is penalised. Diagrams that compress argument, not decoration. Distributional eye — who gains, who pays, who is exposed. Leaks Scheme-name showers without mechanism. Security answers with tactical gossip instead of strategy. Sci-tech answers that never explain the underlying science. Environment answers without the finance/justice dimension. Practice ","chars":1228},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-3-practice/notes","file":"gs-3-practice-system.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-3-practice/detailed-notes/gs-3-practice-system.html","title":"GS III — Practice System","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper III — Practice System"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What GS-3 Demands"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The Data Magazine (load before practice)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Answer Anatomy for GS-3"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Solution-quality Test (three questions)"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Security Answers — Sub-structure"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Weekly Cycle"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Rubric /10"}],"text":"GS Paper III — Practice System Economy, agriculture, science-tech, environment, security & disaster management — turning the pyqs/ bank into marks. 1. What GS-3 Demands GS-3 rewards solution craft : every answer is a mini policy note — diagnose (data), analyse (structure), prescribe (mechanism). It is also the diagram paper : cyclical flows (inflation, disaster cycle), simple graphs (Laffer-style trade-offs, yields), and schematic layouts (security architecture) convert thin knowledge into visible competence. 2. The Data Magazine (load before practice) Economy : GDP growth band, CPI/WPI ball-parks, FRBM targets, GST collections trend, current-account deficit range, forex reserves order of magnitude, unemployment series (PLFS), capital-expenditure ratios. Agriculture : share in employment/GDP, MSP coverage facts, PM-KISAN quantum, irrigation coverage %, foodgrain output band, fertilizer s","chars":2974},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-3-practice/pyqs","file":"gs-3-question-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-3-practice/pyqs/gs-3-question-bank.html","title":"GS Paper III — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks"},{"level":2,"text":"A. Economy"},{"level":2,"text":"B. Agriculture & Food"},{"level":2,"text":"C. Science & Technology"},{"level":2,"text":"D. Environment & Disaster Management"},{"level":2,"text":"E. Internal Security"},{"level":3,"text":"Using this bank"}],"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Question Bank with Answer Frameworks 30 original questions (economy, agriculture, science & technology, environment, disaster management, internal security) with compact answer frameworks. Data points reflect the position as of early 2026 — update numbers before the exam. A. Economy Q1. (15 marks) 'India's growth is investment-led but consumption-constrained.' Examine with reference to recent trends and Budget 2025–26. Body: GFCF ~30% of GDP, public capex ₹11.2 lakh crore; private capex revival uneven; consumption — rural recovery post-2024 monsoon, urban slowdown, K-shape; Budget levers — income-tax relief (₹12 lakh exemption), GST 2.0 (Sept 2025); wage stagnation, household debt (RBI FSR); way forward — labour-intensive manufacturing, urban jobs, credit to MSMEs. Q2. (10 marks) Discuss the rationale and risks of the RBI's shift to a neutral stance and rate cuts ","chars":15091},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-3-practice/short","file":"gs-3-practice-quick.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-3-practice/short-notes/gs-3-practice-quick.html","title":"GS III Practice — Quick Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Quick Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Timing"},{"level":2,"text":"Anatomy"},{"level":2,"text":"Data magazine — refill weekly"},{"level":2,"text":"Solution test — 3 questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Diagram shelf — pre-rehearsed"},{"level":2,"text":"Rubric /10"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper-specific reminders"}],"text":"GS Paper III — Practice Quick Card One-page drill card for the solutions paper. Timing 7/11-minute clock; diagrams get a fixed 60-90 second allowance — pre-rehearsed shapes only. Anatomy Intro: definition + framing datum → causes → impacts (who bears them) → constraints → mechanism solutions (agent, incentive, sequence) → mission link. Data magazine — refill weekly GDP band · CPI band · FRBM 3%/6% (centre/states) · GST trend · PLFS · agri share (~18% GVA, ~45% jobs) · NDC 45% & 500 GW · renewable capacity order · defence exports · LWE district trend. Solution test — 3 questions Who implements? What incentive changes? What constraint is acknowledged? Any \"none\" = slogan; rewrite. Diagram shelf — pre-rehearsed Inflation pass-through flow · disaster cycle ring · energy-transition stacked bar (sketch) · cyber-threat onion · agro-cycle vs MSP line · semiconductor value chain strip. Rubric /10","chars":1200},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-4-practice/bullets","file":"gs-4-practice-points.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-4-practice/bullet-points/gs-4-practice-points.html","title":"GS IV Practice — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"ASCII mindmap"},{"level":2,"text":"Rewards"},{"level":2,"text":"Leaks"},{"level":2,"text":"Drills"},{"level":2,"text":"Golden phrase bank"}],"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Bullet Points Compressed examiner-psychology for GS-4. ASCII mindmap GS-4 PRACTICE MACHINE ├── THEORY ....... define → dimensions → nuance → officer line (7-8 min) ├── CASE ......... annotate 3 min → 7-step memo → safeguards 2 min ├── ARMOURY ...... rule-situation pairs · officer one-liners · concept hooks ├── VOICE ........ first person, lawful floor, humane tone, recorded steps └── LOOP ......... 2 theory + 1 case weekly · sectional Fri · rewrite Sun Rewards Visible structure — headings examiners can tick. Decisions with sequence and documentation, not sermons. The safeguards band — where most answers end early and lose marks. Honest engagement with \"wrong but understandable\" options. Leaks Ethical vocabulary dumped without application. Cases solved with lectures to society instead of personal action. Theory answers without a single administrative example. Overlo","chars":1267},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-4-practice/notes","file":"gs-4-practice-system.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-4-practice/detailed-notes/gs-4-practice-system.html","title":"GS IV — Practice System","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice System"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The Two Genres, One Clock"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Theory Answers — the Compact Essay"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Case Answers — the Decision Memo"},{"level":2,"text":"4. The Example Armoury"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Weekly Cycle"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Rubrics"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Pointers"}],"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice System Theory + case studies — the practice protocol that turns the pyqs/ bank (theory Qs and cases with model approaches) into 110+. 1. The Two Genres, One Clock GS-4's Part A (theory, ~6 questions) and Part B (case studies, usually 4 of 20 marks) share three hours. Budget: theory 7-8 minutes each ; case studies 16-18 minutes each . Theory answers are short essays; case answers are decision memos. Practising them identically is the most common preparation error. 2. Theory Answers — the Compact Essay Define the core concept in line one (quote a thinker only if exact). Two-to-three analytical dimensions with one micro-example each (administrative, never trivia). A counter-nuance or boundary condition (\"but empathy without regulation becomes capture…\"). Close with officer-relevance in one line. 150-200 words; never pad. 3. Case Answers — the Decision Memo Use the 7-s","chars":2436},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-4-practice/pyqs","file":"gs-4-question-bank.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-4-practice/pyqs/gs-4-question-bank.html","title":"GS Paper IV — Practice Question Bank: Theory Questions &amp; Case Studies","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Question Bank: Theory Questions & Case Studies"},{"level":2,"text":"Section A — Theory"},{"level":2,"text":"Section B — Case Studies"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 1 — The Tender and the MLA (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 2 — The Data Breach You Discovered (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 3 — Flood Relief and the Caste Line (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 4 — The Pharmaceutical Inspector (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 5 — The Coaching Class Teacher (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Case Study 6 — The Friend at the Border Post (20 marks)"},{"level":3,"text":"Answering GS-IV under time"}],"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Question Bank: Theory Questions & Case Studies Section A: 20 theory questions (10 marks each ≈ 150 words) with answer approaches. Section B: 6 case studies (20 marks each ≈ 250 words) with a stakeholder–options–decision framework and a model approach. The scoring pattern rewards: (1) identifying the exact ethical issue, (2) applying a named framework or thinker, (3) an implementable, lawful decision, (4) a concrete example. Section A — Theory Q1. Distinguish between ethics, morals and law with examples from public administration. Why does a legal act sometimes remain unethical? Approach: definitions — ethics (reasoned standards), morals (personal/cultural beliefs), law (state-enforced minimum); example — a legally valid eviction of slum-dwellers in winter without rehabilitation; Bentham's 'law is a fence, ethics the garden'; lesson — discretion must be filled by co","chars":19374},{"nav":"mains-practice/gs-4-practice/short","file":"gs-4-practice-quick.html","rel":"content/mains/practice/gs-4-practice/short-notes/gs-4-practice-quick.html","title":"GS IV Practice — Quick Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Quick Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Clock"},{"level":2,"text":"Theory shape (150-200 words)"},{"level":2,"text":"Case shape (7 headings)"},{"level":2,"text":"Example armoury — refill"},{"level":2,"text":"Rubrics"},{"level":2,"text":"Reminders"}],"text":"GS Paper IV — Practice Quick Card One-page drill card for theory + case genres. Clock Theory: 7-8 min each · Case: 16-18 min (3 min annotating, 2 min reserved for safeguards). Theory shape (150-200 words) Definition → 2-3 dimensions with micro-examples → boundary nuance → officer-relevance line. Case shape (7 headings) Facts (+verify) → Dilemma → Stakeholders → Options ≥3 → Evaluation (legality·public interest·proportionality·precedent·conscience) → Decision+steps → Safeguards. Example armoury — refill Rules↔situations (conduct rules, RTI, manuals) · officer one-liners (Seshan, Sreedharan) · concept hooks (talisman, difference principle, Kautilyan audit). Rubrics Theory /10: definition 2 · depth 4 · examples 2 · close 2. Case /20: structure 4 · honesty 4 · tests 4 · decision-detail 5 · safeguards 3. Reminders First-person decisions. Law first, compassion on top, everything recorded. No h","chars":968},{"nav":"prelims-csat/comprehension/bullets","file":"comprehension-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/comprehension/bullet-points/comprehension-points.html","title":"Comprehension — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Comprehension — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Method flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Type flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Speed flash"}],"text":"Comprehension — Bullet Points Method flash. Method flash Questions first → passage second → elimination third. Topic sentences + contrast pivots (but-however-yet) = claim carriers. Qualifiers (only-most-some-may) decide right-extent. Type flash Main idea = covers all paragraphs · inference = necessary support · fact ≠ opinion · vocab = replace-and-reread · tone = adjective audit. Trap flash Reversal · sweeping generalisation · new-fact injection · wrong-extent · half-true pair options. Extreme words (always-never-only) → usually wrong. CSAT tests the passage — outside GK is a trap, not a tool. Speed flash Budget ~90 sec/question; skip-and-return on 1-and-2-only types. Least-wrong beats blank (no negative-marking gamble logic — CSAT qualifying 33%).","chars":758},{"nav":"prelims-csat/comprehension/notes","file":"comprehension-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/comprehension/detailed-notes/comprehension-detailed.html","title":"Reading Comprehension — Approach Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Reading Comprehension — Approach Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Question types"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Method (3-pass)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Trap taxonomy"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Worked micro-example"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Reading Comprehension — Approach Notes CSAT Paper-II asks RC sets (~25-30 marks band). Method beats reading speed: question-first, elimination-second. 1. Question types Main idea / central argument — topic sentence + conclusion synthesis; the answer that covers ALL paragraphs, not one. Inference — must follow from text (necessary, not merely possible); test: would the author agree? Is there textual support? Fact vs opinion — verifiable statements vs judgement words (regrettably, unfortunately). Vocabulary-in-context — replace and re-read; context beats dictionary meaning. Tone-author attitude — critical-cautious-neutral-advocacy clusters; adjectives give it away. 2. Method (3-pass) Pass 1 — read the questions (not options fully): know what to hunt. Pass 2 — read passage at 80% speed: mark topic sentences, contrast pivots (but, however, yet), qualifiers (only, most, some, may). Pass 3 — e","chars":2244},{"nav":"prelims-csat/comprehension/pyqs","file":"comprehension-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/comprehension/pyqs/comprehension-pyqs.html","title":"Comprehension — PYQ-Style Practice","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Comprehension — PYQ-Style Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 1"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 2"},{"level":2,"text":"Answers"}],"text":"Comprehension — PYQ-Style Practice Short-passage drills in CSAT pattern. Pick the best answer; key at the end. Set 1 Q1. 'Urban flooding is less about rainfall and more about concrete.' The passage implies urban flooding is chiefly caused by: (a) excess rainfall (b) paved surfaces reducing absorption (c) poor weather forecasting (d) river mismanagement Q2. 'While electric vehicles cut tailpipe emissions, battery sourcing raises new concerns.' Author's tone is: (a) dismissive of EVs (b) enthusiastic advocacy (c) balanced-cautious (d) neutral reporting with no view Q3. 'Most farmers in the survey adopted the technique after neighbours' visible success.' Inference: (a) government subsidies drove adoption (b) peer demonstration influenced adoption (c) all farmers adopted (d) technique failed Q4. 'The committee neither rejected nor endorsed the proposal; it sought more data.' Committee's stan","chars":2760},{"nav":"prelims-csat/comprehension/short","file":"comprehension-drills.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/comprehension/short-notes/comprehension-drills.html","title":"CSAT Reading Comprehension — Drills &amp; Method","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Reading Comprehension — Drills & Method"},{"level":2,"text":"Method rules (before drills)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill set 1: the three-paragraph read (~4 min)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill set 2: tone & inference (~3 min)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill set 3: tricky negative stems"},{"level":2,"text":"Patterns to pre-empt"},{"level":2,"text":"Error diary fields (use with mocks)"}],"text":"CSAT Reading Comprehension — Drills & Method Method rules (before drills) Stem-first strategy : read the question before a long passage when options are inference-heavy. Passage-only evidence : never use your own knowledge; the correct option is reconstructable strictly from the text. Tone pivots : but/however/yet lines usually determine attitude questions; bold-sketch them on paper. Balanced-passage box : if a passage does problem→counterpoint, the \"title/best summary\" option must include both sides. Elimination markers : strike superlatives (always/never/only) unless the passage explicitly contains them. Drill set 1: the three-paragraph read (~4 min) Passage \"Public libraries in Indian cities increasingly function as study halls rather than as repositories. Middle-class aspirants occupy tables for competitive exams, while browsing for leisure reading declines. The transformation speaks","chars":4098},{"nav":"prelims-csat/decision-making/bullets","file":"decision-making-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/decision-making/bullet-points/decision-making-points.html","title":"Decision-Making — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Decision-Making — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Frame flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Scenario flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Micro flash"}],"text":"Decision-Making — Bullet Points Framework flash. Frame flash Stakeholders → dilemma → option scan → sequence check. Rank → legality → effectiveness → fairness → feasibility. Winning trio → immediate relief + root-cause + communication (+ record). Trap flash Illegally-effective → always wrong · feel-good-passive → wrong · secrecy → wrong. Punitive-first → prefer corrective · utopian-overnight → prefer incremental. 'Ignore' options are never right. Scenario flash Crisis → act first, inform meanwhile · grievance → meet + time-bound plan. Senior pressure → written clarification + record · RTI → comply, accountability follows. Talent vs discipline → counsel-monitor-act ladder. Micro flash Best option = smallest lawful action that solves the problem and is documented.","chars":772},{"nav":"prelims-csat/decision-making/notes","file":"decision-making-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/decision-making/detailed-notes/decision-making-detailed.html","title":"Decision-Making & Problem-Solving — Approach Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Decision-Making and Problem-Solving — Approach Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. What is tested"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Four-step framework"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Ranking heuristics & traps"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Worked micro-example"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Decision-Making and Problem-Solving — Approach Notes Administrative scenario questions: pick the most lawful-effective-proportionate response. Values in action, not theory. 1. What is tested Scenario types: public-grievance handling, crisis-response (flood-riot-accident), interpersonal (subordinate-colleague conflict), integrity-pressure (bribe senior-order), resource-allocation. The 'correct' option is rarely perfect — it is the best-balanced: lawful + effective + fair + feasible now. 2. Four-step framework Stakeholders — who is affected (victim-public-team-department): options ignoring key stakeholders are wrong. Dilemma definition — name the tension (speed vs procedure, empathy vs rule). Option scan — usually 4: ignore/avoid (passive), escalate-first (abdication), rule-breaking-effective (illegal), act-within-law-communicate (correct pattern). Sequence check — try local resolution and","chars":2061},{"nav":"prelims-csat/decision-making/pyqs","file":"decision-making-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/decision-making/pyqs/decision-making-pyqs.html","title":"Decision-Making — PYQ-Style Practice","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Decision-Making — PYQ-Style Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 1"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 2"},{"level":2,"text":"Answers"}],"text":"Decision-Making — PYQ-Style Practice Scenario drills in CSAT pattern. Choose the most appropriate administrative response; key at the end. Set 1 Q1. A flood warning arrives at night; the hostel warden is absent. You should: (a) wait for the warden (b) begin evacuation and inform authorities meanwhile (c) sleep and report in the morning (d) only send a message Q2. A vendor offers a festival gift to clear a pending bill faster. You: (a) accept, it is customary (b) refuse and process the bill on merit per rules (c) refuse and blacklist him (d) ask for a bigger gift Q3. Two teammates clash publicly daily. As leader you: (a) ignore, it will settle (b) hear both separately, then mediate with clear norms (c) transfer both (d) take sides Q4. Your senior orders an improper verbal approval on a tender. You: (a) comply silently (b) seek written clarification citing rules and record your objection (","chars":2610},{"nav":"prelims-csat/decision-making/short","file":"decision-making-drills.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/decision-making/short-notes/decision-making-drills.html","title":"CSAT Decision-Making — Drills &amp; Method","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Decision-Making — Drills & Method"},{"level":2,"text":"Decision rubric (the scoring philosophy)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drills (pick best)"},{"level":2,"text":"Interpersonal-skills micro-principles (RC-adjacent)"}],"text":"CSAT Decision-Making — Drills & Method No negative-marking shading advantage: decision items are normally not penalised in CSAT matching UPSC convention (verify current year's notification statement — historically no negative marking on decision-making items) . Method: institutional courtesy > assertiveness; rule-keeping > improvisation; escalation hierarchy before individual heroics. Decision rubric (the scoring philosophy) Legality first — an option violating law/code/rules is instantly wrong regardless of empathy flavour. Hierarchy discipline — report upward; document; don't bypass the chain absent emergency. Impartiality — family/friend/community options get down-ranked. Process-over-outcome — correct procedures with documented rationale beat \"intuitive\" shortcuts; UPSC rewards the defensible file note. Least harm + maximum benefit — when forced to balance, pick options combining mit","chars":4640},{"nav":"prelims-csat/logical-reasoning/bullets","file":"logical-reasoning-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/logical-reasoning/bullet-points/logical-reasoning-points.html","title":"Logical Reasoning — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Logical Reasoning — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Syllogism flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Relation-direction flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Puzzle flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Statement-logic flash"}],"text":"Logical Reasoning — Bullet Points Toolkit flash. Syllogism flash Venn per statement · conclusion true in ALL diagrams · some-not doesn't convert. All→Some reverse · No→No reverse · two negatives = no conclusion. Relation-direction flash Tree symbols, solve backwards from reference person · 'only child' watch. Compass N-up · facing recomputed each turn · displacement = Pythagoras. Puzzle flash Fix definite clue as anchor · grid + tick-cross matrix · negative clues eliminate. Circular left/right flips when facing outward — confirm before answering. Statement-logic flash Assumption = must-be-true-unstated · strengthen = bridge · weaken = break link. Course-of-action → address cause, avoid extremes. Clock angle |30H − 5.5M| · mirror clock 11:60 − t · calendar odd-days.","chars":775},{"nav":"prelims-csat/logical-reasoning/notes","file":"logical-reasoning-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/logical-reasoning/detailed-notes/logical-reasoning-detailed.html","title":"Logical Reasoning & General Mental Ability — Approach Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Logical Reasoning and General Mental Ability — Approach Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Syllogisms (Venn method)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Blood relations & direction sense"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Puzzles & arrangements"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Statement-assumption-conclusion-argument"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Logical Reasoning and General Mental Ability — Approach Notes CSAT reasoning block: syllogisms, relations, arrangements, statement-logic. Method-solve, don't intuition-solve. 1. Syllogisms (Venn method) Draw minimal-overlap Venns per statement; test each conclusion against ALL valid diagrams — follows only if true in every case; 'possibility' cases = some-can-be. Conversion pairs: All A are B → Some B are A; No A is B → No B is A; Some A are B → Some B are A (Some-not does not convert). Two negative premises → no conclusion; two particulars → no universal conclusion. 2. Blood relations & direction sense Family-tree: build symbolically (♂-♀, =, vertical line), decode from the reference person backwards; watch 'only son/daughter' and in-law chains. Direction: sketch compass with N-up; final displacement via Pythagoras; left-right turns are relative to facing — recompute the facing at every","chars":2137},{"nav":"prelims-csat/logical-reasoning/pyqs","file":"logical-reasoning-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/logical-reasoning/pyqs/logical-reasoning-pyqs.html","title":"Logical Reasoning — PYQ-Style Practice","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Logical Reasoning — PYQ-Style Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 1 — syllogism & relations"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 2 — puzzles & statement logic"},{"level":2,"text":"Answers"}],"text":"Logical Reasoning — PYQ-Style Practice CSAT-pattern drills; key with method at the end. Set 1 — syllogism & relations Q1. Statements: All pens are books. Some books are copies. Conclusions: I. Some copies are pens. II. Some pens are books. Which follows? (a) only I (b) only II (c) both (d) neither Q2. Statements: No cat is a dog. All dogs are animals. Conclusion: some animals are not cats: (a) follows (b) doesn't follow (c) can't say (d) data insufficient Q3. Pointing to a photo, Ravi said, 'She is the daughter of my grandfather's only son.' The girl is Ravi's: (a) sister (b) cousin (c) aunt (d) niece Q4. A man walks 3 km north, turns right walks 4 km. Displacement from start: (a) 7 km (b) 1 km (c) 5 km (d) 6 km Q5. If MOTHER is coded as ODVJHT, FATHER is coded as: (a) HCVJGT (b) HCVJHT (c) GCVJGT (d) HCUJGT Set 2 — puzzles & statement logic Q6. Five friends sit in a row facing north: P ","chars":2126},{"nav":"prelims-csat/logical-reasoning/short","file":"reasoning-drills.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/logical-reasoning/short-notes/reasoning-drills.html","title":"CSAT Logical Reasoning — Drills &amp; Method","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Logical Reasoning — Drills & Method"},{"level":2,"text":"Method anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Rapid-fire with answers"},{"level":2,"text":"Puzzler grid drill (4 min)"},{"level":2,"text":"Data-sufficiency shape"},{"level":2,"text":"Fail-modes to kill"}],"text":"CSAT Logical Reasoning — Drills & Method Method anchors Draw for directions, blood relations, seating — never hold diagrams in head. For series: check difference first , then ratio, then alternating patterns, then alphabetic shifts (A=1 mapping memorised). Puzzles: build the grid skeleton first ; place \"not\"-clues last. Syllogisms: draw the minimal Venn satisfying given premises; ask \"is the conclusion forced in ALL such Venns?\". Rapid-fire with answers Series : 4, 11, 25, 53, ? — differences ×2+3 pattern → 109 (check: 53×2+3). Alpha series : AZ, CY, EX, FW, ? → GV (first letters +1; second −1). Number-logic : In a class, Ram is 12th from left, 18th from right. Total students? 29 (12+18−1). Blood : Pointing to a photograph: \"She is my mother's brother's only sister's daughter.\" That's cousin (maternal aunt's daughter) . Trap: if \"only sister\" is mother herself → sister! Read \"only sister","chars":2814},{"nav":"prelims-csat/numeracy/bullets","file":"numeracy-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/numeracy/bullet-points/numeracy-points.html","title":"Numeracy & DI — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Numeracy & DI — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Percent flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Arithmetic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"DI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Probability flash"}],"text":"Numeracy & DI — Bullet Points Shortcut flash. Percent flash x% of y = y% of x · 1/8 = 12.5 · 1/6 ≈ 16.67 · 1/7 ≈ 14.28. Successive → a + b + ab/100 · change base = old value. Arithmetic flash SP/CP ratio (100+P)/100 · discounts successive via ab/100. CI − SI (2 yr) = P(r/100)² · rule of 72 for doubling. Avg speed (equal distance) = 2ab/(a+b) · trains opposite: speeds add. Work: LCM units · efficiency = 1/time · M₁D₁H₁/W₁ constant. Alligation: cheaper:dearer = (d−m):(m−c). DI flash Title-units-footnote first · percentage-point ≠ % change · per-capita vs total. Pie share ×3.6 = degrees · rank-compare beats exact computation. Probability flash P = fav/total · at least one = 1 − none · independent ×, exclusive +. Letters: n!/repeats · circular (n−1)! · nCr selection.","chars":773},{"nav":"prelims-csat/numeracy/notes","file":"numeracy-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/numeracy/detailed-notes/numeracy-detailed.html","title":"Basic Numeracy & Data Interpretation — Approach Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Basic Numeracy and Data Interpretation — Approach Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Percent-ratio core"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Arithmetic set"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Data interpretation discipline"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Probability & counting"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Basic Numeracy and Data Interpretation — Approach Notes Class-X level maths at speed: percent-ratio core, arithmetic set, DI reading discipline. 1. Percent-ratio core Commutative percent: x% of y = y% of x (instant 18% of 50 = 9); fraction-percent anchors: 1/8 = 12.5%, 1/6 ≈ 16.67%, 1/7 ≈ 14.28%, 1/12 ≈ 8.33%. Successive change: a + b + ab/100 (two 10% rises = 21%); percentage-change = (new − old)/old × 100 with old as base — base-swap is the classic trap. Income-expenditure ratios: assume LCM values; part-whole from ratios (3:5 → 3/8, 5/8). 2. Arithmetic set Profit-loss: markup on CP → discount on MP; SP/CP ratio method ((100+P)/100); successive discounts add via a+b+ab/100 (not simple sum). SI linear; CI compounding; 2-year CI − SI = P(r/100)² ; halves-doubling (rule of 72 ≈ 72/r years). TSD: distance constant → speed × time trade (speed ×1.25 → time ÷1.25); average speed for equal dis","chars":2250},{"nav":"prelims-csat/numeracy/pyqs","file":"numeracy-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/numeracy/pyqs/numeracy-pyqs.html","title":"Numeracy & DI — PYQ-Style Practice","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Numeracy and DI — PYQ-Style Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 1 — arithmetic"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 2 — DI & probability"},{"level":2,"text":"Answers"}],"text":"Numeracy and DI — PYQ-Style Practice CSAT-pattern arithmetic + data drills; key with shortcut at the end. Set 1 — arithmetic Q1. A price rises 20% then falls 20%. Net change: (a) 0% (b) 4% fall (c) 4% rise (d) 2% fall Q2. CP ₹250, marked ₹400, discount 20%. Profit %: (a) 28% (b) 25% (c) 20% (d) 32% Q3. A car travels to a city at 60 km/h and returns at 40 km/h. Average speed: (a) 50 (b) 48 (c) 45 (d) 52 Q4. SI on ₹8,000 at 5% for 3 years vs CI — CI − SI (2 years) on same principal: (a) ₹20 (b) ₹40 (c) ₹60 (d) ₹8 Q5. 12 men finish a job in 20 days; 8 men will finish it in: (a) 30 (b) 26 (c) 24 (d) 32 Set 2 — DI & probability Q6. Wheat output: 2021 = 100 mt, 2024 = 120 mt. Percentage-point annual rise if uniform: (a) 6.67% (b) 20% (c) 6% (d) 5% Q7. A pie chart's sector is 54°. Its share: (a) 15% (b) 18% (c) 12% (d) 20% Q8. Ratio 3:5; total 64. Larger part: (a) 36 (b) 40 (c) 42 (d) 44 Q9. Tw","chars":1452},{"nav":"prelims-csat/numeracy/short","file":"numeracy-drills.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/numeracy/short-notes/numeracy-drills.html","title":"CSAT Numeracy — Drills &amp; Shortcuts","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Numeracy — Drills & Shortcuts"},{"level":2,"text":"Core formula rail (memorise+practise)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill Set A (one-minuteers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill Set B (two-minuteers)"},{"level":2,"text":"Drill Set C (DI-lite table)"},{"level":2,"text":"Shape & mensuration (CSAT-level only)"},{"level":2,"text":"Probability & counting (light)"},{"level":2,"text":"Number system traps"},{"level":2,"text":"Time-wasters caught early"}],"text":"CSAT Numeracy — Drills & Shortcuts Core formula rail (memorise+practise) %↔fraction : 1/8=12.5% · 1/6≈16.67% · 1/7≈14.28% · 1/9≈11.11% · 1/11≈9.09%. CI vs SI spread over 2 yrs = P×(r/100)². Speed : km/h × 5/18 = m/s; average speed (equal distances) = 2ab/(a+b). Work : combined = LCM method; efficiency ratios invert time. Trains : crossing pole = own length; crossing bridge = length+bridge; relative speeds — opposite add, same subtract. Boats : downstream = b+w; upstream = b−w; still-water speed = (d+u)/2. Averages : inclusion/replacement adjust via total-difference method. Ratios : a:b and b:c → a:b:c by b-matching. Profit/loss : SP = CP×(1±p); successive discounts = 1−Π(1−dᵢ); MP vs discount vs profit triangles. Divisibility : 3/9 digit-sum · 4 last two digits · 8 last three · 11 alternate-sum · 7 split-3-digit trick. HCF/LCM : product = HCF×LCM (two numbers); prime factors via division","chars":3184},{"nav":"prelims-csat/pyqs","file":"csat-practice.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/pyqs/csat-practice.html","title":"CSAT — Practice Pyramid","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT — Practice Pyramid"},{"level":2,"text":"Block A — Rapid numeracy (10)"},{"level":2,"text":"Block B — Reasoning set (8)"},{"level":2,"text":"Block C — RC (2 minis)"},{"level":2,"text":"Block D — Practice test mapping sheet (score-keeper)"},{"level":2,"text":"Common fail patterns (final pass)"}],"text":"CSAT — Practice Pyramid Mixed-difficulty question set in official-style proportions; answers at the end of each block. Block A — Rapid numeracy (10) A sum doubles in 8 years at simple interest. SI rate % = ? Average of 7 numbers is 50; removing 68 and 85 yields the average = ? Two pipes fill in 4 h and 6 h together time = ? Selling price with 20% profit = cost ₹840 → ? 48 students sit in rows of 6 — % students of 250? LCM(15,20,24)=? 0.125 as fraction of 0.25 = ? If 10% loss at ₹450 → CP = ? Square root of 1764? 3:5 ratio, sum 64 → smaller share? Ans: 1) 12.5% 2) 46 3) 2.4 h 4) ₹1,008 5) 19.2% 6) 120 7) 1/2 8) ₹500 9) 42 10) 24 Block B — Reasoning set (8) A is 4th from left; B is 9th from right in a 15-line queue — how many between? (2) Leap-year Mondays: Jan 1 2024 = Mon → Jan 1 2025 = ? (Wed — leap step) In a code: CAT=24, DOG=26 → figure rule? (A=1…Z=26 sums: 3+1+20=24; 4+15+7=26) ; w","chars":3222},{"nav":"prelims-csat/short","file":"csat-strategy.html","rel":"content/prelims/csat/short-notes/csat-strategy.html","title":"CSAT — Strategy Frame","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT — Strategy Frame"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper surface (memorise before you touch GS1)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section-wise survival PlayBook"},{"level":3,"text":"RC (Reading Comprehension)"},{"level":3,"text":"Reasoning"},{"level":3,"text":"Numeracy"},{"level":2,"text":"Time & attempt policy"},{"level":2,"text":"Literacy of failure modes"},{"level":2,"text":"Mock economy"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick drills"}],"text":"CSAT — Strategy Frame CSAT = qualifying only; cutoff 33% (~66/200) but aspirants repeatedly fail it. This sheet codifies the survival discipline. Paper surface (memorise before you touch GS1) 80 questions · 200 marks · 2 hrs; marking : 2.5 mark per correct; negative 0.833 per wrong ; unattempted = 0. Mix share (recent): Reading comprehension ~35–40% · logical-analytical reasoning ~30–35% · basic numeracy ~25–30%. Qualifying standard is brutal in absolute numbers: bad RC-block collapse + guesses pulls under 66. Section-wise survival PlayBook RC (Reading Comprehension) Read the question stem first when options are inference-laden (last 50% efficiency gain). Inference-vs-fact decoys: distract \"according to passage\" phrases; answer only with passage-grounded statements (not prior knowledge). Titles/summary questions: map the paragraph ratio — the final answer must cover both sides of a balan","chars":3093},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/bullets","file":"fdi-investment-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/bullet-points/fdi-investment-points.html","title":"FDI & Investment — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"FDI & Investment — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Route flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Flow flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Vehicle flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"FDI & Investment — Bullet Points Route-anchor flash. Route flash Automatic = most manufacturing 100% · approval → multi-brand retail (30% sourcing), print 26% · defence 74% automatic (2020) · insurance-banking 74%. Prohibited → lottery, chit, Nidhi, real-estate trading, tobacco, atomic, plantation (exceptions apply). Press Note 3 (2020) → bordering-country approvals · e-commerce: marketplace only, no inventory. Flow flash FDI = 10%+ lasting control · FPI = listed, no control (2014 FPI merger of FII-QFI). Sources → Singapore > Mauritius > USA · sectors → services, computer, trading, auto. Vehicle flash InvIT → infrastructure trusts (NHAI InvIT) · REIT → property trusts + SM REIT 2024. AIF 2012 → Cat-I (VC-infra-SME), Cat-II (PE-debt), Cat-III (hedge, leverage) · FVCI route. GIFT IFSC → IFSCA 2019, GIFT-Nifty 2023, leasing hub. Trap flash Real estate: 'business/trading' prohibited but cons","chars":1209},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/notes","file":"fdi-investment-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/detailed-notes/fdi-investment-detailed.html","title":"FDI, FPI & Investment Vehicles — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"FDI, FPI and Investment Vehicles — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. FDI routes & sector map"},{"level":2,"text":"2. FDI vs FPI & vehicles"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"FDI, FPI and Investment Vehicles — Detailed Notes Routes, sector caps, vehicles (InvIT-REIT-AIF), and the FDI-FPI distinction. Route-cap questions recur. 1. FDI routes & sector map Automatic route (most sectors: manufacturing 100%); approval route (few: multi-brand retail with 30% local sourcing, print media 26%, banking-private 74%, insurance 74%, defence 74% automatic since 2020 with safeguards, space 100% certain activities 2024, telecom 100% automatic era); prohibited list : lottery-gambling-betting, chit funds, Nidhi, real-estate business (trading; construction-development allowed), tobacco manufacturing, agriculture/plantation (with exceptions), atomic energy, railway operations (services FDI 100% allowed, operations state monopoly) . Policy anchors: 2016+ liberalisation rounds; press-note 3 (2020): land-bordering-country approvals (China screening); DPPM sourcing rules; e-commerce","chars":2313},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/pyqs","file":"fdi-investment-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/pyqs/fdi-investment-mcqs.html","title":"FDI, FPI &amp; Investment Policy — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"FDI, FPI & Investment Policy — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"FDI, FPI & Investment Policy — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about India's FDI policy: 1. The consolidated FDI policy is issued by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade. 2. The Foreign Investment Promotion Board approves all government-route proposals. 3. Investments from countries sharing a land border with India require government approval irrespective of sector. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Foreign direct investment is prohibited in which of the following? 1. Lottery and gambling 2. Atomic energy 3. Manufacturing of cigarettes 4. Construction development of townships (a) 1 and 2 only (b) 1, 2 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Which of the following sectors permit 100% FDI under the automatic route? 1. Telecom services 2. Coal min","chars":3427},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/short","file":"fdi-investment-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/fdi-investment/short-notes/fdi-investment-notes.html","title":"FDI, FPI &amp; Investment Policy — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"FDI, FPI & Investment Policy — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"FDI framework"},{"level":2,"text":"Numbers"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"FDI, FPI & Investment Policy — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › External Sector. FDI routes and caps, FPI rules, investment numbers, key institutions and 2024–25 policy changes. FDI framework Governed by FEMA 1999 + FEM (Non-Debt Instruments) Rules 2019 (Finance Ministry) + Consolidated FDI Policy (DPIIT; 2020 + Press Notes) ; routes — automatic (no approval; most sectors 100%) and government (via DPIIT's Foreign Investment Facilitation Portal — FIPB abolished 2017; sectoral ministry decides within 8–10 weeks); Press Note 3 (Apr 2020) — investments from countries sharing land border (China, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, Myanmar, Afghanistan) or beneficial owner there need government approval (Chinese proposals — easing debated in 2025; Economic Survey 2024 favoured selective FDI; approvals remain case-by-case). Prohibited : lottery, gambling/betting, chit funds, Nidhi, T","chars":11300},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/short","file":"external-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/short-notes/external-prelims.html","title":"External Sector — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"External Sector — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"BoP architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"Forex-management tools"},{"level":2,"text":"FDI rules pockets (verify-ceiling updates)"},{"level":2,"text":"Trade agreements ledger"},{"level":2,"text":"Trade stats pairs"},{"level":2,"text":"Currency-internationalisation moves"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"External Sector — Prelims Drill BoP architecture Current Account = goods trade + services + primary income + secondary income (remittances big here). Capital/Financial Account = FDI + FPI + external loans (ECB/NRI deposits) + banking capital + SDR allocations. Errors & omissions line; overall balance → change in reserves (valuation adjustments non-flow). India's typical shape: merchandise deficit (~$25–30 bn/month recent band) − services surplus (+) + remittance surplus (+) → CAD narrow (~0.5–1.2% GDP recent bands). Forex-management tools Reserves composition: foreign currency assets (dominant) + gold + SDRs + reserve tranche in the IMF; peak ~ $704 bn (Sept–Oct 2024) ; import-cover ~11–12 months; sovereign debt-servicing coverage ratios strengthening. Rupee regime: managed float (no hard band); NEER/REER tracked on 40-currency basket; capital-account convertibility partial; full capital","chars":3840},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/bullets","file":"trade-bop-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/bullet-points/trade-bop-points.html","title":"Trade & BoP — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Trade & BoP — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"BoP flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Profile flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Policy flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Trade & BoP — Bullet Points Account-anchor flash. BoP flash Current a/c → goods + services + primary + secondary income · capital a/c → FDI-FPI-ECB-NRI · reserves = balancing item. India → services surplus + #1 remittances · CAD FY13 4.8% peak → FY24 ~0.7% · reserves top-4. Profile flash Exports → engineering, petro-products, pharma, gems, electronics, rice · imports → crude, gold, electronics, machinery, coal. US #1 destination · China #2 source, largest deficit · IT-BPM services engine. Policy flash RoDTEP 2021 (replaced MEIS) · RoSCTL apparel · EPCG-Advance-SEZ · DGTR 2018 · LEADS index. FTAs → UAE 2022, Australia 2022, EFTA 2024 ($100bn), UK CETA 2025 · RCEP exit 2019 · IPEF partial. FTP 2023 → $2tn by 2030 target. Trap flash FDI/FPI sit in the capital account (not CAD components). Remittances = current account (secondary income). RoDTEP ≠ RoSCTL scope — apparel under RoSCTL. TEPA pa","chars":990},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/notes","file":"trade-bop-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/detailed-notes/trade-bop-detailed.html","title":"External Trade & BoP — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"External Trade and Balance of Payments — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. BoP structure"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Trade profile"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Policy instruments"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"External Trade and Balance of Payments — Detailed Notes BoP structure, India's services-led profile, FTAs and export-promotion instruments. 1. BoP structure Current account : goods (chronic BoT deficit), services (IT-BPM surplus), primary income (interest-dividends, negative), secondary income ( remittances — world #1, ~$120bn band ). Capital account : FDI (stable), FPI (volatile), ECBs, NRI deposits, trade credit. Balancing item = reserve changes. CAD anchors : FY13 peak ~4.8% GDP (taper-tantrum) → FY21 sub-1% → FY24 ~0.7% ; oil-gold sensitivity; rupee managed float; reserves ~$650-700bn band (top-4). 2. Trade profile Merchandise exports: engineering, petroleum products, pharma, gems-jewellery, electronics (rising), agri (rice #1, marine, spices). Imports: crude (largest), gold, electronics, machinery, coal. Partners: US #1 destination, China #2 source (largest deficit), UAE-Gulf. FTP 2","chars":2033},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/pyqs","file":"trade-bop-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/pyqs/trade-bop-mcqs.html","title":"Trade &amp; Balance of Payments — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Trade & Balance of Payments — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Trade & Balance of Payments — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following are recorded in the current account of India's Balance of Payments? 1. Remittances from Indians working abroad 2. Foreign direct investment inflows 3. Software services exports 4. Interest paid on external commercial borrowings (a) 1, 3 and 4 (b) 1 and 2 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Consider India's external sector in 2024–25: 1. The merchandise trade deficit was a record of about $287 billion. 2. The current account deficit was below 1% of GDP. 3. Services exports exceeded $380 billion. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. In 2024–25, India's largest source of imports and the country with which India ran its largest bilateral trade deficit was: (a) United States (b) China (c) Russia (d) UAE Q4. Under the Liberalis","chars":3152},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/short","file":"trade-bop-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/external-sector/trade-bop/short-notes/trade-bop-notes.html","title":"Trade &amp; Balance of Payments — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Trade & Balance of Payments — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Balance of Payments"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Trade & Balance of Payments — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › External Sector. BoP structure, trade numbers FY25, exchange-rate regime, forex reserves, external debt, trade policy instruments and FTAs. Balance of Payments Current account = trade in goods + services + primary income (investment income, compensation) + secondary income (remittances, transfers). Capital/financial account = FDI, FPI, ECBs, NRI deposits, banking capital, trade credit, official (IMF/SDR). Errors & omissions; overall balance = change in reserves . India's BoP in RBI's format (quarterly); CAD FY25 0.6% of GDP ($23.3 bn); Q4 FY25 surplus $13.5 bn (1.3%); FY24 0.7%; FY23 2.0%; FY13 4.8% peak; Q1 FY26 CAD 0.2% ($2.4 bn); Q2 FY26 1.3% ; Goods trade deficit FY25 $287 bn record; services surplus $189 bn; remittances $135 bn (FY25; $129 bn calendar 2024 — world 1st; ~3.4% of GDP; US largest source ~28%, Gulf","chars":12490},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/bullets","file":"capital-markets-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/bullet-points/capital-markets-points.html","title":"Capital Markets — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Capital Markets — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Primary flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Secondary flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Participant flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Reform flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Capital Markets — Bullet Points Term-anchor flash. Primary flash IPO-FPO-rights-bonus-QIP instrument set · book building = price-band discovery · ASBA + UPI-IPO retail · anchor investors = pre-open QIB tranche. MPS = 25% minimum public shareholding · SME platforms on NSE-BSE · T+3 listing now. Secondary flash T+1 (2023) → India first; T+0 pilot 2024 · G-secs via RBI Retail Direct (2021) · corporate bonds = SEBI book platform. F&O → SEBI 2024 rationalisation (weekly index curbs) · commodities → SEBI since 2015 (FMC merger). Participant flash FPI 2-category (2019) · DII = MF-insurance-pension · demat ~15 crore band · algo retail framework 2025. Reform flash SEBI 1992 act; SAT appeals · BRSR-core ESG · sovereign green bonds 2023 · TRI benchmarking 2018 · MF Central 2022. Trap flash QIP = only to QIBs (not public) — instrument trap. G-secs regulated by RBI (not SEBI) — regulator split trap. ","chars":1081},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/notes","file":"capital-markets-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/detailed-notes/capital-markets-detailed.html","title":"Capital Markets — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Capital Markets — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Primary market"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Secondary market & products"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Reforms & mutual funds"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Capital Markets — Detailed Notes Primary-secondary mechanics, debt-derivatives, participants, settlement reforms. SEBI-term precision. 1. Primary market Instruments: IPO-FPO-rights-bonus-private placement-QIP (qualified institutional placement) ; pricing: fixed vs book building (price band, cut-off) ; ASBA (application supported by blocked amount) + UPI-IPO for retail ; anchor investors (50% QIB share pre-open); minimum public shareholding 25% ; SME platforms (NSE Emerge-BSE SME); greenshoe option; delisting norms. Regulation: SEBI ICDR regs; RHP-DRH; merchant bankers, registrars, credit-rating agencies; IPO disclosures via OFSDP; T+3 listing timeline (from T+6). 2. Secondary market & products Exchanges: NSE-BSE (+MSE legacy; Metropolitan defunct), clearing corps; T+1 rolling settlement (Jan 2023; optional opt-in then all — India first major market) ; T+0 beta (2024 limited pilot — same-","chars":2467},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/pyqs","file":"capital-markets-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/pyqs/capital-markets-mcqs.html","title":"Capital Markets &amp; SEBI — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Capital Markets & SEBI — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Capital Markets & SEBI — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following instruments belong to the money market? 1. Treasury bills 2. Certificates of deposit 3. Corporate debentures 4. Commercial paper (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Consider the following statements about SEBI: 1. It was established as a statutory body in 1988. 2. Appeals against its orders lie with the Securities Appellate Tribunal and then the Supreme Court. 3. It regulates commodity derivatives since the merger of the Forward Markets Commission. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. \"T+1 settlement\" in Indian stock exchanges means: (a) Trades are settled one hour after execution (b) Securities and funds change hands one working day after the trade date (c) Traders get one extra day to pay margins (","chars":3248},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/short","file":"capital-markets-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/capital-markets/short-notes/capital-markets-notes.html","title":"Capital Markets &amp; SEBI — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Capital Markets & SEBI — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Structure"},{"level":2,"text":"Instruments & concepts"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Capital Markets & SEBI — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Financial Markets. Primary and secondary markets, instruments, regulators, market infrastructure and recent reforms. Structure Money market (≤1 year: call money, T-bills 91/182/364-day, CPs, CDs, repo/TREPS — RBI regulates) vs capital market (>1 year: equity, bonds — SEBI regulates; G-secs jointly RBI/SEBI); primary market (IPO, FPO, rights, QIP, preferential, OFS, private placement, InvIT/REIT offers) vs secondary market (exchanges — NSE 1992 (largest by volume; world's largest derivatives exchange), BSE 1875 (Asia's oldest), MSEI, NSE IFSC & India INX at GIFT City; Calcutta SE defunct); commodity derivatives — MCX (metals/energy) and NCDEX (agri); FMC merged into SEBI 2015. SEBI : statutory 1992 (set up 1988); chair Tuhin Kanta Pandey (Mar 2025; earlier Madhabi Puri Buch — first woman, 2022–25); board 9 (chair + 2 Fina","chars":8328},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/bullets","file":"financial-inclusion-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/bullet-points/financial-inclusion-points.html","title":"Financial Inclusion — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Financial Inclusion — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Account flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Credit flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Pension-insurance flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Money-market flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Financial Inclusion — Bullet Points Scheme-instrument flash. Account flash PMJDY 2014 → zero-balance, RuPay, ₹2L cover, ₹10k OD · JAM → JDY+Aadhaar+Mobile · DBT ~₹44L cr band · BC doorstep model. Credit flash MUDRA 2015 → Shishu 50k / Kishore 5L / Tarun 10L / Tarun-Plus 20L (2024). KCC 1998 (+2020 animal-fisheries) · Stand-Up India 2016 (SC-ST-women 10L-1cr) · SVANidhi 2020 vendors · Vishwakarma 2023 artisans. NRLM SHGs → Lakhpati Didi 3-cr target. Pension-insurance flash APY 2015 → ₹1-5k guaranteed pension · PMJJBY → ₹2L life (₹436/yr) · PMSBY → ₹2L accident (₹20/yr) · PMFBY 2016 crop · NPS-PFRDA frame. Money-market flash T-bills → 91-182-364 days discount · Cash Management Bills → Call money → overnight interbank · TREPS → repo platform · LAF VRR/VRRR auctions. Trap flash PMJJBY (life) vs PMSBY (accident) — ₹436 vs ₹20 premium pair. Mudra tier order — Shishu smallest, Tarun-Plus newest","chars":1085},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/notes","file":"financial-inclusion-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/detailed-notes/financial-inclusion-detailed.html","title":"Financial Inclusion & Money Markets — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Financial Inclusion and Money Markets — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Inclusion stack"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Money market instruments"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Financial Inclusion and Money Markets — Detailed Notes Inclusion stack (JDY-credit-pension-insurance) plus money-market instrument vocabulary. 1. Inclusion stack PMJDY (28 Aug 2014) : zero-balance, RuPay card + ₹2L accident cover, ₹10k overdraft; ~53 crore accounts. JAM : JDY + Aadhaar + Mobile → DBT (~₹44 lakh crore cumulative — official DBT portal band); BC (business-correspondent) model + doorstep banking standards. Credit : MUDRA (2015; Shishu ≤₹50k, Kishore to ₹5L, Tarun to ₹10L; Tarun Plus ₹20L (2024, repeat successful) ); KCC (1998 animal-fisherman extension 2020; interest subvention); Stand-Up India (2016; SC-ST-women, ₹10L-1cr greenfield) ; PM SVANidhi (2020 street vendors); PM Vishwakarma (2023 artisans); NRLM SHG-bank linkage; DAY-NRLM women credit deepening; 'Lakhpati Didi' 3-crore target . Pension-insurance trio (2015) : APY (unorganised, guaranteed ₹1-5k pension 60+), PMJJB","chars":1903},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/pyqs","file":"financial-inclusion-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/pyqs/financial-inclusion-mcqs.html","title":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments &amp; Insurance — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments & Insurance — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments & Insurance — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about Payments Banks: 1. They cannot extend loans. 2. They can accept demand deposits up to ₹2 lakh per customer. 3. They can issue credit cards. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The Unified Payments Interface is operated by: (a) Reserve Bank of India directly (b) National Payments Corporation of India, a not-for-profit company owned by banks (c) Department of Financial Services (d) Indian Banks' Association Q3. Which of the following payment systems are owned and operated by the RBI itself? 1. RTGS 2. NEFT 3. IMPS 4. UPI (a) 1 and 2 (b) 3 and 4 (c) 1, 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q4. Under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana, the maximum premium payable by farmers for kharif food crops i","chars":3062},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/short","file":"financial-inclusion-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/financial-inclusion/short-notes/financial-inclusion-notes.html","title":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments &amp; Insurance — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments & Insurance — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Banking outreach"},{"level":2,"text":"Digital payments"},{"level":2,"text":"Insurance & pensions"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Financial Inclusion, Digital Payments & Insurance — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Financial Markets. JAM trinity, banking outreach, digital public infrastructure, insurance/pension coverage and the regulators behind them. Banking outreach Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana (Aug 2014) : ~56 crore accounts (Aug 2025; 55% women, 67% rural/semi-urban), deposits ~₹2.6 lakh crore; RuPay card with ₹2 lakh accident cover; overdraft ₹10,000; zero-balance share fell from 58% (2015) to ~8% ; Financial Inclusion Index (RBI, since 2021) 67.0 (Mar 2025; 64.2 previous) ; Global Findex 2025 — India adults with account ~89% . JAM trinity (Jan Dhan + Aadhaar (UIDAI, 2009; Aadhaar Act 2016 — money bill; Puttaswamy 2018 upheld with limits) + Mobile); DBT — ~₹44 lakh crore cumulative; savings ~₹3.5 lakh crore (leakage plugged); Aadhaar-enabled Payment System (AePS) ; Business Correspondents (2006) ;","chars":11738},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/financial-markets/short","file":"finmarkets-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/financial-markets/short-notes/finmarkets-prelims.html","title":"Financial Markets &amp; Inclusion — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Financial Markets & Inclusion — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Market regulator anatomy"},{"level":2,"text":"Market pipelining"},{"level":2,"text":"Bond & debt"},{"level":2,"text":"Inclusion & payments"},{"level":2,"text":"Financial-sector laws/history quick rows"},{"level":2,"text":"Common trap lines"}],"text":"Financial Markets & Inclusion — Prelims Drill Market regulator anatomy SEBI (1992 statutory; earlier 1988 non-stat): securities regulator; replaces separate Controller of Capital Issues. IRDAI (1999 → statutory by Act): insurance; pullback \"Bima Trinity\" 2025 era (Bima Sugam marketplace + Bima Vistaar composite + Bima Vaahak women-led distribution). RBI ( stat under RBI Act 1934; nationalised 1949): monetary authority + banking regulator + payment systems (PSS Act 2007) + Forex (FERA→FEMA 1999) + G-sec manager ( FRBNF story). PFRDA (pensions; NPS/APY regulator). IFSCA (2020; GIFT City unified regulator — banking+capital+insurance at IFSC). Market pipelining Exchanges: NSE (1992 set up; 1994 operations — demutualised electronic pioneer), BSE (oldest in Asia — 1875 as Native Share & Stock Brokers' Association), MCX (commodities), NCDEX (agri), MSE-I metropolitan revival attempts. Indices: ","chars":3402},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/bullets","file":"fiscal-policy-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/bullet-points/fiscal-policy-points.html","title":"Fiscal Policy — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fiscal Policy — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Deficit flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Fund flash"},{"level":2,"text":"FRBM flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Reform flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Fiscal Policy — Bullet Points Deficit-anchor flash. Deficit flash Revenue deficit → rev exp - rev receipts · Fiscal deficit → total exp - (rev receipts + non-debt capital receipts) · Primary deficit → FD - interest. Monetised deficit = legacy RBI-advice borrowing term · effective revenue deficit = RD minus grants-for-creation-of-capital-assets. Fund flash Consolidated 266(1) → all receipts-loans · Public account 266(2) → trust (PF, small savings) · Contingency 267 → President's imprest. FRBM flash FRBM Act 2003 (amended 2018) · targets → FD 3%, Centre debt 40% GDP, general govt 60% · escape clause 0.5% · COVID suspension → FY26 glide 4.4%. States → 3% FD ceiling + Art 293 consent · off-budget items on-book 2020-21 (FCI). Reform flash Rail budget merged + 1 Feb date + plan/non-plan gone → all 2017 · CSS bucketing 2016 · capex-loans to states (50-yr, 2020-25). NMP 2021 (₹6L cr) = asset mon","chars":1309},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/notes","file":"fiscal-policy-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/detailed-notes/fiscal-policy-detailed.html","title":"Fiscal Policy & Budget — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fiscal Policy and Budget — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Budget architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Deficit identities"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Extra-budgetary & fiscal-federal bits"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Budget and Its Constitutional Machinery"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Deficits — Definitions You Must Reproduce Exactly"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The FRBM Framework and Fiscal Consolidation"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Taxation Structure"}],"text":"Fiscal Policy and Budget — Detailed Notes Receipts-expenditure-deficit arithmetic + FRBM + extra-budgetary resources. Deficit-identity questions repeat. 1. Budget architecture Art 112 Annual Financial Statement (union budget; merged rail budget 2017; date advanced to 1 Feb 2017); Consolidated Fund (266(1)) vs Public Account (266(2)) vs Contingency Fund (267 — at President's disposal) ; demand-for-grants + appropriation + finance-bill cycle; guillotine; Vote-on-account; budget documents (AFS + three FRBM statements: macroeconomic framework, medium-term fiscal policy-cum-fiscal policy strategy, medium-term expenditure framework). Receipts: revenue (tax: direct (corporate, income) + indirect (GST); non-tax: dividends, interest, spectrum) + capital (recoveries, disinvestment — now 'asset monetisation' CPSE pipeline + NMP). Expenditure: revenue vs capital; committed (interest > 40% of revenue","chars":10651},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/pyqs","file":"fiscal-policy-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/pyqs/fiscal-policy-mcqs.html","title":"Fiscal Policy, Budget &amp; Taxation — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fiscal Policy, Budget & Taxation — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Fiscal Policy, Budget & Taxation — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Fiscal deficit equals: (a) Revenue expenditure − revenue receipts (b) Total expenditure − (revenue receipts + non-debt capital receipts) (c) Total expenditure − total receipts including borrowings (d) Fiscal deficit − interest payments Q2. Which of the following are non-debt capital receipts of the Union Government? 1. Disinvestment proceeds 2. Recovery of loans 3. Dividend from the RBI 4. Market borrowings (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1, 2 and 3 (c) 2 and 4 (d) 1, 3 and 4 Q3. The Union Budget 2025–26 set the fiscal deficit target at: (a) 4.9% of GDP (b) 4.8% of GDP (c) 4.4% of GDP (d) 3.0% of GDP Q4. Consider GST 2.0 effective 22 September 2025: 1. The 12% and 28% slabs were abolished. 2. A 40% rate applies to de-merit goods such as pan masala and aerated drinks. 3. Ind","chars":2722},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/short","file":"fiscal-policy-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/fiscal-policy/short-notes/fiscal-policy-notes.html","title":"Fiscal Policy, Budget &amp; Taxation — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fiscal Policy, Budget & Taxation — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Deficits & concepts"},{"level":2,"text":"Taxes"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Fiscal Policy, Budget & Taxation — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Macro-economy. Deficits, FRBM, Budget 2025–26 numbers, tax structure (direct, GST 2.0), public debt and fiscal federalism figures. Deficits & concepts Revenue deficit = revenue expenditure − revenue receipts (BE 2025–26: 1.5% of GDP); Effective RD = RD − grants for creation of capital assets; Fiscal deficit = total expenditure − (revenue receipts + non-debt capital receipts) = total borrowing ( BE 2025–26: 4.4% (₹15.69 lakh cr); FY25 RE 4.8%; FY24 5.6%; FY21 9.2% peak ); Primary deficit = FD − interest payments (0.8% BE 2025–26); Monetised deficit (RBI's net credit to govt; ad hoc T-bills ended 1997 → WMA); Budget deficit (obsolete); Debt–GDP: Centre ~56–57%; general govt ~81% (IMF 2025); FRBM target 40 Centre/60 general by 2024–25 (missed); new anchor — debt-GDP 50 ± 1% by 31 Mar 2031 (Budget 2025 — fiscal con","chars":17883},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/bullets","file":"money-banking-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/bullet-points/money-banking-points.html","title":"Money & Banking — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Money & Banking — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Money flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Rate flash"},{"level":2,"text":"MPC flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Bank flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Money & Banking — Bullet Points Rate-and-measure flash. Money flash M1 → currency + demand deposits + RBI other-deposits · M3 = broad money · M0 = high-powered (currency + reserves). Multiplier = M3/M0 · CBDC e-rupee pilots 2022 (retail + wholesale) · demonetisation 2016. Rate flash Repo = policy rate · SDF = corridor floor (2022) · MSF = ceiling (+25bp) · bank rate = penal signal. CRR 4% no-interest · SLR 18% · OMO-VRR/VRRR = liquidity tools. Transmission chain → base rate → MCLR (2016) → EBLR (2019). MPC flash 6 members (3 RBI + 3 external) · Governor casting vote · target 4% CPI ±2% · bi-monthly · failure = 3 quarters outside band → report to Government. Bank flash PSB consolidation → 12 PSBs · payments banks = deposits-only no-credit · SFBs · NBFC scale-based 2022. DFIs → NABARD 1982, SIDBI, NaBFID 2021 · DICGC ₹5 lakh · priority sector 40% (agri 18, MSME 7.5, weaker 12 target). RRB ","chars":1264},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/notes","file":"money-banking-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/detailed-notes/money-banking-detailed.html","title":"Money, Banking & RBI Policy — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Money, Banking and RBI Policy — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Money supply & creation"},{"level":2,"text":"2. RBI instruments (LAF corridor era)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Banking structure & regulation"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Money, Banking and RBI Policy — Detailed Notes Money supply, RBI instruments, MPC frame, banking structure. Rate-name precision decides marks. 1. Money supply & creation RBI measures: M1 = currency + demand deposits + other deposits with RBI ; M2 = M1 + savings with Post Office; M3 = M1 + time deposits + government deposits with RBI (aggregate monetary resources) ; M4 = M3 + total Post Office deposits (ex-NSC). High-powered money (M0) = currency + banks' reserves with RBI. Money multiplier = M3/M0; driven by reserve behaviour; seigniorage; demonetisation 2016 (86% value returned; 99.3% deposited) ; digital currency: e-rupee CBDC (pilots since 2022; retail + wholesale) . 2. RBI instruments (LAF corridor era) Repo rate = single policy rate (since 2021 revised framework docs); SDF (standing deposit facility) = floor (2022, replaced fixed reverse-repo role); MSF = ceiling (+25 bp). Bank rate","chars":2644},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/pyqs","file":"money-banking-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/pyqs/money-banking-mcqs.html","title":"Money, Banking &amp; Monetary Policy — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Money, Banking & Monetary Policy — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Money, Banking & Monetary Policy — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Broad money (M3) in India is defined as: (a) Currency with the public + demand deposits with banks (b) M1 + time deposits with banks (c) M1 + savings deposits with post offices (d) Reserve money + net foreign exchange assets of RBI Q2. Consider the Monetary Policy Committee: 1. The inflation target is fixed by the MPC every five years. 2. External members are appointed for four years and are not eligible for reappointment. 3. If inflation stays outside the tolerance band for three consecutive quarters, the RBI must report to the Central Government. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. In the Liquidity Adjustment Facility corridor since April 2022, the floor and ceiling are respectively: (a) Reverse repo rate and bank rate (b) Standing Depos","chars":3043},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/short","file":"money-banking-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/money-banking/short-notes/money-banking-notes.html","title":"Money, Banking &amp; Monetary Policy — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Money, Banking & Monetary Policy — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Money & RBI"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Money, Banking & Monetary Policy — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Macro-economy. Money supply, RBI tools, banking structure, NPAs, payment systems and 2025 numbers. Money & RBI Money supply : M0 (reserve money = currency in circulation + bankers' deposits with RBI + other deposits); M1 = currency with public + demand deposits + other deposits with RBI ; M2 = M1 + savings deposits with post offices; M3 (broad money) = M1 + time deposits ; M4 = M3 + total post office deposits. Money multiplier = M3/M0 (~5.3) ; velocity; high-powered money. Currency in circulation ~₹38 lakh cr ; ₹2,000 notes withdrawn May 2023 (98.3% returned); ₹500 note largest share (~86% by value); coins by GoI (Coinage Act 2011); ₹1 note signed by Finance Secretary; notes by RBI (RBI Act s 22; minimum reserve system 1957 — ₹200 cr of which ₹115 cr gold); e₹ (CBDC — wholesale Nov 2022, retail Dec 2022; ~₹1,00","chars":13037},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/bullets","file":"national-income-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/bullet-points/national-income-points.html","title":"National Income — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Income — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Ladder flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Institution flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Concept flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"National Income — Bullet Points Formula-anchor flash. Ladder flash GNP = GDP + NFIA (India: NFIA Market price = factor cost + net indirect taxes · basic price = factor cost + production taxes - production subsidies. Expenditure side → C + I + G + NX · deflator = nominal/real x 100. Institution flash NSO (MoSPI) → CSO + NSSO merged 2019 · quarterly GDP + annual NAS calendar · state GSDP parallel. 2015 series → base 2011-12, GVA-basic headline, MCA-21 database · new base 2022-23 in progress. Concept flash PPP comparisons → India ~3rd largest (PPP) · informal ≈ 45% GVA, ~90% employment. HDI/Green GDP = welfare complements · CPI 4±2 = MPC target · WPI ≠ CPI basket. Trap flash GNP vs GDP direction (India's NFIA negative — GNP smaller). Base-price change: 'basic prices' ≠ 'factor cost' (production-tax wedge). Real vs nominal swap in options — check 'constant prices' tag. Deflator uses implicit","chars":934},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/notes","file":"national-income-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/detailed-notes/national-income-detailed.html","title":"National Income & Accounts — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Income and Accounts — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The aggregate ladder"},{"level":2,"text":"2. India's national accounts practice"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Related concepts & debates"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Aggregates and How They Interconnect"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Nominal, Real and the Deflator"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Three Methods of Measurement"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Sectors, Institutions and the Indian Context"}],"text":"National Income and Accounts — Detailed Notes GDP-GVA-NNP ladder, India's 2015 series revision, estimation sides. Formula-statement questions. 1. The aggregate ladder GDP (territorial): market value of final goods-services within domestic territory. GNP = GDP + NFIA (net factor income from abroad — negative for India). NNP = GNP - depreciation (consumption of fixed capital). NDP/NNP at factor cost = NNI ; market price = factor cost + net indirect taxes (indirect taxes minus subsidies). Three approaches: income (compensation + operating surplus + mixed income), product/value-added (GVA by sector), expenditure (C + I + G + NX). Identities hold ex-post; statistical discrepancy line. Per-capita income = NNI/population; real vs nominal (constant 2011-12 prices); GDP deflator = nominal/real x 100 (India uses WPI-CPI hybrid implicit deflator). 2. India's national accounts practice Compiled by N","chars":9068},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/pyqs","file":"national-income-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/pyqs/national-income-mcqs.html","title":"National Income &amp; Growth Accounting — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Income & Growth Accounting — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"National Income & Growth Accounting — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. GDP at market prices is obtained from GVA at basic prices by: (a) Adding production taxes and subtracting production subsidies (b) Adding product taxes and subtracting product subsidies (c) Subtracting depreciation (d) Adding net factor income from abroad Q2. Consider the following: 1. Net National Product at factor cost is called National Income. 2. India's GNP is larger than its GDP because net factor income from abroad is positive. 3. Per capita income is computed using Net National Income. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Which of the following are included in the estimation of GDP? 1. Imputed rent of owner-occupied houses 2. Old-age pensions paid by the Government 3. Sale of a second-hand car 4. Government spending on salaries o","chars":2929},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/short","file":"national-income-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/national-income/short-notes/national-income-notes.html","title":"National Income &amp; Growth Accounting — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Income & Growth Accounting — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Concepts"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"National Income & Growth Accounting — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Macro-economy. GDP/GVA/GNP/NNP definitions, base years, deflators, India's growth numbers and the concepts UPSC keeps testing. Concepts GDP = market value of all final goods & services produced within domestic territory in a year. Three methods — production (GVA + product taxes − subsidies), income (wages + rent + interest + profit + mixed income + depreciation + net indirect taxes), expenditure (C + I + G + (X − M)). GVA (basic prices) = output − intermediate consumption; GDP (market prices) = GVA + product taxes − product subsidies (GST, excise, customs are product taxes; fertiliser/food subsidies are product subsidies). GVA at factor cost = GVA basic − production taxes + production subsidies (production taxes: land revenue, stamp duty, professional tax). GNP = GDP + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) ; ","chars":6232},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/macro-economy/short","file":"macro-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/macro-economy/short-notes/macro-prelims.html","title":"Macroeconomy — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Macroeconomy — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"National-income algebra"},{"level":2,"text":"Inflation set"},{"level":2,"text":"Money & monetary policy"},{"level":2,"text":"Banking anatomy"},{"level":2,"text":"Fiscal ledger"},{"level":2,"text":"Populations/economy metric CA hooks"}],"text":"Macroeconomy — Prelims Drill National-income algebra GDP (market) = Σ GVA (basic) + product taxes − product subsidies; GNP = GDP + NFIA. Depreciation shift: NNP = GNP − depreciation; national income = NNP at factor cost . Base years: National accounts 2011–12 ; IIP 2011–12; WPI 2011–12 (L=100); CPI-C 2012; CPI-AL/RL 1986–87 (revision underway). Real vs nominal: real uses constant prices (base yr); nominal current prices; GDP deflator = nominal/real × 100 . Growth maths: 6.5% real growth approx doubling in ~11 years (rule of 72). Inflation set Types: demand-pull / cost-push / built-in (wage-price spiral) / creeping-walking-galloping-hyper stagflation = stagnation + inflation. Measures: CPI-C (MPC anchor: 4% ± 2% band to Mar 2031 , extended 2025), CPI-IW (industrial workers → DA linkage; Labour Bureau), CPI-AL/RL (rural labour/agri labour), WPI (697-commodities; base 2011–12; no services).","chars":3233},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/bullets","file":"human-development-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/bullet-points/human-development-points.html","title":"Human Development — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Human Development — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"HDI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"MPI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Poverty-line flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Welfare flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Human Development — Bullet Points Index-anchor flash. HDI flash HDI 1990 UNDP (Haq-Sen) · dims → life, schooling (mean+expected), GNI-pc · IHDI inequality · PHDI planet (2020). GII → health-empowerment-labour · GDI → f/m HDI ratio · India → medium band, ~130/193. MPI flash MPI 2010 = UNDP+OPHI · 3 dims, 10 indicators · Alkire-Foster · India MPI = NITI, NFHS-based. NITI MPI → 24.85% (2015-16) → 14.96% (2019-21) · ~11% extrapolation. Poverty-line flash Chain → Alagh 1979 → Lakdawala 1993 → Tendulkar 2009 (21.9%) → Rangarajan 2014 (~29.5%) → MPI era. Calories → 2400/2100 · WB extreme line → $2.15/day 2017 PPP. Welfare flash NFSA 2013 → 67%, PHH 5 kg, AAY 35 kg/hh · ONORC 2019 · ePOS-TPDS · PMGKAY extension. SECC 2011 → deprivation targeting · NSAP pensions · DBT stack. Trap flash HDI income = GNI per capita PPP — GDP options wrong. MPI: 3 dimensions / 10 indicators — number pair. Tendulkar ","chars":1040},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/notes","file":"human-development-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/detailed-notes/human-development-detailed.html","title":"Human Development & Poverty Metrics — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Human Development and Poverty Metrics — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The UNDP family"},{"level":2,"text":"2. India's poverty-line evolution"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Welfare architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Human Development and Poverty Metrics — Detailed Notes HDI family + India's poverty-line evolution + welfare architecture. Index-publisher-value recall. 1. The UNDP family HDI (1990; Mahbub ul Haq-Amartya Sen lineage) : geometric mean of health (life expectancy), education (mean + expected years of schooling), income (GNI-pc PPP, log). IHDI = inequality-discounted; GII = reproductive health + empowerment + labour market; GDI = female/male HDI ratio; MPI (2010, UNDP + OPHI, Alkire-Foster) = 3 dimensions x 10 indicators; PHDI (2020+) = planet-pressure-adjusted. India: medium-human-development band; rank ~130/193 (2025 report) ; policy-note: SL-Bhutan above India in recent reports — comparative line. 2. India's poverty-line evolution Alagh (1979) → Lakdawala (1993) → Tendulkar (2009 report; 2011-12 base: 21.9% poor; added education-health, MRP consumption) → Rangarajan (2014; ~29.5%) → MPI ","chars":1954},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/pyqs","file":"human-development-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/pyqs/human-development-mcqs.html","title":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment &amp; Social Sector — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment & Social Sector — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment & Social Sector — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following pairs of indices and their publishers: 1. Human Development Index — UNDP 2. Global Hunger Index — FAO 3. Global Gender Gap Index — World Economic Forum 4. Global Innovation Index — WIPO Which pairs are correctly matched? (a) 1, 3 and 4 (b) 1 and 2 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. In the Human Development Report 2025, India was placed at rank: (a) 116 (b) 130 (c) 134 (d) 142 Q3. The Periodic Labour Force Survey for 2023–24 reported which of the following? 1. Labour force participation rate (15+, usual status) of about 60% 2. Unemployment rate of about 3.2% 3. Female labour force participation rate below 25% (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. The Global Multidimensional Poverty Index is joint","chars":3117},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/short","file":"human-development-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/human-development/short-notes/human-development-notes.html","title":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment &amp; Social Sector — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment & Social Sector — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Global indices (latest editions)"},{"level":2,"text":"Poverty & inequality (India data)"},{"level":2,"text":"Employment & education & health numbers"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Human Development, Poverty, Employment & Social Sector — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Planning & Growth. Global indices and India's ranks, poverty and inequality data, employment surveys, and flagship social-sector schemes. Global indices (latest editions) Index Publisher India Human Development Index (HDR 2025) UNDP 130/193 (0.685; medium; up from 133) Gender Inequality Index UNDP 102/193 Global Gender Gap 2025 WEF 131/148 (economic participation weakest) Global Hunger Index 2025 Concern/Welthungerhilfe 102/123 (score 25.8, 'serious'; India disputes) Multidimensional Poverty Index 2024 UNDP/OPHI 16.4% poor (234 million — largest number) World Happiness Report 2025 Oxford/Gallup 118/147 Global Innovation Index 2025 WIPO 38/139 Human Capital Index 2020 World Bank 116/174 (0.49) Corruption Perceptions Index 2024 Transparency Intl 96/180 Press Freedom 2025 RSF 151/180 Global P","chars":14518},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/bullets","file":"niti-aayog-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/bullet-points/niti-aayog-points.html","title":"NITI Aayog — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"NITI Aayog — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Era flash"},{"level":2,"text":"NITI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Transfer flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"NITI Aayog — Bullet Points Body-anchor flash. Era flash Planning Commission → 15 Mar 1950 executive reso · NDC → 6 Aug 1952 · Mahalanobis → 2nd plan · plan-holiday → 1966-69 · rolling plan → 1978-80 · last = 12th (2012-17). NITI flash 1 Jan 2015 cabinet resolution · GC = PM + CMs + UT-admins · 7 pillars · no fund allocation. Indices → SDG, Innovation, Water, Health, MPI (NFHS-based) · Aspirational Districts 2018/112 · Blocks 2023/500 · 3-Year Action Agenda · Viksit Bharat 2047. Transfer flash 14th FC → 42% · 15th FC → 41% (2021-26, N.K. Singh) · criteria → demography, forest, tax-effort. Cess-surcharge → outside pool · CSS → 60:40; 90:10 NE/Himalaya. Trap flash NITI = advisory (not statutory/constitutional) — both wrong options. Gadgil formula = plan-era assistance mechanism. 15th FC share = 41% (42% was the 14th). NITI MPI uses NFHS data (not consumption surveys).","chars":877},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/notes","file":"niti-aayog-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/detailed-notes/niti-aayog-detailed.html","title":"NITI Aayog & Planning Evolution — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"NITI Aayog and the Planning Evolution — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Plan era (1950-2017)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. NITI Aayog (1 Jan 2015)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Transfer architecture post-plan"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. From Planning Commission to NITI Aayog"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Composition and Structure"},{"level":2,"text":"8. What NITI Aayog Actually Produces"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Critiques and the Federalism Debate"}],"text":"NITI Aayog and the Planning Evolution — Detailed Notes From Five-Year Plans to cooperative-competitive federalism. Body-year-role anchors. 1. Plan era (1950-2017) Planning Commission (15 Mar 1950, executive resolution) — extra-constitutional, non-statutory; chair = PM; NDC (6 Aug 1952) = CMs + Union ministers forum. Model lineage: I (Harrod-Domar, agriculture), II (Mahalanobis heavy industry) , III (self-reliance), plan-holiday (1966-69), IV-V (Garibi Hatao), rolling plan (1978-80), VI-XII (reform era). Gadgil formula for central assistance; plan-transfers vs FC-devolution tension. 12th plan (2012-17) = last; critique: one-size-fits-all, input-based approvals. 2. NITI Aayog (1 Jan 2015) Cabinet resolution; Governing Council = PM + CMs + UT administrators (+ special invitees) ; regional councils; vice-chairperson (Suman Bery current era), CEO, members. No fund-allocation or PSU-evaluation","chars":8457},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/pyqs","file":"niti-aayog-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/pyqs/niti-aayog-mcqs.html","title":"Planning, NITI Aayog &amp; Growth Strategy — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Planning, NITI Aayog & Growth Strategy — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Planning, NITI Aayog & Growth Strategy — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about NITI Aayog: 1. It was created by an Act of Parliament in 2015. 2. Its Governing Council comprises the Prime Minister, Chief Ministers and Lieutenant Governors/administrators of Union Territories. 3. It allocates plan funds to states. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 only (c) 1 and 3 (d) 2 and 3 Q2. Match the Five-Year Plans with their features: 1. Second Plan — Mahalanobis heavy-industry strategy 2. Eighth Plan — launched after two years of annual plans and coincided with liberalisation 3. Twelfth Plan — theme \"faster, more inclusive and sustainable growth\" Which of the above are correctly matched? (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. The \"plan holiday\" in India's planning history refers to: (a) 1978–80 when t","chars":3196},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/short","file":"niti-aayog-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/niti-aayog/short-notes/niti-aayog-notes.html","title":"Planning, NITI Aayog &amp; Growth Strategy — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Planning, NITI Aayog & Growth Strategy — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Planning history"},{"level":2,"text":"Growth strategy & numbers"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Planning, NITI Aayog & Growth Strategy — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Planning & Growth. From Five-Year Plans to NITI Aayog, its indices and reports, and the numbers behind India's growth targets. Planning history Early plans : Visvesvaraya's Planned Economy for India (1934); National Planning Committee (1938, Nehru); Bombay Plan (1944, industrialists); People's Plan (M.N. Roy); Gandhian Plan (S.N. Agarwal); Sarvodaya Plan (Jayaprakash Narayan). Planning Commission set up by Cabinet resolution March 1950 (non-statutory, extra-constitutional; PM chairman; Deputy chairman first Gulzarilal Nanda; last Montek Singh Ahluwalia); National Development Council 1952. Five-Year Plans : 1st (1951–56; Harrod-Domar; agriculture; achieved 3.6% vs 2.1% target); 2nd (1956–61; Mahalanobis heavy-industry model; Bhilai/Rourkela/Durgapur steel) ; 3rd (1961–66; wars, drought → plan holidays 1966","chars":14494},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/planning-growth/short","file":"planning-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/planning-growth/short-notes/planning-prelims.html","title":"Planning, Growth &amp; Development — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Planning, Growth & Development — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Planning lineage"},{"level":2,"text":"Poverty lines — succession"},{"level":2,"text":"Employment measures"},{"level":2,"text":"Growth arithmetic anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"Human development indices"},{"level":2,"text":"Distribution picture"},{"level":2,"text":"Growth-with-jobs puzzle lines"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Planning, Growth & Development — Prelims Drill Planning lineage Planning Commission (1950 executive resolution; PM chair; NDC approved plans) → NITI Aayog 2015 (resolution — \"Think tank + cooperative federalism\" mandate; Governing Council = PM + CMs + LGs; no fund-allocation power (that moved to Finance Commission & line ministries)). Five-Year Plans history: 1st (1951-56, Harrod-Domar; target 2.1% achieved 3.6%); 2nd (Mahalanobis heavy-industry; 4.5% target; 4.3% achieved); 7th, 8th etc; 10th (8% target; 7.6/7.7 achieved); 11th (faster-inclusive; ~6.8/7.9 range achieved); 12th (2012-17; 8% target; ~6.5-7 achieved). 1951 → green revolution (mid-60s, HYV wheat/rice), 1970s poverty-line discourse, 1991 crisis-reform pivot (BoP crisis; IMF conditionalities; LPG liberalisation-privatisation-globalisation), 1997 Asian crisis insulated, 2008 GFC stimulus/deficit surge, 2020 COVID V-shape. Pove","chars":4182},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/pyqs","file":"economy-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/pyqs/economy-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Indian Economy — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Indian Economy — Prelims PYQs Macro-patterned; answers original; cross-check live CA before exam. Q1. Which correctly describes the components of revenue deficit ? (a) Total expenditure minus total receipts (b) Revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts (c) Fiscal deficit minus interest payments (d) Capital expenditure minus capital receipts Model answer: (b). Fiscal deficit uses total figures; (c) is primary deficit. Q2. The MPC's inflation target band as of March 2031 framework is: (a) 2% ± 1% (b) 4% ± 2% (c) 5% ± 1% (d) 6% point-target Model answer: (b). 4% ± 2% band, extended via 2025 government notification through FY30-31. Q3. Which tool drains liquidity without explicit collateral transfer to the RBI? (a) Repo (b) Reverse repo (c) Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) (d) MSF Model answer: (c). SDF (introduced April 2022) absorbs liquidity without collateral — floor of the LAF corridor.","chars":2988},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/bullets","file":"agriculture-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/bullet-points/agriculture-points.html","title":"Agricultural Economy — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agricultural Economy — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Share flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Price-market flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Input-tech flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Allied flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Agricultural Economy — Bullet Points Number-scheme flash. Share flash GVA ~18% vs jobs ~46% · holding 1.08 ha · marginal 86% · foodgrain 330+ mt · exports ~$50bn (rice #1). Edible-oil imports ~55-60% · horticulture value > foodgrain. Price-market flash MSP → CACP recommends (22+copra) · PM-AASHA 2018 = PSF + PDPS · C2 vs A2+50% debate. eNAM 2016 · FPO 2020 · repeal 2021 · model acts 2003/2018 · silo programme 2023. Input-tech flash NBS 2010 · nano-urea 2021 · PM-PRANAM 2023 · Bt-cotton only GM · SDN-1/2 exempt 2022. AgriStack + Krishi-DSS 2024 · Kisan e-Mitra · Namo Drone Didi · natural farming 2024. Allied flash Dairy → world #1 ~24% · Operation Flood 1970 · Kurien-NDDB · PMMSY 2020 fisheries · NBHM bees · MSDP bamboo. Trap flash Jobs share (46) > GVA share (18) — direction trap. CACP recommends, Cabinet notifies — verbs matter. PDPS = price-deficiency cash; PSF = physical procurement. ","chars":1000},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/notes","file":"agriculture-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/detailed-notes/agriculture-detailed.html","title":"Agricultural Economy — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agricultural Economy — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Sector profile"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Markets, prices, reforms"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Inputs, credit, allied"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Why Agriculture Dominates the Prelims Economy Section"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Cropping Seasons, Patterns and Classification"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Soils, Irrigation and the Water Constraint"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Prices, Procurement and Marketing"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Credit, Insurance and Institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Revolutions, Allied Sectors and Sustainability"}],"text":"Agricultural Economy — Detailed Notes Shares, market reforms, input stack, allied sectors. Number-bands + scheme-name recall (FY25 budget vintage). 1. Sector profile ~18% of GVA but ~46% of employment (PLFS) — productivity-gap headline; average holding ~1.08 ha; ~86% small-marginal (Agriculture Census) ; foodgrain ~330+ mt band; buffer norms (rice-wheat monthly); agri-exports ~$50bn — rice #1, marine, spices, sugar cycles. Cropping: rice-wheat dominance, pulses (MP-MH-RJ), oilseeds gap (edible-oil imports ~55-60%), sugarcane (FRP-SAP), cotton, horticulture value > foodgrain (~355 mt band). 2. Markets, prices, reforms MSP: CACP recommends (22 crops + copra), Cabinet notifies; procurement FCI-NAFED-NCCF; PM-AASHA (2018: PSF + PDPS arms) ; Swaminathan C2 vs A2+50% debate; 2020 farm acts repealed Nov 2021 . Reform arc: APMC model acts (2003, 2018), eNAM (2016; 1,380+ mandis) , FPO scheme (20","chars":11256},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/pyqs","file":"agriculture-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/pyqs/agriculture-mcqs.html","title":"Agriculture Economy — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture Economy — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Agriculture Economy — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider Minimum Support Prices: 1. MSP is recommended by the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices and approved by the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs. 2. MSP has statutory backing under the Essential Commodities Act. 3. The Fair and Remunerative Price for sugarcane is announced separately from MSP. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The MSP formula adopted since 2018–19 fixes MSP at 1.5 times the: (a) C2 cost (b) A2 cost (c) A2 + FL cost (d) Market price of the previous year Q3. Which of the following statements about India's agricultural production is/are correct? 1. Horticulture production has exceeded foodgrain production since 2012–13. 2. India is the world's largest producer of milk, pulses and jute. 3. India is the largest exporte","chars":2982},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/short","file":"agriculture-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/agriculture/short-notes/agriculture-notes.html","title":"Agriculture Economy — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Agriculture Economy — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Size & structure"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Agriculture Economy — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Sectors. Production numbers, MSP/procurement, credit, marketing reforms, subsidies, insurance and agri-trade — the economics side (physical geography of crops sits under Geography › Indian Geography › Soils & Agriculture). Size & structure Agriculture & allied GVA share ~16% (FY25) but 46.1% employment (PLFS 2023–24) ; growth FY25 4.6% (record kharif/rabi); allied sectors (livestock 30% of agri GVA, fisheries 7%) faster than crops; Agriculture Census 2015–16: 14.6 cr holdings; avg 1.08 ha; small & marginal 86% (47% area) ; 11th Agri Census 2021–22 ongoing (digital); foodgrain 2024–25 (4th AE): 354.6 MT record — rice 149.1, wheat 117.5, maize 42.3, pulses 25.2, oilseeds 42.6 MT; sugarcane 450 MT; cotton 30.7 mn bales; horticulture 367 MT (> foodgrain since 2012–13) ; India ranks : 1st milk (239 MT 2023–24; 25% world), pulses","chars":13935},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/bullets","file":"industry-msme-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/bullet-points/industry-msme-points.html","title":"Industry & MSME — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Industry & MSME — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Definition flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Credit flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Scheme flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Labour flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Industry & MSME — Bullet Points Definition-scheme flash. Definition flash Micro ≤1/5 · Small ≤10/50 · Medium ≤50/250 (₹ cr; 2025: investment 2.5x, turnover 2x) · Udyam 2020 · 45-day rule. Credit flash CGTMSE guarantee ₹5 cr (2023) · PMEGP · Mudra tiers · TReDS 3 platforms · ECLGS wound down. Scheme flash Vishwakarma 18 trades (2023) · ZED · SFURTI · PLI 14 · Make-in-India 2014 · NMP 2021 · Gati Shakti 2021 + NLP 2022. Labour flash 4 codes → Wages 2019 + IR-SS-OSH 2020 · staged rollout · e-Shram registry · B-READY = new WB index. Trap flash Composite test = investment AND turnover (2020 definitions). CGTMSE guarantees — government doesn't lend directly. PLI = 14 sectors (not 16/10). Labour codes enacted but not fully operational — phrasing trap. TReDS = buyer-receivable discounting platform.","chars":801},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/notes","file":"industry-msme-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/detailed-notes/industry-msme-detailed.html","title":"Industry & MSME — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Industry and MSME — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. MSME frame"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Industrial policy & manufacturing"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Industry and MSME — Detailed Notes MSME definitions, schemes, industrial-policy frame. Definition-table precision is the exam skill. 1. MSME frame Udyam registration (1 Jul 2020; self-declared online) ; composite definition (investment + turnover) : Micro ≤₹1 cr / ≤₹5 cr; Small ≤₹10 cr / ≤₹50 cr; Medium ≤₹50 cr / ≤₹250 cr — 2025 budget: limits raised (investment 2.5x, turnover 2x) ; 45-day payment rule (MSME Samadhaan); Udyam Assist for informal units. Credit: CGTMSE (collateral-free guarantee; ₹5 cr cover 2023) , PMEGP, Mudra tiers, TReDS invoice-discounting platforms (RXIL-M1xchange-Invoicemart), ECLGS wound down. Skill-quality: ZED certification , Lean clusters, tech centres; PM Vishwakarma (2023; 18 trades) ; SFURTI; Aatmanirbhar-era packages. 2. Industrial policy & manufacturing Lineage: IPR 1948-56 (licence-quota) → 1980s easing → NIP 1991 (de-licensing, 51% auto-FDI) → Make in Ind","chars":1639},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/pyqs","file":"industry-msme-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/pyqs/industry-msme-mcqs.html","title":"Industry, Manufacturing &amp; MSMEs — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Industry, Manufacturing & MSMEs — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Industry, Manufacturing & MSMEs — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Under the MSME classification effective 1 April 2025, a \"small\" enterprise is one with investment in plant and machinery not exceeding ₹25 crore and turnover not exceeding: (a) ₹50 crore (b) ₹100 crore (c) ₹250 crore (d) ₹500 crore Q2. Consider the Index of Industrial Production: 1. Manufacturing has a weight of about 78%. 2. It is compiled by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade. 3. Its base year is 2011–12. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Which of the following industries still require compulsory industrial licensing? 1. Cigarettes 2. Alcoholic drinks 3. Cement 4. Industrial explosives (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q4. Consider the four Labour Codes: 1. They subsume 29 central labou","chars":2927},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/short","file":"industry-msme-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/industry-msme/short-notes/industry-msme-notes.html","title":"Industry, Manufacturing &amp; MSMEs — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Industry, Manufacturing & MSMEs — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Policy timeline & structure"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Industry, Manufacturing & MSMEs — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Sectors. Industrial policy timeline, IIP, PLI/Make in India, MSME definitions & schemes, labour codes, key sector facts (steel, auto, textiles, pharma, electronics, semiconductors). Policy timeline & structure Industrial Policy Resolutions 1948 (mixed economy), 1956 (Schedule A/B/C; 'economic constitution'; Mahalanobis), 1977 (small-scale; Janata), 1980, 1991 New Industrial Policy (24 July; delicensing except 18 → now 4 (alcohol, cigarettes, hazardous chemicals, explosives (+defence/aerospace electronics via IDR Act); MRTP threshold removed; FDI 51% automatic 34 industries; public sector reserved 8 → 2 (atomic energy, railway operations)); New Industrial Policy (draft 2022–23, pending); Make in India (25 Sept 2014; 25 sectors; manufacturing 25% of GDP by 2025 — still ~17%) ; PLI (Mar 2020; 14 sectors ₹1.97 lakh ","chars":16077},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/bullets","file":"services-infrastructure-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/bullet-points/services-infrastructure-points.html","title":"Services & Infrastructure — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Services flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Pipeline flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Energy-urban flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Bullet Points Pipeline-anchor flash. Services flash ~55% GVA · IT-BPM ~8% GDP, $200bn exports · GCC 1,600+ · NASSCOM body. Tourism → SD 2.0 + PRASHAD · medical travel · LPI 38th · LEADS domestic · 7.5% logistics target. Pipeline flash NIP 2019 ₹111L cr · NMP ₹6L cr · NIIF 2015 · Gati Shakti Oct 2021 · capex ~3.4% GDP. DFC east+west · Bharatmala · UDAN · Vadhavan 2024 · NW-1 cargo. Energy-urban flash Surya Ghar 2024 (1 cr rooftops) · KUSUM pumps · RDSS 2021 · OSOWOG grid idea. AMRUT 2.0 · SBM-U 2.0 · PM-eBus 10k · SVAMITVA cards · CCTS 2023 carbon market. Trap flash NIP (pipeline) ≠ NMP (monetisation) — numbers and acronyms swap. LEADS = Indian survey; LPI = World Bank ranking. Gati Shakti (2021 platform) ≠ NLP (2022 policy document). UDAN = air connectivity, not highways. SVAMITVA = rural property; SVANidhi = vendor credit — name trap.","chars":875},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/notes","file":"services-infrastructure-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/detailed-notes/services-infrastructure-detailed.html","title":"Services & Infrastructure — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Services and Infrastructure — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Services economy"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Infrastructure pipeline"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Services and Infrastructure — Detailed Notes Services engine + infrastructure pipeline. Pipeline-number and scheme-name recall. 1. Services economy ~55% of GVA ; IT-BPM ~7.5-8% of GDP, exports ~$200bn (NASSCOM industry body); GCC boom (1,600+ centres) ; startups (3rd-largest ecosystem band); ONDC open network. Tourism: Swadesh Darshan 2.0 (destinations) + PRASHAD (pilgrimage) ; medical-value travel; hospitality rebound. Logistics: cost ~13-14% → 7.5% target; LPI 2023: India 38th ; LEADS = domestic survey; PM Gati Shakti multimodal layer. Finance-professional: GIFT IFSC, education-skill exports (NEP internationalisation). 2. Infrastructure pipeline NIP (2019): ₹111 lakh crore (FY20-25) ; NMP monetisation (₹6 lakh crore, 2022-25) ; NIIF funds (2015); PM Gati Shakti (Oct 2021; GIS master-plan) ; central capex thrust (~3.4% GDP band). Transport: DFCs Eastern + Western (largely operational) ,","chars":1571},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/pyqs","file":"services-infrastructure-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/pyqs/services-infrastructure-mcqs.html","title":"Services &amp; Infrastructure — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider India's power sector as of mid-2025: 1. Non-fossil sources account for about half of installed capacity. 2. Coal still contributes roughly 70% of electricity generation. 3. Installed solar capacity exceeds installed wind capacity. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Which of the following is India's first dedicated deep-water container transshipment port? (a) Vadhavan (b) Vizhinjam (c) Galathea Bay (d) Mundra Q3. Consider the Dedicated Freight Corridors: 1. The Eastern DFC runs from Ludhiana to Dankuni. 2. The Western DFC connects Dadri to Jawaharlal Nehru Port. 3. Both corridors were fully commissioned by 2020. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. The Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam, 2024 replaces the: (a) Airports Authori","chars":3241},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/short","file":"services-infrastructure-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/services-infrastructure/short-notes/services-infrastructure-notes.html","title":"Services &amp; Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Services"},{"level":2,"text":"Infrastructure"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Services & Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Economy › Sectors. Services (IT, tourism, telecom), and infrastructure — roads, rail, ports, airports, power, energy, urban — with programme names, numbers and regulators. Services ~55% of GVA, ~30% of employment; services exports $387 bn FY25 (India 7th largest services exporter; ~4.3% share) ; IT-BPM $283 bn (Nasscom FY25; exports $224 bn; 5.8 mn jobs; US 55%); GCCs 1,800+; Services PMI ~60 (2025) ; Trade in services surplus ~$190 bn offsets goods deficit ~$287 bn ; Mode 1–4 (GATS) ; Tourism : FTAs 9.95 mn 2024 (10.93 mn 2019 peak); forex $28 bn; domestic 2.5 bn visits; Tourism 5% GDP, 7.6 cr jobs (WTTC) ; Swadesh Darshan 2.0, PRASHAD (Pilgrimage), Dekho Apna Desh, Chalo India, e-Visa 172 countries, 'Iconic 50 destinations (Budget 2025 — states' challenge mode)', 'Heal in India', 'Travel for LiFE', 'India ranked 39 in WEF Tra","chars":23116},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/short","file":"sectors-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/sectors-indian-economy/short-notes/sectors-prelims.html","title":"Sectors of the Indian Economy — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectors of the Indian Economy — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Three-sector shares (GVA)"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Industry pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Services pack"},{"level":2,"text":"Infra/energy flash"}],"text":"Sectors of the Indian Economy — Prelims Drill Three-sector shares (GVA) Services ~55% · Industry ~27% · Agriculture & allied ~18% — but employment share inverts: agriculture still employs the largest slice (~45%) (PLFS 2023-24 band). Agriculture pack Crops: staging (Kharif/Rabi/Zaid), MSP — 23 crops (14 Kharif + 6 Rabi + 2 commercial + Toria/copra etc count variants); fixing formula 1.5 × A2+FL (paid-out costs + family labour) per Budget-2018 line; announced by CCEA on CACP recommendation . NFSA 2013 : priority-household entitlement 5 kg/person/month; Antyodaya 35 kg/family/month; coverage up to 75% rural / 50% urban ; central issue prices (rice ₹3, wheat ₹2, coarse ₹1/kg; free grains via PMGKAY continuation through Dec 2023 → 5-year free run to 2028 announced ). Buffer norms + FCI economics; decentralised procurement states. Missions: PM-KISAN (₹6,000/yr) · PMFBY (2016; premium 2% khari","chars":4170},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/economy/short","file":"economy-prelims-revision.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/economy/short-notes/economy-prelims-revision.html","title":"Indian Economy — Prelims Rapid Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Economy — Prelims Rapid Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Macro anchor set"},{"level":2,"text":"Inflation mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"Money & banking"},{"level":2,"text":"Budget arithmetic"},{"level":2,"text":"Trade & external"},{"level":2,"text":"Programmes CDN tag"},{"level":2,"text":"Ranking-formats"},{"level":2,"text":"Sectors order drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Quick watch (2025–26)"}],"text":"Indian Economy — Prelims Rapid Revision Number-heavy recall; growth + inflation + fiscal ladder questions. Macro anchor set GDP base year 2011–12 (national accounts & IIP); WPI 2011–12; CPI combined 2012 base. GDP vs GNP: GDP = domestic territory; GNP adds NFIA (net factor income from abroad). GVA vs GDP: GDP = ΣGVA + net product taxes. Fiscal deficit = total expenditure − non-debt receipts; primary = fiscal − interest; revenue deficit = revenue exp − revenue receipts. Inflation mechanics CPI-C headline target band 4% ± 2% (MPC, affirmed to March 2031 ); WPI tracks wholesale. Demand-pull vs cost-push vs built-in (wage-price); stagflation = stagnation + inflation together. Core inflation = CPI minus food & fuel (sticky services); headline volatility from food basket weight ~45%. Money & banking Repo: overnight; MSF: marginal standing facility at repo+; SDF: standing deposit facility (2022","chars":3248},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/bullets","file":"acts-conventions-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/bullet-points/acts-conventions-points.html","title":"Acts & Conventions — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Acts & Conventions — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Acts flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Species-convention flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Chemical-waste flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Acts & Conventions — Bullet Points Instrument flash. Acts flash Water 1974 (CPCB-SPCB) · Air 1981 · EP 1986 umbrella (EIA 2006 sits here). WPA 1972 (Sched I-VI; 2022 CITES-align) · FCA 1980 → Van Adiniyam 2023. BD 2002 (NBA; 2023 amend) · NGT 2010 tribunal. Species-convention flash Ramsar 1971 wetlands · CITES 1975 trade · CMS 1979 migratory · World Heritage 1972. CBD 1992 → Cartagena 2000 (biosafety) → Nagoya 2010 (ABS) → Kunming-Montreal GBF 2022 (30×30). Chemical-waste flash Montreal 1987 ODS → Kigali 2016 HFC · Basel 1989 waste-trade (+2019 plastic) · Stockholm 2001 POPs. Rotterdam 1998 PIC · Minamata 2013 mercury · UNCCD 1994 land (LDN). Trap flash EP Act 1986 = umbrella (Bhopal aftermath) — not water act. Nagoya = ABS (not biosafety) — protocol pair. Basel regulates movement (not bans outright) — scope ask. UNCCD = desertification (not climate) — Rio-trio separation. Kigali sits IN","chars":938},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/notes","file":"acts-conventions-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/detailed-notes/acts-conventions-detailed.html","title":"Environmental Acts & Conventions — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Acts and Conventions — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Indian acts"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Global conventions"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Constitutional Basis of Environmental Law"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Major Indian Statutes"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Environmental Impact Assessment"},{"level":2,"text":"9. International Conventions"}],"text":"Environmental Acts and Conventions — Detailed Notes India's statutory stack + global convention grid. Instrument-year-scope triples. 1. Indian acts Water Act 1974 (first environment act; CPCB-SPCB creation); Air Act 1981 (1987 amendment: noise-air authority widening); EP Act 1986 (post-Bhopal umbrella act; sec 3 rule-making; EIA notification 1994→2006 under it) ; WPA 1972 (schedules; 2002 tightening; 2022 CITES-alignment); Forest Conservation Act 1980 → 2023 Van (Sanrakshan evam Samvardhan) Adiniyam (de-reservations: 100-km border strategic projects, zoos-ecotourism exemptions, forest-land definition narrowing — asked as changes). Biological Diversity Act 2002 (NBA-SBB-BMC; 2023 amendment: decriminalisation + AYUSH fast-track + foreign-controlled-company reclassification) ; NGT Act 2010 (tribunal); Wildlife-transit rules; EIA 2006: category A-B1-B2, scoping-EAC, public consultation, 4-st","chars":10862},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/pyqs","file":"acts-conventions-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/pyqs/acts-conventions-mcqs.html","title":"Environmental Acts &amp; International Conventions — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Acts & International Conventions — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Environmental Acts & International Conventions — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The National Green Tribunal has jurisdiction over civil cases arising under which of the following laws? 1. Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 2. Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 3. Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 4. Biological Diversity Act, 2002 (a) 1, 2 and 3 (b) 1, 3 and 4 (c) 2 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Which of the following were introduced by the Constitution (Forty-second Amendment) Act, 1976? 1. Article 48A directing the State to protect the environment 2. Fundamental duty under Article 51A(g) 3. Transfer of \"forests\" and \"wildlife\" to the Concurrent List (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Consider the following pairs of conventions and their subjects: 1. Rotterdam Convention — prior informed co","chars":3691},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/short","file":"acts-conventions-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/acts-conventions/short-notes/acts-conventions-notes.html","title":"Environmental Acts &amp; International Conventions — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Acts & International Conventions — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Indian legislation timeline"},{"level":2,"text":"International conventions ready-reckoner"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Environmental Acts & International Conventions — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Acts, Policies & Institutions. Domestic legislation timeline and the treaty ready-reckoner (year, place, purpose, India's status). Indian legislation timeline Year Act / instrument Core points 1927 Indian Forest Act Reserved, protected, village forests; still the base forest law; colonial revenue orientation 1960 Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act Animal Welfare Board (1962); Jallikattu — SC 2014 ban; state amendments (TN/Karnataka/Maharashtra 2017) upheld by SC Constitution Bench May 2023 1972 Wild Life (Protection) Act Schedules; NP/WLS/CR/CoR; CZA 1992; NBWL & NTCA (2002/2006); 2022 amendment — 4 schedules, CITES, invasive species; Art 48A & 51A(g) added by 42nd Amendment 1976; 'forests' & 'wildlife' moved to Concurrent List (1976) 1974 Water Act Art 252; CPCB/SPCBs; consent; 20","chars":13924},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/bullets","file":"institutions-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/bullet-points/institutions-points.html","title":"Environment Institutions — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment Institutions — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Regulator flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Statutory flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Mission flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Environment Institutions — Bullet Points Body-role flash. Regulator flash MoEFCC policy · CPCB-SPCB standards-consent · CAQM 2021 NCR (ex-EPCA). NGT 2010 tribunal → principal Delhi + zonals · appeal → SC · expert-judge mix. Statutory flash NTCA 2006 (tiger, ch-IVC) · NBA 2003 Chennai (ABS) · CZA 1992 (zoos). NMCG-NGC 2016 Ganga frame (PM chairs NGC) · NBWL PM-chaired · WII-FSI-ICFRE Dehradun cluster. BSI = flora survey · ZSI = fauna survey — pair. Mission flash NAPCC 2008 = 8 missions · GIM 2014 · Namami Gange 2014 · LiFE 2022 · CAMPA = compensatory afforestation purse. Trap flash Project Tiger (1973 scheme) ≠ NTCA (2006 body) — date pair. CPCB born under Water Act (1974), not EP Act. CAQM = NCR-region (not national) — scope trap. NGT = tribunal (not court); appeals lie to SC. BSI flora / ZSI fauna — letter-swap trap.","chars":829},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/notes","file":"institutions-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/detailed-notes/institutions-detailed.html","title":"Environmental Institutions — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Institutions — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Apex & regulators"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Statutory & scientific bodies"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Mission vehicles"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Environmental Institutions — Detailed Notes Ministry-regulator-tribunal-mission bodies and their statutory anchors. Body-role-year recall. 1. Apex & regulators MoEFCC (policy; carries UNFCCC-CBD portfolios; PS division); CPCB (Water Act 1974 statutory; functions widened by Air 1981) + SPCBs/PCCs — standards-monitoring-consent mechanism; CAQM (2021, commission for NCR-airshed; supersedes EPCA; statutory under EP Act 1986 frame) . NGT (2010 act; scheduled: principal bench New Delhi + zonal benches; judges + experts; appointment and term per the act; jurisdiction: substantial environment questions; appeals to Supreme Court; not strictly bound by CPC — enquiry-bound by natural justice principles) . 2. Statutory & scientific bodies NTCA (2006, Wildlife Act chapter IV-C; tiger conservation plans, core-buffer grammar) ; NBA (2003, Chennai; ABS approvals) ; National Ganga Council / NMCG (2016 Ri","chars":2240},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/pyqs","file":"institutions-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/pyqs/institutions-mcqs.html","title":"Environmental Institutions — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Institutions — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Environmental Institutions — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following pairs of bodies and their chairpersons: 1. National Board for Wildlife — Prime Minister 2. National Tiger Conservation Authority — Union Environment Minister 3. National Disaster Management Authority — Union Home Minister Which pairs are correctly matched? (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The Central Pollution Control Board was constituted under: (a) The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 (b) The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 (c) The Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 (d) An executive resolution of 1972 Q3. Which of the following bodies were created by amendments to the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972? 1. Central Zoo Authority 2. National Tiger Conservation Authority 3. Wildlife","chars":3233},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/short","file":"institutions-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/institutions/short-notes/institutions-notes.html","title":"Environmental Institutions: MoEFCC, CPCB, NGT, Wildlife Boards — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Institutions: MoEFCC, CPCB, NGT, Wildlife Boards — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Ministry level"},{"level":2,"text":"Statutory bodies (parent law → composition → function)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Environmental Institutions: MoEFCC, CPCB, NGT, Wildlife Boards — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Acts, Policies & Institutions. Who does what — statutory vs executive, parent law, chair, and the trap facts. Ministry level MoEFCC (1985 as MoEF; 'Climate Change' added 2014; Minister Bhupender Yadav; MoS Kirti Vardhan Singh); nodal for UNFCCC, CBD, UNCCD, CITES, Ramsar, Montreal; divisions/bodies under it : CPCB, FSI, WII, BSI, ZSI, ICFRE, IIFM, IPIRTI, GBPNIHE, NBA, CZA, NTCA, WCCB, NMCG (authority), NAEB, Animal Welfare Board, GEAC, FAC, NBWL (Standing Committee), National Afforestation & Eco-development Board, ENVIS, Green Skill , PARIVESH (2018; single-window clearances) , Directorate of Forest Education , Indira Gandhi National Forest Academy (Dehradun — IFS training) , Indian Forest Service (1966; All-India Service under Art 312 — created via 1966 Act; cadre c","chars":8521},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/short","file":"acts-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/acts-policies-env/short-notes/acts-prelims.html","title":"Environmental Acts &amp; Institutions — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Acts & Institutions — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Acts timeline (chronological spine)"},{"level":2,"text":"Convention ledger (city-year pairing)"},{"level":2,"text":"Committees & awards"},{"level":2,"text":"Institutions quicks"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Environmental Acts & Institutions — Prelims Drill Acts timeline (chronological spine) Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972 — schedules I–VI (2022 amendment rationalised them into four: I & II animal-protection tiers, III plants, IV vermin — provisions took effect April 2023); protected areas NPs/WLS; community reserves; the NBWL is chaired by the PM. Water (Prevention & Control of Pollution) Act 1974 — CPCB/SPCBs born; consent-to-operate regime. Air (Prevention & Control of Pollution) Act 1981 . Environment (Protection) Act 1986 — post-Bhopal umbrella; Section 5 powers (directions incl closure), Section 3 central rules engine; EIA/CRZ/waste rules hang under it. Public Liability Insurance Act 1991 (immediate relief). Biological Diversity Act 2002 — NBA Chennai + SBBs + BMCs ; benefit-sharing; 2023 amendment relaxed penalties→monetary exemptions debate. Scheduled Tribes & Other Traditional Fores","chars":4369},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/bullets","file":"biodiversity-india-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/bullet-points/biodiversity-india-points.html","title":"Biodiversity India — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biodiversity India — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Hotspot flash"},{"level":2,"text":"IUCN flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Law flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Biodiversity India — Bullet Points Hotspot-term flash. Hotspot flash 4 hotspots → Himalaya · Indo-Burma · Western Ghats-Sri Lanka · Sundaland (Nicobar). Megadiverse 17-18 club · endemism peaks: Ghats amphibians, Himalaya flora. Invasives → Lantana, water hyacinth, Prosopis juliflora. IUCN flash EX → EW → CR → EN → VU → NT → LC (+DD) · threatened = CR+EN+VU. GIB-vulture CR · tiger EN · snow leopard VU · criteria = decline-range-fragmentation. Red List ≠ Red Data Book. Law flash BD Act 2002 → NBA (Chennai) + SBB + BMC · PBR at BMC level. 2023 amendment → decriminalised, AYUSH fast-track, adjudication. Nagoya 2010 = ABS · in-situ triad = park-sanctuary-biosphere · ex-situ = NBPGR-zoo-cryo. Trap flash Nicobar → Sundaland (not Indo-Burma) — hotspot trap. NT ≠ threatened — category ladder ask. NBA seat = Chennai (not Delhi). FRA 2006 ≠ BD Act 2002 — act-pair (MFP vs ABS). Keystone = functional","chars":934},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/notes","file":"biodiversity-india-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/detailed-notes/biodiversity-india-detailed.html","title":"Biodiversity & India — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biodiversity and India — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. India's biodiversity profile"},{"level":2,"text":"2. IUCN framework"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Legal & institutional frame"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Biodiversity and India — Detailed Notes Hotspots, species-terms, IUCN categories, legal-institutional frame (2002 Act + 2023 amendment). 1. India's biodiversity profile Megadiverse country (~17 of world's stated species on 2.4% land — figure bands vary; 4/36 global hotspots): Himalaya, Indo-Burma, Western Ghats-Sri Lanka, Sundaland (Nicobar group) ; high endemism in Ghats (amphibians-reptiles) + Himalaya; India: ~45,000+ plant, ~91,000+ animal species described (recall bands, not decimals). Species-role terms: keystone (disproportionate trophic effect — sea otter-tiger) , flagship (tiger-panda PR), umbrella (habitat-wide protection), indicator (amphibians-lichens for pollution), endemic (restricted range), invasive (Lantana-Water hyacinth-Prosopis). 2. IUCN framework Red List categories: Extinct → Extinct in Wild → Critically Endangered → Endangered → Vulnerable → Near Threatened → Least","chars":2463},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/pyqs","file":"biodiversity-india-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/pyqs/biodiversity-india-mcqs.html","title":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots &amp; Protected Areas — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots & Protected Areas — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots & Protected Areas — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following global biodiversity hotspots extend into Indian territory? 1. Himalaya 2. Indo-Burma 3. Sundaland 4. Western Ghats–Sri Lanka (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 4 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Consider the following statements: 1. A National Park cannot have its boundaries altered except by a resolution of the State Legislature. 2. Grazing of livestock can be permitted inside a Wildlife Sanctuary by the Chief Wildlife Warden. 3. Community Reserves are declared on land owned by the government. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the following superlatives: 1. Largest National Park — Hemis 2. First Biosphere Reserve — Nilgiri 3. Largest Tiger Reserve — Nagarjunsagar-Srisailam 4. State with most Ramsa","chars":3230},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/short","file":"biodiversity-india-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/biodiversity-india/short-notes/biodiversity-india-notes.html","title":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots &amp; Protected Areas — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots & Protected Areas — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Biodiversity basics"},{"level":2,"text":"Protected area network (WII ENVIS, 2025)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Biodiversity of India: Hotspots & Protected Areas — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Ecology & Biodiversity. Numbers, categories and the \"which is where\" facts that recur in every prelims. Biodiversity basics Biodiversity (Walter Rosen 1985; E.O. Wilson popularised) — genetic, species, ecosystem levels; alpha (within habitat), beta (between habitats), gamma (landscape). Species–area relationship (Alexander von Humboldt; log S = log C + Z log A; Z 0.1–0.2 for small areas, 0.6–1.2 for continents). Latitudinal gradient — tropics richer (Amazon ~40,000 plant species). Rivet popper hypothesis (Paul Ehrlich). Evil quartet of extinction (habitat loss/fragmentation, over-exploitation, alien invasion, co-extinction). Sixth mass extinction (Anthropocene); IUCN: >47,000 species threatened (2025; 28% assessed); Living Planet Index 2024: −73% since 1970 (WWF). India : 2.4% of ","chars":12323},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/bullets","file":"ecosystems-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/bullet-points/ecosystems-points.html","title":"Ecosystems — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ecosystems — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Levels flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Function flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Aquatic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Ecosystems — Bullet Points Concept flash. Levels flash Organism → population → community → ecosystem → biome → biosphere. Ecotone = transition zone · edge effect · niche = functional role · habitat = address. Function flash Energy one-way 10% (Lindeman) · nutrients cycle · grazing vs detritus chain. Pyramids: energy always upright · number/biomass may invert. Gaseous: C-N-O · sedimentary: P-S · P has no gas phase. Succession: lichen pioneer → climax · primary vs secondary · hydrarch vs xerarch. Aquatic flash GPP − R = NPP · NPP top = reefs-tropical-estuary-mangrove · open ocean low per-unit. Ramsar ~90 sites India (Asia-top) · mangrove: vivipary + pneumatophores · coral: zooxanthellae bleaching. Trap flash Nutrients cycle; energy does not — one-way trap. P = sedimentary only (no significant atmospheric pool). Open ocean = low productivity despite size — area-vs-rate trap. Bleaching ≠ imm","chars":1011},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/notes","file":"ecosystems-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/detailed-notes/ecosystems-detailed.html","title":"Ecosystems: Structure & Function — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ecosystems — Structure and Function"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Structural levels"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Energy flow & nutrient cycling"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Productivity & aquatic systems"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Structure of an Ecosystem"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Energy Flow — the Laws That Govern It"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Biogeochemical Cycles"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Succession, Interactions and Major Ecosystem Types"}],"text":"Ecosystems — Structure and Function Levels, energy-nutrient logic, succession, productivity, aquatic ecosystems. Concept-pair recall. 1. Structural levels Organism → population → community → ecosystem (biotic + abiotic interacting) → biome (large climatic region) → biosphere. Ecotone = transition zone between ecosystems (mangrove-estuary, grassland-forest edge) with edge effect ; ecological niche = functional role (habitat = address, niche = profession). Components: producers (autotrophs), consumers (herbivore-carnivore-omnivore; trophic levels), decomposers (fungi-bacteria — mineralisers), abiotic (inorganic-organic-climatic). 2. Energy flow & nutrient cycling Energy: one-way, ~10% transfer between trophic levels (Lindeman) ; food chain (grazing vs detritus) + food web stability ; energy pyramids always upright (biomass-number pyramids can invert — parasitic-tree chains). Nutrient cycle","chars":10776},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/pyqs","file":"ecosystems-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/pyqs/ecosystems-mcqs.html","title":"Ecosystems, Food Chains &amp; Nutrient Cycles — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ecosystems, Food Chains & Nutrient Cycles — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Ecosystems, Food Chains & Nutrient Cycles — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following ecological pyramids can be inverted? 1. Pyramid of energy 2. Pyramid of biomass in an aquatic ecosystem 3. Pyramid of numbers in a single large tree ecosystem (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Consider the following statements about the phosphorus cycle: 1. Unlike carbon and nitrogen, it has no significant gaseous phase. 2. Phosphorus is often the limiting nutrient in freshwater ecosystems. 3. Weathering of rocks is the main natural source of phosphates. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Which one of the following correctly sequences the steps of the nitrogen cycle by which ammonium is converted to atmospheric nitrogen? (a) Nitrification → assimilation → denitrification (b) Ammonificat","chars":3047},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/short","file":"ecosystems-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/ecosystems/short-notes/ecosystems-notes.html","title":"Ecosystems, Food Chains &amp; Nutrient Cycles — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ecosystems, Food Chains & Nutrient Cycles — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Basic concepts"},{"level":2,"text":"Energy flow & productivity"},{"level":2,"text":"Biogeochemical cycles"},{"level":2,"text":"Major ecosystem types (quick facts)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Ecosystems, Food Chains & Nutrient Cycles — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Ecology & Biodiversity. Core ecological concepts that UPSC tests through statement-based questions. Basic concepts Ecology (Haeckel 1866); ecosystem (Tansley 1935) = biotic + abiotic components interacting; biome = large regional ecosystem defined by climate and dominant vegetation (tundra, taiga, temperate deciduous, grassland, desert, tropical rainforest, savanna, chaparral); biosphere ; ecotone = transition zone (edge effect — higher diversity; e.g., mangroves, estuaries, grassland–forest edge); ecotype ; ecological niche (Grinnell/Elton/Hutchinson — functional role; Gause's competitive exclusion principle); habitat = address, niche = profession. Levels : individual → population → community → ecosystem → landscape → biome → biosphere. Population attributes : natality, mortality, sex ra","chars":9369},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/short","file":"ecology-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/short-notes/ecology-prelims.html","title":"Ecology &amp; Biodiversity — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ecology & Biodiversity — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Bio-foundations"},{"level":2,"text":"Conservation hotspots (global designation logic)"},{"level":2,"text":"Protected-area numbers (use ranges at exam)"},{"level":2,"text":"Species ↔ habitat pair-index"},{"level":2,"text":"IUCN status vocabulary"},{"level":2,"text":"Biodiversity institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"National statuses/memoranda"},{"level":2,"text":"Vegetation shorthand (Champion & Seth flavour)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Ecology & Biodiversity — Prelims Drill Bio-foundations Ecosystem functions: producers → consumers → decomposers; 10% energy-transfer law (Lindeman) ; productivity (GPP=NPP+respiration); food chains (grazing vs detritus) & webs. Biomes quick: tundra · taiga (boreal conifer) · temperate deciduous · Mediterranean · grassland · savanna · desert · tropical rainforest; India spans tropical forest→montane→desert. Conservation hotspots (global designation logic) Criteria (Conservation International): ≥1,500 endemic vascular plants + ≥70% habitat loss. India's four : (1) The Himalayas (eastern), (2) Indo-Burma, (3) Western Ghats & Sri Lanka, (4) Sundaland (Nicobar group). Protected-area numbers (use ranges at exam) National Parks ~107 · Wildlife Sanctuaries ~570+ · Conservation Reserves ~100 · Community Reserves ~200+ · Tiger Reserves 57+ (newest: Ratapani & Guru Ghasidas–Tamor Pingla cohort 2024","chars":4221},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/bullets","file":"species-conservation-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/bullet-points/species-conservation-points.html","title":"Species Conservation — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Species Conservation — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Project flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Legal flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Number flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Species Conservation — Bullet Points Project-flash. Project flash Tiger 1973 (NTCA 2006, 58 TRs) · Elephant 1992 (MIKE-Gaja) · Snow Leopard 2009 (GSLEP). Lion 2020 (Gir + Barda) · Dolphin 2021 (Gangetic + oceanic) · Cheetah 2022 (Kuno → Gandhi Sagar). Vulture plan (diclofenac ban 2006) · GIB Sam Jaisalmer · pygmy hog-sangai-gharial reintro pairs. Legal flash WPA 1972: Sch I-II highest · Sch V vermin · Sch VI plants · 2022 CITES alignment. CITES = trade permits (App I-III) · CMS = migratory · IBCA HQ India · GTF New Delhi. Number flash Tigers ~3,600+ (2022) · elephants ~30k · lions 600s · rhinos ~4k · Ramsar ~90 · TRs 58. Trap flash Cheetah = reintroduction (extinct 1952) — not relocation. MIKE = elephant mortality monitoring (not tiger). Schedule VI = plants (not 'least animals') — schedule ask. CMS: India = party — do not mark non-party. Lion 2020 vs Dolphin 2021 vs Cheetah 2022 — year ","chars":905},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/notes","file":"species-conservation-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/detailed-notes/species-conservation-detailed.html","title":"Species Conservation Programmes — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Species Conservation Programmes — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Flagship projects"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Legal instruments"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Protected Area Categories — Get the Legal Hierarchy Right"},{"level":2,"text":"7. IUCN, Red List and Conservation Status"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Species-Specific Conservation Projects"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Biodiversity Hotspots, Endemism and Threats"}],"text":"Species Conservation Programmes — Detailed Notes Flagship projects, legal schedules, treaties. Project-year-agency recall. 1. Flagship projects Project Tiger (1973; NTCA statutory 2006; 58 reserves 2025 band; M-STrIPES monitoring) ; Project Elephant (1992; MIKE + Gaja Yatra + corridor push) ; Project Snow Leopard (2009; GSLEP global) ; Project Lion (2020; Gir landscape + Barda expansion) ; Project Dolphin (2021; Gangetic + oceanic; Dolphin Day 5 Oct) ; Project Cheetah (2022; Kuno-Palpur translocation from Namibia-South Africa; Gandhi Sagar second site) ; vulture action plan (diclofenac ban 2006; multi-vulture centres); GIB breeding centre (Rajasthan, Jaisalmer Sam) . Results-recall band: tiger ~3,600+ (2022 AITE census), elephants ~30,000, lions ~670+ (2025 count band), rhino ~4,000 (Kaziranga core). 2. Legal instruments WPA 1972: Schedules I-VI (I-II highest protection; V = vermin; VI =","chars":9436},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/pyqs","file":"species-conservation-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/pyqs/species-conservation-mcqs.html","title":"Species in News &amp; Conservation Projects — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Species in News & Conservation Projects — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Species in News & Conservation Projects — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. In the IUCN Red List, which of the following categories together constitute \"threatened\" species? (a) Extinct in the Wild, Critically Endangered, Endangered (b) Critically Endangered, Endangered, Vulnerable (c) Endangered, Vulnerable, Near Threatened (d) Critically Endangered, Endangered, Vulnerable, Near Threatened Q2. Consider the following statements about CITES: 1. Appendix I species may be traded commercially under a quota system. 2. All eight species of pangolins are listed in Appendix I. 3. Red sanders of India is listed in Appendix II. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Which of the following species were downlisted (moved to a lower threat category) by IUCN in recent years? 1. Snow leopard 2. Greater adjutant stork 3. Grea","chars":3156},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/short","file":"species-conservation-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/ecology-biodiversity/species-conservation/short-notes/species-conservation-notes.html","title":"Species in News &amp; Conservation Projects — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Species in News & Conservation Projects — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Classification systems"},{"level":2,"text":"Species in news (2024–25 quick list)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Species in News & Conservation Projects — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Ecology & Biodiversity. IUCN categories, CITES appendices, WPA schedules and a ready-reckoner of species that appear in current affairs. Classification systems IUCN Red List (1964) : EX → EW → CR → EN → VU → NT → LC → DD → NE ; threatened = CR + EN + VU; criteria A–E (population decline, range, small population, very small, quantitative). Green Status of Species (2021) measures recovery. India CR examples : Great Indian Bustard, Bengal florican, Jerdon's courser, forest owlet (EN since 2018), white-rumped/Indian/slender-billed/red-headed vultures, gharial, pygmy hog (EN), Malabar civet, Namdapha flying squirrel, Kashmir stag (CR), sociable lapwing, spoon-billed sandpiper, Siberian crane, northern river terrapin, red-crowned roofed turtle, Kondana rat, large rock rat, hawksbill turtle, Sumat","chars":15858},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/bullets","file":"climate-change-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/bullet-points/climate-change-points.html","title":"Climate Change — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate Change — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Treaty flash"},{"level":2,"text":"India flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Science flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Climate Change — Bullet Points Regime flash. Treaty flash UNFCCC 1992 → Kyoto 1997 (CDM-JI-ET) → Paris 2015 (NDC + ratchet + GST). COP chain → Glasgow 2021 (phase-down) → Sharm 2022 (loss-damage) → Dubai 2023 (transition away) → Baku 2024 (NCQG $300bn) → Belém 2025 next. Art 6 = carbon markets · CBDR-RC = equity principle. India flash Panchamrit → 500 GW · 50% RE · 1 bn t cut · 45% intensity · net-zero 2070. ISA Gurugram · CDRI 2019 · LiFE 2022 · LeadIT with Sweden · green H2 mission. CCTS 2023 (EC Act amendment 2022) · PAT legacy · sovereign green bonds 2022-23. Science flash IPCC 1988 · AR6 2021-23 · WG I-II-III trio · methane GWP horizon-dependent. Tipping: AMOC-permafrost-ice sheets · acidification = CO2 uptake · SLR = expansion + melt. Trap flash Kyoto = binding (Annex-I only); Paris = pledge-review — regime-type pair. 'Phase-down' coal (Glasgow), not 'phase-out' — wording trap. Net","chars":1039},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/notes","file":"climate-change-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/detailed-notes/climate-change-detailed.html","title":"Climate Change Regime — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate Change Regime — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Treaty architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Science & assessment"},{"level":2,"text":"3. India: NDC, Panchamrit, markets"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Climate Change Regime — Detailed Notes UNFCCC → Kyoto → Paris chain, IPCC basics, COP milestones, India's NDC-Panchamrit, carbon markets. 1. Treaty architecture UNFCCC (Rio 1992; entered force 1994; Annex-I developed vs Non-Annex-I; CBDR-RC principle) ; Kyoto Protocol (1997; 2005 force; binding targets for Annex-I; flexible mechanisms: CDM, JI, emissions trading; first period 2008-12, second 2013-20 Doha) ; Paris Agreement (2015; force 2016; 'well below 2°C, pursue 1.5°C'; NDCs every 5 years; global stocktake every 5 years; ratchet mechanism; art 6 market mechanisms) . COP milestones: COP21 Paris ; COP26 Glasgow (2021: Glasgow Climate Pact — 'phase-down' unabated coal, phase-out inefficient subsidies, rulebook completion, art-6 operationalised) ; COP27 Sharm (2022: loss-and-damage fund agreed) ; COP28 Dubai (2023: first Global Stocktake — 'transition away from fossil fuels', tripling ren","chars":2888},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/pyqs","file":"climate-change-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/pyqs/climate-change-mcqs.html","title":"Climate Change, IPCC &amp; Carbon Markets — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate Change, IPCC & Carbon Markets — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Climate Change, IPCC & Carbon Markets — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change: 1. It was established jointly by WMO and UNEP in 1988. 2. It conducts its own original climate research. 3. Its Sixth Assessment cycle concluded with a Synthesis Report in 2023. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Which of the following greenhouse gases also depletes stratospheric ozone? (a) Methane (b) Nitrous oxide (c) Sulphur hexafluoride (d) Carbon dioxide Q3. India's updated Nationally Determined Contribution (2022) commits to which of the following by 2030? 1. Reducing the emissions intensity of GDP by 45% from 2005 levels 2. Achieving 50% of cumulative installed electric power capacity from non-fossil sources 3. Reaching net-zero emissions (a","chars":3453},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/short","file":"climate-change-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/climate-change/short-notes/climate-change-notes.html","title":"Climate Change, IPCC &amp; Carbon Markets — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate Change, IPCC & Carbon Markets — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Science basics"},{"level":2,"text":"IPCC & international regime"},{"level":2,"text":"India's policy & carbon markets"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Climate Change, IPCC & Carbon Markets — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Environmental Issues. Science, negotiations, India's commitments and the market mechanisms that dominate current-affairs questions. Science basics Greenhouse effect (Fourier 1824; Tyndall; Arrhenius 1896); GHGs — water vapour (largest natural), CO₂ (~76% of anthropogenic forcing; 425 ppm 2025 vs 280 pre-industrial; Mauna Loa Keeling curve) , CH₄ (GWP-100 ~28–30; 84 over 20 yr; 1,930 ppb; sources — wetlands, rice, livestock enteric fermentation, landfills, fossil leaks; Global Methane Pledge 2021 (30% cut by 2030; India not signatory)) , N₂O (GWP ~273; fertiliser; ozone-depleting) , fluorinated gases (HFCs GWP thousands — Kigali; SF₆ GWP 24,300 highest; PFCs; NF₃) , ozone (tropospheric — a GHG; stratospheric — protective) ; black carbon (short-lived climate pollutant; not a gas); aerosols cool","chars":25088},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/bullets","file":"pollution-waste-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/bullet-points/pollution-waste-points.html","title":"Pollution & Waste — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Pollution & Waste — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Air flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Water-soil-noise flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Waste flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Pollution & Waste — Bullet Points Rule-year flash. Air flash AQI 6 bands × 8 pollutants · NCAP 2019 (131 cities, 40% by 2026) · CAQM 2021 NCR · GRAP I-IV. PM2.5 deep-lung · SAFAR forecast · CAAQMS monitors · stubble-decomposer push. Water-soil-noise flash DO-BOD-COD + coliform · 351 priority stretches (2022 band) · eutrophication-HABs. As-F groundwater belts · Noise Rules 2000 (silence 100 m; night lower dB). Waste flash SWM 2016 segregation · PWM 2021-22: SUP ban 1 Jul 2022 + 4-category EPR. E-Waste 2022 EPR targets · BMW 2016 CBWTF · Batteries 2022 EPR · C&D 2016. Remedies → bio vs phyto remediation · WtE · circular-economy baskets. Trap flash NCAP (plan) vs CAQM (statutory body) — instrument-type pair. SUP ban = 19 items (not all plastics) — scope trap. DO inversely related to BOD — direction trap. E-waste rule year = 2022 (not 2016) — rule-date swap. Silence zone = 100 m radius (hosp","chars":937},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/notes","file":"pollution-waste-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/detailed-notes/pollution-waste-detailed.html","title":"Pollution & Waste Management — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Pollution and Waste Management — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Air pollution"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Water, soil, noise"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Waste rules stack (2016-22 generation)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Air Pollution — Pollutants, Standards and Sources"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Water and Soil Pollution"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Solid, Plastic, E-Waste and Hazardous Waste"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Institutions, Instruments and Critique"}],"text":"Pollution and Waste Management — Detailed Notes Air-water-soil-noise pollution frames + India's waste-rule stack. Rule-year-EPR recall. 1. Air pollution AQI (CPCB 2014-15): 6 bands good-severe on 8 pollutants; PM2.5-PM10-O3-NO2-SO2-CO-NH3-Pb ; health: PM2.5 deep-lung penetration; NCAP (2019; 2022-26 target ~40% PM reduction in 131 non-attainment cities; PRANA portal, city action plans) ; CAQM (2021 statutory, NCR-adjoining airshed; Graded Response Action Plan stages I-IV) ; sources-stubs: Delhi winter inversion, farm stubble (bio-decomposer push). Tools: SAFAR forecasts, continuous monitoring CAAQMS, satellite assimilation; indoor air + ASHRAE-type guidance skip; odd-even, anti-smog guns, cloud-seeding pilots (2025) = response arsenal. 2. Water, soil, noise Water-quality trio: DO-BOD-COD + faecal coliform ; CPCB priority polluted river stretches (351 band 2022 list) ; eutrophication-alga","chars":11397},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/pyqs","file":"pollution-waste-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/pyqs/pollution-waste-mcqs.html","title":"Pollution, Waste Management &amp; EIA — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Pollution, Waste Management & EIA — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Pollution, Waste Management & EIA — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following are secondary air pollutants? 1. Ground-level ozone 2. Sulphur dioxide 3. Peroxyacetyl nitrate 4. Carbon monoxide (a) 1 and 3 (b) 2 and 4 (c) 1, 2 and 3 (d) 1, 3 and 4 Q2. Consider the following statements about the Commission for Air Quality Management: 1. It is a statutory body established in 2021. 2. Its jurisdiction covers the National Capital Region and adjoining areas. 3. Stage IV of the Graded Response Action Plan is triggered when the AQI exceeds 450. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016, discarded sharps such as needles and scalpels are to be disposed of in: (a) Yellow bags (b) Red bags (c) White translucent puncture-proof containers (d) Blue cardboard boxes","chars":3377},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/short","file":"pollution-waste-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/pollution-waste/short-notes/pollution-waste-notes.html","title":"Pollution, Waste Management &amp; EIA — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Pollution, Waste Management & EIA — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Air pollution"},{"level":2,"text":"Waste rules (2016 family + updates)"},{"level":2,"text":"Environmental Impact Assessment"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Pollution, Waste Management & EIA — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Environment & Ecology › Environmental Issues. Air, water, soil and noise pollution; the waste rules; and the environmental clearance process. Air pollution Pollutants : primary (SO₂, NOₓ, CO, PM, VOCs, NH₃, Pb) vs secondary (ground-level ozone (NOₓ + VOC + sunlight), PAN, sulphate/nitrate aerosols, smog); PM₂.₅ (≤2.5 µm; combustion; deepest lung penetration) vs PM₁₀ ; NAAQS 2009 (12 pollutants incl. benzene, BaP, As, Ni, NH₃, O₃, Pb; PM₂.₅ annual 40 µg/m³, 24-hr 60; WHO 2021 guideline 5/15) — revision drafted 2025; AQI (2014; 'One Number One Colour One Description'; 8 pollutants; 0–50 good … 401–500 severe; IITK developed) ; SAFAR (IITM Pune) ; National Clean Air Programme (2019; 131 non-attainment cities; target 20–30% → revised 40% PM₁₀ reduction by 2025–26 vs 2017; ₹11,000 cr plus FC-15 air-quality grants for million","chars":25208},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/short","file":"issues-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/environmental-issues/short-notes/issues-prelims.html","title":"Environmental Issues — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environmental Issues — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate change mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"Ozone & air"},{"level":2,"text":"Water & waste"},{"level":2,"text":"National programmes"},{"level":2,"text":"Nuclear & radiation basics"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Environmental Issues — Prelims Drill Climate change mechanics GHGs: CO2 · CH4 (GWP-100 ≈ 27–30) · N2O (≈273) · HFCs · PFCs · SF6 · NF3 (Kyoto basket+ Montreal-siblings); water vapour = feedback not anthropogenic forcing. IPCC: formed 1988 (WMO+UNEP); AR6 (2021–23) : observed ~1.1°C warming; likely 1.5°C breach-window early-2030s on current policies; WG I (physical), II (impacts), III (mitigation). UNFCCC: launched Rio-1992 → Kyoto Protocol 1997 (CDM/JI/ET mechanisms; Doha amendment) → Paris 2015 (NDCs, net-zero language, 5-year ratchet, GST cycles). COP beats: COP26 Glasgow 2021 (Methane Pledge 100+ signatories; \"phase down\" coal wording) → COP27 Sharm el-Sheikh 2022 (Loss & Damage fund agreed) → COP28 Dubai 2023 (first Global Stocktake; \"transition away from fossil fuels\"; L&D operationalised) → COP29 Baku 2024 (NCQG $300 bn/yr-by-2035 vs $1.3 tn ask; India registered dissent at adoptio","chars":4026},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/pyqs","file":"environment-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/pyqs/environment-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Environment &amp; Ecology — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Ecology — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Environment & Ecology — Prelims PYQs Q1. [PYQ-pattern] Which of the following is under \"Right to environment\" interpretation lineage? (a) Art 48A only (b) Art 21 via M C Mehta line + Art 48A/51A(g) (c) Fundamental Duties only (d) Art 19(1)(a) Model answer: (b). Judicially read into Art 21; restated by M K Ranjitsinh (2024) via Arts 14+21. Q2. The Keoladeo National Park vs Montreux Record: (a) First Indian site; listed 1981, Montreux 1990 due water shortage (b) listed 1981; NOT on Montreux (c) world's highest Ramsar elevation (d) marine category Model answer: (a). It was placed on Montreux Record in 1990 — classic shade trap. Q3. Which hydrologic feature corresponds to the Bhagirathi-Hooghly formation? (a) Ganga distributary below Farakka (b) Yamuna tributary (c) Brahmaputra channel (d) Indus canal Model answer: (a). Downstream distributary story; Farakka barrage moment. Q4. Consider pair","chars":2736},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/environment-ecology/short","file":"environment-prelims-revision.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/environment-ecology/short-notes/environment-prelims-revision.html","title":"Environment &amp; Ecology — Prelims Rapid Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Environment & Ecology — Prelims Rapid Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Conventions quick-pair"},{"level":2,"text":"IUCN categories & Red List"},{"level":2,"text":"Species-state pairing drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Vegetation-biome shorthand"},{"level":2,"text":"Protected network quick count"},{"level":2,"text":"Pollution & waste rules"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate set"},{"level":2,"text":"Multilateral environmental-agreement fallbacks"}],"text":"Environment & Ecology — Prelims Rapid Revision Species/state/conservation pairing floods this area. Pair with Mains env set for narrative. Conventions quick-pair Ramsar (1971) — wetlands; Montreux Record = threatened register. CITES (Washington 1973) — flora/fauna trade; app I/II/III gradation; CoP19 Panama 2022; CoP20 2025 Samarkand, Uzbekistan . CBD (Rio 1992) — with Nagoya ABS (2010) , Cartagena biosafety (2000) , Kunming-Montreal GBF (2022, 30×30) . Bonn CMS (1979) — migratory species (India hosted CoP13 Gandhinagar 2020). UNFCCC (Rio 1992) + Kyoto + Paris. UNCCD Bonn 1994 — India COP14 (2019, New Delhi NCR) land degradation neutrality goal 2030. Stockholm Convention (POPs, 2001); Rotterdam PIC (1998); Basel (Waste 1989); Minamata (Mercury 2013 — Japan city). Montreal Protocol 1987 (+Kigali 2016 HFC amendment; India ratified Kigali 2021). VCLT not environmental. IUCN categories & Red","chars":3363},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/bullets","file":"climate-india-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/bullet-points/climate-india-points.html","title":"Indian Climate — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Climate — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Winter flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Summer flash"},{"level":2,"text":"SW monsoon flash"},{"level":2,"text":"NE monsoon flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Belt flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Indian Climate — Bullet Points Season-anchor flash. Winter flash NE monsoon = dry over India · western disturbances → NW winter rain-snow (rabi aid) · subtropical westerly jet drives WDs · cold waves-fog north plains. Summer flash Loo → NW heat winds · kalbaisakhi → Bengal-Assam thunder (tea) · mango showers → Kerala-Karnataka · pre-monsoon cyclones → Bay-Sri Lanka belt. SW monsoon flash Onset Kerala ~1 Jun · Arabian branch → W-Ghats windward deluge · Bay branch → NE (Mawsynram) · rain-shadow → leeward Deccan interior. Breaks → trough to foothills → plains dry, NE rain · Somali jet + Tibetan low + ITCZ = drivers. NE monsoon flash October heat + withdrawal NW→SE · TN coast = main beneficiary · Bay cyclones peak Oct-Dec (Odisha-AP-TN). Belt flash Highest rain → Mawsynram (Meghalaya) · driest → Ladakh-W Rajasthan-Kutch · Cwb island → Shillong · Am → Kerala coast. LPA ~87 cm; normal = 96-104","chars":1179},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/notes","file":"climate-india-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/detailed-notes/climate-india-detailed.html","title":"Indian Climate & Seasons — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Climate and Seasons — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The four seasons (IMD frame)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Rain-belt map & variability"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Why India Has a Monsoon Climate"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Four Seasons in Detail"},{"level":3,"text":"Cold weather season (December–February)"},{"level":3,"text":"Hot weather season (March–May)"},{"level":3,"text":"South-west monsoon / advancing monsoon (June–September)"},{"level":3,"text":"Retreating monsoon (October–November)"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Rainfall Distribution and Variability"},{"level":2,"text":"9. ENSO, IOD and the Drivers of a Good or Bad Monsoon"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Climatic Regions and Climate Change"}],"text":"Indian Climate and Seasons — Detailed Notes Four-season cycle + rain-belt geography. IMD terminology, jet-stream mechanics, cyclone seasonality. 1. The four seasons (IMD frame) Cold weather (Dec-Feb) : NE monsoon dry; western disturbances (Mediterranean lows along subtropical westerly jet) → NW rain-snow (rabi aid) ; cold waves-fog; Jaisalmer-Bikaner day-warm nights-cold contrast. Hot weather (Mar-May) : loo (NW dust winds), thunderstorms — kalbaisakhi/Nor'westers (Bengal-Assam tea aid) , mango showers (Kerala-Karnataka pre-monsoon) , pre-monsoon cyclones (Sri Lanka-Bay belt). SW monsoon (Jun-Sep) : onset Kerala ~1 Jun (±4 days now rule); two arms: Arabian Sea branch (Western Ghats orographic, Mumbai-Delhi track) + Bay branch (NE India, Mawsynram uplift) ; monsoon trough shifts (breaks = trough to foothills → plains dry, NE wet); rain-shadow: leeward Maharashtra-Karnataka interior . Retr","chars":10599},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/pyqs","file":"climate-india-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/pyqs/climate-india-mcqs.html","title":"Climate of India — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate of India — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Climate of India — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Western disturbances that bring winter rain to north-west India originate over the: (a) Bay of Bengal (b) Arabian Sea (c) Mediterranean Sea (d) Caspian Sea Q2. Consider the Arabian Sea branch of the monsoon: 1. It causes heavy rain on the windward Western Ghats. 2. The Aravallis lie parallel to it, so Rajasthan receives little rain. 3. It reaches Delhi before the Bay of Bengal branch. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Tamil Nadu receives most of its annual rainfall during: (a) June–September (b) October–December (c) January–February (d) March–May Q4. Kalbaisakhi refers to: (a) Pre-monsoon dust storms of Uttar Pradesh (b) Pre-monsoon thunderstorms of West Bengal and Assam (c) Winter fog of Punjab (d) Retreating-monsoon cyclones of Odisha Q5. As per the re","chars":2072},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/short","file":"climate-india-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/climate-india/short-notes/climate-india-notes.html","title":"Climate of India: Monsoon Mechanism, Seasons &amp; Rainfall Distribution — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climate of India: Monsoon Mechanism, Seasons & Rainfall Distribution — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Controls"},{"level":2,"text":"Seasons (IMD)"},{"level":2,"text":"Rainfall distribution"},{"level":2,"text":"IMD & forecasting"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate change signals (India)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Climate of India: Monsoon Mechanism, Seasons & Rainfall Distribution — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. India-specific layer on top of the climatology card: seasons, rainfall map, drought/flood geography, IMD terminology. Controls Latitude (Tropic of Cancer bisects — tropical S, sub-tropical N), Himalaya (blocks Siberian cold; forces monsoon rain), land–sea contrast, altitude (Shimla vs Ludhiana), relief (Ghats windward/leeward), jet streams (STJ south of Himalaya in winter → western disturbances; TEJ in summer), pressure systems (Siberian high winter; Thar heat low summer), ENSO/IOD, distance from sea (continentality — Delhi range 20 °C+ vs Kochi 3 °C), currents negligible. Seasons (IMD) Winter (Jan–Feb) : NE trades; Siberian high; clear skies, inversion, frost; western disturbances (Mediterranean; via Iran-Afghanistan; 4–5/month; rabi rain Punjab-Haryana-U","chars":5781},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/bullets","file":"drainage-rivers-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/bullet-points/drainage-rivers-points.html","title":"Drainage & Rivers — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Drainage & Rivers — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Indus flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Ganga flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Brahmaputra flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Peninsular flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Dispute-dam flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Drainage & Rivers — Bullet Points River-anchor flash. Indus flash IWT 1960 → Ravi-Beas-Sutlej = India · Indus-Jhelum-Chenab = Pakistan · broker = World Bank. Jhelum source → Verinag · Sutlej entry → Shipki la · Indus rises near → Mansarovar (Tibet). Ganga flash Ganga born → Devprayag (Bhagirathi + Alaknanda) · largest trib → Yamuna (right bank). Left-bank trio → Ghaghara, Gandak, Kosi (sorrow of Bihar) · Son = south-bank, from Amarkantak. Distributary → Bhagirathi-Hooghly (Kolkata) · Tehri dam on Bhagirathi. Brahmaputra flash Tibet name → Tsangpo · India entry → Dihang (Arunachal) · Majuli = largest river island · Bangladesh name → Jamuna. Peninsular flash Largest peninsular → Godavari (Trimbakeshwar; Indravati trib) · Krishna → Mahabaleshwar (Tungabhadra-Bhima) · Kaveri → Talakaveri (Mettur; rice-bowl delta). Rift-valley west-flowers → Narmada (Amarkantak; Sardar Sarovar; Jabalpur marbl","chars":1694},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/notes","file":"drainage-rivers-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/detailed-notes/drainage-rivers-detailed.html","title":"Indian Drainage & Rivers — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Drainage and Rivers — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Two systems"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Indus & Ganga systems"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Peninsular detail"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Management & disputes"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Drainage Concepts and Patterns"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Himalayan vs Peninsular Drainage — the Standard Comparison"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Three Himalayan Systems"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Peninsular Rivers"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Lakes, Water Disputes and Management"}],"text":"Indian Drainage and Rivers — Detailed Notes Himalayan vs peninsular systems, interlinking, disputes, dams. River-atlas recall is the skill. 1. Two systems Himalayan (perennial, snow-fed, antecedent) : deep gorges, dendritic-braided reaches, large deltas. Peninsular (mostly rain-fed, seasonal, graded) : east-flowing giants (Mahanadi-Godavari-Krishna-Kaveri → Bay) + west-flowing rift rivers (Narmada-Tapi-Sabarmati-Mahi-Luni → Arabian Sea) + interior Yamuna tributaries (Chambal-Betwa-Ken-Son) + independent east-coast streams (Subarnarekha-Baitarani). 2. Indus & Ganga systems Indus : rises near Mansarovar (Tibet), enters India in Ladakh; Indus Water Treaty 1960 (World Bank-brokered): India = Ravi-Beas-Sutlej (eastern rivers); Pakistan = Indus-Jhelum-Chenab (western) . Jhelum source = Verinag; Sutlej enters via Shipki la. Ganga : Bhagirathi (Gangotri) + Alaknanda → Devprayag confluence; Panch","chars":11161},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/pyqs","file":"drainage-rivers-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/pyqs/drainage-rivers-mcqs.html","title":"Drainage &amp; Rivers of India — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Drainage & Rivers of India — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Drainage & Rivers of India — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following rivers rises in the Rakas Tal near Mansarovar? (a) Indus (b) Sutlej (c) Brahmaputra (d) Ghaghara Q2. Match the confluence with the tributary at the Panch Prayag: A. Rudraprayag — 1. Pindar · B. Karnaprayag — 2. Mandakini · C. Nandprayag — 3. Nandakini (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider: 1. The Pranhita is the largest tributary of the Godavari. 2. The Tungabhadra joins the Krishna in Andhra Pradesh. 3. The Kabini, a tributary of the Kaveri, rises in Kerala. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Which river flows through a rift valley and ends in an estuary in the Gulf of Khambhat? (a) Mahanadi (b) Narmada (c) Krishna (d) Pennar Q5. The Ken–Betwa link project, the first under the Inter","chars":2246},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/short","file":"drainage-rivers-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/drainage-rivers/short-notes/drainage-rivers-notes.html","title":"Drainage System &amp; Major Rivers of India — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Drainage System & Major Rivers of India — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Classification"},{"level":2,"text":"Indus system (2,880 km; India 1,114; Indus Waters Treaty 1960 — India: Ravi-Beas-Sutlej; Pakistan: Indus-Jhelum-Chenab; India held IWT \"in abeyance\" April 2025)"},{"level":2,"text":"Ganga system (2,525 km; longest in India; basin 26% area; National River 2008; Namami Gange 2014 ; NMCG)"},{"level":2,"text":"Brahmaputra system (2,900 km; India 916 km; largest volume; Yarlung Tsangpo — Chemayungdung/Angsi glacier near Mansarovar; flows east 1,200 km in Tibet; Namcha Barwa U-turn (Great Bend; China's Medog mega-dam 2024–25 ); enters Arunachal as Siang/Dihang ; joins Dibang & Lohit at Sadiya → Brahmaputra; Assam braided 700 km; Majuli ; Dhubri → Bangladesh as Jamuna ; joins Padma at Goalundo; Meghna (Barak) at Chandpur)"},{"level":2,"text":"Peninsular rivers"},{"level":2,"text":"Water disputes, tribunals & links"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Drainage System & Major Rivers of India — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. Source–tributary–dam–city matching, plus recent river-linked news. Classification Himalayan (perennial; antecedent gorges; meanders) vs Peninsular (seasonal; older; graded; Narmada, Tapi, Mahi, Sabarmati, Periyar westward — rift/estuary; Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri, Mahanadi eastward — deltas). Drainage patterns: dendritic (Ganga), trellis (Singhbhum), radial (Amarkantak), rectangular (Vindhya), centripetal (Loktak), parallel (W Ghats streams), deranged. Water divide: Delhi ridge–Aravalli–Sahyadri (Bay of Bengal 77% vs Arabian Sea 23%). Antecedent rivers: Indus, Sutlej, Brahmaputra (cut Himalaya); superimposed: Damodar, Subarnarekha, Chambal, Banas. Indus system (2,880 km; India 1,114; Indus Waters Treaty 1960 — India: Ravi-Beas-Sutlej; Pakistan: Indus-Jhelum-Chenab; India held IWT \"in ","chars":11487},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/bullets","file":"minerals-industries-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/bullet-points/minerals-industries-points.html","title":"Minerals & Industries — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Minerals & Industries — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Mineral flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Steel flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Core-industry flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Hub flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Minerals & Industries — Bullet Points Location-pair flash. Mineral flash Best iron → hematite: Odisha-Jharkhand-Chhattisgarh (Bailadilla) + Bellary-Hospet (KA) · coal → Jharia-Bokaro-Raniganj-Korba-Talcher · lignite → Neyveli (TN). Bauxite → Kalahandi-Koraput (OD) + Gujarat · manganese → OD-MP-KA · mica → Koderma (JH) + Nellore (AP). Gold → Hutti (KA; Kolar closed 2001) · uranium → Jaduguda (JH) + Tummalapalle (AP) · thorium → monazite sands Kerala-TN · lithium 2023 → Reasi (J&K). Belts → NE plateau richest · offshore → Mumbai High + KG basin. Steel flash TISCO Jamshedpur 1907 (first) · IISCO Burnpur · Bhilai = Soviet · Rourkela = West Germany · Durgapur = UK · Bokaro = USSR · Vizag-Salem modern. Core-industry flash 8 core = refinery (28%), electricity (19.9%), steel (17.9%), coal (10.3%), crude (8.98%), gas (6.88%), cement (5.37%), fertilisers (2.63%) — ~40% of IIP. Hub flash Textiles →","chars":1513},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/notes","file":"minerals-industries-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/detailed-notes/minerals-industries-detailed.html","title":"Minerals & Industries — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Minerals and Industries — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Mineral geography"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Industrial geography"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Mineral Classification and Geological Distribution"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Industrial Location Theory and Factors"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Major Industries and Regions"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Policy, Governance and Sustainability"}],"text":"Minerals and Industries — Detailed Notes Mineral belts, iron-steel plants, core-industry weights, industrial corridors. Location-pair recall. 1. Mineral geography Iron ore : best = hematite (Odisha-Jharkhand-Chhattisgarh belt; Bailadilla-Chhattisgarh; Bellary-Hospet-Karnataka; Kudremukh park-adjacent mining ended); magnetite (Karnataka-Goa); India ranks among the world's top iron-ore producers . Coal (Gondwana 98% + Tertiary) : Jharia-Bokaro-Raniganj-Korba-Talcher-Singrauli-Neyveli (lignite-TN); India = 2nd largest producer; Coal India (1975). Bauxite : Odisha (Kalahandi-Koraput-Sambalpur), Gujarat, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh; Manganese : Odisha-MP-Karnataka-MH; Mica : Koderma-Hazaribagh (JH), Bhilwara (RJ), Nellore (AP); Gold : Kolar (closed 2001)-Hutti (KA), Sonbhadra (UP claims); Uranium : Jaduguda-Turamdih (JH), Tummalapalle (AP); Thorium : monazite sands — Kerala-TN coasts; Lithium (20","chars":12018},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/pyqs","file":"minerals-industries-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/pyqs/minerals-industries-mcqs.html","title":"Minerals, Energy &amp; Industries — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Minerals, Energy & Industries — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Minerals, Energy & Industries — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Match the mine with the mineral: A. Rampura-Agucha — 1. Chromite · B. Sukinda — 2. Zinc · C. Malanjkhand — 3. Copper (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q2. Consider Indian coal: 1. Gondwana coalfields account for about 98% of reserves. 2. Neyveli is a bituminous coalfield. 3. Raniganj is the oldest coalfield. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. India's largest onshore oil field is located in: (a) Assam (b) Gujarat (c) Rajasthan (d) Andhra Pradesh Q4. Which steel plant was set up with Soviet collaboration and is known for producing rails? (a) Rourkela (b) Durgapur (c) Bhilai (d) Salem Q5. Consider the eight core industries index: 1. Refinery products carry the highest weight. 2. Cement has a higher weight than coal.","chars":1826},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/short","file":"minerals-industries-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/minerals-industries/short-notes/minerals-industries-notes.html","title":"Minerals, Energy Resources &amp; Industries of India — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Minerals, Energy Resources & Industries of India — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Minerals (Indian Minerals Yearbook; leaders 2022–23)"},{"level":2,"text":"Energy (installed capacity ~ 490 GW, Jul 2025)"},{"level":2,"text":"Industries — location logic & belts"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Minerals, Energy Resources & Industries of India — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. Mineral–state–mine matching, energy mix numbers, industrial location logic. Minerals (Indian Minerals Yearbook; leaders 2022–23) Mineral Leading states/mines Hooks Iron ore Odisha (~55%; Keonjhar, Sundargarh, Mayurbhanj), Chhattisgarh (Bailadila — Dantewada; exported via Vizag), Karnataka (Bellary-Hospet, Kudremukh, Bababudan), Jharkhand (Singhbhum — Noamundi, Gua), Goa haematite (Odisha-Jharkhand-CG) & magnetite (Karnataka/Goa); 4th producer; export duty 2022 Manganese MP (Balaghat), Maharashtra (Nagpur-Bhandara), Odisha (Sundargarh), Karnataka; MOIL ferro-alloys Bauxite Odisha (~50%; Koraput — Panchpatmali/NALCO; Kalahandi — Lanjigarh/Vedanta; Niyamgiri) , Gujarat (Jamnagar), Jharkhand (Lohardaga), MH, CG (Mainpat), Andhra aluminium — NALCO Angul, Hindalco Renukoot, BALCO K","chars":11127},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/bullets","file":"physiography-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/bullet-points/physiography-points.html","title":"Physiography of India — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physiography of India — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Himalaya flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Plains flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Peninsula flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Coast-island flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Desert flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Physiography of India — Bullet Points Division-anchor flash. Himalaya flash N→S: Karakoram/Ladakh → Great (Himadri) → Lesser (Himachal) → Siwalik-Dun. India's highest peak → Kanchenjunga (Sikkim) · syntaxial bends → Nanga Parbat + Namcha Barwa. Passes → Zoji la-Banihal (J&K) · Shipki la (HP) · Mana-Niti (Uttarakhand) · Nathu-Jelep (Sikkim) · Bomdi la (Arunachal). Plains flash Belt order from hills → Bhabar (gravel) → Terai (marsh) → Bhangar (old alluvium) → Khadar (new). Doabs → Bist (Beas-Sutlej), Bari (Ravi-Beas), Rechna (Chenab-Ravi), Chaj (Chenab-Jhelum) · Majuli → Brahmaputra island. Peninsula flash Aravalli = oldest fold, eroded (Guru Shikhar) · Deccan Traps = flood basalt · Sahyadri continuous-west, E-Ghats discontinuous. Peaks → Anaimudi 2695 (Anaimalai), Doddabetta (Nilgiri), Guru Shikhar (Mount Abu) · gaps → Thal-Bhor, Palghat, Shencottah. Plateaus → Malwa, Chotanagpur (mineral","chars":1659},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/notes","file":"physiography-india-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/detailed-notes/physiography-india-detailed.html","title":"Physiography of India — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physiography of India — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The Himalaya"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Northern Plains"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Peninsular plateau"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Coasts, islands, desert"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Six Physiographic Divisions"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Himalaya — Longitudinal and Regional Divisions"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Northern Plain"},{"level":2,"text":"9. The Peninsular Plateau, Desert, Coasts and Islands"}],"text":"Physiography of India — Detailed Notes Five divisions: Himalaya, Northern Plains, Peninsula, Coasts-Islands, Desert. Passes, doabs, gaps — direct ask zone. 1. The Himalaya North-to-south: Trans-Himalaya (Karakoram-Ladakh-Zanskar; K2 Godwin-Austen) → Great Himalaya (Himadri; Everest-Sagarmatha; Kanchenjunga = India's highest) → Lesser/Middle (Himachal; Dhauladhar-Pir Panjal-Mussoorie-Nag Tibba) → Siwalik/Duns (outer; Bhabar belt below) . Regional: Punjab Himalaya (Indus-Sutlej), Kumaon (Sutlej-Kali), Nepal (Kali-Teesta), Assam (Teesta-Dihang). Syntaxial bends: Nanga Parbat (west), Namcha Barwa/Dihang (east) . Origin: Tethys closure, India-Asia collision (~50-40 Ma), phased uplift. Major passes : Zoji la-Banihal (J&K), Shipki la (HP-Sutlej entry), Mana-Niti (Uttarakhand), Nathu la-Jelep la (Sikkim), Bomdi la-Diphu (Arunachal). 2. Northern Plains Depositional mega-plain (Indus-Ganga-Brahmap","chars":10698},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/pyqs","file":"physiography-india-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/pyqs/physiography-india-mcqs.html","title":"Physiography of India — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physiography of India — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Physiography of India — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The highest peak located wholly within Indian territory is: (a) Kangchenjunga (b) Nanda Devi (c) Kamet (d) Nanga Parbat Q2. Arrange from north to south: 1. Nine Degree Channel 2. Eight Degree Channel 3. Ten Degree Channel (a) 3-1-2 (b) 1-3-2 (c) 3-2-1 (d) 2-1-3 Q3. Match the pass with the state/UT: A. Shipki La — 1. Sikkim · B. Nathu La — 2. Himachal Pradesh · C. Lipulekh — 3. Uttarakhand (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. Consider the Tropic of Cancer: 1. It passes through eight Indian states. 2. Mizoram and Tripura are among them. 3. It passes through Bihar. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q5. The Palghat (Palakkad) gap connects: (a) Madurai and Kollam (b) Coimbatore and Palakkad (c) Mumbai and Pune (d) Kolhapur and ","chars":1909},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/short","file":"physiography-india-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/physiography-india/short-notes/physiography-india-notes.html","title":"Physiography of India: Himalayas, Plains, Plateau, Coasts &amp; Islands — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physiography of India: Himalayas, Plains, Plateau, Coasts & Islands — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Frame"},{"level":2,"text":"Himalayas (young fold; Tethys; ~2,400 km arc, 150–400 km wide; syntaxial bends Nanga Parbat & Namcha Barwa)"},{"level":2,"text":"Northern plains (7 lakh km²; 2,400 km; alluvium up to 2,000 m thick)"},{"level":2,"text":"Peninsular plateau (oldest — Gondwana; Archaean)"},{"level":2,"text":"Coasts & islands"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Physiography of India: Himalayas, Plains, Plateau, Coasts & Islands — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. Map-anchored facts: ranges, passes, peaks, hills, coastal plains and islands. Frame Area 32.87 lakh km² (7th; 2.4% of world land; 17.7% population); lat 8°4′–37°6′ N, long 68°7′–97°25′ E; N–S 3,214 km, E–W 2,933 km; Tropic of Cancer through 8 states (Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, WB, Tripura, Mizoram); IST 82°30′ E (Mirzapur); land frontier 15,200 km (Bangladesh 4,096 longest → China 3,488 → Pakistan 3,323 → Nepal 1,751 → Myanmar 1,643 → Bhutan 699 → Afghanistan 106); coastline 7,516 km ( Gujarat longest ~1,600; then Andhra/TN; new 2024 measurement 11,098 km); southernmost point Indira Point (Great Nicobar; 6°45′), mainland Kanyakumari (8°4′); Indira Col northernmost. Himalayas (young fold; Tethys; ~2,400 km arc, 150–400 km wide; syntaxia","chars":8114},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/short","file":"indian-geo-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/short-notes/indian-geo-prelims.html","title":"Indian Geography — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Geography — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Physiography stack (north → south)"},{"level":2,"text":"Rivers (which-bank drill)"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate & monsoon"},{"level":2,"text":"Crops ↔ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"Minerals & energy (state-pair)"},{"level":2,"text":"National waterways & ports"}],"text":"Indian Geography — Prelims Revision Physiography stack (north → south) Himalayas (2,400 km arc): Trans (Karakoram/K2-Godwin Austen 8,611 m is POK side; Kanchenjunga 8,586 m India's highest on-map), Great (Nanda Devi 7,816 m highest fully-Indian), passes Zoji La (Srinagar–Leh), Shipki La (Sutlej entry), Nathu La (Sikkim–Tibet), Bomdi La (Arunachal); Lesser/Inner (Pir Panjal, Dhauladhar — hill stations: Shimla-Mussoorie-Nainital-Darjeeling); Shiwaliks outermost (duns: Dehradun/Kotli/Patli). Northern Plains : bhabar → terai → alluvial (bangar old / khadar new); deltaic east. Peninsular Plateau : two units — Central Highlands (Malwa/Bundelkhand/Baghelkhand + Aravalli residual, Guru Shikhar 1,722 m on Mt Abu; Chambal badlands; Bundelkhand ravines) & Deccan Plateau (tilt east → rivers flow east; Western Ghats continuous high wall (Anamudi 2,695 m tallest south; passes: Thal/Bhor/Palghat); East","chars":5087},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/bullets","file":"soils-agriculture-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/bullet-points/soils-agriculture-points.html","title":"Soils & Agriculture — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Soils & Agriculture — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Soil flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Season flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Policy flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Concept flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Soils & Agriculture — Bullet Points Soil-crop-scheme flash. Soil flash Alluvial → largest, khadar-bangar, Zn-deficient · black-regur → cotton, Deccan basalt, self-ploughing · red → millets-pulses plateau. Laterite → leached, tea-cashew-brick · arid → kankar, wind erosion · saline-usara → canal-tail waterlogging · peaty → Kerala. Season flash Kharif Jun-Oct → rice-cotton-bajra-groundnut-tur · rabi Oct-Mar → wheat-mustard-gram-barley · zaid → melons-fodder. Jute = WB · tea = Assam-Darjeeling-Nilgiri · coffee = Karnataka (~70%) · rubber = Kerala · cotton = GJ-MH-TS black-soil belt. Policy flash MSP → CACP recommends, 22+copra crops, FCI-NAFED procure · PM-AASHA 2018 umbrella. PMFBY 2016 insurance · eNAM 2016 mandi platform · SHC 2015 · PM-KISAN 2019 ₹6,000 · FPO 2020 (10,000) · KCC · PKVY organic. GR 1966-67 → Borlaug-Swaminathan, Punjab-Haryana-W-UP · millets year 2023 (Shree Anna) · Namo ","chars":1364},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/notes","file":"soils-agriculture-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/detailed-notes/soils-agriculture-detailed.html","title":"Soils, Agriculture & Cropping — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Soils, Agriculture and Cropping — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Soils (ICAR 8-fold)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Cropping seasons & crop pairs"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Policy & institutions stack"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Soils, Agriculture and Cropping — Detailed Notes ICAR soil map + crop-calendar + policy stack. Crop-region pairs and scheme numbers are the ask. 1. Soils (ICAR 8-fold) Alluvial (largest ~43%) : Indus-Ganga-Brahmaputra-coasts; khadar (new, fine, flood-refreshed) vs bangar (old, kankar); very fertile but zinc-deficiency common. Black/regur (~15%) : Deccan basalt (MH-MP-KA-TS); cotton-sugarcane; moisture-retentive, self-ploughing (crack-swell), poor in N-P but rich in Fe-Mg-Al; sticky when wet. Red (~18%) : crystalline weathering (eastern-southern plateau); fine for millets-pulses with inputs. Laterite : intense-leach product (Western Ghats, NE, Chotanagpur); poor fertility — tea-coffee-cashew-casuarina; brick-making. Arid : west Rajasthan-Kutch-Gujarat north; high Ca-kankar, wind erosion; saline-usara : UP-west, Haryana, Punjab canal-tails (waterlogging + evaporation). Peaty : Kerala backw","chars":2789},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/pyqs","file":"soils-agriculture-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/pyqs/soils-agriculture-mcqs.html","title":"Soils, Irrigation &amp; Agriculture — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Soils, Irrigation & Agriculture — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Soils, Irrigation & Agriculture — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following crops is most closely associated with black (regur) soil? (a) Jute (b) Cotton (c) Tea (d) Rice Q2. Match the state with its leading crop: A. Assam — 1. Coffee · B. Karnataka — 2. Tea · C. Kerala — 3. Rubber (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider the Minimum Support Price system: 1. MSP is recommended by the CACP and approved by the CCEA. 2. Sugarcane is covered by an FRP rather than an MSP. 3. MSP is announced for 22 mandated crops. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. The largest source of irrigation in India by net irrigated area is: (a) Canals (b) Tanks (c) Wells and tube-wells (d) Lift irrigation from rivers Q5. Cropping intensity is calculated as: (a) Net sown area ÷ gross crop","chars":1954},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/short","file":"soils-agriculture-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/soils-agriculture/short-notes/soils-agriculture-notes.html","title":"Soils, Irrigation &amp; Agriculture Patterns of India — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Soils, Irrigation & Agriculture Patterns of India — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Land use & basics (2021–22, ~328 Mha reporting ~307)"},{"level":2,"text":"Crops: conditions, leaders (Agri Stats 2023–24)"},{"level":2,"text":"Irrigation"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture policy & institutions (prelims hooks)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Soils, Irrigation & Agriculture Patterns of India — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. Crop–condition–state matching, irrigation sources, cropping seasons; soil detail sits in the biogeography card. Land use & basics (2021–22, ~328 Mha reporting ~307) Net sown area ~140 Mha (~46%); gross cropped ~220 Mha; cropping intensity ~155% ; forests ~72 Mha; fallow ~25 Mha; culturable waste ~12; barren ~17; net irrigated ~76 Mha ( ~55% of net sown ); rainfed ~45%. Agriculture ~16–18% GVA, ~45% workforce. Landholdings avg 1.08 ha (Agri Census 2015–16); 86% small & marginal. Cropping seasons: Kharif (Jun–Oct; rice, maize, jowar, bajra, cotton, jute, groundnut, soybean, tur, moong, urad, sugarcane planting), Rabi (Oct–Mar; wheat, barley, gram, mustard, peas, linseed, lentil/masoor), Zaid (Mar–Jun; watermelon, cucumber, fodder, moong). Kerala rice: virippu/mundakan/punja ; ","chars":9929},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/bullets","file":"transport-settlements-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/bullet-points/transport-settlements-points.html","title":"Transport & Settlements — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Transport & Settlements — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Road flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Rail flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Port-waterway flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Settlement flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Transport & Settlements — Bullet Points Number-anchor flash. Road flash NH-44 → longest (Srinagar-Kanyakumari) · GQ → 5846 km, 4 metros · Bharatmala 2017 → corridor-based phase-1. Agencies → NHAI (1995), NHIDCL (borders-NE) · rural → PMGSY 2000 · safety → MVAA 2019. Rail flash First → Bombay-Thane 16 Apr 1853 · zones → 18 · DFCs → Eastern (Ludhiana) + Western (JNPT-Dadri). UNESCO hill railways → Darjeeling, Nilgiri, Kalka-Shimla · Vande Bharat + Amrit Bharat = modern pair · Kavach = safety. Port-waterway flash 12 major ports; JNPT = container king · Vizag = deepest · Vadhavan = newest approved. NW-1 → Haldia-Varanasi (Ganga) · NW-2 → Brahmaputra · NW-3 → Kollam-Kottapuram canal. Sagarmala 2015 → port-led; UDAN → regional air. Settlement flash Compact → plains · linear → roads-rivers · circular → ponds (UP) · star → crossroads · dispersed → hills-forests. Census town → 4000+ pop, 75% male","chars":1254},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/notes","file":"transport-settlements-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/detailed-notes/transport-settlements-detailed.html","title":"Transport, Ports & Settlements — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Transport, Ports and Settlements — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Roads"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Railways"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Ports, waterways, air"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Settlement geography"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Transport, Ports and Settlements — Detailed Notes Roads-rail-ports-waterways-air + settlement typology. Corridor-number anchors dominate. 1. Roads Network: world's 2nd largest (~63 lakh km total incl. rural); NH ~1.46 lakh km (MoRTH), carry ~40% traffic; NH numbering grid (even E-W, odd N-S). NH-44 = longest (Srinagar-Kanyakumari) ; older NH-7 merged. Golden Quadrilateral (5846 km: Delhi-Mumbai-Chennai-Kolkata) + NS-EW corridors; Bharatmala Pariyojana (2017; 34,800 km phase-1; corridors-based) ; Gati Shakti (2021) multimodal GIS ; expressway list: Mumbai-Nagpur Samruddhi, Delhi-Mumbai (underway), Dwarka, Yamuna, Purvanchal. Institutions: NHAI (1995 act), NHIDCL (NE-borders), PMGSY (2000 rural roads, now 4.0), road-safety MVAA 2019. 2. Railways First run Bombay (Bori Bunder)-Thane 16 Apr 1853 ; network ~68,000 route-km (4th largest); 18 operational zones ; DFCs: Eastern (Ludhiana-Sonnagar","chars":2925},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/pyqs","file":"transport-settlements-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/pyqs/transport-settlements-mcqs.html","title":"Transport, Trade &amp; Settlements — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Transport, Trade & Settlements — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Transport, Trade & Settlements — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Under the 2010 National Highway numbering scheme: (a) Odd numbers run north–south (b) Even numbers run north–south (c) Numbers increase from south to north for E–W highways (d) All NHs have three-digit numbers Q2. Match the port with its description: A. Visakhapatnam — 1. Largest container port · B. JNPT — 2. Riverine port · C. Kolkata — 3. Deepest natural landlocked harbour (a) A-3 B-1 C-2 (b) A-1 B-3 C-2 (c) A-2 B-1 C-3 (d) A-3 B-2 C-1 Q3. Consider a \"census town\" as per Census 2011: 1. Minimum population of 5,000. 2. At least 75% of male main workers engaged in non-agricultural pursuits. 3. Governed by a municipality. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Which of the following was India's most urbanised state in Census 2011? (a) Maharashtra","chars":2071},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/short","file":"transport-settlements-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/indian-geography/transport-settlements/short-notes/transport-settlements-notes.html","title":"Transport, Trade &amp; Human Settlements — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Transport, Trade & Human Settlements — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Roads (~63.7 lakh km — 2nd largest network; NH 1,46,000 km = 2% length, 40% traffic)"},{"level":2,"text":"Railways (68,584 route km; 4th largest; 1853 Bori Bunder–Thane 34 km ; 17 zones (+South Coast Vizag 2019 = 18); 8,000 stations; 1.2 crore passengers/day; ~1.6 bn t freight)"},{"level":2,"text":"Water & ports (95% trade by volume, 68–70% by value; 12 major + ~200 non-major)"},{"level":2,"text":"Air (3rd largest domestic market; ~160 operational airports; UDAN 2016 — RCS; 600+ routes; UDAN 2.0 2025 — 120 new destinations)"},{"level":2,"text":"Pipelines & others"},{"level":2,"text":"Trade (2024–25: goods exports $437 bn , services $387 bn ; total exports $825 bn record; imports goods $915 bn; trade deficit goods ~$283 bn; top partners — US #1 export destination ($86 bn) & largest trading partner ($132 bn) , China largest import source & deficit (~$99 bn), UAE, Saudi, Russia (oil), Singapore, Netherlands; top exports — petroleum products, gems & jewellery, electronics (smartphones ~$24 bn), drugs, engineering, rice, textiles; imports — crude, gold (~$58 bn), electronics, coal, machinery, vegetable oil; FTAs — ASEAN (2010), Japan, Korea CEPA, SAFTA, UAE CEPA 2022, Australia ECTA 2022, EFTA TEPA 2024 ($100 bn investment; in force Oct 2025), UK CETA July 2025 , Mauritius; under negotiation — EU, US BTA (2025 tariff 50% shock Aug 2025), Oman, Peru, Chile, Israel; RCEP opted out 2019; IPEF (3 of 4 pillars); WTO (Doha; MC13 Abu Dhabi 2024 — e-commerce moratorium extended, fisheries, agriculture stalemate; India's PSH); ports — JNPT/Mundra/Chennai; Foreign Trade Policy 2023 (no sunset; $2 tn by 2030; districts as export hubs; e-commerce; rupee trade; RoDTEP; Towns of Export Excellence ; Advance authorisation; EPCG; SEZ → DESH Bill pending); INSTC (Chabahar–Bandar Abbas–Astrakhan), IMEC (2023 G20) , Chennai–Vladivostok , Kaladan , BBIN , BIMSTEC (Bangkok summit 2025; maritime cooperation), Northern Sea Route; GIFT-IFSC ; Forex reserves ~$700 bn (peak $704 bn Sept 2024; ~$690 bn Aug 2025); rupee ~₹87–88/$ (2025)."},{"level":2,"text":"Settlements (Census 2011; next Census 2027 — houselisting April 2026; caste enumeration approved 2025)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Transport, Trade & Human Settlements — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Indian Geography. Network firsts and numbers, ports/waterways, Census-2011 settlement definitions and urbanisation data. Roads (~63.7 lakh km — 2nd largest network; NH 1,46,000 km = 2% length, 40% traffic) Classification: NH (MoRTH/NHAI 1995; numbering 2010 — odd E-W, even N-S; NH-44 longest Srinagar–Kanyakumari 3,745 km; NH-548/118 shortest), SH, district, rural (PMGSY 2000 — 500/250 population; PMGSY-IV 2024), border roads ( BRO 1960 ; Project Himank; Umling La), Golden Quadrilateral (5,846 km; Delhi-Mumbai-Chennai-Kolkata; NHDP 1998), N-S (Srinagar–Kanyakumari) & E-W (Silchar–Porbandar) corridors , Bharatmala (2017; 34,800 km; economic corridors), expressways (Delhi–Mumbai 1,386 km — longest under construction; Purvanchal, Ganga (594 km), Bundelkhand, Samruddhi (Nagpur–Mumbai), Agra–Lucknow, Yamuna, Mu","chars":11624},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/maps-india-world/bullets","file":"maps-india-world-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/maps-india-world/bullet-points/maps-india-world-points.html","title":"Maps & Location Drills — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Maps & Location Drills — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"India flash"},{"level":2,"text":"World flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Projection flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Maps & Location Drills — Bullet Points Locate-fast flash. India flash Tropic of Cancer → 8 states: GJ-RJ-MP-CG-JH-WB-TR-MZ · southern tip → Indira Point (Nicobar) · mainland south → Kanyakumari. IST meridian → 82.5E Mirzapur · extremes → Indira Col (N), Kibithu (E), Guhar Moti (W). Lakes → Chilika (OD), Pulicat (AP-TN), Vembanad (KL), Loktak (MN), Wular (J&K), Sambhar (RJ), Kolleru (AP). City-rivers → Delhi-Yamuna, Ahmedabad-Sabarmati, Lucknow-Gomti, Surat-Tapi, Vijayawada-Krishna, Cuttack-Mahanadi, Srinagar-Jhelum, Guwahati-Brahmaputra. World flash Straits → Hormuz (gulf exit), Malacca (busiest), Bab-el-Mandeb (Red Sea), Bosphorus-Dardanelles (Black Sea), Gibraltar (Atlantic-Med), Palk (India-Lanka). Lines → Equator: Ecuador-Kenya-Indonesia · IDL: zigzag 180 · Prime Meridian: Greenwich. Regions → Sahel (Sahara-savanna belt), Horn of Africa, Maghreb, Caucasus, ASEAN spine, stans belt. Pr","chars":1415},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/maps-india-world/notes","file":"maps-india-world-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/maps-india-world/detailed-notes/maps-india-world-detailed.html","title":"Maps of India & World — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Maps of India and the World — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Map projections & map-reading"},{"level":2,"text":"2. India map drills"},{"level":2,"text":"3. World map drills"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Exam technique"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Maps of India and the World — Detailed Notes Map-based prelims questions test three skills: projection literacy, India micro-location, and world chokepoint placement. 1. Map projections & map-reading Mercator : conformal (directions true) — navigation; gross polar distortion (Greenland ≈ Africa lie). Gall-Peters : equal-area, shapes stretched. Robinson : compromise (NatGeo standard). Conic (mid-latitudes strips), polar azimuthal, Interrupted Goode. Scale: large-scale = more detail (village maps); small-scale = more area (atlases) ; RF 1:50,000 = Survey of India topo; contour rules (closely spaced = steep); graticule vs grid; UTC + IST 82.5E (Mirzapur reference). 2. India map drills Tropic of Cancer passes through 8 states (Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, WB, Tripura, Mizoram). Extremes: north = Indira Col (Siachen), south = Indira Point (Great Nicobar) , east = Kibithu (","chars":2538},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/maps-india-world/pyqs","file":"maps-india-world-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/maps-india-world/pyqs/maps-india-world-pyqs.html","title":"Maps & Location — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Maps and Location — PYQ-Style Practice"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 1 — Location match (2016-2020 pattern)"},{"level":2,"text":"Set 2 — Straits, lines & projections"},{"level":2,"text":"Answers"}],"text":"Maps and Location — PYQ-Style Practice Map-frame questions from UPSC prelims patterns, rebuilt as drills. Answers at the end. Set 1 — Location match (2016-2020 pattern) Q1. The 'Phumdis' (floating islands) are found in which lake? (a) Chilika (b) Loktak (c) Wular (d) Kolleru Q2. Which one of the following pairs is NOT correctly matched? (a) Sambhar — Rajasthan (b) Pulicat — Andhra Pradesh-Tamil Nadu (c) Vembanad — Karnataka (d) Chilika — Odisha Q3. Consider the rivers and cities: 1. Ahmedabad–Sabarmati 2. Lucknow–Gomti 3. Surat–Mahanadi 4. Cuttack–Tapi. Which pairs are correctly matched? (a) 1,2 (b) 2,3 (c) 1,4 (d) 3,4 Q4. Indira Point, the southernmost point of India, is located in: (a) Lakshadweep (b) Great Nicobar (c) Minicoy (d) Car Nicobar Q5. Which of the following states does the Tropic of Cancer pass through? 1. Rajasthan 2. Chhattisgarh 3. Tripura 4. Odisha. Choose: (a) 1,2,3 (b","chars":1974},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/maps-india-world/short","file":"map-drills.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/maps-india-world/short-notes/map-drills.html","title":"Map Practice — Method &amp; Drills (Prelims)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Map Practice — Method & Drills (Prelims)"},{"level":2,"text":"Method"},{"level":2,"text":"India lists to rehearse"},{"level":2,"text":"World list"},{"level":2,"text":"Self-test format"},{"level":2,"text":"Common map-question patterns in UPSC"}],"text":"Map Practice — Method & Drills (Prelims) Map questions almost always reward position/sequence recall. Build a weekly 15-minute drill loop using blank outline maps (Sketchy/Exammaps books or blank printouts). Method Triangle anchors : fix 3 memory points per region first (e.g., S Asia: Gwadar-Karachi West, Dhaka East, Assam's Sadiya NE) then fill middles by interpolation. Sequence runs : learn orders, not singles — e.g., Himalayan passes west→east; major ports north→south on each coast. Relation frames : Which desert is east of Andes? (Patagonian rain-shadow). What strait connects Bay of Bengal–S China Sea region? (Malacca). Learn via \"through-X-one-reaches-Y\" phrasing. News-anchored marking : every fortnight, plot 10 current-affairs locations (conflicts, projects, storms) on an outline map. India lists to rehearse Mountain passes W→E : Banihal (Pir Panjal tunnel corridor) → Zoji La → Bur","chars":3183},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/bullets","file":"biogeography-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/bullet-points/biogeography-points.html","title":"Biogeography — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biogeography — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Biome flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Diversity flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Island flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Soil flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Biogeography — Bullet Points Term-anchor flash. Biome flash Max NPP → tropical rainforest · taiga = boreal conifer · savanna = grass+scattered trees · Mediterranean = winter-rain scrub. India forests → Champion-Seth 1968; tropical deciduous largest; mangrove = littoral-swamp type. Diversity flash Alpha → within-habitat · beta → between-habitat · gamma → landscape total. Hotspots (36 global) → India 4: Himalaya, Indo-Burma, W-Ghats-Sri Lanka, Sundaland. IUCN order → EX, EW, CR, EN, VU, NT, LC (+DD, NE). Keystone → ecosystem-defining · flagship → charismatic · indicator → health signal. Island flash MacArthur-Wilson 1967 → richness ↑ with area, ↓ with isolation · SIBA application in reserve design. Soil flash Alluvial largest (khadar new / bangar old) · black = regur, cotton, basalt, self-ploughing · red = crystalline weathering. Laterite → leaching, brick-cashew-tea · arid → kankar · sali","chars":1252},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/notes","file":"biogeography-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/detailed-notes/biogeography-detailed.html","title":"Biogeography — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biogeography — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Biomes & vegetation belts"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Biodiversity metrics & hotspots"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Soil-vegetation geography"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Biogeography — Detailed Notes Biomes, diversity metrics, island theory, soil-vegetation links. Light-read area with trick MCQs. 1. Biomes & vegetation belts Whittaker's frame: temperature x precipitation → biome. Sequence pole-to-equator: tundra → taiga (boreal coniferous) → temperate deciduous-grassland → Mediterranean scrub → desert → tropical rainforest (max NPP) → savanna. India forest types (Champion-Seth 1968, 16 major groups) : tropical evergreen (Western Ghats-NE-Andaman), tropical deciduous (most extensive — moist then dry), tropical thorn (NW), montane (oak-conifer above), littoral-swamp (mangroves), alpine. Forest Survey of India (ISFR biennial): forest+tree cover ~25% of geographic area (verify latest ISFR figure). 2. Biodiversity metrics & hotspots Metrics: alpha (within habitat), beta (between-habitat change), gamma (landscape total). Endemism : species confined to an area ","chars":2420},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/pyqs","file":"biogeography-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/pyqs/biogeography-mcqs.html","title":"Biogeography (Soils &amp; Vegetation) — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biogeography (Soils & Vegetation) — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Biogeography (Soils & Vegetation) — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. \"Self-ploughing\" nature and high moisture retention are characteristics of: (a) Alluvial soil (b) Black (regur) soil (c) Laterite soil (d) Red soil Q2. Consider laterite soils: 1. They form under high temperature and heavy rainfall with alternating wet–dry seasons. 2. They are rich in silica and humus. 3. They harden irreversibly on exposure and are cut into bricks. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the temperate grassland with its region: A. Pampas — 1. South Africa · B. Veld — 2. Argentina · C. Downs — 3. Australia (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. Which soil-forming process dominates in cool, humid coniferous regions, producing ash-grey acidic soils? (a) Laterisation (b) Calcification (c) Podzol","chars":2049},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/short","file":"biogeography-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/biogeography/short-notes/biogeography-notes.html","title":"Biogeography: Soils &amp; Natural Vegetation — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biogeography: Soils & Natural Vegetation — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Soil formation & profile"},{"level":2,"text":"Indian soils (ICAR 8 major)"},{"level":2,"text":"World biomes & natural vegetation (climate → vegetation → soil → fauna)"},{"level":2,"text":"India's vegetation (Champion & Seth 1968 — 16 types; ISFR 2023: forest cover 7,15,343 km² = 21.76%, tree cover 3.41%, total 25.17% ; MP largest area, Mizoram highest %; Lakshadweep ~90%; mangroves 4,992 km² — WB 42%)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Biogeography: Soils & Natural Vegetation — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Physical Geography. Soil-type traits (ICAR classification) and biome–climate matching. Soil formation & profile Factors (Jenny): parent material, climate, relief, organisms, time ( Cl-O-R-P-T ). Processes: weathering → humification → eluviation/illuviation, laterisation (hot-wet; silica leached, Fe/Al oxides stay), podzolisation (cool-wet coniferous; acidic, ash-grey), calcification (semi-arid; CaCO₃), salinisation/alkalisation (arid; capillary rise), gleisation (waterlogged). Profile: O (organic) → A (topsoil, humus; eluviation) → E → B (illuviation; subsoil) → C (regolith) → R (bedrock). Texture triangle (sand-silt-clay; loam best); pH (6.5–7.5 ideal); soil health card (12 parameters — NPK, S, Zn, Fe, Cu, Mn, B, pH, EC, OC); NBSS&LUP (Nagpur). Indian soils (ICAR 8 major) Soil Area & regions Traits C","chars":6410},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/bullets","file":"climatology-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/bullet-points/climatology-points.html","title":"Climatology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climatology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Layer flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Wind flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Cyclone flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Koppen flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Monsoon flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Climatology — Bullet Points Anchor flash. Layer flash Troposphere → weather, 6.5C/km lapse · stratosphere → ozone (15-35 km) · mesosphere → meteors · thermosphere → aurora-ionosphere. Albedo ~30% · inversion types → radiation (valley), frontal, subsidence, advection. Wind flash ITCZ → doldrums, migrates with sun · horse latitudes ~30 · westerlies 30-60. Subtropical westerly jet → winter, western disturbances · tropical easterly jet → summer monsoon. Local winds → loo (N India heat), kalbaisakhi (Bengal thunder), mango showers (Kerala), chinook-foehn (warm), mistral-bora (cold), sirocco (Sahara→Europe), harmattan (W Africa dust). Cyclone flash Tropical → warm core, eye, 5-20 lat, SST>27C, IMD 13-country naming · Bay of Bengal dominance. Temperate → fronts (warm-cold-occluded), winter western disturbances → rabi rain-snow. Koppen flash Am → Malabar · Aw → peninsula · BShw → NW interior · C","chars":1379},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/notes","file":"climatology-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/detailed-notes/climatology-detailed.html","title":"Climatology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climatology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Atmosphere structure"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Pressure belts & winds"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Cyclones & precipitation"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Koppen-in-India & monsoon"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Climatology — Detailed Notes Atmosphere layers, circulation, cyclones, Koppen-in-India, and monsoon mechanics — the prelims workhorse. 1. Atmosphere structure Layers: troposphere (0-12 km, weather, lapse ~6.5C/km) → tropopause → stratosphere (calm; ozone layer 15-35 km; jets sit near its base) → mesosphere (meteors burn) → thermosphere-ionosphere (aurora, radio) → exosphere. Insolation budget: albedo ~30%, absorbed ~70%; heat transfer (conduction-convection-advection); temperature inversion types (radiation — valley fog, frontal, subsidence, advection). Greenhouse effect basics; aerosols-CCN role in cloud physics. 2. Pressure belts & winds Belts: equatorial low (ITCZ, doldrums), subtropical highs (horse latitudes ~30), subpolar lows, polar highs; seasonal migration follows the sun (ITCZ shifts over India in monsoon). Winds: planetary (trades NE/SE, westerlies, polar easterlies) , periodi","chars":2752},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/pyqs","file":"climatology-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/pyqs/climatology-mcqs.html","title":"Climatology — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climatology — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Climatology — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Tropical cyclones do not form within about 5° of the equator because: (a) Sea surface temperatures are too low (b) The Coriolis force is negligible (c) Wind shear is too strong (d) Humidity is insufficient Q2. Consider the Tropical Easterly Jet: 1. It flows over India during the summer monsoon. 2. It results from heating of the Tibetan plateau. 3. It lies south of the Subtropical Westerly Jet. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the local wind with its region: A. Chinook — 1. Adriatic · B. Bora — 2. Rockies · C. Harmattan — 3. West Africa (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. A positive Indian Ocean Dipole is characterised by: (a) Warmer waters off Sumatra than off East Africa (b) Warmer western Indian Ocean and cooler easter","chars":2076},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/short","file":"climatology-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/climatology/short-notes/climatology-notes.html","title":"Climatology: Atmosphere, Cyclones &amp; Monsoon — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Climatology: Atmosphere, Cyclones & Monsoon — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Atmosphere"},{"level":2,"text":"Tropical cyclones"},{"level":2,"text":"Monsoon (India)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Climatology: Atmosphere, Cyclones & Monsoon — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Physical Geography. Mechanism questions (why/where) dominate; keep the diagrams in your head. Atmosphere Composition: N₂ 78.08%, O₂ 20.95%, Ar 0.93%, CO₂ ~0.04% (≈420 ppm 2024) ; water vapour 0–4%; ozone (10–50 km, peak ~25 km; Dobson unit). Layers: troposphere (8 km poles–18 km equator; lapse rate 6.5 °C/km; all weather; tropopause) → stratosphere (ozone; temperature rises; jets fly) → mesosphere (coldest −90 °C; meteors burn) → thermosphere/ionosphere (aurora, radio reflection; ISS ~400 km) → exosphere. Homosphere/heterosphere split at 80 km. Insolation & heat budget: ~1.94 cal/cm²/min solar constant; albedo ~30–35% (planetary); 51% absorbed by surface; greenhouse (H₂O, CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, O₃, CFCs); terrestrial radiation long-wave; latitudinal heat balance → winds/currents; aphelion 4 July, perihelio","chars":6701},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/bullets","file":"geomorphology-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/bullet-points/geomorphology-points.html","title":"Geomorphology — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geomorphology — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Interior flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Plate flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Quake-volcano flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Landform flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Geomorphology — Bullet Points Concept-anchor flash. Interior flash Conrad → within crust · Moho → crust-mantle · Gutenberg → mantle-core · Lehmann → inner core. S-wave shadow → liquid outer core · isostasy → Airy (roots) vs Pratt (density). Plate flash Wegener 1912 drift · Hess 1961 sea-floor spreading · 7 major plates, Indian ~5-6 cm/yr NE. Convergent trio → Andes (o-c), Japan (o-o), Himalaya (c-c) · divergent → mid-Atlantic ridge · transform → San Andreas. Hotspots → Hawaii intra-plate; Reunion → Deccan Traps. Quake-volcano flash Richter = magnitude (energy) · Mercalli = intensity (damage) · P-waves fastest, solids+liquids. India zones II-V · zone V → Himalaya-NE-Kutch-Andaman · Barren Island only active. Intrusive set → batholith-laccolith-lopolith-phacolith-sill-dyke. Landform flash Fluvial → V-gorge-waterfall (young), meander-oxbow (mature), delta-peneplain (old). Arid → yardang-zeu","chars":1326},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/notes","file":"geomorphology-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/detailed-notes/geomorphology-detailed.html","title":"Geomorphology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geomorphology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Earth's interior"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Plate tectonics"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Earthquakes & volcanoes"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Erosional agents & landforms"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Geomorphology — Detailed Notes Interior, plate tectonics, earthquakes-volcanoes, and the four erosional agents. Statement-true/false territory. 1. Earth's interior Layers: crust (continental ~35-70 km vs oceanic ~5-10 km; SIAL-SIMA terminology legacy ), mantle (to ~2900 km; upper asthenosphere = partial-melt, plate lubricant), core (outer liquid, inner solid — Fe-Ni). Discontinuities : Conrad (crust-internal), Moho (crust-mantle), Repetti (mantle-internal), Gutenberg (mantle-core), Lehmann (inner core). Evidence: direct (mining, drilling — deepest Kola ~12 km) vs indirect (seismic P-S wave shadow zones, meteorites density, isostasy — Airy vs Pratt). 2. Plate tectonics Theory chain: continental drift (Wegener 1912: Pangaea-Panthalassa, jigsaw-fit, Glossopteris flora evidence) → sea-floor spreading (Hess 1961; palaeomagnetic stripes, youngest-at-ridge) → plate tectonics (1960s; 7 major pla","chars":3153},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/pyqs","file":"geomorphology-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/pyqs/geomorphology-mcqs.html","title":"Geomorphology — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geomorphology — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Geomorphology — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The fact that S-waves do not travel through the outer core indicates that it is: (a) Very dense solid (b) Liquid (c) Made of iron only (d) At very low pressure Q2. Match the landform with the agent: A. Drumlin — 1. Wind · B. Barchan — 2. Glacier · C. Stalagmite — 3. Groundwater (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider: 1. Sedimentary rocks cover about three-fourths of the earth's land surface but form a small fraction of crustal volume. 2. Marble is a metamorphosed form of sandstone. 3. Gneiss is a foliated metamorphic rock. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. A \"bird's-foot\" delta is best exemplified by the: (a) Nile (b) Ganga (c) Mississippi (d) Tiber Q5. Which of the following are landforms of glacial erosion ? 1. Cirque","chars":1873},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/short","file":"geomorphology-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/geomorphology/short-notes/geomorphology-notes.html","title":"Geomorphology: Earth, Landforms &amp; Rocks — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geomorphology: Earth, Landforms & Rocks — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Earth's interior"},{"level":2,"text":"Plate tectonics & continental drift"},{"level":2,"text":"Rocks & minerals"},{"level":2,"text":"Landforms by agent (define-by-feature bank)"},{"level":2,"text":"Mountains & structural forms"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Geomorphology: Earth, Landforms & Rocks — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Physical Geography. Concept-definition questions (which landform is formed by X?) plus earth-interior facts. Earth's interior Layers: crust (continental sial 30–70 km, oceanic sima 5–10 km; Conrad discontinuity between) → Mohorovičić (Moho) → mantle (to 2,900 km; asthenosphere 100–200 km partially molten; Repetti 700 km) → Gutenberg → outer core (liquid; no S-waves; nife ) → Lehmann → inner core (solid; 5,150–6,371 km). Evidence: seismic waves — P (longitudinal, all media, fastest), S (transverse, solids only; shadow zone 105°–145° for S entirely beyond 105°; P shadow 105°–145°), L/R surface waves (most destructive). Density 2.7 crust → 13 core; temperature ~5,500 °C core. Earthquakes: focus/hypocentre & epicentre ; scales — Richter (magnitude, log₁₀; energy ×31.6 per unit), Mercalli (intensity I–XII),","chars":6349},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/bullets","file":"oceanography-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/bullet-points/oceanography-points.html","title":"Oceanography — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Oceanography — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Salinity flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Current flash"},{"level":2,"text":"ENSO-IOD flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Tide-resource flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Oceanography — Bullet Points Current-and-fact flash. Salinity flash Mean 35 ppt · max belt 20-30 lat · Red Sea ~41 highest · Baltic lowest · equator lower (rain dilution). Current flash Warm → Gulf Stream-NAD, Kuroshio, Brazil, Agulhas, East Australian · cold → Peru-Humboldt, Benguela, Canary, California, Oyashio, Labrador, West Australian. N Indian Ocean → unique monsoon drift reversal · gyres → clockwise N hemisphere. Upwelling fisheries → Peru, Benguela, Somali (summer monsoon). ENSO-IOD flash El Nino → east Pacific warm, weak monsoon bias · La Nina → opposite · IOD positive → good monsoon · MJO → 30-60-day pulse. Walker circulation weakens in El Nino years. Tide-resource flash Spring tide → full-new moon alignment · neap → quarter 90 degrees · Gulf of Khambhat highest Indian range. Mangroves → Sundarbans largest, Bhitarkanika second · reefs → fringing-barrier-atoll; bleaching = warm ","chars":1319},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/notes","file":"oceanography-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/detailed-notes/oceanography-detailed.html","title":"Oceanography — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Oceanography — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Relief, salinity, temperature"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Currents & oscillations"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Tides, coasts, resources"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Oceanography — Detailed Notes Ocean relief, salinity-temperature, currents, ENSO-IOD, tides-mangroves-resources. Statement-match territory. 1. Relief, salinity, temperature Relief: continental shelf (richest fishing-oil; India ~west coast wider), slope, rise, abyssal plain, trenches (Mariana ~11 km deepest; Java trench near India); mid-ocean ridges; seamounts-guyots. Salinity : mean 35 ppt; max belts = subtropical highs (~20-30 lat) due to evaporation>precipitation; Red Sea ~41 ppt (highest) , Dead Sea lake ~330; Baltic-lowest oceans-side (freshwater dilution); equator-lower (rain). Temperature: surface warm belts; annual range small (water heat capacity); thermocline layer; cold currents flow along the west coasts of continents (Benguela, Canary, California, Peru) . 2. Currents & oscillations Drivers: wind (trade push), Coriolis (gyres — clockwise N, anticlockwise S), density-thermohali","chars":2791},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/pyqs","file":"oceanography-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/pyqs/oceanography-mcqs.html","title":"Oceanography — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Oceanography — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Oceanography — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following is a cold current? (a) Kuroshio (b) Agulhas (c) Benguela (d) Brazil Q2. Consider ocean salinity: 1. It is highest at the equator due to high temperature. 2. The Bay of Bengal is less saline than the Arabian Sea. 3. Lake Van has salinity higher than the Dead Sea. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Spring tides occur: (a) Only in spring (b) At the first and third quarters of the moon (c) At new and full moon (d) When the moon is at apogee Q4. Under UNCLOS, the Exclusive Economic Zone extends up to: (a) 12 nautical miles (b) 24 nautical miles (c) 200 nautical miles (d) 350 nautical miles Q5. The Grand Banks of Newfoundland are a rich fishing ground because of the meeting of: (a) Kuroshio and Oyashio (b) Gulf Stream and Labrador (c) Brazil ","chars":1917},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/short","file":"oceanography-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/oceanography/short-notes/oceanography-notes.html","title":"Oceanography: Relief, Temperature, Salinity, Currents &amp; Tides — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Oceanography: Relief, Temperature, Salinity, Currents & Tides — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Relief & basics"},{"level":2,"text":"Temperature & salinity"},{"level":2,"text":"Currents (gyres clockwise NH, anticlockwise SH — Coriolis; western boundary currents warm, fast, narrow)"},{"level":2,"text":"Tides"},{"level":2,"text":"Coral & marine ecology hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Oceanography: Relief, Temperature, Salinity, Currents & Tides — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › Physical Geography. Current–coast matching, salinity anomalies, and tide mechanics. Relief & basics Oceans ~71% (361 million km²); Pacific (largest, deepest — Mariana Trench/Challenger Deep ~10,994 m ), Atlantic (S-shaped; Puerto Rico trench), Indian (Java/Sunda trench), Southern (60° S; 2000), Arctic (smallest, shallowest). Relief: continental shelf (avg 80 km; 150–200 m; 7.5%; fishing/oil — Siberian widest), continental slope (2–5°; shelf break; submarine canyons), continental rise, abyssal plain (40%), mid-ocean ridges (Mid-Atlantic; 65,000 km system), trenches (subduction), seamounts/guyots (flat-topped), atolls, submarine canyons (Congo), banks (Dogger, Grand Banks), Sea knolls. Sediments: terrigenous (mud), biogenic oozes (calcareous — Globigerina, pteropod (<4,000 m; CCD); ","chars":5141},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/physical-geography/short","file":"physical-geo-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/physical-geography/short-notes/physical-geo-prelims.html","title":"Physical Geography — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Physical Geography — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Earth's interior & rocks"},{"level":2,"text":"Tectonics & landforms"},{"level":2,"text":"Atmosphere & climate"},{"level":2,"text":"Oceans"},{"level":2,"text":"Soils & vegetation (world frame)"},{"level":2,"text":"Common traps"}],"text":"Physical Geography — Prelims Revision Earth's interior & rocks Layers: Crust (SIAL continental / SIMA oceanic) → Mantle (asthenosphere ductile zone ~80–200 km) → Core (NiFe — NIFE; outer liquid drives magnetosphere). Seismic traps: P-waves pass solid+liquid; S-waves solid only → outer-core liquid proof; shadow zones beyond 103°/142°. Rocks: Igneous (granite acidic, basalt basic; Deccan traps = flood basalt) → Sedimentary (sandstone/limestone/shale; fossils live here) → Metamorphic (gneiss from granite; marble from limestone; slate from shale; quartzite from sandstone). Tectonics & landforms Plates: 7 majors + minors; Indian plate = Indo-Australian fragment; boundaries: convergent (Himalayas = continent-continent), divergent (Mid-Atlantic ridge, East African Rift), transform (San Andreas). Fold vs fault-block vs volcanic mountains: Himalayas (fold), Vosges/Black Forest (block/horst), Fuji","chars":3910},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/pyqs","file":"geography-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/pyqs/geography-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Geography — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Geography — Prelims PYQs Map-pairing & pattern MCQs — answers original. Q1. [PYQ-pattern] Between India and East Asia, the navigation-time and petroleum-shipment matter revolves around: (a) Red Sea (b) Mediterranean Sea (c) Malacca Strait (d) Bering Strait Model answer: (c). Malacca is the choke point for India's east-bound energy & trade flows. Q2. Which of these rivers does not flow into the Bay of Bengal? (a) Godavari (b) Mahanadi (c) Narmada (d) Kaveri Model answer: (c). Narmada (west-flowing, joins Arabian Sea through Gulf of Khambhat estuary). Q3. Consider pairs: Pampas — Argentina Prairies — Australia Steppes — Eurasia Which are correct? (a) 1 and 3 only (b) 2 only (c) 1 and 2 only (d) 1, 2 and 3 Model answer: (a). Australia's grasslands are the Downs ; pair 2 is the distractor. Q4. Which tributary-side pairing is correct for the Ganga? (a) Ramganga — right bank (b) Kosi — right b","chars":2691},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/short","file":"geography-prelims-revision.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/short-notes/geography-prelims-revision.html","title":"Geography — Prelims Rapid Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Geography — Prelims Rapid Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Physiography of India (order from north)"},{"level":2,"text":"Rivers (tributary-side drill)"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate & monsoon mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"Soils pairing"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture-ecology pairs"},{"level":2,"text":"Mineral mapping"},{"level":2,"text":"World geography quick-hits"},{"level":2,"text":"Map practice pairs"}],"text":"Geography — Prelims Rapid Revision Map-pairing and pattern questions dominate. Pair with Mains geography sets for depth. Physiography of India (order from north) Himalayas : Trans (Karakoram-Ladakh-Zanskar-Kailash), Great (Himadri: passes Zoji La → Shipki La), Lesser (Pir Panjal-Dhauladhar-Mahabharat: hill stations sit here), Shiwaliks (duns between). Peninsular plateau : Deccan (trap), Central Highlands (Malwa/Bundelkhand/Chota Nagpur), NE extension (Meghalaya Plateau — one-time fractured eastward bit; Karbi-Anglong). Coastal plains : Western (Konkan+Karnataka+Malabar) vs Eastern (Utkal+Andhra+Coromandel) — estuaries vs deltas signature contrast. Islands : Andaman-Nicobar (volcanic line; Barren Island active volcano) vs Lakshadweep (coral). Rivers (tributary-side drill) Ganga: left-bank Ramganga/Gomti/Ghaghara/Gandak/Kosi; right-bank Yamuna (+ Chambal-Betwa-Ken lineage)/Son. Indus: Jhel","chars":3443},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/short","file":"world-geo-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/short-notes/world-geo-prelims.html","title":"World Geography — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Geography — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Extreme points drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Mountain & river pairs (world-map)"},{"level":2,"text":"Straits & canals (ships' choke points)"},{"level":2,"text":"Themes via region"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate-Koppen world quickloc"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC-typical \"sailor\" questions"},{"level":2,"text":"Map-point news cycle (2024–26)"}],"text":"World Geography — Prelims Revision Extreme points drill Highest: Everest (8,849 m, Nepal-China); K2 (8,611, Karakoram); Andean giant = Aconcagua (6,961 m, Argentina) ; Africa = Kilimanjaro (5,895); Europe = Elbrus (5,642, Caucasus — borderline-continent debate); Antarctica = Vinson (4,892); Australia = Kosciuszko; N.America = Denali (6,190); S.America = Aconcagua. Deepest/largest waters: Mariana Trench (Challenger Deep ~10,935 m) ; largest lake enclosed by area = Caspian Sea ; deepest + largest freshwater by volume = Baikal ; largest freshwater by surface = Lake Superior ; largest salt-water lake = Caspian (as \"lake\"), True-salt Great Salt Lake; largest river Amazon by discharge (~20% world river discharge), Nile by longest debate (Amazon-Nile contest revises). Deserts: Sahara (largest hot) · Arabian · Gobi (cold, Mongolia/China) · Kalahari · Atacama (driest, Andean rain-shadow) · Patago","chars":4041},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/bullets","file":"world-economic-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/bullet-points/world-economic-points.html","title":"World Economic — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Economic — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Mineral flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Industry-trade flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"World Economic — Bullet Points Belt-anchor flash. Agriculture flash Plantation → tea (India-Sri Lanka-Kenya), coffee (Brazil-Colombia), rubber (SE Asia), cocoa (Ivory Coast) · Mediterranean → citrus-olive-vine, winter rain. Dairy → NW Europe-NZ · grain → prairies-pampas-Australia · palm → Indonesia-Malaysia (~85%) · jhum → Amazon-Congo-NE India. Mineral flash Oil reserves → Persian Gulf ~48% · OPEC 1960 Baghdad (13 members; OPEC+ with Russia). Copper → Chile-Peru · cobalt → DRC · lithium triangle → Chile-Argentina-Bolivia (+Australia) · nickel → Indonesia. Rare earths → China dominance · uranium → Kazakhstan · iron exports → Australia-Brazil. Industry-trade flash Regions → Great Lakes belt, Ruhr-Rhine, Japan Pacific, China SEZ coast, Vietnam-Mexico rise. Arteries → Suez (~12-15% trade; 2021 Ever Given; 2024 reroute), Malacca busiest, Hormuz 20% oil, Panama drought caps. Ports → Shanghai ","chars":1272},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/notes","file":"world-economic-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/detailed-notes/world-economic-detailed.html","title":"World Economic Geography — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Economic Geography — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Agricultural systems"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Mineral-energy belts"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Industry regions & trade"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"World Economic Geography — Detailed Notes Agriculture types, resource belts, industry regions, trade arteries — the human-economy map. 1. Agricultural systems Types: subsistence-intensive (monsoon Asia), plantation (equatorial: tea-India-Sri Lanka-Kenya, coffee-Brazil-Colombia, rubber-SE Asia, cocoa-Ivory Coast), Mediterranean (citrus-olives-vines: Spain-Italy-Greece-California-Chile-Cape) , commercial grain (US-Canada prairies, Argentina pampas, Australia wheat belt), dairy (NW Europe, Denmark-Netherlands, NZ) , mixed farming, shifting (Amazon-Congo-NE India jhum) , ranching (US-Brazil-Australia), viticulture zones. Food-security geography: breadbaskets (Ukraine 'granary', US Midwest), rice bowls (Mekong delta-Thailand-Vietnam), palm (Indonesia-Malaysia ~85%), soy (Brazil-US-Argentina). 2. Mineral-energy belts Oil-Gas : Persian Gulf (~48% proven reserves; Saudi-Iran-Iraq-Kuwait-UAE), Ru","chars":2593},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/pyqs","file":"world-economic-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/pyqs/world-economic-mcqs.html","title":"World Economic &amp; Human Geography — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Economic & Human Geography — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"World Economic & Human Geography — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which country is the largest exporter of tea although China and India are the largest producers? (a) Sri Lanka (b) Kenya (c) Vietnam (d) Türkiye Q2. Match the mine/region with the mineral: A. Escondida — 1. Iron ore · B. Pilbara — 2. Copper · C. Bayan Obo — 3. Rare earths (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-2 C-1 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider cobalt: 1. The Democratic Republic of Congo supplies about 70% of world mine output. 2. China dominates cobalt refining. 3. Indonesia has emerged as the second-largest producer. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Which of the following pairs of shifting cultivation names and regions is/are correct? 1. Milpa — Mexico 2. Ladang — Malaysia 3. Chena — Sri Lanka (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d)","chars":1930},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/short","file":"world-economic-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-economic/short-notes/world-economic-notes.html","title":"World Economic &amp; Human Geography — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Economic & Human Geography — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture: leading producers (FAO 2023) & regions"},{"level":2,"text":"Minerals & energy: leaders"},{"level":2,"text":"Industry & trade geography"},{"level":2,"text":"Population & human geography (World Population Prospects 2024)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"World Economic & Human Geography — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › World Geography. Producer-rankings, resource belts, population/migration facts and geopolitical geography that UPSC pulls from current affairs. Agriculture: leading producers (FAO 2023) & regions Crop Top producers Regions/hooks Rice China, India, Bangladesh, Indonesia, Vietnam India largest exporter (40%) Wheat China, India, Russia, USA, France Russia largest exporter; Ukraine \"breadbasket\"; Prairies, Pampas, Punjab, Murray-Darling Maize USA (Corn Belt), China, Brazil, Argentina, India Soybean Brazil, USA, Argentina, China, India Cerrado deforestation Sugarcane Brazil, India, China, Thailand Cotton China, India, USA, Brazil, Pakistan Coffee Brazil, Vietnam (robusta), Colombia, Indonesia, Ethiopia (origin) India 7th Tea China, India, Kenya (largest exporter), Sri Lanka, Turkey Cocoa Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana (60%+","chars":15026},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/bullets","file":"world-physical-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/bullet-points/world-physical-points.html","title":"World Physical — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Physical — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Continent flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Landform flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Extreme flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"World Physical — Bullet Points Superlative flash. Continent flash Largest → Asia · plateau continent → Africa · flattest → Australia · ice world → Antarctica (98%) · peninsular mosaic → Europe. Oceans → Pacific (largest-deepest), Atlantic (S), Indian (monsoon), Southern (circumpolar), Arctic (smallest). Landform flash Highest range → Himalaya · longest → Andes · Europe-Asia divide → Urals · highest plateau → Tibet · largest plain → West Siberian. Rivers → Amazon (volume), Nile-Amazon (length race), Yangtze (Asia), Congo (deepest), Volga (Europe), Murray-Darling (Australia). Extreme flash Deserts → Sahara hot-king, Atacama driest, Gobi cold-winter, Danakil hottest · lowest land → Dead Sea. Lakes → Caspian largest-saline, Baikal deepest, Superior freshwater king · waterfall → Angel · island → Greenland. Straits → Hormuz-oil, Malacca-busiest, Bab-el-Mandeb-choked, Gibraltar-Atlantic · canal","chars":1291},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/notes","file":"world-physical-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/detailed-notes/world-physical-detailed.html","title":"World Physical Geography — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Physical Geography — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Continental anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Mountains, plateaus, rivers"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Deserts, extremes, straits"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"World Physical Geography — Detailed Notes Continents, mountains, plateaus, rivers, deserts, straits — atlas-speed recall. 1. Continental anchors Asia (largest ~44.6 Mkm2; Everest; Dead Sea lowest land), Africa (plateau continent; Sahara; Nile-Amazon river-length debate; Great Rift Valley; Kilimanjaro), North America (Rockies; Great Lakes; Mississippi-Missouri), South America (Andes longest; Amazon largest discharge; Atacama driest), Antarctica (~98% ice; Vinson Massif; no natives), Europe (peninsulas; Alps; Rhine), Australia/Oceania (flattest; Great Dividing Range; Great Barrier Reef). Oceans: Pacific largest-deepest (Mariana), Atlantic S-shape (mid-ridge), Indian (monsoon-driven north), Southern (Antarctic circumpolar), Arctic smallest-shallowest. 2. Mountains, plateaus, rivers Ranges: Himalaya-Karakoram (highest), Andes (longest), Rockies, Alps (fold-young), Urals (Europe-Asia divide),","chars":2494},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/pyqs","file":"world-physical-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/pyqs/world-physical-mcqs.html","title":"World Physical Geography — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Physical Geography — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"World Physical Geography — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which strait connects the Red Sea with the Gulf of Aden? (a) Hormuz (b) Bab-el-Mandeb (c) Tiran (d) Malacca Q2. Match the river with the sea/ocean it drains into: A. Volga — 1. Black Sea · B. Danube — 2. Caspian Sea · C. Rhine — 3. North Sea (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider Lake Baikal: 1. It is the deepest freshwater lake in the world. 2. It holds about 20% of the world's unfrozen surface freshwater. 3. It lies in a rift zone. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. The Atacama desert's extreme aridity is primarily due to: (a) Its location on the east coast of South America (b) The cold Humboldt current and rain-shadow of the Andes (c) Its high latitude (d) The warm Brazil current Q5. Which of the following ","chars":1961},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/short","file":"world-physical-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/geography/world-geography/world-physical/short-notes/world-physical-notes.html","title":"World Physical Geography: Continents, Oceans, Mountains, Rivers &amp; Climates — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"World Physical Geography: Continents, Oceans, Mountains, Rivers & Climates — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Continents & extremes"},{"level":2,"text":"Oceans, seas & straits (chokepoints)"},{"level":2,"text":"World climatic types (Köppen; region–vegetation–features)"},{"level":2,"text":"Volcanoes, quakes & hazards in news"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"World Physical Geography: Continents, Oceans, Mountains, Rivers & Climates — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Geography › World Geography. Map-anchored: place-in-news questions need a mental atlas; this card is that atlas in compressed form. Continents & extremes Asia (44.6 mn km²; Everest 8,849; Dead Sea −430 m lowest land; Caspian largest lake; Baikal deepest 1,642 m & largest by volume; Tibet highest plateau; Gobi cold desert; Arabian, Thar, Taklamakan, Kyzylkum, Karakum deserts; Yangtze longest 6,300; Mekong; Ob-Irtysh, Yenisei, Lena (Arctic); Amur; Indus–Ganga–Brahmaputra; Tigris–Euphrates (Mesopotamia; Shatt al-Arab); Aral Sea shrinkage; Lake Balkhash, Issyk-Kul, Tonlé Sap (reversing flow), Urmia, Van). Africa (30.4; Kilimanjaro 5,895 — stratovolcano Tanzania; Lake Assal Djibouti −155 m; Sahara largest hot desert; Kalahari, Namib (Sossusvlei), Danakil; Nile 6,650 longest (White Nil","chars":11581},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/bullets","file":"mahajanapadas-mauryan-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/bullet-points/mahajanapadas-mauryan-points.html","title":"Mahajanapadas & Mauryas — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mahajanapadas & Mauryas — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Order flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Mahajanapadas & Mauryas — Bullet Points Pair-recall sheet. Pairs flash 16 list source → Anguttara Nikaya · republics → Vajji, Malla, Shakya. Pataliputra founder → Udayin · siege machines → Ajatashatru. 2nd Council → Vaishali, Kalashoka · Nanda epithet → 'second Parashurama' (Mahapadma). Alexander's battle → Hydaspes/Jhelum × Porus · turn-back → Beas. Arthashastra → Kautilya · Indica → Megasthenes · Seleucus treaty → 305 BCE. Kalinga war → 261 BCE, 13th RE · dhamma officials → mahamattas. Provinces → Ujjain, Taxila, Tosali, Suvarnagiri · script NW → Kharoshthi. Named Ashoka edicts → Maski, Gujarra, Nettur, Udegolam · bilingual → Kandahar. Lion capital → Sarnath · first caves → Barabar (Lomas Rishi) · Didarganj → Yakshini. Tax → bhaga 1/6 · coin → karshapana/pana · spy → gudhapurusha · city head → nagaraka. Order flash Haryanka → Shishunaga → Nanda → Maurya (dynasties of Magadha). Maurya l","chars":1380},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/notes","file":"mahajanapadas-mauryan-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/detailed-notes/mahajanapadas-mauryan-detailed.html","title":"Mahajanapadas & Mauryas — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mahajanapadas and Mauryas — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The 16 Mahajanapadas (6th c. BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Magadhan rise"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Alexander (326 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Mauryan Empire (c. 322–185 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Mahajanapadas and Mauryas — Detailed Notes The making of the first empire: 16 states → Magadha → Nandas → Maurya → Ashoka's dhamma. UPSC loves ruler→measure and edict→place pairs. 1. The 16 Mahajanapadas (6th c. BCE) Source: Anguttara Nikaya (Buddhist) + Bhagavati Sutra (Jain list varies). Monarchies (Magadha, Kosala, Kashi, Vatsa...) vs ganasanghas/republics ( Vajji — Licchavis of Vaishali, Malla, Shakyas of Kapilavastu). Why Magadha wins: iron ore (Jharkhand belts), fertile Ganga valley, elephants, riverine trade, ambitious rulers, capitals Rajagriha → Pataliputra (Udayin — confluence point). 2. Magadhan rise Dynasty Key rulers Notes Haryanka Bimbisara (contemporary of Buddha/Mahavira — matrimony with Kosala/Lichchavi), Ajatashatru (Vaisali war; war-engines mahashilakantaka, rathamushala ), Udayin (founds Pataliputra) c. 544–412 BCE Shishunaga Shishunaga (destroys Avanti power), Kalash","chars":3208},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/pyqs","file":"mahajanapadas-mauryan-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/pyqs/mahajanapadas-mauryan-mcqs.html","title":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism &amp; Mauryas — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism & Mauryas — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism & Mauryas — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which Mahajanapada lay south of the Vindhyas? (a) Avanti (b) Chedi (c) Ashmaka (d) Matsya Q2. Match the Buddhist councils with patrons: A. First — 1. Kalashoka · B. Second — 2. Ajatashatru · C. Fourth — 3. Kanishka (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-2 B-3 C-1 (d) A-3 B-2 C-1 Q3. Consider Jainism: 1. Parshvanatha prescribed four vows; Mahavira added celibacy. 2. The final Jain canon was compiled at the Vallabhi council. 3. Mahavira attained kaivalya at Pavapuri. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. In which of the following places does Ashoka's personal name appear? (a) Girnar (b) Maski (c) Dhauli (d) Topra Q5. The Kandahar bilingual edict of Ashoka is in: (a) Brahmi and Kharosthi (b) Greek and Aramaic (c) Prakrit and Greek (d) ","chars":2261},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/short","file":"mahajanapadas-mauryan-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/mahajanapadas-mauryan/short-notes/mahajanapadas-mauryan-notes.html","title":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism &amp; the Mauryan Empire — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism & the Mauryan Empire — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Sixteen Mahajanapadas (c. 6th BCE; Anguttara Nikaya, Bhagavati Sutra)"},{"level":2,"text":"Buddhism"},{"level":2,"text":"Jainism"},{"level":2,"text":"Mauryan Empire (321–185 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Mahajanapadas, Buddhism, Jainism & the Mauryan Empire — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Ancient History. Heaviest-weighted ancient block: religion facts + Ashokan edicts + Mauryan administration. Sixteen Mahajanapadas (c. 6th BCE; Anguttara Nikaya, Bhagavati Sutra) Monarchies : Magadha (Rajgir/Girivraja → Pataliputra), Kosala (Shravasti; Prasenajit), Vatsa (Kaushambi; Udayana), Avanti (Ujjain/Mahishmati; Pradyota), Anga (Champa), Kashi (Varanasi), Chedi (Shuktimati), Matsya (Viratnagar), Shurasena (Mathura), Ashmaka (Potali/Paudanya — only one south of Vindhyas, Godavari), Gandhara (Taxila), Kamboja (Rajpur/Hatak). Republics (gana-sanghas) : Vajji/Vrijji (Vaishali; Licchavis), Malla (Kusinara & Pava), Kuru (Indraprastha), Panchala (Ahichchhatra & Kampilya). Magadha's rise: Haryanka ( Bimbisara — Gandhaka army, matrimonial; Ajatashatru — war engines rathamusala , maha","chars":5745},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/bullets","file":"post-mauryan-gupta-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/bullet-points/post-mauryan-gupta-points.html","title":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta Age — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta — Bullet Points Pair-recall sheet. Pairs flash Milindapanha → Menander × Nagasena · Heliodorus pillar → Besnagar. Junagadh (Sudarshana) → Rudradaman · St. Thomas → Gondophernes. Saka era → 78 CE Kanishka · council → 4th, Kundalvana · capitals → Purushpura + Mathura. Gautamiputra's eulogist → mother Gautami Balashri (Nasik) · Satavahana coins → lead. Muziris → Chera pepper port · Puhar → Chola · pearl → Pandya. Prayaga Prashasti → Harisena · Nalanda → Kumaragupta I · Fa-Hien → CG II. Aryabhatiya → 499 · iron pillar → 'Chandra' · Deogarh → Dashavatara temple. Kalidasa court → CG II · Banabhatta → Harsha (Harshacharita) · Xuanzang → Nalanda + Kannauj assembly. Land grants first regular → Satavahanas/Guptas (agrahara) · kumaramatya → Gupta officer. Silk Road empire → Kushana · Buddha image first → Gandhara & Mathura. Trap flash Samudragupta's pillar inscription is on Ash","chars":1182},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/notes","file":"post-mauryan-gupta-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/detailed-notes/post-mauryan-gupta-detailed.html","title":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta Age — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Post-Mauryan and Gupta Age — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Successor states (185 BCE – 320 CE)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Gupta age (320–550)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Post-Mauryan and Gupta Age — Detailed Notes Successor states (185 BCE–320 CE) → the 'classical' Guptas (320–550) → Harsha (606–647). Tests: ruler→book/inscription, era→founder, art→school. 1. Successor states (185 BCE – 320 CE) Power Key facts Sungas (185–73) Pushyamitra kills Brihadratha; Pataliputra/Vidisha; horse sacrifice revival; Sanchi stupa-2 railing; Patanjali (Mahabhashya) tradition Indo-Greeks First coins with king-portraits in India; Menander (Milinda) — dialogue with Nagasena = Milindapanha ; Heliodorus pillar (Besnagar — Bhagavata worship proof) Shakas 5 branches; Western Kshatrapas long-lived; Rudradaman I — Junagadh inscription (first major chaste-Sanskrit record; Sudarshana lake repaired, originally Mauryan); Vikram Samvat 57 BCE = victory over Shakas legend Parthians (Pahlavas) Gondophernes — St. Thomas mission tradition Kushanas (1st–3rd CE) Kujula → Vima → Kanishka (78","chars":3353},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/pyqs","file":"post-mauryan-gupta-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/pyqs/post-mauryan-gupta-mcqs.html","title":"Post-Mauryan &amp; Gupta — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Post-Mauryan & Gupta — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Junagadh rock inscription of 150 CE, the first long Sanskrit inscription, belongs to: (a) Gautamiputra Satakarni (b) Rudradaman I (c) Kanishka (d) Samudragupta Q2. The Heliodorus pillar at Besnagar records the devotion of a Greek ambassador to: (a) Buddha (b) Shiva (c) Vasudeva (d) Surya Q3. Match: A. Gandhara — 1. red sandstone · B. Mathura — 2. white marble · C. Amaravati — 3. grey schist (a) A-3 B-1 C-2 (b) A-1 B-3 C-2 (c) A-2 B-1 C-3 (d) A-3 B-2 C-1 Q4. Consider the Satavahanas: 1. They issued coins mainly in lead. 2. They used matronymics. 3. Naneghat inscription records the earliest land grant to Brahmanas. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q5. The Allahabad pillar inscription was composed by: (a) Harishena (b) Ravikirti (c) Banabhatta (d) Vas","chars":2081},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/short","file":"post-mauryan-gupta-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/post-mauryan-gupta/short-notes/post-mauryan-gupta-notes.html","title":"Post-Mauryan Age &amp; Gupta Empire (c. 185 BCE–550 CE) — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Post-Mauryan Age & Gupta Empire (c. 185 BCE–550 CE) — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Post-Mauryan dynasties"},{"level":2,"text":"Art schools"},{"level":2,"text":"Guptas (c. 320–550 CE)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Post-Mauryan Age & Gupta Empire (c. 185 BCE–550 CE) — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Ancient History. Dynasty–ruler–inscription/coin–art school matching, plus Gupta \"golden age\" facts. Post-Mauryan dynasties Shungas (185–73 BCE; Pushyamitra — ashvamedha; Patanjali Mahabhashya ; Bharhut stupa railings, Sanchi gateways began; Heliodorus pillar Besnagar 113 BCE — Garuda pillar, Bhagavata; Agnimitra — Kalidasa's Malavikagnimitram ) → Kanvas (Vasudeva 73 BCE). Satavahanas (Deccan; Simuka; Pratishthana/Paithan & Amaravati; Gautamiputra Satakarni — Nasik prashasti by Gautami Balashri; destroyed Shakas (Nahapana coins restruck); Vasishthiputra Pulumavi; Yajna Sri Satakarni — ship coins; lead coins ; Prakrit; matronymics; land grants to Brahmanas (earliest — Naneghat, Naganika); Karle, Bhaja, Kanheri chaitya; Amaravati school, Nagarjunakonda; Gathasaptasati (Hala); Brihatkat","chars":5106},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/pyqs","file":"ancient-history-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/pyqs/ancient-history-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Ancient History — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ancient History — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Ancient History — Prelims PYQs Q1. The Prayag Prashasti (Allahabad Pillar inscription) eulogises: (a) Chandragupta I (b) Samudragupta (c) Ashoka (d) Harsha Model answer: (b). Composed by court poet Harisena in Sanskrit — the classic match question. Q2. Milindapanho records a dialogue between: (a) Ashoka & Upagupta (b) Menander & Nagasena (c) Kanishka & Vasumitra (d) Harsha & Hieun Tsang Model answer: (b). Indo-Greek king Menander (Milinda) with monk Nagasena. Q3. [PYQ-pattern] Which of the following are associated with jainism? Rishabhanatha — 1st tirthankara 2. Parshvanatha — 22nd (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (a). Parshvanatha was the 23rd tirthankara; Mahavira 24th. Classic \"off-by-one\" trap. Q4. The Junagadh rock carries inscriptions of: (a) Ashoka only (b) Rudradaman I & Skandagupta only (c) Ashoka, Rudradaman I & Skandagupta (d) Kanishka & Samudragupta Mo","chars":2768},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/short","file":"ancient-history-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/short-notes/ancient-history-prelims.html","title":"Ancient History — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Ancient History — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Prehistory & IVC"},{"level":2,"text":"Vedic & Later Vedic"},{"level":2,"text":"Buddhism & Jainism"},{"level":2,"text":"Mauryas (322–185 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"Post-Mauryan & Deccan"},{"level":2,"text":"Guptas (320–550 CE)"},{"level":2,"text":"Text-author pairs (heavy rotation)"},{"level":2,"text":"Coins shorthand"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims traps"}],"text":"Ancient History — Prelims Revision Pairing-heavy card: sites↔findings, texts↔authors, rulers↔inscriptions. Complement history-culture/art-culture and mains indian-heritage-culture . Prehistory & IVC Palaeolithic → Mesolithic → Neolithic (Mehrgarh earliest agri-evidence ~7000 BCE); Chalcolithic. Bhimbetka (MP) rock shelters — UNESCO 2003. IVC (Mature 2600–1900 BCE) : Harappa (1921, Ravi; Daya Ram Sahni), Mohenjo-daro (1922, Indus; R D Banerji — Great Bath, Granary), Dholavira (Gujarat — water-harvesting, three-part city; UNESCO 2021); Lothal (dockyard), Kalibangan (ploughed field, fire altars), Chanhudaro (bead factory, no citadel), Banawali (barley hoard), Rakhigarhi (largest footprint claims), Sutkagendor (trading post), Surkotada (horse-bone debate). IVC craft: seals (humped bull iconic), steatite, bronze dancing girl (lost-wax), red sandstone torso (Harappa), script undeciphered (righ","chars":5667},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/bullets","file":"stone-bronze-age-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/bullet-points/stone-bronze-age-points.html","title":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Site flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Culture flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Confusables"}],"text":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Bullet Points Rapid-fire pairs. Cover the right column, recall the left. Site flash Attirampakkam — Acheulian, oldest Indian handaxe culture (TN). Bhimbetka — rock shelters, paintings (MP). Bagor / Adamgarh — earliest animal domestication (Raj/MP). Mehrgarh — wheat-barley farming, pre-Harappan (Balochistan). Burzahom — pit-dwellings, dog burials (Kashmir). Chirand — bone tools (Bihar). Hallur — ash mounds, cattle (Karnataka). Daimabad — bronze chariot/rhinoceros finds (Jorwe, MH). Inamgaon — Jorwe village, granary (MH). Brahmagiri — megaliths + ash mounds (Karnataka). Culture flash Palaeolithic → handaxes → Mesolithic → microliths → Neolithic → farming, pottery → Chalcolithic → copper + painted ware → Iron/Megalith → burials, plough. Black-and-red ware = Chalcolithic & Megalith signature. First rice: Koldihwa/Lahuradewa (UP); first wheat-barley: Mehrgarh. Conf","chars":1174},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/notes","file":"stone-bronze-age-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/detailed-notes/stone-bronze-age-detailed.html","title":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Palaeolithic (Old Stone Age) — c. 2.6 mya to c. 12,000 BCE"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Mesolithic (c. 10,000–6,000 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Neolithic (c. 7000–1000 BCE) — first farmers"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Chalcolithic (c. 2800–700 BCE) — copper + stone"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Iron Age & Megalithic (c. 1500–500 BCE south)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Reading Prehistory: How We Know What We Know"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Palaeolithic in Depth"},{"level":3,"text":"Lower Palaeolithic — the Acheulian world"},{"level":3,"text":"Middle Palaeolithic — flakes and prepared cores"},{"level":3,"text":"Upper Palaeolithic — blades, bone and the first art"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Mesolithic Transition"},{"level":2,"text":"9. The Neolithic: Regional Trajectories, Not One Revolution"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Chalcolithic Cultures — the Village Interlude"},{"level":2,"text":"11. The Iron Age and the Megalithic Complex"},{"level":2,"text":"12. Prelims Discrimination Drill"}],"text":"Stone, Bronze & Iron Ages — Detailed Notes MCQ core: match culture → tool type → site → region. Names and pairings below are the tested facts. 1. Palaeolithic (Old Stone Age) — c. 2.6 mya to c. 12,000 BCE Lower : handaxes, cleavers, choppers. Sites: Attirampakkam (Tamil Nadu — Acheulian dates pushed back to ~1.5 million years, among the oldest in India), Sohan valley (Pakistan), Bhimbetka (MP), Hunsgi (Karnataka). Middle : flakes, scrapers, borers (Nevasa, Belan valley). Upper : blades, burins, bone tools begin (Bhimbetka paintings begin late phase — hunting, dancing scenes). 2. Mesolithic (c. 10,000–6,000 BCE) Tiny microliths (composite tools); first rock paintings peak (Bhimbetka). Early domestication evidence: Bagor (Rajasthan), Adamgarh (MP) — sheep/goat; Langhnaj (Gujarat). 3. Neolithic (c. 7000–1000 BCE) — first farmers Site Region Firsts / features Mehrgarh Balochistan (Bolan rive","chars":15526},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/pyqs","file":"stone-bronze-age-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/pyqs/stone-bronze-age-mcqs.html","title":"Stone Age &amp; Indus Valley — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Stone Age & Indus Valley — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Stone Age & Indus Valley — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Match: A. Ploughed field — 1. Lothal · B. Dockyard — 2. Kalibangan · C. Only city without citadel — 3. Chanhudaro (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-2 C-1 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q2. Consider Harappan civilisation: 1. They used iron tools. 2. Their script is written boustrophedon. 3. The unicorn is the most common motif on seals. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Which Neolithic site in Kashmir is known for pit-dwellings and dog burials? (a) Gufkral (b) Burzahom (c) Chirand (d) Mehrgarh Q4. The Mesopotamian texts refer to the Indus region as: (a) Dilmun (b) Magan (c) Meluhha (d) Aratta Q5. Which Harappan site is UNESCO-inscribed (2021) and known for its water reservoirs and a large signboard? (a) Rakhigarhi (b) Dholavira (c) Lothal (d) Banawali Q6","chars":1689},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/short","file":"stone-bronze-age-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/stone-bronze-age/short-notes/stone-bronze-age-notes.html","title":"Prehistory: Stone Age &amp; Indus Valley Civilisation — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Prehistory: Stone Age & Indus Valley Civilisation — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Stone Age ladder"},{"level":2,"text":"Harappan/Indus (c. 2600–1900 BCE mature)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Prehistory: Stone Age & Indus Valley Civilisation — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Ancient History. Site–feature matching (especially Harappan) recurs almost every year. Stone Age ladder Palaeolithic (up to ~10,000 BCE): Lower — Soan valley (pebble tools), Bhimbetka, Attirampakkam, Hunsgi; Middle — Nevasa, Bhimbetka (flakes); Upper — Bhimbetka paintings, Patne, bone tools at Kurnool caves; Homo erectus Hathnora (Narmada) skull. Mesolithic (~10,000–6,000 BCE): microliths; Bagor (Rajasthan; earliest domestication evidence claim), Adamgarh (MP), Langhnaj (Gujarat), Sarai Nahar Rai & Mahadaha (UP; burials), Bhimbetka rock art. Neolithic : Mehrgarh (Balochistan; c. 7000 BCE; wheat-barley, cattle; pre-Harappan), Burzahom (Kashmir; pit dwellings, dog burials), Gufkral, Chirand (Bihar; bone tools), Koldihwa (rice claim ~6000 BCE), Hallur, Maski, Piklihal, Sanganakallu, Utnu","chars":3468},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/bullets","file":"vedic-age-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/bullet-points/vedic-age-points.html","title":"Vedic Age — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vedic Age — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Early → Later shift (6 keywords)"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Vedic Age — Bullet Points Fact pairs for last-hour revision. Pairs flash Oldest Veda → Rigveda · family mandalas → II–VII · latest → X (Purusha sukta). Iron → shyama ayas · plough → sira/ langala · god-of-rain-early → Indra. Sacrifices → rajasuya (coronation), vajapeya (race), ashvamedha (empire). Tax early → bali (voluntary) · officer → senani/gramani/purohita. Brahmana pairs → Aitareya-Rig · Satapatha-Yajur · Tandya/Panchavimsha-Sama · Gopatha-Atharva. Upanishad count → 108 (Muktika); principal → 11-13; Brihadaranyaka = largest. Women RV seers → Lopamudra, Ghosha, Apala, Vishwavara. Upaveda → Ayur/Dhanur/Gandharva/Shilpa × Rig/Yajur/Sama/Atharva. Soma = plant juice oblation · Yajna = fire ritual · Rita = cosmic order (Varuna). Kuru-Panchala = Later-Vedic heartland (Brahmana literature zone). Early → Later shift (6 keywords) Pastoral → agrarian-iron · tribe → janapada · elected-ish → he","chars":1257},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/notes","file":"vedic-age-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/detailed-notes/vedic-age-detailed.html","title":"Vedic Age — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vedic Age — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Early Vedic / Rigvedic (c. 1500–1000 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Later Vedic (c. 1000–600 BCE)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. The text ladder (memorise order + pairs)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Sources and the Problem of Chronology"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Early (Rigvedic) Period, c. 1500–1000 BCE"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Later Vedic Period, c. 1000–600 BCE"}],"text":"Vedic Age — Detailed Notes Two Vedic ages, one text ladder. UPSC tests text→content, god→period, term→meaning. 1. Early Vedic / Rigvedic (c. 1500–1000 BCE) Homeland: Sapta-Sindhu (Indus + Saraswati belt). Rigveda's oldest mandalas = II–VII (family books); X (Purusha sukta — varna origin). Economy: pastoral — cow = wealth unit (go-dhana); godhuli = distance; agriculture minor; nishka = gold ornament (later coin-name). Polity: tribe ( jana ) based; raja = war-leader elected-ish; sabha & samiti check king; officers: senani, gramani, purohita; bali voluntary tribute (no regular tax yet). Society: dasa varna (early class colour), women visible (Lopamudra, Ghosha, Apala; no child marriage, widow could remarry). Religion: personified nature — Indra (~250 hymns, war/rain), Varuna (rita), Agni (messenger), Soma; worship = hymn + sacrifice, no temples/idols . 2. Later Vedic (c. 1000–600 BCE) Texts","chars":10642},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/pyqs","file":"vedic-age-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/pyqs/vedic-age-mcqs.html","title":"Vedic Age — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vedic Age — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Vedic Age — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Gayatri mantra is found in which mandala of the Rig Veda and addressed to which deity? (a) 10th; Indra (b) 3rd; Savitri (c) 1st; Agni (d) 7th; Varuna Q2. Match: A. Chandogya — 1. Yajur · B. Brihadaranyaka — 2. Sama · C. Mundaka — 3. Atharva (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. The Battle of Ten Kings (Dasarajna) was fought on the river: (a) Sindhu (b) Vitasta (c) Parushni (d) Sarasvati Q4. Consider Rig Vedic polity: 1. Vidatha was the oldest assembly. 2. Bali was a compulsory tax. 3. The rajan was assisted by the purohita and senani. (a) 1 and 3 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 2 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q5. Painted Grey Ware culture is generally associated with: (a) Harappan phase (b) Rig Vedic period (c) Later Vedic period (d) Mauryan period Q6. Which Vedanga deals ","chars":1730},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/short","file":"vedic-age-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/ancient-history/vedic-age/short-notes/vedic-age-notes.html","title":"Vedic Age &amp; Later Vedic Period — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vedic Age & Later Vedic Period — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Sources & chronology"},{"level":2,"text":"The four Vedas"},{"level":2,"text":"Rig Vedic society & polity"},{"level":2,"text":"Later Vedic changes"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Vedic Age & Later Vedic Period — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Ancient History. Text-classification and social-term questions dominate. Sources & chronology Early/Rig Vedic c. 1500–1000 BCE (Sapta Sindhu — Punjab, Afghanistan); Later Vedic c. 1000–600 BCE (Ganga-Yamuna doab; Kuru-Panchala ); archaeology: Painted Grey Ware (PGW) = later Vedic; iron ( shyama/krishna ayas ) from ~1000 BCE (Atranjikhera). Boghaz-Koi (Hittite–Mitanni) inscription 1400 BCE — Indra, Mitra, Varuna, Nasatya. The four Vedas Veda Content Brahmana Upanishad (main) Hook Rig 1,028 hymns, 10 mandalas (2–7 family books oldest; 10th latest — Purusha Sukta, Nasadiya) Aitareya, Kaushitaki Aitareya, Kaushitaki Gayatri mantra (3rd mandala, Vishvamitra, to Savitri); hotri priest Sama melodies (1,549 verses; 75 original) Panchavimsha, Jaiminiya Chandogya, Kena udgatri ; origin of Indian music Yajur sacri","chars":3512},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/bullets","file":"architecture-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/bullet-points/architecture-points.html","title":"Indian Architecture — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Architecture — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Style-order flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Indian Architecture — Bullet Points Match-pair drills. Pairs flash Lomas Rishi chaitya-arch → Barabar (Ashoka) · grandest chaitya → Karle. Sanchi toranas → Satavahana carvings · Amaravati → white marble narrative. Kailasa Ellora → Krishna I (Rashtrakuta), top-down · Elephanta Shiva → Rashtrakuta line. Kandariya → Chandelas · Konark → Eastern Gangas · Modhera → Solankis. Brihadeshwara → Rajaraja I 1010 · Gangaikondacholapuram → Rajendra I. Pattadakal → Chalukya (UNESCO) · Belur-Halebidu → Hoysala star plans. Alai Darwaza → first true arch+gate dome · sloping walls → Tughlaqs. First garden tomb → Humayun's · Buland Darwaza → Fatehpur Sikri (Akbar). Pietra dura → Taj · Panch Mahal → Fatehpur Sikri · Peacock throne → Shah Jahan. Adina mosque → Bengal Pandua · curved roofs → Bengal style · Victoria Memorial → Indo-Saracenic. Style-order flash Stupa anatomy: medhi → anda → harmika → chhatra; g","chars":1339},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/notes","file":"architecture-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/detailed-notes/architecture-detailed.html","title":"Indian Architecture — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Architecture — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Stupas & caves"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Temple styles"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Indo-Islamic arc"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Indian Architecture — Detailed Notes Four exam arenas: stupas & caves, temple styles, Indo-Islamic, modern-colonial. Every question is a match-the-pair. 1. Stupas & caves Stupa anatomy : anda (dome) on medhi, harmika railing-box, chhatra umbrellas, vedika railing, torana gates (Sanchi's four — Satavahana-era carvings: Jatakas, aniconic Buddha). Sequence: Ashoka core (Sanchi-1, Bharhut, Piprahwa relic-claims) → Sunga railings → Satavahana gates (Sanchi) → Amaravati (white-marble narrative, Satavahana-Ikshvaku) → Gandhara dharmarajika (Kanishka's relic chambers). Caves : Barabar (Ashoka, Lomas Rishi chaitya-arch facade — earliest) → western chaitya-vihara schools (Bhaja, Kondane, Karle — Karle chaitya grandest, Nashik Pandav leni inscriptions of Gautamiputra/Ushavadata) → Ajanta (Hinayana + Mahayana phases: 9,10,12,13 early; 1,2,16,17 Vakataka) → Ellora (Buddhist-Hindu-Jain; Kailasa temple","chars":3054},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/pyqs","file":"architecture-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/pyqs/architecture-mcqs.html","title":"Architecture — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Architecture — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Architecture — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following is a feature of the Nagara style rather than Dravida? (a) Enclosure walls with gopurams (b) Curvilinear shikhara topped by an amalaka (c) Storeyed pyramidal vimana (d) A temple tank within the complex Q2. Consider the Hoysala temples: 1. Built mainly of soapstone. 2. Have stellate (star-shaped) plans. 3. Belur and Halebidu were inscribed as UNESCO sites in 2023. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match: A. Rekha deul — 1. Khajuraho · B. Panchayatana on high jagati — 2. Odisha · C. Trefoil arches — 3. Kashmir (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. At Ellora, the Kailasa temple is cave number: (a) 10 (b) 12 (c) 16 (d) 29 Q5. The \"double dome\" technique in India appears first in: (a) Alai Darwaza (b) Sikandar L","chars":1854},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/short","file":"architecture-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/architecture/short-notes/architecture-notes.html","title":"Architecture: Temple Styles, Forts &amp; Monuments — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Architecture: Temple Styles, Forts & Monuments — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Temple styles (Shilpa texts: Nagara north, Dravida south, Vesara Deccan)"},{"level":2,"text":"Indo-Islamic (arch, dome, minaret, pietra dura , jali , charbagh )"},{"level":2,"text":"Colonial"},{"level":2,"text":"UNESCO recent (India total 43 as of 2024)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Architecture: Temple Styles, Forts & Monuments — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Art & Culture. Style-identification by feature is the exam's approach; learn the vocabulary first. Temple styles (Shilpa texts: Nagara north, Dravida south, Vesara Deccan) Feature Nagara Dravida Vesara Superstructure shikhara curvilinear (rekha-prasada); amalaka + kalasha on top vimana pyramidal storeys ( talas ); stupi/shikhara octagonal cap hybrid; shorter vimana Enclosure usually none high walls, gopuram gateways, tank — Plan panchayatana ; garbhagriha, antarala, mandapa, ardha/maha-mandapa garbhagriha, antarala, mandapa , Nandi mandapa , dvarapalas stellate plans (Hoysala) Sub-schools Odisha/Kalinga ( deul , jagamohana , rekha/pidha/khakhara deul ; Lingaraja, Konark, Jagannath, Mukteshwar), Khajuraho/Chandela (Kandariya Mahadeva; sandstone; erotic panels; high jagati ), Solanki/Maru-","chars":4626},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/bullets","file":"fairs-festivals-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/bullet-points/fairs-festivals-points.html","title":"Fairs & Festivals — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fairs & Festivals — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Fairs & Festivals — Bullet Points State-festival match drills. Pairs flash Shipra → Ujjain Kumbh · Godavari → Nashik · sangam → Prayagraj · Ganga → Haridwar. Hornbill → Nagaland (Dec 1) · Chapchar Kut → Mizoram · Moatsu → Ao Naga · Wangala → Garo. Onam-Vallam Kali → Kerala · Thrissur Pooram → Sakthan Thampuran · Theyyam season → Kerala north. Chhath → Bihar (Surya Shashthi) · Nuakhai → Odisha · Sarhul → Jharkhand · Bihula → Bengal-Bihari folk. Pushkar → camel + Brahma temple · Sonepur → cattle/elephants · Surajkund → crafts · Bateshwar → cattle (UP). Hemis → Padmasambhava mask (Ladakh) · Losar → Tibetan new year · Ambubachi → Kamakhya tantric. Uttarayan kites → Gujarat · Gudi Padwa → Maharashtra · Ugadi → Karnataka-AP · Poush Sankranti → Bengal. Kumbh ICH → 2017 · Durga Puja → 2021 · Garba → 2023 · Yoga → 2016. Ramlila 2005 · Ramman (Uttarakhand) 2009 · Mudiyettu + Chhau + Kalbelia 2010 ","chars":1234},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/notes","file":"fairs-festivals-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/detailed-notes/fairs-festivals-detailed.html","title":"Fairs & Festivals — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fairs and Festivals — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Kumbh Mela"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Regional festival anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Fair classics (commodity + faith)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage — India"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Fairs and Festivals — Detailed Notes Anchor facts only: where, when, why, and the UNESCO-intangible list. Prelims loves state-festival mismatch. 1. Kumbh Mela 4 sites rotation: Haridwar (Ganga), Prayagraj (sangam), Ujjain (Shipra — Simhastha), Nashik (Godavari); 12-year cycle + ardha/magh variants; UNESCO ICH 2017 ; akhara processions (shahi snan procession order); 2019 Prayagraj largest gathering record. 2. Regional festival anchors Harvest trio: Pongal (TN, Jan — Thai month; Mattu day), Baisakhi/Lohri/Maghi (Punjab — Khalsa founding 1699 hook), Bihu (Assam — Bohag magh kati trio; Rongali = spring). South: Onam (Kerala — Mahabali-Vamana; pookalam, snake-boat Vallam Kali), Thrissur Pooram (Sakthan Thampuran origin), Hampi Utsav , Pongal-adjacent Mattu Pongal. East: Durga Puja (WB — UNESCO ICH 2021), Chhath (Bihar-Jharkhand — Surya-Shashthi, Kartik), Rath Yatra (Puri — 3 chariots; Jaganna","chars":2647},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/pyqs","file":"fairs-festivals-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/pyqs/fairs-festivals-mcqs.html","title":"Fairs, Festivals &amp; Heritage — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fairs, Festivals & Heritage — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Fairs, Festivals & Heritage — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Match: A. Chapchar Kut — 1. Nagaland · B. Hornbill — 2. Mizoram · C. Wangala — 3. Meghalaya (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q2. Which of the following Bihu festivals marks the Assamese New Year in April? (a) Bhogali Bihu (b) Kongali Bihu (c) Rongali Bihu (d) Magh Bihu Q3. Consider the Kumbh Mela: 1. It is held at four places on three different rivers plus the Sangam. 2. It was inscribed on UNESCO's ICH list in 2017. 3. The Ujjain Kumbh is called Simhastha. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. The Sonepur mela of Bihar is famous as: (a) A camel fair (b) Asia's largest cattle fair (c) A tribal barter fair (d) A crafts fair Q5. Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched? 1. Jonbeel mela — Assam, barter syst","chars":2006},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/short","file":"fairs-festivals-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/fairs-festivals/short-notes/fairs-festivals-notes.html","title":"Fairs, Festivals, Puppetry &amp; Cultural Heritage — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fairs, Festivals, Puppetry & Cultural Heritage — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Harvest & seasonal festivals (state map)"},{"level":2,"text":"Fairs (melas)"},{"level":2,"text":"Heritage institutions & tags"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Fairs, Festivals, Puppetry & Cultural Heritage — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Art & Culture. Festival–state–season matching, GI tags and heritage institutions. Harvest & seasonal festivals (state map) Makar Sankranti cluster (Jan 14–15): Pongal (TN; Bhogi, Thai/Surya, Mattu, Kaanum), Lohri (Punjab, day before), Bihu — Magh/Bhogali (Assam; also Rongali/Bohag Apr, Kati/Kongali Oct), Uttarayan kites (Gujarat), Makaravilakku (Sabarimala), Poush Sankranti (Bengal). Spring/new year (Apr): Baisakhi (Punjab; Khalsa 1699), Vishu (Kerala), Puthandu (TN), Poila Boishakh (Bengal), Bohag Bihu, Ugadi (AP/Karnataka), Gudi Padwa (Maharashtra), Cheti Chand (Sindhi), Navreh (Kashmir), Sajibu Cheiraoba (Manipur). Monsoon/autumn: Onam (Kerala; Mahabali; Vallamkali; Pookalam; Thiruvathira), Hornbill (Nagaland; Dec 1–10; Kisama), Nuakhai (Odisha), Wangala (Garo; 100 drums), Ka Pomblang","chars":5632},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/bullets","file":"music-dance-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/bullet-points/music-dance-points.html","title":"Music & Dance — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Music & Dance — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Dance flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Music flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Folk flash (state)"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Music & Dance — Bullet Points Match-pairs rapid fire. Dance flash Tribhanga → Odissi · tarangam plate → Kuchipudi · pacha green → Kathakali. Gharanas → Kathak (Lucknow/Jaipur/Benares) · Ras Leela → Manipuri · gotipua boys → Odissi. Sattras → Sattriya (Sankaradeva) · lasya left-bun → Mohiniyattam · Kalakshetra → Bharatanatyam revival. Nattuvanar → Bharatanatyam conductor · gayan-bayan → Sattriya ensemble. Music flash Carnatic Trinity → Tyagaraja, Dikshitar, Syama Sastri · kriti structure → pallavi-anupallavi-charanam. Khayal → Amir Khusrau (attribution) · dhrupad → oldest gravitas · thumri → semi-classical. Tansen → Akbar court, Miyan ki malhar · Bismillah Khan → shehnai · Shivkumar Sharma → santoor. Melakarta 72 → Carnatic raga scheme · rasa count → 8 (+shanta). Mizhavu → Kutiyattam · nadaswaram → temple south · sarangi → Hindustani accompaniment. Folk flash (state) Bihu-Assam · Garba-Gu","chars":1340},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/notes","file":"music-dance-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/detailed-notes/music-dance-detailed.html","title":"Music & Dance — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Music and Dance — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The 8 classical dances (Sangeet Natak Akademi)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Music systems"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Folk & theatre anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Music and Dance — Detailed Notes Two lists to memorise: 8 classical dances and the two music systems' anchors, plus folk favourites. 1. The 8 classical dances (Sangeet Natak Akademi) Dance State Signatures Bharatanatyam Tamil Nadu Solo temple (agamic) art; adavus (units), abhinaya (expression); repertory: alarippu→jatiswaram→varnam→tillana; nattuvanar conducts; devadasi ('sadhir') lineage; Rukmini Devi revival (Kalakshetra 1936) Kathak UP/North Storytelling 'katha'; gharanas: Lucknow (grace, Wajid Ali Shah era), Jaipur (virtuoso footwork), Benares; chakkars, tatkar; nritta+nritya; Mughal court phase Kathakali Kerala 'Story-play'; all-male tradition; vesham colour codes ( pacha green = noble , red beard = evil); mudra lexicon; chenda-maddalam; Krishnanattam-Ramanattam lineage Kuchipudi Andhra Village Kuchelapuram; Brahmin-bhagavathulu troupe plays; tarangam (plate dance on brass); Bhama K","chars":3171},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/pyqs","file":"music-dance-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/pyqs/music-dance-mcqs.html","title":"Music, Dance &amp; Theatre — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Music, Dance & Theatre — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Music, Dance & Theatre — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which classical dance uses the tarangam , performed on the rim of a brass plate? (a) Odissi (b) Kuchipudi (c) Mohiniyattam (d) Sattriya Q2. Consider: 1. Sattriya was recognised as a classical dance in 2000. 2. It originated in the Vaishnava monasteries founded by Sankaradeva. 3. It is performed to the chenda and maddalam . (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match: A. Tholu Bommalata — 1. Rajasthan · B. Kathputli — 2. Andhra Pradesh · C. Ravanachhaya — 3. Odisha (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. The 72 melakarta scheme of Carnatic music was systematised by: (a) Purandara Dasa (b) Venkatamakhin (c) Tyagaraja (d) Bhatkhande Q5. Khayal as a Hindustani form is associated with the patronage of: (a) Akbar (b) Muhammad Shah","chars":1770},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/short","file":"music-dance-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/music-dance/short-notes/music-dance-notes.html","title":"Music, Dance &amp; Theatre Forms — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Music, Dance & Theatre Forms — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Classical dance (8 recognised by Sangeet Natak Akademi; Ministry lists 8 — Chhau is SNA-listed too)"},{"level":2,"text":"Classical music"},{"level":2,"text":"Theatre & folk drama"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Music, Dance & Theatre Forms — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Art & Culture. State–form–exponent matching plus instrument classification. Classical dance (8 recognised by Sangeet Natak Akademi; Ministry lists 8 — Chhau is SNA-listed too) Dance State Text/tradition Hooks & exponents Bharatanatyam Tamil Nadu Natya Shastra , Abhinaya Darpana (Nandikeshvara) Sadir → revived by E. Krishna Iyer, Rukmini Devi Arundale (Kalakshetra 1936); Margam (alarippu → jatiswaram → shabdam → varnam → padam → tillana); ekaharya ; Balasaraswati, Padma Subrahmanyam, Alarmel Valli Kathak UP (Lucknow, Jaipur, Banaras gharanas) Kathakars, Bhakti (Raslila), Mughal courts tatkar , chakkars , gat , Wajid Ali Shah patron; Birju Maharaj, Sitara Devi, Shambhu Maharaj Kathakali Kerala Ramanattam , Krishnanattam male, masks-like make-up ( pacha green heroes, kathi , thadi , kari , minukku ); chenda,","chars":5339},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/bullets","file":"painting-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/bullet-points/painting-points.html","title":"Indian Painting — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Painting — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Indian Painting — Bullet Points School → patron → signature drills. Pairs flash Padmapani/Dying Princess → Ajanta (tempera) · Lepakshi → Vijayanagara murals · Mattancherry → Kerala. Pala → palm-leaf Buddhist · Jain school → Kalpasutra, Gujarat gold. Hamzanama/Tutinama/Razmnama → Akbar · Mansur flora-fauna → Jahangir · Padshahnama polish → Shah Jahan. Mir Sayyid Ali + Abdus Samad → arrive under Humayun · Bichitr/Bishandas → Jahangir portraits embassy scene. Chawand Ragamala → Mewar 1605 · Bani-Thani → Kishangarh (Nihal Chand) · Rasamanjari → Basohli · Gita-Govinda lyrical → Kangra. Manaku-Nainsukh → Guler-Kangra family · Sansar Chand → Kangra patron raja. Srikalahasti → pen kalamkari · Machilipatnam → block kalamkari. Kohbar → Madhubani · tarpa + chauk → Warli · Pabuji scroll → Phad · Jagannath → Odisha pata. Thangka → Ladakh/Sikkim · Cheriyal → Telangana · Sanjhi stencil → Mathura. Golco","chars":1349},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/notes","file":"painting-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/detailed-notes/painting-detailed.html","title":"Indian Painting — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Indian Painting — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Wall murals"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Miniature arc"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Folk-tribal living traditions"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Indian Painting — Detailed Notes Chain: wall murals → palm-leaf minis → Mughal atelier → Rajasthani-Pahari offshoots → folk living traditions. Pair school-patron-signature. 1. Wall murals Ajanta (2nd BCE–6th CE): tempera (fresco-secco) on mud-plaster; icons: Padmapani (cave 1), Dying Princess (16), Bodhisattva & Jataka panels (17); Chalukya Badami, Pallava Panamalai panels; Kailasa (Ellora) Shaiva panels; Lepakshi (Vijayanagara, Veerabhadra — hanging pillar lore); Kerala: Mattancherry (Ramayana panels), Guruvayur; Odisha: Raghurajpur pata-chitra (palm + cloth). Technique note: true fresco (wet lime) vs Indian tempera (dry plaster + binder) — one-line differentiator. 2. Miniature arc Pala (Bengal-Bihar, 8th-12th): palm-leaf Buddhist manuscripts, monastic patronage, Vajrayana icons. Jain (western Indian) : Kalpasutra/Kalakacharya katha; Gujarat-Rajasthan; profuse gold, 'pata' style; profil","chars":3351},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/pyqs","file":"painting-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/pyqs/painting-mcqs.html","title":"Painting — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Painting — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Painting — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Ajanta murals were executed in the: (a) True fresco (buon fresco) technique (b) Tempera on dry plaster (c) Oil on canvas (d) Encaustic technique Q2. Bani Thani is a celebrated painting of the: (a) Bundi school (b) Kishangarh school (c) Kangra school (d) Mewar school Q3. Consider Mughal painting: 1. Ustad Mansur was famed for natural-history studies under Jahangir. 2. The Hamzanama was commissioned by Shah Jahan. 3. Mir Sayyid Ali and Abdus Samad came with Humayun from Persia. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Match: A. Warli — 1. Bihar · B. Madhubani — 2. Maharashtra · C. Gond — 3. Madhya Pradesh (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q5. The \"farther eye\" protruding beyond the face is a hallmark of: (a) Pala manuscripts (b) Western ","chars":1643},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/short","file":"painting-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/painting/short-notes/painting-notes.html","title":"Painting: Cave, Miniature, Mughal &amp; Modern Schools — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Painting: Cave, Miniature, Mughal & Modern Schools — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Pre-historic & cave"},{"level":2,"text":"Manuscript/miniature (east & west)"},{"level":2,"text":"Mughal school"},{"level":2,"text":"Rajasthani (Rajput) schools"},{"level":2,"text":"Pahari schools"},{"level":2,"text":"Company & Modern"},{"level":2,"text":"Folk & tribal (GI-tagged mostly)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Painting: Cave, Miniature, Mughal & Modern Schools — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Art & Culture. School-identification by patron/theme/palette; a favourite for \"consider the following\" questions. Pre-historic & cave Bhimbetka (MP; Wakankar 1957; Mesolithic hunting scenes; UNESCO 2003). Ajanta (2nd BCE–6th CE; tempera/fresco-secco on dry plaster; Padmapani & Vajrapani (cave 1), Dying Princess (16), Jataka narratives; Vakataka phase), Bagh (MP; Gupta; secular), Sittanavasal (TN; Pandya; Jain; lotus pond), Ellora (Kailasa ceiling), Badami (Chalukya Mangalesha, cave 3 — earliest Brahmanical), Lepakshi (Vijayanagara; Veerabhadra; no primary colours, black outlines), Armamalai, Jogimara (Chhattisgarh; earliest c. 1st BCE), Kerala murals (Mattancherry, Padmanabhapuram). Vishnudharmottara Purana ( Chitrasutra ; shadanga — six limbs of painting, cited via Vatsyayana). Manu","chars":4595},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/pyqs","file":"art-culture-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/pyqs/art-culture-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Art &amp; Culture — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Art & Culture — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Art & Culture — Prelims PYQs Q1. Which of these classical dances draws from Sankardeva's Vaishnavite monasteries? (a) Manipuri (b) Sattriya (c) Odissi (d) Kathak Model answer: (b). Classical since 2000; ankia naats + borgeet core. Q2. The \"Bani Thani\" painting belongs to which school? (a) Bundi (b) Kishangarh (c) Kangra (d) Kangra-Basohli hybrids Model answer: (b). Nihal Chand's iconic curvilinear-portrait style, Kishangarh court (Rajasthan). Q3. Regarding manuscripts/scripts: Kharoshthi was written right-to-left. Brahmi was used in most Ashokan edicts. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (c). Kharoshthi right-to-left (Gandhara region) vs Brahmi left-to-right — standard dual trap. Q4. UNESCO intangible heritage entry for Garba corresponds to which year? (a) 2017 (b) 2021 (c) 2023 (d) 2024 Model answer: (c). Sequencing anchors: Kumbh 2017, Durga Puja 2021, Garba 2023,","chars":2672},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/bullets","file":"scriptures-languages-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/bullet-points/scriptures-languages-points.html","title":"Scriptures, Languages & Scripts — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Scriptures & Scripts — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Script flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Language flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Scripture flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Scriptures & Scripts — Bullet Points Match-drills. Script flash Indus → undeciphered, ~400 signs · Brahmi → Prinsep 1837, L-to-R · Kharosthi → NW, R-to-L, Aramaic kin. Devanagari line → Brahmi → Gupta → Siddham · Tamil line → Tamil-Brahmi → Vatteluttu. Gandhari manuscripts → Kharosthi birch-bark (Buddhist). Language flash Gathasaptashati → Hala, Maharashtri · Brihatkatha → Gunadhya · Milindapanha → Pali. Ashtadhyayi → Panini · shabdanushasana → Chandragomin. Avadhi → Tulsidas Ramcharitmanas · Braj → Surdas · Nastaliq → Urdu. Manipravalam → Malayalam-Sanskrit blend · Ezhuthachan → Malayalam father-figure. Classical tags → Tamil first (2004), then Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, Odia (+ recent adds — re-verify). Scripture flash Shruti vs Smriti → heard vs remembered corpora · 4 Vedas → Rig-Yajur-Sama-Atharva. Tipitaka → Vinaya-Sutta-Abhidhamma · Visuddhimagga → Buddhaghosa · Kangyur-","chars":1395},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/notes","file":"scriptures-languages-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/detailed-notes/scriptures-languages-detailed.html","title":"Scriptures, Languages & Scripts — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Scriptures, Languages and Scripts — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Scripts"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Languages & literature"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Scripture corpora (pair faith → canon)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Scriptures, Languages and Scripts — Detailed Notes Three chains: scripts (Indus → Brahmi → derivatives), language families (Indo-Aryan/Dravidian/Austro-Asiatic/Tibeto-Burman), and the scripture corpora per faith. 1. Scripts Indus : c.2600-1900; sign-count ~375-400; undeciphered; direction debated (right-to-left majority view), boustrophedon on some seals. Brahmi : Ashokan 3rd c BCE, first deciphered 1837 by James Prinsep ); written left-to-right; parent of Devanagari, Tamil-Brahmi, Kannada-Telugu, Bengali-Assamese, Odia, Gujarati, Sinhala, Tibetan lineages. Kharosthi : NW (Gandhara), right-to-left, Aramaic-derived; dies ~3rd c CE; Gandhari Prakrit + Kharosthi manuscripts (Buddhist, found as Gandhara birch-bark scrolls). Evolution mnemonic: Brahmi → Gupta script → Siddham → Devanagari (c.11th); southern: Tamil-Brahmi → Vatteluttu & Tamil. 2. Languages & literature Indo-Aryan : Vedic Sansk","chars":3449},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/pyqs","file":"scriptures-languages-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/pyqs/scriptures-languages-mcqs.html","title":"Scriptures, Languages &amp; Literature — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Scriptures, Languages & Literature — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Scriptures, Languages & Literature — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Tirukkural belongs to which corpus of Tamil literature? (a) Ettutogai (b) Pattuppattu (c) Padinenkilkanakku (d) Tirumurai Q2. Match: A. Rajatarangini — 1. Somadeva · B. Kathasaritsagara — 2. Kalhana · C. Harshacharita — 3. Banabhatta (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q3. Consider the Eighth Schedule: 1. It currently lists 22 languages. 2. English is one of them. 3. Bodo, Dogri, Maithili and Santali were added by the 92nd Amendment. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Which of the following classical languages is NOT in the Eighth Schedule? (a) Odia (b) Malayalam (c) Prakrit (d) Kannada Q5. The Kharosthi script: 1. Was written right to left. 2. Was derived from Aramaic. 3. Was used in the Shahbazgarhi and Mans","chars":1748},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/short","file":"scriptures-languages-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/scriptures-languages/short-notes/scriptures-languages-notes.html","title":"Scriptures, Languages &amp; Literature — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Scriptures, Languages & Literature — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Religious & philosophical texts"},{"level":2,"text":"Secular classics"},{"level":2,"text":"Languages & scripts"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Scriptures, Languages & Literature — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Art & Culture. Text–author–language matching and the Eighth Schedule/classical language list. Religious & philosophical texts Vedic corpus (see Vedic card): Samhitas → Brahmanas → Aranyakas → Upanishads; Vedangas; Sutras (Shrauta, Grihya, Dharma — Gautama, Baudhayana, Apastamba, Vasishtha); Smritis — Manusmriti (c. 200 BCE–200 CE), Yajnavalkya (Mitakshara by Vijnaneshvara; Dayabhaga — Jimutavahana, Bengal), Narada, Parashara. Epics: Mahabharata (Vyasa; 18 parvas; Jaya 8,800 → Bharata 24,000 → 1,00,000 shatasahasri samhita ; Bhagavad Gita in Bhishma parva, 18 chapters, 700 shlokas), Ramayana (Valmiki; 7 kandas; 24,000 verses; adikavya ). Puranas (18 Maha; pancha-lakshana — sarga, pratisarga, vamsha, manvantara, vamshanucharita; Vishnu, Bhagavata, Matsya (oldest), Vayu, Markandeya (Devi Mahatmya), Agn","chars":8809},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/art-culture/short","file":"art-culture-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/art-culture/short-notes/art-culture-prelims.html","title":"Art &amp; Culture — Prelims Drill Sheet","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Art & Culture — Prelims Drill Sheet"},{"level":2,"text":"Temples ↔ dynasty ↔ state"},{"level":2,"text":"Paintings ↔ themes"},{"level":2,"text":"Dance-theatre-state (memorise as row)"},{"level":2,"text":"Music pairs"},{"level":2,"text":"Languages / literature"},{"level":2,"text":"UNESCO intangible (India) quick list"},{"level":2,"text":"GI firsts & icons"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap lines"}],"text":"Art & Culture — Prelims Drill Sheet Speed-pairing volume; overlaps mains indian-heritage-culture — that set carries the depth; this is the pairing grid. Temples ↔ dynasty ↔ state Kandariya Mahadeva — Chandela — MP (Khajuraho UNESCO) Lakshmana, Vishvanatha, Parshvanatha — Chandela — Khajuraho cluster Brihadisvara — Rajaraja I Chola — Thanjavur TN (UNESCO 1987 core) Gangaikonda Cholapuram — Rajendra I — TN Airavatesvara — Rajaraja II — Darasuram TN Shore temple, Pancha Rathas — Pallava — Mahabalipuram (Narasimhavarman I/II) Kailasa (cave 16) — Rashtrakuta Krishna I — Ellora MH Trimurti — Elephanta caves — MH (Kalachuri-era attribution) Sun temple Konark — Narasimhadeva I E Ganga — Odisha (13th c.) Jagannath Puri — Eastern Ganga — Odisha Lingaraja — Somavamshi-Kesari lineage — Bhubaneswar Hoysaleswara Halebidu, Chennakesava Belur — Hoysala — Karnataka (UNESCO 2023) Vithala & Virupaksha — Vi","chars":4776},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/bullets","file":"delhi-sultanate-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/bullet-points/delhi-sultanate-points.html","title":"Delhi Sultanate — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Bullet Points Pair-recall sheet. Pairs flash Iqta + tanka-jital + Chihalgani → Iltutmish · zil-i-Ilahi spy net → Balban. dagh-chehra + 4 markets + Shahna-i-Mandi → Alauddin · Malik Kafur → Deccan raids. Token currency + Daulatabad + diwan-i-amir-kohi → M.B. Tughlaq · Rihla → Ibn Battuta. Canals + jizya + Firoz Shah Kotla pillar → Firoz Shah · Timur 1398 → Sayyid rise. Agra 1504 + gaz-i-sikandari → Sikandar Lodi · Panipat-1 → Ibrahim vs Babur 1526. Alai Darwaza → first true arch · octagonal tombs + garden trend → Lodis · sloping walls → Tughlaq. Tarikh-i-Firoz Shahi + Fatwa-i-Jahandari → Barani · Futuh-us-Salatin → Isami. Chishti line → Moinuddin → Nizamuddin → Nasiruddin Chirag · Suhrawardi → Bahauddin Multani. Marco Polo → Pandyas · Abdur Razzaq + Nicolo Conti → Vijayanagara · Nikitin → Bahmani. Nizamuddin Auliya → Chishti Delhi · Amir Khusrau → his disciple, khayal-ta","chars":1338},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/notes","file":"delhi-sultanate-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/detailed-notes/delhi-sultanate-detailed.html","title":"Delhi Sultanate — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The five dynasties"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Administration, economy, society"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Art, architecture, travellers"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Detailed Notes Five dynasties 1206–1526. Exam anchors: institutional terms (iqta, dagh), market reforms, architecture firsts, traveller visits. 1. The five dynasties Dynasty Span Anchors Mamluk (Slave) 1206-90 Aibak (Lahore; Arhai-din-ka-jhompra), Iltutmish (Delhi shift; iqta system; tanka-jital bimetallic; Chihalgani 'the Forty'; completes Qutub; iqta-tul-mash), Razia (1236-40, only woman), Balban (divine-right theory 'zil-i-Ilahi', spy system, chihalgani crushed, Persian etiquette sijda-paibos) Khalji 1290-1320 Jalaluddin murdered by Alauddin ; Chittor 1303 (Padmini tradition) ; Devagiri; Deccan raids (Malik Kafur — Hoysalas, Pandyas); market reforms : 4 markets, price control, dagh (brand) + chehra (descriptive roll) , grain state-monopoly, land-measure (biswa), first permanent standing-pay army, first Muslim conquest of South fully ; Ziauddin Barani chronicles Tughl","chars":3567},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/pyqs","file":"delhi-sultanate-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/pyqs/delhi-sultanate-mcqs.html","title":"Delhi Sultanate — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Delhi Sultanate — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Turkan-i-Chahalgani was: (a) Alauddin's market regulation (b) A corps of forty Turkish nobles created by Iltutmish (c) A Mongol frontier garrison (d) Firoz Tughlaq's slave department Q2. Consider Alauddin Khalji's military reforms: 1. Introduction of dagh and chehra 2. Payment of soldiers through iqta only 3. Maintenance of a large standing army paid in cash (a) 1 and 3 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 2 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Diwan-i-Kohi was a department of: (a) Charity (b) Agriculture (c) Slaves (d) Correspondence Q4. Which Sultan shifted two Ashokan pillars to Delhi? (a) Iltutmish (b) Alauddin Khalji (c) Firoz Shah Tughlaq (d) Sikandar Lodi Q5. Match: A. Tabaqat-i-Nasiri — 1. Barani · B. Tarikh-i-Firozshahi — 2. Minhaj-us-Siraj · C. Futuh-us-Salatin — 3. Isami (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-","chars":1827},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/short","file":"delhi-sultanate-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/delhi-sultanate/short-notes/delhi-sultanate-notes.html","title":"Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Dynasties & rulers (five, in order)"},{"level":2,"text":"Administration vocabulary"},{"level":2,"text":"Culture & architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Medieval History. Ruler–reform–monument matching plus a few economic-administration terms. Dynasties & rulers (five, in order) Dynasty Years Rulers & hooks Mamluk / Slave 1206–90 Aibak (Qutb Minar begun, Quwwat-ul-Islam, Adhai-din-ka-Jhonpra; died playing chaugan 1210) · Iltutmish (real founder; capital Lahore→Delhi; silver tanka & copper jital ; Iqta system; Chahalgani/Turkan-i-Chahalgani (40); mansab of Caliph 1229; completed Qutb Minar) · Raziya 1236–40 (first woman ruler; Yakut) · Balban (1266–87; Zil-i-Ilahi , sijda & paibos , Persian Nauroz ; blood-and-iron; crushed Chahalgani; Diwan-i-Arz military dept) Khalji 1290–1320 Jalaluddin · Alauddin (1296–1316) — market control ( Diwan-i-Riyasat , Shahna-i-Mandi ), first standing army with dagh (branding) & chehra (descriptive roll), land revenue 50% ( khar","chars":3485},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/bullets","file":"early-medieval-kingdoms-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/bullet-points/early-medieval-kingdoms-points.html","title":"Early Medieval Kingdoms — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Early Medieval Kingdoms — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Early Medieval Kingdoms — Bullet Points Power → anchor drills. Pairs flash Elected Gopala → Palas · Vikramashila + Somapura → Dharmapala · Gitagovinda court → Lakshmanasena. Gwalior prashasti + Adivaraha coins → Mihira Bhoja (Pratihara) · Arab shield → Nagabhata I. Kailasa Ellora → Krishna I · Kavirajamarga → Amoghavarsha · Manyakheta → Rashtrakuta capital. Khajuraho Kandariya → Chandelas · Modhera + Dilwara + Somnath → Solankis · Bhojshala + Samarangana → Bhoja (Paramara). Chandawar 1194 → Jaichandra (Gahadavala) · Nadia sack → Bakhtiyar Khalji vs Senas. Brihadeshwara → Rajaraja I · Srivijaya raid + Gangaikondacholapuram → Rajendra I · Uttaramerur kudavolai → Chola sabha. Ainnurruvar Five Hundred → Chola trade guild · manigramam → south guild too · Song China embassy 1077. Sindh 712 → M.B. Qasim · Somnath 1025 → Mahmud Ghazni · Tarain II 1192 → Ghori. Trap flash Tripartite war contestan","chars":1266},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/notes","file":"early-medieval-kingdoms-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/detailed-notes/early-medieval-kingdoms-detailed.html","title":"Early Medieval Kingdoms (750-1200) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Early Medieval Kingdoms (c.750–1200) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. North & Deccan powers"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Imperial Cholas (850–1279)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Pan-Indian features"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Early Medieval Kingdoms (c.750–1200) — Detailed Notes Post-Harsha political mosaic: tripartite Kannauj war (Palas-Pratihara-Rashtrakuta), Deccan switches, southern imperial Cholas, and eastern/northern satellites. 1. North & Deccan powers Power Core anchors Palas (Bengal-Bihar) Gopala (elected — Tibetan tradition); Dharmapala (founded Vikramashila + Somapura; Kannauj nominee Chakrayudha), Devapala (Balaputradeva letters, Nalanda grants); Buddhist patrons; Mahipala I late revival Gurjara-Pratiharas Nagabhata I (shield against Arab raids), Vatsaraja-Mihira Bhoja (Adivaraha coins; Gwalior prashasti traces the 'Pratihara' line), Mahendrapala I (Kashmir poet reach — Rajasekhara); Kannauj seat; decline → Ghazni window Rashtrakutas Dantidurga overthrows Chalukyas; Krishna I — Kailasa Ellora ; Dhruva & Govinda III (both Kannauj campaigns); Amoghavarsha ( Kavirajamarga — earliest Kannada poetics ","chars":3262},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/pyqs","file":"early-medieval-kingdoms-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/pyqs/early-medieval-kingdoms-mcqs.html","title":"Rajputs, Palas &amp; Cholas — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Rajputs, Palas & Cholas — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Rajputs, Palas & Cholas — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Kailasa temple at Ellora was built under the patronage of: (a) Chalukya Pulakeshin II (b) Rashtrakuta Krishna I (c) Chola Rajaraja I (d) Pallava Narasimhavarman Q2. The Uttaramerur inscriptions describing the kudavolai system belong to the reign of: (a) Rajaraja I (b) Rajendra I (c) Parantaka I (d) Kulottunga I Q3. Match: A. Somapura Mahavihara — 1. Dharmapala · B. Vikramashila — 2. Dharmapala · C. Odantapuri — 3. Gopala (a) A-1 B-2 C-3 (b) A-3 B-1 C-2 (c) A-2 B-3 C-1 (d) A-1 B-3 C-2 Q4. Consider: 1. Rajendra I led a naval expedition against the Srivijaya kingdom. 2. He assumed the title Gangaikonda after reaching the Ganga. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Q5. Which temple is associated with the Eastern Ganga ruler Narasimhadeva I? (a) Lingaraja (b) Jag","chars":1851},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/short","file":"early-medieval-kingdoms-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/early-medieval-kingdoms/short-notes/early-medieval-kingdoms-notes.html","title":"Rajput Kingdoms, Palas &amp; Cholas (c. 750–1200) — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Rajput Kingdoms, Palas & Cholas (c. 750–1200) — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Tripartite struggle for Kannauj (8th–9th c.)"},{"level":2,"text":"Rajput clans"},{"level":2,"text":"Cholas (imperial phase 850–1279)"},{"level":2,"text":"Other south"},{"level":2,"text":"Arab & Turkish contact"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Rajput Kingdoms, Palas & Cholas (c. 750–1200) — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Medieval History. Dynasty–capital–founder–monument matching is the pattern. Tripartite struggle for Kannauj (8th–9th c.) Palas (Bengal-Bihar; Gopala elected c. 750; Dharmapala — Vikramashila, Somapura/Paharpur; Devapala — Nalanda patron; Buddhist), Pratiharas (Nagabhata I repelled Arabs; Mihira Bhoja ; Kannauj capital; Al-Masudi calls them Al-Juzr), Rashtrakutas (Dantidurga; Krishna I — Kailasa temple Ellora ; Amoghavarsha — Kavirajamarga , Manyakheta; Govinda III; Indra III sacked Kannauj 916). Rajput clans Chauhans/Chahamanas (Ajmer-Delhi; Prithviraj III — Tarain 1191 win, 1192 loss; Prithviraj Raso Chand Bardai), Chandelas (Khajuraho; Kandariya Mahadeva c. 1030; Vidyadhara vs Mahmud), Paramaras (Malwa; Bhoja — Bhojpur, Bhojshala, Samarangana Sutradhara ), Solankis/Chaulukyas (Gujarat; ","chars":2972},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/bullets","file":"marathas-vijayanagara-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/bullet-points/marathas-vijayanagara-points.html","title":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Bullet Points Pair-drills. Pairs flash Harihara-Bukka 1336 → Vijayanagara (Sangama) · Alauddin Hasan 1347 → Bahmani (Gulbarga). Raichur 1520 + Amuktamalyada + Ashtadiggajas → Krishnadevaraya · Conti-Abdur Razzaq → Devaraya I-II visits. Talikota 1565 → Rama Raya death, Hampi sack · Aravidu = last dynasty. Mahmud Gawan → Bahmani wazir, executed 1481 · 5 sultanates → Nizam-Adil-Qutb-Imad-Barid. Kitab-i-Nauras + Jagadguru → Ibrahim Adil Shah II · Hyderabad 1591 → Quli Qutb Shah. Pratapgad 1659 → Afzal Khan · Lal Mahal 1663 → Shaista Khan · Purandar 1665 → Jai Singh · Agra escape 1666. Coronation 1674 Raigad → Chatrapati Shivaji · ashtapradhan 8 · chauth-sardeshmukhi. Sambhaji executed 1689 · Jinji → Rajaram · Tarabai regency · Shahu 1707 return. Peshwa line → Balaji Vishwanath → Baji Rao I → Balaji Baji Rao (Panipat-3 1761) → Madhavrao → Baji Rao II. Salbai","chars":1358},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/notes","file":"marathas-vijayanagara-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/detailed-notes/marathas-vijayanagara-detailed.html","title":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani and Marathas — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Vijayanagara (1336–1646 shell)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Bahmani & Deccan sultanates"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Marathas (Shivaji → Peshwas → annexation)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani and Marathas — Detailed Notes Three Deccan-centred powers: Vijayanagara (1336–1565+), Bahmani & successors (1347–1600s), Marathas (1640s–1818). 1. Vijayanagara (1336–1646 shell) Founding: Harihara I + Bukka (Sangama dynasty; Hampi — UNESCO; Vidyanagara Muslim-episode lineage claim); dynasties: Sangama → Saluva → Tuluva → Aravidu . Devaraya I (dam on Tungabhadra; Italian travellers — Nicolo Conti), Devaraya II (Gajapati wars; Abdur Razzaq visit; Muslim archers in his army), Krishnadevaraya (1509-29; Raichur doab win 1520 ; Gulbarga-Bijapur pressure; Amuktamalyada (Telugu) and 'Usha Parinayam'; patron of Ashtadiggajas; Vitthala temple completion era) → Achyuta → Sadasiva (puppet) under Rama Raya → TALIKOTA 1565 (Rakkasa-Tangadi; confederacy of Deccan sultanates; Rama Raya executed; Hampi sacked) → Aravidu shell till 1640s. Administration: nayankara system (nayak milit","chars":3265},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/pyqs","file":"marathas-vijayanagara-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/pyqs/marathas-vijayanagara-mcqs.html","title":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani &amp; Marathas — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Marathas — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Arrange the Vijayanagara dynasties in order: 1. Tuluva 2. Saluva 3. Aravidu 4. Sangama (a) 4-2-1-3 (b) 4-1-2-3 (c) 2-4-1-3 (d) 4-2-3-1 Q2. The Persian envoy Abdur Razzaq visited Vijayanagara during the reign of: (a) Harihara II (b) Deva Raya II (c) Krishnadeva Raya (d) Achyuta Raya Q3. Amuktamalyada , composed by Krishnadeva Raya, is a work in: (a) Sanskrit (b) Kannada (c) Telugu (d) Tamil Q4. Which Deccan Sultanate did NOT join the coalition at Talikota (1565)? (a) Bijapur (b) Golconda (c) Ahmednagar (d) Berar Q5. Mahmud Gawan is associated with: (a) Vijayanagara (b) Bahmani kingdom (c) Bijapur (d) Golconda Q6. In Shivaji's Ashtapradhan, the Sumant was responsible for: (a) Finance (b) Foreign affairs (c) Justice (d) Religious matters Q7. Consider: 1. Chauth ","chars":1703},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/short","file":"marathas-vijayanagara-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/marathas-vijayanagara/short-notes/marathas-vijayanagara-notes.html","title":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani &amp; Maratha Kingdoms — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Maratha Kingdoms — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Vijayanagara (1336–1646)"},{"level":2,"text":"Bahmani (1347–1527) & Deccan Sultanates"},{"level":2,"text":"Marathas"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Vijayanagara, Bahmani & Maratha Kingdoms — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Medieval History. Dynasties, travellers, battles, and Maratha administrative terms. Vijayanagara (1336–1646) Founded 1336 by Harihara & Bukka (Sangama; guru Vidyaranya); capital Hampi (Tungabhadra). Four dynasties: Sangama (1336–1485; Deva Raya II — \"Gajabetekara\", Turkish archers, Abdur Razzaq visit 1443) → Saluva (1485–1505; Narasimha) → Tuluva (1505–70; Krishnadeva Raya 1509–29 — Amuktamalyada (Telugu), Jambavati Kalyanam (Sanskrit), Ashtadiggajas (Allasani Peddana — Andhra Kavita Pitamaha), Raichur 1520, Hazara Rama & Vitthala temples, Krishnapura; Portuguese Domingo Paes, Duarte Barbosa; Nuniz under Achyuta Raya) → Aravidu (1570–1646; Penukonda, Chandragiri, Vellore). Talikota/Rakshasi-Tangadi 1565 — Rama Raya killed by Deccan Sultanate coalition (Bijapur, Ahmednagar, Golconda, Bidar; Ber","chars":3996},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/bullets","file":"mughal-empire-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/bullet-points/mughal-empire-points.html","title":"Mughal Empire — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mughal Empire — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Mughal Empire — Bullet Points Emperor → anchor drills. Pairs flash Panipat-1 + Khanwa + Ghaghra → Babur · Baburnama (Turki) · tulughma flank. Sher Shah 1540-55 → rupee-dam, sarais, GT road, patta-qabuliyat, Sasaram tomb. Panipat-2 (Hemu) → Akbar-Bairam · Ibadat Khana 1575 · Din-i-Ilahi 1582 · sulh-i-kul. mansabdari zat-sawar → Akbar · dahsala/zabti → Todarmal · Ain-i-Akbari → Abul Fazl · Muntakhab-ut-Tawarikh → Badayuni. Fatehpur Sikri + Buland Darwaza + Salim Chishti → Akbar · navaratna list recall. Itmad-ud-Daula (pietra dura first tomb) + Chain of Justice + Tuzuk + Mansur + Roe → Jahangir (Nur Jahan junta). Taj 1632-53 + Red Fort + Peacock Throne + Moti Masjid → Shah Jahan · Samugarh 1658 → succession. jizya 1679 + Fatawa-e-Alamgiri + Tegh Bahadur 1675 + 27-yr Deccan → Aurangzeb · Bijapur 1686 + Golconda 1687. Rangeela + Nadir Shah 1739 → Muhammad Shah · Panipat-3 → Abdali vs Marathas","chars":1452},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/notes","file":"mughal-empire-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/detailed-notes/mughal-empire-detailed.html","title":"Mughal Empire — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mughal Empire — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The Great Mughals"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Later Mughals & decline mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Economy, society, culture"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Mughal Empire — Detailed Notes 1526–1857. Six greats + later decline. Exam anchors: battles, reforms (mansab-zabt), culture outputs, traveller accounts, decline mechanics. 1. The Great Mughals Emperor Span Anchors Babur 1526-30 Panipat-1 1526 (Ibrahim Lodi; tulughma + artillery — 'Rumi Khan' gunners), Khanwa 1527 (Rana Sanga), Chanderi, Ghaghra 1529; Baburnama (Turki memoir) ; Aram Bagh garden at Agra; Humayun succeeds (Chausa 1539 + Kannauj 1540 losses → Sher Shah interregnum 1540-55 : rupee-dam standardisation, Grand Trunk (sarais, sadak), patta-qabuliyat land deeds; Sasaram tomb) Akbar 1556-1605 Panipat-2 1556 (Hemu; Bairam Khan regent), Chittor 1568, Ranthambhor, Gujarat, Bengal; sulh-i-kul ; Ibadat Khana 1575, Din-i-Ilahi 1582 ; mansabdari (zat-sawar ranks), dahsala/zabti (Todarmal) , karori experiment; suba-sarkar-pargana frame; Fatehpur Sikri (1571+; Salim Chishti), Allahabad fort","chars":3991},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/pyqs","file":"mughal-empire-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/pyqs/mughal-empire-mcqs.html","title":"Mughal Empire — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mughal Empire — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Mughal Empire — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Ain-i-Dahsala revenue system was formulated by: (a) Bairam Khan (b) Todar Mal (c) Abul Fazl (d) Mir Fathullah Shirazi Q2. Consider: 1. Akbar abolished jizya in 1564. 2. Aurangzeb reimposed it in 1679. 3. Jahangir abolished the pilgrim tax. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. The du-aspa sih-aspa rank was introduced by: (a) Akbar (b) Jahangir (c) Shah Jahan (d) Aurangzeb Q4. Pietra dura inlay work first appears on a large scale in: (a) Humayun's tomb (b) Itimad-ud-Daula's tomb (c) Taj Mahal (d) Akbar's tomb at Sikandra Q5. Match: A. Razmnama — 1. Dara Shukoh · B. Sirr-i-Akbar — 2. Persian Mahabharata · C. Muntakhab-ut-Tawarikh — 3. Badauni (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q6. The Battle of Samugarh (1658) was fought between: ","chars":1729},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/short","file":"mughal-empire-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/mughal-empire/short-notes/mughal-empire-notes.html","title":"Mughal Empire (1526–1707) — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Mughal Empire (1526–1707) — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Rulers & battles"},{"level":2,"text":"Administration terms"},{"level":2,"text":"Culture"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Mughal Empire (1526–1707) — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Medieval History. Battles, buildings, books and revenue terms — the four B-R pillars. Rulers & battles Babur (1526–30): Panipat I 21 Apr 1526 ( tulughma , araba ; Ibrahim Lodi), Khanwa 1527 (Rana Sanga; jihad , tamgha abolished), Chanderi 1528, Ghaghra 1529; Baburnama (Turki); Kabuli Bagh mosque Panipat. Humayun (1530–40, 1555–56): Chausa 1539 & Kannauj 1540 vs Sher Shah; regained Delhi 1555 (Machhiwara, Sirhind); died 1556 (library fall); Gulbadan's Humayunnama ; Dinpanah. Sur interregnum (1540–55): Sher Shah — rupiya (178 grains), dam , Grand Trunk Road repair & sarais, patta-qabuliyat , land measurement ( sikandari gaz , zabt seeds), Purana Qila, Sasaram tomb, Rohtasgarh; died Kalinjar 1545; Tarikh-i-Sher Shahi (Abbas Khan Sarwani). Akbar (1556–1605): Panipat II 1556 (Hemu; Bairam Khan regent till 1560), ","chars":3750},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/pyqs","file":"medieval-history-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/pyqs/medieval-history-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Medieval History — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Medieval History — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Medieval History — Prelims PYQs Q1. [PYQ-pattern] The token-currency experiment is associated with: (a) Alauddin Khilji (b) Muhammad bin Tughlaq (c) Sher Shah Suri (d) Balban Model answer: (b). ~1330 bronze/brass token tanka; parallel trap: market control is Khilji's. Q2. Which ordered pair of foreign travellers–rulers is correct? (a) Ibn Battuta — Akbar (b) Marco Polo — Pandyan ruler Maravarman Kulasekhara era (Madurai courts) (c) Nicolo Conti — Krishnadevaraya court (d) Sir Thomas Roe — Jahangir Model answer: (b) and (d) are correct — trap format: if forced one, (d) is the canonical pair (1615, Mughal court), while (c) is wrong (Conti came ~1420, Devaraya II era). Q3. Battle of Talikota/Rakkasa-Tangadi (1565) led to: (a) End of Delhi Sultanate (b) Collapse of Vijayanagara power (c) First Maratha expansion (d) Mughal accession Model answer: (b). Deccan sultanate confederacy defeated Ram","chars":2676},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/short","file":"medieval-history-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/medieval-history/short-notes/medieval-history-prelims.html","title":"Medieval History — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Medieval History — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526)"},{"level":3,"text":"Slave/Mamluk (1206–1290)"},{"level":3,"text":"Khilji (1290–1320)"},{"level":3,"text":"Tughlaq (1320–1414)"},{"level":2,"text":"Vijayanagara & Deccan"},{"level":2,"text":"Bhakti & Sufi"},{"level":2,"text":"Mughals (1526–1857)"},{"level":2,"text":"Marathas & regional"},{"level":2,"text":"Travellers ledger"},{"level":2,"text":"Prelims traps"}],"text":"Medieval History — Prelims Revision Sultanate–Mughal–Maratha rail; Bhakti/Sufi pairs are the perennial supply. Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) Slave/Mamluk (1206–1290) Qutb-ud-din Aibak (Lahori start, Lakh Baksh charitable reputation; Qutub Minar begins; died 1210 chaugan accident). Iltutmish (1211–36) : consolidator — Iqta system formalised, Chalisa/Chahalgani (Forty), Silver Tanka + Copper Jital coinage, completed Qutb Minar; Raziya Sultan (1236–40) — first woman ruler of Delhi; Balban (1266–87) — Divine Right theory (Zil-e-Ilahi), Sijda & Paibos protocol, crushed Mewatis, spy system (barids). Khilji (1290–1320) Alauddin (1296–1316) : market control (3 markets: food, cloth, horses+slaves+ghee/oil — Mint + Shahana-i-Mandi supervision), first major Mongol-repeller (Kili 1299 etc.), Deccan expeditions via Malik Kafur (Devagiri/Warangal/Dwarasamudra/Madurai), Alai Darwaza + Siri fort, Raja-Koh","chars":6893},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/bullets","file":"constitutional-development-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/bullet-points/constitutional-development-points.html","title":"British Constitutional Development — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Acts flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Constitutional Development — Bullet Points Act → provision drills. Acts flash 1773 Regulating → first GG (Hastings) + Council + SC Calcutta 1774 · 1781 Amending → SC-council clarity. 1784 Pitt's → Board of Control dual rule · 'British possessions' phrase. 1813 Charter → trade open (tea-China kept), ₹1L education · 1833 → GG-of-India (Bentinck), Macaulay law member · 1853 → open competition + 12-member legislative council. 1858 → Company ends; Secy of State + Council of India (15); Viceroy = Canning. 1861 → Indian nominees + portfolio system (Canning) + ordinances · 1892 → indirect-election hint, budget questions. 1909 Morley-Minto → separate electorates + Sinha first Indian exec-councillor. 1919 Montford → dyarchy (provinces), bicameral centre, direct elections, Lee Commission promise. 1927 Simon (all-white) → Nehru Report 1928 (dominion+FR) → Jinnah 14 Points 1929 → Purna Swaraj Lahore ","chars":1651},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/notes","file":"constitutional-development-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/detailed-notes/constitutional-development-detailed.html","title":"British Constitutional Development — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"British Constitutional Development — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Company-era framework (1773-1858)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Crown-era gradualism (1861-1919)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Endgame acts (1928-1947)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"British Constitutional Development — Detailed Notes Charter acts → Crown rule → gradual Indianisation: each act's one-line signature. 1. Company-era framework (1773-1858) Regulating Act 1773 : first written intervention — Governor-General (Warren Hastings) + 4-member council, Supreme Court Calcutta 1774 (Impey), Bombay-Madras subordination partial, parliamentary oversight seeds; Amending Act 1781 (clarifies SC vs council — Act of Settlement). Pitt's India Act 1784 : dual government — Board of Control (political) + Court of Directors (commercial) frame; 'British possessions in India' first constitutional phrase. Charter Acts : 1793 (routine renewal; salaries from India), 1813 (trade monopoly ends except tea-China; ₹1 lakh education-religion; missionaries open), 1833 (Company becomes purely administrative trustee; GG-of-India title — Bentinck first; law member Macaulay; central legislative","chars":3476},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/pyqs","file":"constitutional-development-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/pyqs/constitutional-development-mcqs.html","title":"Constitutional Development — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Constitutional Development — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which Act designated the Governor-General of Bengal as the Governor-General of India? (a) Regulating Act 1773 (b) Charter Act 1833 (c) Charter Act 1853 (d) GoI Act 1858 Q2. Consider: 1. The Charter Act of 1853 introduced open competition for the civil services. 2. The same Act fixed the Company's charter for another 20 years. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Q3. The portfolio system in the Governor-General's council was introduced under: (a) Indian Councils Act 1861 (b) Indian Councils Act 1892 (c) Indian Councils Act 1909 (d) GoI Act 1919 Q4. The first Indian to join the Viceroy's Executive Council was: (a) Sir Dinkar Rao (b) Satyendra Prasad Sinha (c) Tej Bahadur Sapru (d) Srinivasa Sastri Q5. Which of the following was introduced by the GoI Act 1919? 1","chars":2022},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/short","file":"constitutional-development-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/constitutional-development/short-notes/constitutional-development-notes.html","title":"Constitutional Development &amp; Government of India Acts — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Development & Government of India Acts — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Company rule (1773–1853)"},{"level":2,"text":"Crown rule (1858–1947)"},{"level":2,"text":"Franchise & representation ladder"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Constitutional Development & Government of India Acts — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History (also feeds Polity). \"Which Act introduced X?\" — this card is the answer bank. Company rule (1773–1853) Act Key provisions Regulating Act 1773 Governor of Bengal → Governor-General of Bengal (Warren Hastings) with 4-member council; Supreme Court at Calcutta 1774 (Impey); Company servants barred from private trade; Court of Directors to report revenue/civil/military affairs Amending Act 1781 (Act of Settlement) Governor-General & council exempted from SC jurisdiction for official acts Pitt's India Act 1784 Board of Control (political) + Court of Directors (commercial) — \"double government\"; territories first called \"British possessions in India\" Act of 1786 Cornwallis could override council; GG also C-in-C Charter Act 1793 20-year charter renewal; salaries from India","chars":3706},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/bullets","file":"east-india-company-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/bullet-points/east-india-company-points.html","title":"East India Company & Expansion — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"EIC & Expansion — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"EIC & Expansion — Bullet Points Match-drills. Pairs flash 1600 charter → Elizabeth I · Machilipatnam 1611 first factory · Swally 1612 → Surat · Roe → Jahangir farman. 1717 farman → Farrukhsiyar (dastak abuse) · Bombay 1668 → Catherine dowry lease. Carnatic wars 1746-63 · Wandiwash 1760 → Eyre Coote vs Lally (Dupleix era). Plassey 1757 → Clive-Siraj-Mir Jafar · Buxar 1764 → Munro vs triumvirate · Diwani 1765 → Allahabad treaty. Regulating Act 1773 → Warren Hastings · Pitt's Act 1784 → Board of Control · impeachment → Burke, acquitted 1795. Mysore 4 wars → Haidar-Tipu · Seringapatam 1799 · Subsidiary alliance → Wellesley (Hyderabad, Bassein 1802). Lapse → Dalhousie: Satara 1848, Jhansi 1853, Nagpur 1854 · Awadh 1856 'misrule'. Punjab 1849 → Dalhousie (2 Sikh wars; Chilianwala-Gujarat) · Sind 1843 → Napier · Sugauli 1816 → Nepal. Sati 1829 → Bentinck (+Rammohan) · English 1835 → Macaulay mi","chars":1494},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/notes","file":"east-india-company-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/detailed-notes/east-india-company-detailed.html","title":"East India Company & Expansion — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"East India Company and Expansion — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Entry & early trade"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Conquest arc"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Governor-General anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Land revenue systems"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"East India Company and Expansion — Detailed Notes Traders → rulers (1600–1858). Anchors: battles, Governor-Generals + reforms, land systems, subsidiary diplomacy. 1. Entry & early trade EIC chartered 1600 (Elizabeth I); first factory Machilipatnam 1611 ; Surat factory after the Battle of Swally 1612 (vs Portuguese); Jahangir farman via Thomas Roe 1615-18 ; 1717 Farrukhsiyar farman = 'magna carta' duty-free Bengal trade (dastaks abused); Bombay ceded 1668 (Catherine dowry → Company lease). Rivals: Portuguese (decline post-Swally), Dutch (Batavia focus), Danes, French Compagnie 1664; Dupleix — Carnatic wars 1746-63 (3 wars) : Madras 1746, Trichinopoly-Severndroog arc , Wandiwash 1760 (Eyre Coote vs Lally) → Pondicherry ceded militarily-ended. 2. Conquest arc Plassey 1757 (Clive; Siraj-ud-Daula; Mir Jafar conspiracy; Jagat Seth bankers; Omichund bribe plot), Buxar 1764 (Munro; Mir Qasim + S","chars":3246},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/pyqs","file":"east-india-company-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/pyqs/east-india-company-mcqs.html","title":"Advent of Europeans &amp; EIC — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans & EIC — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans & EIC — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions (statement, sequence, match). Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Arrange in chronological order of establishing a settlement in India: 1. Dutch 2. Danes 3. French 4. Portuguese (a) 4-1-2-3 (b) 4-1-3-2 (c) 1-4-2-3 (d) 4-2-1-3 Q2. Consider the statements about the Treaty of Allahabad (1765): It granted the Diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa to the Company. It was signed with Siraj-ud-Daulah. Kora and Allahabad were given to Shah Alam II. Which are correct? (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. The \"Dual Government\" of Bengal was abolished by: (a) Robert Clive (b) Warren Hastings (c) Cornwallis (d) Wellesley Q4. Which of the following was annexed by Dalhousie on grounds of misgovernance rather than the Doctrine of Lapse? (a) Jhansi (b) Satara (c) Awadh (d) Nagpur Q5. Match the land-reven","chars":2246},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/short","file":"east-india-company-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/east-india-company/short-notes/east-india-company-notes.html","title":"Advent of Europeans &amp; East India Company (1757–1857) — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company (1757–1857) — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Arrival sequence (the classic ordering question)"},{"level":2,"text":"Bengal: from trader to ruler"},{"level":2,"text":"Expansion wars & instruments"},{"level":2,"text":"Land-revenue systems"},{"level":2,"text":"Regulating framework (Acts you must sequence)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Advent of Europeans & East India Company (1757–1857) — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History. Dedicated leaf card; the branch pack is ../../short-notes/modern-history-prelims-revision.html and the practice set is ../pyqs/east-india-company-mcqs.html . Arrival sequence (the classic ordering question) Portuguese 1498 (Vasco da Gama, Calicut; Cochin factory 1503; Goa captured by Albuquerque 1510) → Dutch 1602 VOC (Masulipatnam 1605, Pulicat; defeated at Bedara 1759) → English EIC charter 31 Dec 1600 (Surat factory 1613; Madras 1639; Bombay 1668 from Charles II; Calcutta 1690 Job Charnock) → Danes 1616 (Tranquebar, Serampore) → French 1664 (Surat 1668, Pondicherry 1674 by François Martin). Carnatic Wars: I (1746–48, Aix-la-Chapelle) · II (1749–54, Dupleix recalled) · III (1758–63, Wandiwash 1760 , Paris 1763 — French reduced to trading posts). Bengal: from trade","chars":3154},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/bullets","file":"gandhian-era-1919-1947-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/bullet-points/gandhian-era-1919-1947-points.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Bullet Points Movement → anchor drills. Pairs flash Karachi khilafat conf 1921 → Ali brothers · Chauri Chaura 5 Feb 1922 → Bardoli withdrawal · Swarajists → Motilal-C.R. Das (1923 councils). Dandi 12 Mar-6 Apr 1930 · Khudai Khidmatgars → Ghaffar Khan (NWFP) · Dharsana raid → Sarojini Naidu. Gandhi-Irwin 5 Mar 1931 → RTC-2 (sole rep) · Karachi 1931 → FR+economic resolution (Bhagat Singh mourning). Communal Award 16 Aug 1932 → separate dalit seats · Poona Pact 24 Sep 1932 → joint electorate+reserved (Ambedkar-Gandhi). HSRA 1928 → Bhagat Singh trio; Saunders 1928; Assembly 1929; executions 23 Mar 1931 · Chittagong 1930 → Surya Sen (Pritilata-Kalpana). 1937 ministries → 8 provinces, resign Oct 1939 · Lahore 23 Mar 1940 → Pakistan resolution · August Offer 1940 · Individual satyagraha first → Vinoba. Cripps Mar 1942 → 'post-dated cheque' · Quit India 8 Aug 1942 → Gowa","chars":1865},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/notes","file":"gandhian-era-1919-1947-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/detailed-notes/gandhian-era-1919-1947-detailed.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Khilafat & Non-Cooperation (1920-22)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Civil Disobedience (1930-34)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Ministries, wartime, Quit India (1937-45)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Wartime-end negotiations → Partition (1945-47)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Detailed Notes Mass politics: Non-Cooperation → Civil Disobedience → provincial ministries → Quit India → partition settlement. 1. Khilafat & Non-Cooperation (1920-22) Khilafat movement (Ali brothers; Karachi khilafat conference 1921 ; Istanbul abolition 1924 ends cause) + Punjab grievances + Swaraj demand → Calcutta special session Sept 1920 → Nagpur Dec 1920 programme : boycott councils-courts-schools-titles-foreign cloth; Chauri Chaura 5 Feb 1922 → Bardoli resolution Feb 1922 withdrawal (Nehru-Bose dismay); Swarajists (Motilal-C.R. Das — council entry 1923) vs No-changers (constructive: khadi, untouchability, Harijan 1933 paper ). 2. Civil Disobedience (1930-34) Salt March: Sabarmati 12 Mar 1930 → Dandi 6 Apr (241 miles; 78 volunteers); parallel Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (Khudai Khidmatgars, NWFP) , Sholapur rising, Dharsana raid ( Sarojini Naidu ; Webb Miller d","chars":3445},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/pyqs","file":"gandhian-era-1919-1947-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/pyqs/gandhian-era-1919-1947-mcqs.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Non-Cooperation programme was ratified with a new Congress constitution at the: (a) Calcutta special session 1920 (b) Nagpur session 1920 (c) Ahmedabad session 1921 (d) Gaya session 1922 Q2. Which session adopted the resolution on Fundamental Rights and National Economic Programme? (a) Lahore 1929 (b) Karachi 1931 (c) Faizpur 1936 (d) Haripura 1938 Q3. Consider the statements on the Poona Pact (1932): 1. It replaced separate electorates for depressed classes with reserved seats under joint electorates. 2. It was signed between Gandhi and Jinnah. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Q4. Arrange chronologically: 1. Cripps Mission 2. August Offer 3. Quit India 4. Individual Satyagraha (a) 2-4-1-3 (b) 4-2-1-3 (c) 2-1-4-3 (d) 1-2-4-3 Q5. The parallel \"Jatiya Sarka","chars":1960},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/short","file":"gandhian-era-1919-1947-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/gandhian-era-1919-1947/short-notes/gandhian-era-1919-1947-notes.html","title":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"1919–1922: Khilafat & Non-Cooperation"},{"level":2,"text":"1927–1931: Simon to Gandhi–Irwin"},{"level":2,"text":"1932–1939: Communal Award to war"},{"level":2,"text":"1940–1947: Endgame"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Gandhian Era 1919–1947 — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History. The dense timeline questions come from here; memorise the year-to-event ladder. 1919–1922: Khilafat & Non-Cooperation Khilafat Committee 1919 (Ali brothers, Maulana Azad); All-India Khilafat Conference Nov 1919 Delhi; Gandhi president. Non-Cooperation adopted at Calcutta special session Sept 1920 (Lala Lajpat Rai), confirmed Nagpur Dec 1920 (C. Vijayaraghavachariar) — new Congress constitution, PCCs on linguistic basis, 4-anna membership. Programme: boycott of titles, courts, schools, councils, foreign cloth; Tilak Swaraj Fund (₹1 crore); national institutions (Jamia Millia, Kashi Vidyapeeth, Gujarat Vidyapeeth). Chauri Chaura 5 Feb 1922 → Bardoli resolution 12 Feb withdraws; Gandhi tried March 1922 (Judge Broomfield, 6 years). Swaraj Party 1923 (C.R. Das president, Motilal Nehru secretary; Gaya","chars":3695},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/bullets","file":"national-movement-1885-1919-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/bullet-points/national-movement-1885-1919-points.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Bullet Points Match-drills. Pairs flash INC 1885 Bombay → Hume + Bonnerjee (72) · safety-valve debate → Bipan Chandra refutation. Drain theory → Naoroji · Poverty & Un-British Rule → same · Economic History of India → R.C. Dutt. East India Association 1866 (London) → Naoroji · Indian Association 1876 → S.N. Banerjee. Partition 16 Oct 1905 → Curzon · Swadeshi resolution 7 Aug → Calcutta · Bengal National College principal → Aurobindo. Swadeshi Steam → V.O.C. Pillai · Swadesh Bandhab → Ashwini Dutta · Bharat Mata painting → Abanindranath. Lal-Bal-Pal trio · Kesari+Maratha → Tilak · Bande Mataram → Pal · Ganapati 1893/Shivaji 1895 → Tilak. Surat split 1907 (Rash Behari Ghosh) · reunion 1916 Lucknow (with League pact). Muzaffarpur 1908 → Khudiram-Prafulla Chaki · Alipore case → Aurobindo acquitted · Ghadar 1913 → Har Dayal · Komagata Maru 1914. League Dacca 1906","chars":1578},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/notes","file":"national-movement-1885-1919-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/detailed-notes/national-movement-1885-1919-detailed.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Pre-INC political associations"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Moderates (1885-1905) & economic critique"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Partition of Bengal & Swadeshi (1905-11) + Extremists"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Morley-Minto, Muslim League, Home Rule, Lucknow Pact"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Detailed Notes From moderate petitions to mass agitation: INC genesis, Swadeshi explosion, Extremist surge, Home Rule bridge, Gandhian launch. 1. Pre-INC political associations Zamindari Association 1838, British Indian Association 1851, East India Association (London, Naoroji 1866), Indian Association (Surendranath Banerjee, 1876), Madras Mahajana Sabha + Bombay Presidency Association 1884; A.O. Hume retires-Indian-organiser → INC 1885 Bombay (W.C. Bonnerjee first president; safety-valve thesis debate — Bipan Chandra refutes) . 2. Moderates (1885-1905) & economic critique Moderates : Naoroji ( 'Grand Old Man'; poverty & un-British rule; DRAIN THEORY — Poverty and Un-British Rule in India ), R.C. Dutt ( Economic History of India ), Ranade-Gokhale (liberal constitutionalism), W.C. Bonnerjee, S.N. Banerjee; methods: prayers-petitions ('3P'), constitutional mea","chars":3737},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/pyqs","file":"national-movement-1885-1919-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/pyqs/national-movement-1885-1919-mcqs.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919 — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The first session of the Indian National Congress (1885) was presided over by: (a) A.O. Hume (b) W.C. Bonnerjee (c) Dadabhai Naoroji (d) Badruddin Tyabji Q2. Consider: 1. The Surat split of 1907 occurred under the presidency of Rash Behari Ghosh. 2. The Moderates and Extremists reunited at the Lucknow session of 1916. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Q3. The \"Drain of Wealth\" theory is most closely associated with: (a) R.C. Dutt (b) Dadabhai Naoroji (c) M.G. Ranade (d) G.K. Gokhale Q4. Arrange chronologically: 1. Partition of Bengal 2. Founding of Muslim League 3. Morley-Minto Reforms 4. Ghadar Party (a) 1-2-3-4 (b) 2-1-3-4 (c) 1-3-2-4 (d) 1-2-4-3 Q5. Which of the following Home Rule League statements is correct? (a) Besant's League was founded before Ti","chars":2048},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/short","file":"national-movement-1885-1919-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/national-movement-1885-1919/short-notes/national-movement-1885-1919-notes.html","title":"National Movement 1885–1919: Moderates, Extremists &amp; Early Gandhi — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"National Movement 1885–1919: Moderates, Extremists & Early Gandhi — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Pre-Congress associations"},{"level":2,"text":"Congress founding & sessions"},{"level":2,"text":"Moderate phase 1885–1905 — methods & economic critique"},{"level":2,"text":"Extremist phase 1905–1919"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"National Movement 1885–1919: Moderates, Extremists & Early Gandhi — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History. Session-president and year-event matching is the exam's favourite here. Pre-Congress associations Landholders' Society 1838 · British Indian Association 1851 (Calcutta) · East India Association 1866 London (Naoroji) · Poona Sarvajanik Sabha 1870 (Ranade, Joshi) · Indian Association 1876 (S.N. Banerjea, Anandamohan Bose) · Madras Mahajan Sabha 1884 · Bombay Presidency Association 1885. Ilbert Bill controversy 1883 (Ripon) → \"white mutiny\" → lesson in organisation. Congress founding & sessions 28 Dec 1885 , Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College, Bombay; 72 delegates; W.C. Bonnerjee president; A.O. Hume general secretary. Firsts: Badruddin Tyabji (1887 Madras, first Muslim) · George Yule (1888, first Englishman) · Annie Besant (1917 Calcutta, first woman) · Sar","chars":3231},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/pyqs","file":"modern-history-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/pyqs/modern-history-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Modern History — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Modern History — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Modern History — Prelims PYQs Pairs with short-notes/socio-religious-reform-quick.html in this folder. Pattern-faithful MCQs. Q1. The correct chronological order is: (a) Regulating Act → Charter Act 1833 → Pitts India Act → Act of 1858 (b) Pitts India Act → Regulating Act → Charter Act 1833 → Act of 1858 (c) Regulating Act → Pitts India Act → Charter Act 1833 → Act of 1858 (d) Charter Act 1833 → Regulating Act → Pitts India Act → Act of 1858 Model answer: (c). 1773 Regulating → 1784 Pitt's → 1833 Charter → 1858 Crown takeover. Q2. Permanent Settlement (1793) was implemented by: (a) Cornwallis (b) Wellesley (c) Hastings (d) Bentinck Model answer: (a). Bengal-Bihar-Orissa zamindari freeze; contrast with Ryotwari (Munro/Read) & Mahalwari. Q3. [PYQ-pattern] The Ilbert Bill controversy (1883) concerned: (a) European judges over Indian subjects (b) Indian judges over European subjects (c) Pres","chars":2630},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/bullets","file":"revolt-1857-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/bullet-points/revolt-1857-points.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Centres flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Cause flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Aftermath flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Bullet Points Match-drills. Centres flash Delhi → Bahadur Shah II + Bakht Khan · Kanpur → Nana Saheb + Tantia Tope · Lucknow → Begum Hazrat Mahal · Jhansi → Rani Lakshmibai · Bareilly → Khan Bahadur Khan · Jagdishpur → Kunwar Singh · Faizabad → Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah. Spark → Barrackpore, Mangal Pandey, 34th NI (29 Mar 1857) · start → Meerut 10 May 1857 · Delhi fell Sept 1857 (Nicholson). Cause flash Cartridge → Enfield grease rumour · Enlistment Act 1856 → overseas clause · Religious Disabilities Act 1850 → convert inheritance. Lapse victims → Satara, Jaitpur, Sambalpur, Jhansi, Nagpur · Awadh 1856 → 'misgovernment'. Historians → Savarkar 'FWI' (1909), S.N. Sen / Majumdar nuances, British 'sepoy mutiny'. Aftermath flash GoI Act 1858 → Company ends, Secy of State + 15-member Council, Viceroy = Canning. Proclamation 1 Nov 1858 → Allahabad, 'Magna Carta' tag. Army → Europe","chars":1422},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/notes","file":"revolt-1857-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/detailed-notes/revolt-1857-detailed.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Causes"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Spread & leaders"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Suppression & aftermath"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Detailed Notes First major challenge to Company rule: causes, spread, leaders, suppression, and the 1858 settlement. 1. Causes Political: Lapse annexations (Jhansi, Nagpur, Satara), Awadh 1856, Mughal-pension fear (Dalhousie's succession plan), disrespect to Nawabs/rajas. Economic: land-tax squeeze, deindustrialisation (duty-biased trade), drain theory seeds (Dadabhai later articulates), zamindari distress, taluqdari grievance in Awadh. Socio-religious: missionary push, Religious Disabilities Act 1850 (convert-inheritance), rail-telegraph suspicion, sea-voyage loss-of-caste fear. Military: sepoy-european ratio, pay-rank gaps, General Service Enlistment 1856 (cross-the-sea clause), Enfield rifle greased cartridge (cow-pig fat rumour) — Barrackpore Mangal Pandey 29 March 1857 (hanged 8 April). Historiography tags: 'Sepoy Mutiny' (British school) vs 'First War of Independen","chars":2805},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/pyqs","file":"revolt-1857-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/pyqs/revolt-1857-mcqs.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched? Lucknow — Begum Hazrat Mahal · 2. Bareilly — Khan Bahadur Khan · 3. Arrah — Maulvi Ahmadullah (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The General Service Enlistment Act (1856) is associated with the Revolt because it: (a) Cut the bhatta of sepoys serving in Sindh (b) Required new recruits to serve overseas if ordered (c) Introduced the greased cartridges (d) Barred Brahmins from the Bengal Army Q3. Consider the statements: 1. Bahadur Shah Zafar was tried and exiled to Rangoon. 2. Tantia Tope was captured due to betrayal by Man Singh and executed in 1859. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Q4. After the Revolt, the ratio of European to Indian troops in the Bengal Army was fixed at: (a) 1:1 (b) 1:2 (c)","chars":2187},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/short","file":"revolt-1857-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/revolt-1857/short-notes/revolt-1857-notes.html","title":"Revolt of 1857 &amp; Aftermath — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Revolt of 1857 & Aftermath — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Trigger & spread"},{"level":2,"text":"Centres and leaders (high-yield match)"},{"level":2,"text":"Who stayed aloof"},{"level":2,"text":"Causes checklist"},{"level":2,"text":"Aftermath — institutional redesign"},{"level":2,"text":"Interpretations (names only, for match questions)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Revolt of 1857 & Aftermath — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History. Mains-depth notes exist under mains/gs-1-heritage-geography-society/modern-history/revolt-1857/ ; this card is the MCQ-precision layer. Trigger & spread Immediate spark: greased cartridges of the Enfield P-53 rifle ; Mangal Pandey , 34th BNI, Barrackpore, 29 March 1857 . Outbreak 10 May 1857 Meerut (3rd Light Cavalry) → Delhi 11 May → Bahadur Shah Zafar proclaimed emperor. Not a mutiny alone: peasants, taluqdars of Awadh, artisans; ~half of Bengal Army's sepoys were from Awadh/NWP. Centres and leaders (high-yield match) Centre Indian leader British suppressor Delhi Bahadur Shah II, Gen. Bakht Khan John Nicholson, Hudson (captured Zafar) Kanpur Nana Saheb, Tantia Tope, Azimullah Khan Colin Campbell Lucknow Begum Hazrat Mahal (son Birjis Qadr) Havelock, Outram, Campbell Jhansi Rani Lakshmibai ","chars":3214},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/short","file":"socio-religious-reform-quick.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/short-notes/socio-religious-reform-quick.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Quick Prelims Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Quick Prelims Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"The big picture"},{"level":2,"text":"Reformist (liberal, often western-educated founders)"},{"level":2,"text":"Revivalist (assertive, tradition-based)"},{"level":2,"text":"Women & caste reformers"},{"level":2,"text":"Acts to remember"},{"level":2,"text":"PYQ favourites"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Quick Prelims Notes The big picture Reform movements (19th–early 20th c.) responded to colonial modernity + missionary criticism. Two strands: reformist (within tradition) and revivalist (assert tradition). Reformist (liberal, often western-educated founders) Movement Founder Year Key points Brahmo Samaj Rammohan Roy 1828 Against sati, idolatry, caste; monotheism; \"father of Indian renaissance\" Young Bengal Henry Vivian Derozio 1820s Radical rationalism; free-thinking; The Bengal Gazette Prarthana Samaj Dadoba Pandurang / Atmaram Pandurang 1867 Bombay; theistic worship, social reform; M. G. Ranade joined Aligarh Movement Sir Syed Ahmed Khan 1875 MAO College (Aligarh); modern education for Muslims; Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq Theosophical Society H. P. Blavatsky & Col. Olcott (India: Annie Besant) 1875 / 1882 Revive Hindu/Buddhist spirituality; Besant → Home Rule L","chars":2462},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/bullets","file":"socio-religious-reforms-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/bullet-points/socio-religious-reforms-points.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Bullet Points Reformer → org → year drills. Pairs flash Brahmo Sabha 1828 → Rammohan · Tattvabodhini 1839 → Debendranath · 1866 split → Keshab Sen · Young Bengal → Derozio. Sati 1829 → Bentinck + Rammohan · Widow Act XV 1856 → Vidyasagar · Age of Consent 1891 → Phulmoni-Rukhmabai cases. Prarthana Samaj 1867 → Atmaram Pandurang · Ranade → IN Conference 1883 + widow push · Servants of India 1905 → Gokhale. Arya Samaj 1875 → Dayananda (Satyarth Prakash, Shuddhi, DAV 1886) · Theosophy Adyar 1882 → Olcott-Besant · BHU seed → Besant's Central Hindu College. Chicago 1893 + RK Mission 1897 → Vivekananda · 'Daridra Narayana' service creed. Satyashodhak 1873 + Gulamgiri → Jyotirao Phule · girls' school 1848 → Savitribai. Aruvippuram 1888 + SNDP 1903 → Narayana Guru · Self-Respect 1925 → Periyar · Villuvandi yatra → Ayyankali · Mahad 1927 → Ambedkar. MAO 1875 + Tahzeeb-ul-","chars":1610},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/notes","file":"socio-religious-reforms-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/detailed-notes/socio-religious-reforms-detailed.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Bengal & the Brahmo line"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Western & southern reform"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Revivalist & Muslim-Sikh reform"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Social-legislation anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Detailed Notes 19th-early 20th century awakening: reformer → organisation → year → focus. Prelims staple. 1. Bengal & the Brahmo line Rammohan Roy : Atmiya Sabha 1815, Brahmo Sabha 1828 (→ Brahmo Samaj), Sati pamphlets ('Sahamaran' debate vs Dharma Sabha and Srinath Roy), sati abolition 1829 (Reg XVII, Bentinck) ; Persian-style works: Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin; 'Precepts of Jesus'; Sambad Kaumudi paper. Debendranath Tagore (Tattvabodhini Sabha 1839; Brahmo covenant 1843); Keshab Chandra Sen (split 1866 — Brahmo Samaj of India; Special Marriage Act III 1872 lineage) vs Adi Brahmo (Devendranath); Henry Derozio — Young Bengal (Academic Association) ; Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar (widow remarriage champion — Act XV 1856 ; Bengali primer; Metropolitan institution). 2. Western & southern reform Prarthana Samaj 1867 (Atmaram Pandurang, Bombay) ; M.G. Ranade (widows'-r","chars":3235},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/pyqs","file":"socio-religious-reforms-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/pyqs/socio-religious-reforms-mcqs.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reforms — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Match: A. Satyashodhak Samaj — 1. Dadoba Pandurang · B. Paramahansa Mandali — 2. Jyotiba Phule · C. Prarthana Samaj — 3. Atmaram Pandurang (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-2 B-3 C-1 (d) A-3 B-1 C-2 Q2. The Sadharan Brahmo Samaj (1878) was formed after a split caused by: (a) Debendranath's rejection of Vedic infallibility (b) Keshab Chandra Sen marrying his minor daughter to the Cooch Behar prince (c) Rammohan Roy's death (d) The Age of Consent Act Q3. Which Act fixed the minimum age of marriage at 14 for girls and 18 for boys? (a) Native Marriage Act 1872 (b) Age of Consent Act 1891 (c) Sarda Act 1929 (d) Special Marriage Act 1954 Q4. Consider: 1. Arya Samaj was founded at Lahore in 1875. 2. The DAV movement and the Gurukul Kangri represented rival wings within A","chars":2256},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/short","file":"socio-religious-reforms-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/history-culture/modern-history/socio-religious-reforms/short-notes/socio-religious-reforms-notes.html","title":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Founders, years, places (the match bank)"},{"level":2,"text":"Legislation ladder"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Socio-Religious Reform Movements — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › History & Culture › Modern History. Match-the-founder questions dominate; this card is built for that. Founders, years, places (the match bank) Movement / body Founder Year · Place Hook Atmiya Sabha Rammohan Roy 1815 Calcutta precursor to Brahmo Brahmo Sabha / Samaj Rammohan Roy 1828 Calcutta Sati abolished 1829 (Bentinck, Regulation XVII) Tattvabodhini Sabha Debendranath Tagore 1839 joined Brahmo 1843 Brahmo Samaj of India Keshab Chandra Sen 1866 split; Adi Brahmo (Debendranath) Sadharan Brahmo Samaj Anandamohan Bose, Shivnath Shastri 1878 after Cooch Behar marriage Young Bengal Henry Vivian Derozio 1820s Hindu College radicals Dharma Sabha Radhakanta Deb 1830 orthodox, opposed Sati abolition Paramahansa Mandali Dadoba Pandurang 1849 Bombay secret Prarthana Samaj Atmaram Pandurang (Keshab's influence) 1867 Bombay Ranade","chars":3275},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/bullets","file":"amendments-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/bullet-points/amendments-points.html","title":"Constitutional Amendments — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Amendments — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Classic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Modern flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Procedure flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Constitutional Amendments — Bullet Points Number-year-change flash. Classic flash 1st 1951 → Ninth Schedule, 15(4) · 7th 1956 → states reorg · 24th 1971 → FRs amendable · 25th → 31C. 36th 1975 → Sikkim state · 42nd 1976 → mini-constitution (words + FDs + tribunals) · 44th 1978 → property out of FRs, 'armed rebellion'. 52nd 1985 → anti-defection · 61st 1989 → vote 18 · 69th 1991 → Delhi NCT · 73rd-74th 1992 → PRIs/municipalities. Modern flash 86th 2002 → 21A + FD-11 · 91st 2003 → cabinet 15% · 97th 2011 → cooperatives · 99th 2014 → NJAC (struck) · 100th 2015 → Bangladesh LBA. 101st 2016 → GST · 102nd 2018 → NCBC · 103rd 2019 → EWS 10% · 104th 2020 → SC-ST seats extended, Anglo-Indian nomination ends · 105th 2021 → SEBC state power · 106th 2023 → women's reservation. Procedure flash Simple majority outside 368 (states, citizenship, salaries) · special majority most · +½ ratification for Pr","chars":1353},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/notes","file":"amendments-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/detailed-notes/amendments-detailed.html","title":"Constitutional Amendments — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Amendments — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Amendment procedure (Art 368)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Landmark amendments"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Article 368 — the Amending Power and Its Three Procedures"},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Basic Structure Doctrine — the Judicial Arc"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Landmark Amendments"}],"text":"Constitutional Amendments — Detailed Notes Art 368 procedure + the high-yield amendment list with year anchors. 1. Amendment procedure (Art 368) Simple-majority amendable (outside 368): new states, citizenship, salaries, Fifth/Sixth Schedules. Special majority: most of the Constitution. Special + ½-state ratification: President's election, extent of executive power, Seventh Schedule lists, HC-SC representation, Art 368 itself. No joint sitting for constitutional amendment bills; President must assent (24th); FR inclusion in amendment scope restored by 24th after Golaknath. 2. Landmark amendments Amdt Year Change 1st 1951 Ninth Schedule, Art 15(4), 19(2) expanded (land-reform shield) 7th 1956 states reorganisation, A-B-C-D classes abolished 24th/25th 1971 FRs amendable; Art 31C (Kesavananda curtailed its sweep) 31st 1973 LS seats raised to 545 36th 1975 Sikkim full state 42nd 1976 'Mini-C","chars":10933},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/pyqs","file":"amendments-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/pyqs/amendments-mcqs.html","title":"Amendments &amp; Landmark Judgements — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Amendments & Landmark Judgements — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Amendments & Landmark Judgements — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the procedure under Article 368: 1. A Constitution Amendment Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha. 2. There is no provision for a joint sitting in case of disagreement between the Houses. 3. State legislatures must ratify within six months where ratification is required. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 only (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Which of the following can be amended by a simple majority of Parliament outside Article 368? 1. Formation of new states (Art 3) 2. Abolition of legislative councils (Art 169) 3. Fundamental Rights 4. Fifth Schedule (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Match the amendment with its subject: A. 61st — 1. Anti-defection · B. 52nd — 2. Voting age 18 · C. 91st — 3. Council of Ministers capped at 15% ","chars":2947},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/short","file":"amendments-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/amendments/short-notes/amendments-notes.html","title":"Amendments &amp; Landmark Judgements — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Amendments & Landmark Judgements — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Procedure (Art 368, Part XX; South Africa model)"},{"level":2,"text":"Amendments to remember (year — theme)"},{"level":2,"text":"Landmark cases ladder (name → principle)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Amendments & Landmark Judgements — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Constitution. Amendment procedure, the \"must-know\" amendments list (106 as of 2025), and a case-law ladder. Procedure (Art 368, Part XX; South Africa model) Bill in either House (not state legislature); by minister or private member; no prior Presidential recommendation ; passed in each House separately by special majority (majority of total membership + ≥2/3 present & voting); no joint sitting ; President must assent (24th Amdt 1971); for federal provisions — ratification by ≥half the state legislatures by simple majority (no time limit; states can't amend). Three routes: (i) simple majority (outside 368) — Arts 2, 3, 4 (states), 169 (legislative councils), 5th & 6th Schedule paras, citizenship, salaries, quorum, English use, SC judges number, etc.; (ii) special majority — FRs, DPSP, most provisions; (iii) spec","chars":8785},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/bullets","file":"federal-structure-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/bullet-points/federal-structure-points.html","title":"Federal Structure — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Federal Structure — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Lists flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Administrative flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Financial flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Cases & commissions flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Federal Structure — Bullet Points Article-anchor flash. Lists flash List I ~97 (defence, banking, currency) · List II ~66 (police, health, agriculture) · List III ~52 (education, forests, criminal law) · residuary → Union (248). State-list intrusion routes → 249 (RS 2/3), 250 (emergency), 252 (consent), 253 (treaties), 356. 254 → Union prevails; presidential-assent state law can locally prevail. Administrative flash 256-257 → directions · 258 → entrustment · 262 → water-dispute tribunals (Cauvery-Krishna) · 263 → Inter-State Council (1990, Sarkaria). Zonal councils → 1956 SRA statutory (5 + NE) · All-India Services → 312 (IAS-IPS-IFoS only). Financial flash 268-270 → levy-collect pattern · 271 → surcharges out of pool · 280 → Finance Commission (5-yearly) · 275/282 → grants. GST Council 279A → FM chairs; states 2/3 + Centre 1/3; 3/4 threshold. 292/293 → borrowing; states need Centre cons","chars":1437},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/notes","file":"federal-structure-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/detailed-notes/federal-structure-detailed.html","title":"Federal Structure & Centre-State Relations — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Federal Structure and Centre-State Relations — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Legislative relations (245-255)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Administrative relations (256-263)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Financial relations (268-293)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Emergency shifts & commissions"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Federal Structure and Centre-State Relations — Detailed Notes Quasi-federal design: three lists, administrative compass, fiscal flows, emergency centralisation, plus commissions (Sarkaria, Punchhi). 1. Legislative relations (245-255) Seventh Schedule : Union ~97 (defence, foreign, banking, currency, posts, insurance), State ~66 (police, public order, health, agriculture, land), Concurrent ~52 (criminal law, education, forests, marriage, electricity). Residuary → Parliament (248; Entry 97) . Parliament can legislate on State List when: RS 2/3 resolution (249), national emergency (250), states consent (252), treaties (253), President's rule (356). 254 repugnancy : Union law prevails; state law with presidential assent prevails in that state (Parliament may override later). 42nd moved five subjects to Concurrent (education, forests, weights-measures, wildlife protection, administration of j","chars":2829},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/pyqs","file":"federal-structure-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/pyqs/federal-structure-mcqs.html","title":"Federal Structure &amp; Centre–State Relations — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Federal Structure & Centre–State Relations — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Federal Structure & Centre–State Relations — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Under Article 249, Parliament can legislate on a State List subject if: (a) The Lok Sabha passes a resolution by two-thirds majority (b) The Rajya Sabha passes a resolution by two-thirds of members present and voting (c) Two or more states request it (d) The President issues an ordinance Q2. Consider Article 252: 1. Parliament legislates on a State List matter at the request of two or more state legislatures. 2. Such a law applies only to the requesting states and any state that later adopts it. 3. The state legislatures may amend or repeal the law. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the timeline with the emergency: A. Approval within one month by special majority — 1. Article 360 · B. Approval within two months by simple ","chars":3191},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/short","file":"federal-structure-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/federal-structure/short-notes/federal-structure-notes.html","title":"Federal Structure &amp; Centre–State Relations — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Federal Structure & Centre–State Relations — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Federal features vs unitary bias"},{"level":2,"text":"Legislative relations (Part XI, Arts 245–255)"},{"level":2,"text":"Administrative relations (Part XI, Arts 256–263)"},{"level":2,"text":"Financial relations (Part XII, Arts 264–293)"},{"level":2,"text":"Emergency provisions (Part XVIII, Arts 352–360)"},{"level":2,"text":"Inter-state relations & bodies"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Federal Structure & Centre–State Relations — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Constitution. Legislative/administrative/financial relations, emergency provisions, inter-state bodies and the federalism cases. Federal features vs unitary bias Federal: dual polity, written Constitution, division of powers (7th Schedule), supremacy of Constitution, rigidity (368 proviso), independent judiciary, bicameralism. Unitary: strong Centre, single Constitution (J&K exception ended 2019), single citizenship, flexibility, integrated judiciary, All-India Services (312), emergency provisions, Governor as Centre's agent, states' unequal RS representation, Parliament can alter state boundaries (Art 3 — states' views only, not consent; Presidential recommendation needed), integrated election machinery/audit, veto over state bills (200/201), Art 249/252/253, residuary with Centre (248; Entry 97). \"In","chars":17476},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/bullets","file":"fundamental-rights-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/bullet-points/fundamental-rights-points.html","title":"Fundamental Rights & DPSP — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fundamental Rights & DPSP — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"FR flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Writ flash"},{"level":2,"text":"DPSP flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Cases flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Fundamental Rights & DPSP — Bullet Points Article-map flash. FR flash 14 equality-before-law · 15 non-discrimination · 16 opportunity · 17 untouchability · 18 titles. 19 six-freedoms · 20 conviction safeguards · 21 life+privacy · 21A education · 22 arrest. 23 begar-traffic · 24 child labour · 25-28 religion · 29-30 minorities · 32 remedies (SC). Writ flash Habeas corpus → detention · mandamus → public duty · prohibition → during case (superior→inferior) · certiorari → quash after · quo warranto → public office claim. HC writs (226) cover legal rights too — wider than SC (32, FRs only). DPSP flash Socialist → 38, 39, 39A legal aid, 41, 42, 43 · Gandhian → 40 panchayats, 43 cottage, 46, 47, 48 cows · Liberal → 44 UCC, 45 ECCE, 50 sep judiciary, 51 peace. 42nd additions → 39A, 43A, 48A, 50 · 44th changed 38(2) · 43B via 97th (cooperatives). Cases flash Champakam Dorairajan 1951 → FR dominan","chars":1371},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/notes","file":"fundamental-rights-dpsp-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/detailed-notes/fundamental-rights-dpsp-detailed.html","title":"Fundamental Rights & DPSP — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Fundamental Rights article map"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Writs"},{"level":2,"text":"3. DPSP & FDs"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles — Detailed Notes Part III (12-35) vs Part IV (36-51): rights enforceable, directives non-enforceable. Know the article map, writs, and land cases. 1. Fundamental Rights article map Art 14-18 Equality : 14 equality before law + equal protection (foreigners too; reasonable classification allowed); 15 non-discrimination (15(3) women-children specials, 15(4) SEBC via 1st Amdt, 15(5) via 93rd); 16 equal opportunity (16(4) reservation); 17 untouchability (PCR Act 1955); 18 titles. Art 19-22 Freedom : 19 (six freedoms: speech, assemble, association, move, reside, profession — property removed by 44th); 20 (ex-post-facto, double jeopardy, self-incrimination); 21 life-liberty (Maneka Gandhi 1978 — due-process reading; privacy Puttaswamy 2017); 21A free education 6-14 (86th Amdt 2002); 22 arrest (24h magistrate, counsel; preventive-detention exceptions).","chars":3012},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/pyqs","file":"fundamental-rights-dpsp-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/pyqs/fundamental-rights-dpsp-mcqs.html","title":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP &amp; Duties — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP & Duties — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP & Duties — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following Fundamental Rights is available to foreigners as well as citizens? (a) Article 15 (b) Article 16 (c) Article 19 (d) Article 21 Q2. Consider Article 20: 1. Protection against ex post facto laws applies to civil liabilities also. 2. Protection against double jeopardy is available only in proceedings before a court or judicial tribunal. 3. Protection against self-incrimination extends to compulsory production of material objects. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) 1 and 3 (d) 2 and 3 Q3. The writ of quo warranto : 1. Can be sought only by the aggrieved person. 2. Is issued to examine the legality of a person's claim to a public office. 3. Cannot be issued against a private office. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Under Artic","chars":3003},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/short","file":"fundamental-rights-dpsp-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/fundamental-rights-dpsp/short-notes/fundamental-rights-dpsp-notes.html","title":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP &amp; Fundamental Duties — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP & Fundamental Duties — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Fundamental Rights (Part III, Arts 12–35; \"Magna Carta of India\"; originally 7 — property removed by 44th (1978) → Art 300A legal right)"},{"level":2,"text":"Directive Principles (Part IV, Arts 36–51; Ireland; non-justiciable Art 37 but \"fundamental in governance\"; \"novel features\" — Ambedkar; \"cheque payable at convenience\" — K.T. Shah; \"manifesto of aims\" — Austin \"conscience\")"},{"level":2,"text":"Fundamental Duties (Part IVA, Art 51A; 42nd 1976 — Swaran Singh Committee (recommended 8); 10 duties; 11th by 86th 2002 ; USSR; non-justiciable; citizens only)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Fundamental Rights, DPSP & Fundamental Duties — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Constitution. Article-level precision on Part III, IV and IVA plus the landmark-case anchors. Fundamental Rights (Part III, Arts 12–35; \"Magna Carta of India\"; originally 7 — property removed by 44th (1978) → Art 300A legal right) Art 12 State (includes local & \"other authorities\" — statutory bodies, PSUs; judiciary in administrative capacity; BCCI not State); Art 13 judicial review (laws inconsistent void; \"law\" includes ordinances, bye-laws, customs; constitutional amendments excluded per 13(4) — 24th Amdt, but subject to basic structure). Equality (14–18) : 14 equality before law (negative, UK) & equal protection (positive, USA) — reasonable classification test (intelligible differentia + rational nexus), exceptions (President/Governor Art 361, MPs 105, foreign sovereigns); 15 no discrimination o","chars":10657},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/bullets","file":"making-features-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/bullet-points/making-features-points.html","title":"Making & Features — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Making & Features — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Dates flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Source flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Committee flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Making & Features — Bullet Points Pair-drills. Dates flash 9 Dec 1946 → first CA sitting (Sinha temp chair) · 11 Dec → Prasad President · 13 Dec → Objectives Resolution moved. 22 Jan 1947 → Objectives Resolution adopted · 29 Aug 1947 → Drafting Committee (Ambedkar +6). 26 Nov 1949 → adopted (Constitution Day) · 24 Jan 1950 → last session · 26 Jan 1950 → in force (Republic Day). 2y 11m 18d duration · 11 sessions · ₹64 lakh · 299 members post-partition. Source flash GoI Act 1935 → federal scheme, governor, emergency · UK → parliamentary, single citizenship, rule of law. USA → FRs, judicial review, impeachment · Ireland → DPSP · Canada → strong centre, residuary with Union. Australia → concurrent list, joint sitting · USSR → FDs · France → republic · Germany → FR suspension · South Africa → amendment method · Japan → procedure established by law. Committee flash Drafting (Ambedkar) · Union ","chars":1454},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/notes","file":"making-features-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/detailed-notes/making-features-detailed.html","title":"Making of the Constitution & Salient Features — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Making of the Constitution and Salient Features — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Constituent Assembly"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Borrowed features (sources map)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Salient features"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Making of the Constitution and Salient Features — Detailed Notes Dates, committees, sources, and features. Prelims favourites: 26 Nov vs 26 Jan, Drafting Committee members, borrowed features map. 1. Constituent Assembly Cabinet Mission scheme: provincial 292 + princely-state 93 + chief-commissioners 4 = 389 seats (reduced to 299 post-partition). Indirectly elected by provincial assemblies (1946); Muslim League boycotts until 1947. Officers: Sachchidananda Sinha (temporary chairman, 9 Dec 1946), Rajendra Prasad (President, 11 Dec 1946), H.C. Mukherjee vice-president, B.N. Rau constitutional adviser . Objectives Resolution moved 13 Dec 1946 (Nehru), adopted 22 Jan 1947 — seed of the Preamble. Drafting Committee (29 Aug 1947, 7 members) : Ambedkar (chair), N. Gopalaswamy Ayyangar, Alladi Krishnaswamy Ayyar, K.M. Munshi, Syed Mohammad Saadullah, N. Madhava Rao (replaced B.L. Mitter), T.T. Kr","chars":3367},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/pyqs","file":"making-features-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/pyqs/making-features-mcqs.html","title":"Making of the Constitution &amp; Preamble — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Making of the Constitution & Preamble — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Making of the Constitution & Preamble — Practice MCQs 8 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Constituent Assembly's first meeting was held on 9 December 1946 under the temporary chairmanship of: (a) Rajendra Prasad (b) Sachchidananda Sinha (c) B.N. Rau (d) H.C. Mukherjee Q2. Consider the Objectives Resolution: 1. It was moved by Jawaharlal Nehru on 13 December 1946. 2. It was adopted on 22 January 1947. 3. Its modified version forms the Preamble. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the borrowed feature with its source: A. Directive Principles — 1. Australia · B. Concurrent List — 2. Ireland · C. Procedure established by law — 3. Japan (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C-1 Q4. Which words were added to the Preamble by the 42nd Amendment (1976)? (a) Sovereign, Democratic, Republic (b) Socialis","chars":2109},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/short","file":"making-features-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/making-features/short-notes/making-features-notes.html","title":"Making of the Constitution, Preamble &amp; Basic Features — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Making of the Constitution, Preamble & Basic Features — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Constituent Assembly"},{"level":2,"text":"Sources (borrowed features)"},{"level":2,"text":"Preamble (42nd Amendment 1976 added Socialist, Secular, Integrity — only amendment so far)"},{"level":2,"text":"Salient features"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Making of the Constitution, Preamble & Basic Features — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Constitution. Dates, committees, sources and Preamble keywords — the highest-frequency polity block. Constituent Assembly Idea: M.N. Roy 1934; Congress demand 1935; August Offer 1940 accepted principle; Cabinet Mission May 1946 — framework: 389 members (296 British India — provinces 292 + chief commissioners' provinces 4; 93 princely states); indirect election by provincial assemblies (proportional representation, single transferable vote); seats by population 1:10 lakh; communities — Muslims, Sikhs, General. Elections July–Aug 1946: Congress 208, League 73, others 15. After partition: 299 members (229 provinces + 70 states). First meeting 9 Dec 1946 (League boycott); Sachchidananda Sinha temporary president; Rajendra Prasad elected president 11 Dec 1946; H.C. Mukherjee & V.T. Krishnamachari","chars":9508},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/constitution/short","file":"constitution-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/constitution/short-notes/constitution-prelims.html","title":"Constitution — Prelims Revision (Deep Drill)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitution — Prelims Revision (Deep Drill)"},{"level":2,"text":"Parts (1–22) & Schedules (12)"},{"level":2,"text":"Article-precision bank"},{"level":2,"text":"Constitution Bench rulings to name-drop"},{"level":2,"text":"Amendments — the rotation set"},{"level":2,"text":"Federal pressure points"},{"level":2,"text":"Language bits"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Constitution — Prelims Revision (Deep Drill) Subject-level sheet exists at prelims/gs1/polity-governance/short-notes/polity-prelims-revision.html . This card handles article-level precision. Parts (1–22) & Schedules (12) Parts-index pulses: Part I Union-Territory · II Citizenship · III FRs · IV DPSP · IVA FDs · V Union · VI States · IX Panchayats · IXA Municipalities · IXB Co-operatives (97th) · X Scheduled & Tribal Areas · XI Relations · XII Finance-Property · XIII Trade · XIV Services · XIVA Tribunals · XV Elections · XVII Language · XVIII Emergency · XIX Misc (Art 361 immunities) · XX Amendment · XXII short-title. 12 Schedules : 1 territory · 2 salaries & emoluments of high offices · 3 forms of oaths & affirmations · 4 RS allocation · 5 Scheduled Areas/Tribes (non-6th) · 6 Tribal Areas of Assam-Meghalaya-Mizoram-Tripura (ADCs) · 7 three lists · 8 languages (22) · 9 immunity (Ninth Sch","chars":4541},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/bullets","file":"constitutional-bodies-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/bullet-points/constitutional-bodies-points.html","title":"Constitutional Bodies — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Pairs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Removal flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Bullet Points Body → article → removal flash. Pairs flash EC → 324, CEC removal like SC judge · 2023 appointment act → PM+LoP+minister committee. UPSC → 315-323 · CAG → 148, DPC Act 1971, reports → President/Governor-PAC. AG → 76 (pleasure, speaks no vote) · Advocate-General state → 165. Finance Commission → 280, quinquennial, chairman+4 · 15th FC → N.K. Singh, 41% share. SC/ST → 338/338A · OBC-NCBC → 338B (102nd, 2018; earlier statutory 1993) · linguistic minorities → 350B. RPA 1950 → rolls · RPA 1951 → conduct · Lily Thomas 2013 → instant MP/MLA bar. Removal flash SC-judge-style removal → CEC, ECs (rec.), CAG, UPSC chair (SC reference for misbehaviour) · pleasure → AG, Advocate-General. Trap flash Solicitor General ≠ constitutional post (AG is; SG/ASG executive). EC = multi-member since 1993 (single CEC before) — not from the start. NCBC was statutory before 102","chars":1076},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/notes","file":"constitutional-bodies-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/detailed-notes/constitutional-bodies-detailed.html","title":"Constitutional Bodies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Election Commission (324-329)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. UPSC, CAG, AG"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Commissions & others"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. What Makes a Body \"Constitutional\""},{"level":2,"text":"7. The Election Commission of India — Article 324"},{"level":2,"text":"8. The Comptroller and Auditor General — Article 148"},{"level":2,"text":"9. UPSC, Finance Commission and the Law Officers"},{"level":2,"text":"10. The Commissions for SCs, STs and Backward Classes"}],"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Detailed Notes Constitution-created institutions: article, composition, removal, functions. Compare with statutory/executive bodies (next topic). 1. Election Commission (324-329) Art 324 superintendence-direction-control of elections (President, VP, Parliament, assemblies). Composition: CEC + 2 ECs (since 1993 multi-member). CEC removal like SC judge (special majority); ECs on CEC's recommendation . TN Seshan era enforcement; 2023 Act: was reformed — Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners (Appointment) Act 2023 replaced collegium with committee (PM, LoP LS, Union minister) — CJI dropped. Model Code of Conduct, EVM-VVPAT, delimitation advisory. RPA 1950-51 frame: 1950 rolls, 1951 conduct; disqualification 8(1)-(3) (conviction 2y+ = 6y post-release ban); Lily Thomas 2013: immediate bar on MP/MLA conviction. 2. UPSC, CAG, AG UPSC (315-323) : cha","chars":11199},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/pyqs","file":"constitutional-bodies-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/pyqs/constitutional-bodies-mcqs.html","title":"Constitutional Bodies — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Under the Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners (Appointment, Conditions of Service and Term of Office) Act, 2023, the selection committee consists of: (a) The Prime Minister, the Chief Justice of India and the Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha (b) The Prime Minister, a Union Cabinet Minister nominated by the PM and the Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha (c) The President, the Vice-President and the Speaker (d) The Prime Minister, the Home Minister and the Law Minister Q2. Consider the Election Commission of India: 1. It conducts elections to the offices of President and Vice-President. 2. It conducts elections to Panchayats and Municipalities. 3. Election Commissioners can be removed only on the recommendation of the Chief Election Commis","chars":3750},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/short","file":"constitutional-bodies-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/constitutional-bodies/short-notes/constitutional-bodies-notes.html","title":"Constitutional Bodies — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Comparison table"},{"level":2,"text":"Election Commission (Art 324–329)"},{"level":2,"text":"UPSC & SPSC (Arts 315–323)"},{"level":2,"text":"Finance Commission (Art 280) — see federal-structure card; CAG (Art 148–151)"},{"level":2,"text":"National Commissions (338/338A/338B), Linguistic minorities, others"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Constitutional Bodies — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Governance. Election Commission, UPSC/SPSC, Finance Commission, CAG, AG, NCSC/NCST/NCBC, Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities, GST Council, and a comparison table. Comparison table Body Article Composition Appointed by Tenure Removal Election Commission 324 CEC + 2 ECs (multi-member since 1993; 1989–90 briefly) President — 2023 Act: PM + Union Minister + LoP committee (Search committee: Law Minister + 2 secretaries) 6 yrs or 65 CEC like SC judge (special majority); ECs on CEC's recommendation UPSC 315–323 Chairman + members (≤11; half with 10 yrs govt service) President 6 yrs or 65 President on SC inquiry (misbehaviour) or directly (insolvent, paid employment, infirmity) SPSC 315 Chairman + members Governor 6 yrs or 62 President (same grounds) Finance Commission 280 Chairman + 4 President as specified — CAG 148–151 si","chars":14900},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/bullets","file":"e-governance-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/bullet-points/e-governance-points.html","title":"E-Governance — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"E-Governance — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Stack flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Platform flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Law flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"E-Governance — Bullet Points Platform-anchor flash. Stack flash NeGP 2006 (27 MMPs) → e-Kranti 2015 (44) → Digital India 2015 (9 pillars, 3 visions). JAM → Jan Dhan + Aadhaar + Mobile · DBT → 2013, DBT Mission under Cabinet Secretariat. India Stack layers → identity (Aadhaar), payments (UPI), data (DEPA), docs (DigiLocker), eSign. Platform flash UMANG → unified service app · GeM → govt procurement (2016) · ONDC → open commerce network · Gati Shakti → multi-ministry logistics GIS · CoWIN → vaccination. BharatNet → GP fibre · CSCs → rural access points (Digital India pillar) · PMGDISHA → rural digital literacy. Law flash IT Act 2000 (amended 2008) · Sec 66A struck → Shreya Singhal 2015 · DPDP Act 2023 (Board, consent, ₹250cr cap, state-function carve-outs). CERT-In 2022 directions → 6-hour incident reporting · NCIIPC → critical infrastructure. Trap flash UPI = NPCI — an RBI-initiated secti","chars":1142},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/notes","file":"e-governance-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/detailed-notes/e-governance-detailed.html","title":"E-Governance & Digital India — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"E-Governance and Digital India — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Evolution"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Platforms & infrastructure"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Law & policy frame"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. What E-Governance Actually Means"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Institutional Architecture and Missions"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Digital Public Infrastructure and Direct Benefit Transfer"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Emerging Technologies in Governance"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Barriers, Risks and the Way Forward"}],"text":"E-Governance and Digital India — Detailed Notes Model + platforms + law. Expect statement-based questions: Aadhaar status, DBT, DPDP Act 2023, UPI scale. 1. Evolution National e-Governance Plan 2006 (27 Mission Mode Projects) → e-Kranti 2015 (44 MMPs, 'Transforming e-Governance') → Digital India (1 Jul 2015) : three visions (infrastructure-as-utility, governance-services-on-demand, digital empowerment) + nine pillars (broadband highways, mobile connectivity, public internet access (CSCs) , e-governance reform, e-Kranti, info-for-all, electronics (ESDM), IT-jobs, early harvest). JAM trinity : Jan Dhan (PMJDY 2014), Aadhaar (UIDAI 2009; Aadhaar Act 2016 ; Puttaswamy 2018: upheld with restrictions — no mandatory private-use without law), Mobile. DBT (2013) — official estimates cite large cumulative savings (~₹2.2 lakh crore). 2. Platforms & infrastructure UMANG (unified app), DigiLocker (do","chars":10991},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/pyqs","file":"e-governance-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/pyqs/e-governance-mcqs.html","title":"E-Governance &amp; Digital Public Infrastructure — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"E-Governance & Digital Public Infrastructure — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"E-Governance & Digital Public Infrastructure — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023: 1. It applies to personal data collected in digital form or digitised later. 2. The Data Protection Board of India can impose penalties of up to ₹250 crore for failure to take reasonable security safeguards. 3. It mandates that all personal data of Indians be stored only in India. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Which of the following are Section 8 (not-for-profit) companies? 1. National Payments Corporation of India 2. Open Network for Digital Commerce 3. Goods and Services Tax Network 4. Unique Identification Authority of India (a) 1, 2 and 3 (b) 1 and 4 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Match the institution with its parent: A. CERT-In — 1. Ministry of Electroni","chars":3568},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/short","file":"e-governance-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/e-governance/short-notes/e-governance-notes.html","title":"E-Governance &amp; Digital Public Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"E-Governance & Digital Public Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Evolution & models"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"E-Governance & Digital Public Infrastructure — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Governance. Digital India pillars, India Stack/DPI, key platforms, data laws (DPDP, IT Rules), cyber-security institutions and current numbers. Evolution & models NIC 1976; NICNET 1987; National e-Governance Plan 2006 (27 → 31 MMPs) ; IT Act 2000 (amended 2008 — s 66A struck 2015 Shreya Singhal; s 69A blocking upheld; s 79 intermediary safe harbour); Digital India 1 July 2015 (9 pillars — broadband highways, universal mobile, public internet access, e-governance reform, e-Kranti, information for all, electronics manufacturing, IT for jobs, early-harvest; ~₹15,000 cr; extended to 2026 ₹14,903 cr); e-Kranti (44 MMPs); Models — G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E ; Stages (Gartner) — presence, interaction, transaction, transformation ; SMART governance (Simple, Moral, Accountable, Responsive, Transparent — 2nd ARC 11th ","chars":12065},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/bullets","file":"local-govt-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/bullet-points/local-govt-points.html","title":"Local Government — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Local Government — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Panchayat flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Municipal flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Committee flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Scheme flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Local Government — Bullet Points Number-anchor flash. Panchayat flash 243A Gram Sabha · 243B 3-tier (intermediate optional <20 lakh) · 243D reservations (SC-ST proportional, women ≥1/3) · 243G XI Schedule 29 subjects. 243H taxes · 243-I SFC 5-yearly · 243K SEC (HC-judge-style removal). Term 5y; re-election within 6 months of dissolution · Nagaur, Rajasthan 2 Oct 1959 first launch. Municipal flash Three types → nagar panchayat / council / corporation · wards committees >3 lakh · XII Schedule 18 subjects. 243ZD DPC (rural+urban consolidation) · 243ZE MPC (>10 lakh metros). Exemptions → Nagaland, Meghalaya, Mizoram; PESA covers Schedule V (1996). Committee flash Balwant Rai Mehta 1957 → 3-tier democratic decentralisation · Ashok Mehta 1977 → 2-tier, Mandal panchayat · G.V.K. Rao 1985 → district as planning unit · L.M. Singhvi 1986 → constitutional status call. Scheme flash SVAMITVA → drone ","chars":1403},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/notes","file":"local-govt-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/detailed-notes/local-govt-detailed.html","title":"Local Government (73rd/74th) — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Local Government (73rd and 74th Amendments) — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Panchayats (Part IX, 243-243O)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Municipalities (Part IX-A, 243P-243ZG)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Performance & schemes"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Local Government (73rd and 74th Amendments) — Detailed Notes 1992 acts (in force 24 Apr / 1 Jun 1993): constitutionalised panchayats + municipalities. Values, reservations, institutions — direct question zone. 1. Panchayats (Part IX, 243-243O) Definitions: 243 panchayat vs district; 243A Gram Sabha powers state-determined; 243B three-tier (village-intermediate-district; intermediate optional below 20 lakh). Elections: direct, adult franchise; 5-year term (fresh elections within 6 months of dissolution); disqualification; 243D reservations : SC-ST proportional; women ≥1/3 (incl. chairperson posts); state may rotate; OBC state option (many states 27-50%). 243G powers : economic development + social justice, XI Schedule 29 subjects; 243H taxes-funds; 243-I State Finance Commission every 5y; 243K State Election Commission (SEC — removal like an HC judge; constitutionally protected autonomy).","chars":2780},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/pyqs","file":"local-govt-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/pyqs/local-govt-mcqs.html","title":"Local Government — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Local Government — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Local Government — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following provisions of the 73rd Amendment are compulsory for states? 1. Reservation of one-third of seats for women 2. Reservation of seats for Other Backward Classes 3. Constitution of a State Finance Commission every five years 4. Endowing Panchayats with powers to levy taxes (a) 1 and 3 (b) 1, 2 and 3 (c) 2 and 4 (d) 1, 3 and 4 Q2. The State Election Commissioner can be removed from office: (a) By the Governor at his pleasure (b) In the same manner and on the same grounds as a judge of a High Court (c) By a resolution of the state legislature by simple majority (d) By the Chief Election Commissioner Q3. Consider the Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996: 1. It applies to the Fifth Schedule areas of ten states. 2. Every Panchayat in a Sched","chars":3202},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/short","file":"local-govt-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/local-govt/short-notes/local-govt-notes.html","title":"Local Government — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Local Government — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Evolution"},{"level":2,"text":"73rd Amendment — Part IX (Arts 243–243O) + 11th Schedule (29 subjects)"},{"level":2,"text":"74th Amendment — Part IXA (Arts 243P–243ZG) + 12th Schedule (18)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Local Government — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Governance. Panchayati Raj (73rd), Municipalities (74th), PESA, Schedule areas, urban governance, and the numbers examiners love. Evolution Rig-vedic sabha/samiti; Chola kudavolai (Uttaramerur inscription — Parantaka I 920 CE); Mughal kotwal; Lord Ripon 1882 — \"Magna Carta of local self-government\" ; Mayo 1870 resolution; Royal Commission on Decentralisation 1907 (Hobhouse); Montagu-Chelmsford 1919 (transferred subject); GoI Act 1935 (provincial autonomy); Art 40 DPSP ; Gandhi's Gram Swaraj; Community Development Programme 1952 (2 Oct) , National Extension Service 1953 → Balwant Rai Mehta 1957 (3-tier; \"democratic decentralisation\"; Rajasthan Nagaur 2 Oct 1959 first (Nehru); Andhra second) ; Ashok Mehta 1978 (2-tier — zila parishad + mandal panchayat; party-based; constitutional recognition; Nyaya Panchayats) ; G.V.K. Rao 1985 ","chars":11799},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/bullets","file":"schemes-policies-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/bullet-points/schemes-policies-points.html","title":"Flagship Schemes — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Flagship Schemes — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Year flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Number flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Ministry flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Flagship Schemes — Bullet Points Year-number flash (verify current numbers before prelims). Year flash MGNREGA 2005 · PMJDY 2014 · SBM 2014 · MUDRA 2015 · AMRUT-SmartCity 2015 · Ayushman trio + JJBY-PMSBY 2015-18. POSHAN 2018 · PM-JAY 2018 · PM-KISAN 2019 · JJM 2019 · SVAMITVA 2020 · Poshan-PMPoshan 2021 · Surya Ghar 2024. Number flash MGNREGA 100 days · PM-JAY ₹5 lakh/family · PM-KISAN ₹6,000/yr · PMJJBY/PMSBY ₹2 lakh cover · MUDRA Shishu ≤₹50k. Smart Cities 100 · Aspirational Districts 112 · PM SHRI 14,500 · PM Vishwakarma 18 trades · FPO 10,000. Ministry flash JJM → Jal Shakti · PMKVY → Skill Development · PM-JAY → Health (SHA) · AMRUT/SCM → MoHUA · PM-KISAN → Agriculture · Vishwakarma → MSME. Trap flash NRLM renamed DAY-NRLM (Deendayal Antyodaya) — 'Aajeevika' legacy name. PM-JAY covers hospitalisation only (not OPD); Ayushman Arogya Mandirs = OPD-wellness. Smart Cities Mission = MoH","chars":1081},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/notes","file":"schemes-policies-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/detailed-notes/schemes-policies-detailed.html","title":"Flagship Schemes & Policies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Flagship Schemes and Policies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Poverty & rural"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Financial inclusion & pension"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Health-education-skill"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Water-energy-urban"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. How to Organise Schemes for the Exam"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Livelihood, Employment and Rural Development"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Food, Nutrition, Health and Education"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Financial Inclusion, Women, Children and Vulnerable Groups"}],"text":"Flagship Schemes and Policies — Detailed Notes Scheme → ministry → launch year → headline number. High-frequency prelims zone; verify current figures each cycle. 1. Poverty & rural MGNREGA 2005 (100 days/household; wage-material 60:40; unemployment allowance; social audit (Sec 17); demand-driven (DBT wages via NREGASoft)). PMAY-G/U (rural pucca homes; urban CLSS component), DAY-NRLM (women SHGs — Ajeevika lineage; Lakhpati Didi target 3cr), PM-KISAN 2019 (₹6,000/yr, landholding farmer families, exclusion caps), PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (NFSA top-up; made free 2023), Ayushman Bharat : PM-JAY (₹5 lakh/family secondary-tertiary) + HWC/AB-HWC now Ayushman Arogya Mandirs. 2. Financial inclusion & pension PMJDY 2014 (zero-balance, RuPay, overdraft ₹10k), PMJJBY (life ₹2L) + PMSBY (accident ₹2L) + APY (pension) trio 2015, MUDRA (Shishu ≤50k, Kishore 50k-5L, Tarun 5-10L; now Tarun-Plus 20L), ","chars":10477},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/pyqs","file":"schemes-policies-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/pyqs/schemes-policies-mcqs.html","title":"Government Schemes &amp; Policies — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Government Schemes & Policies — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Government Schemes & Policies — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider PM-KISAN: 1. It is a Centrally Sponsored Scheme with 60:40 funding. 2. It provides ₹6,000 per year in three equal instalments. 3. It is implemented by the Ministry of Rural Development. (a) 2 only (b) 1 and 2 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana, the maximum premium payable by farmers for kharif food crops is: (a) 1.5% of the sum insured (b) 2% of the sum insured (c) 5% of the sum insured (d) 10% of the sum insured Q3. Match the scheme with the ministry: A. PM Vishwakarma — 1. Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises · B. PM-KUSUM — 2. Ministry of New and Renewable Energy · C. SVAMITVA — 3. Ministry of Panchayati Raj (a) A-1 B-2 C-3 (b) A-2 B-1 C-3 (c) A-1 B-3 C-2 (d) A-3 B-2 C-1 Q4. Consider the National Foo","chars":3198},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/short","file":"schemes-policies-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/schemes-policies/short-notes/schemes-policies-notes.html","title":"Government Schemes &amp; Policies — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Government Schemes & Policies — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Rural development, employment & livelihoods"},{"level":2,"text":"Agriculture & food"},{"level":2,"text":"Health & education"},{"level":2,"text":"Finance, inclusion, infrastructure, welfare"},{"level":2,"text":"How to remember funding patterns"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Government Schemes & Policies — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Governance. Ministry-wise flagship schemes with launch year, target group, funding pattern and the one detail UPSC tests. Rural development, employment & livelihoods Scheme Year Ministry Testable detail MGNREGA 2005 (Act; 2 Feb 2006 Anantapur) Rural Dev 100 days legal guarantee; unemployment allowance; 60:40 wage:material at GP level; wage 90:10 Centre; social audit s 17; ABPS mandatory 2024; NMMS app; 2025–26 BE ₹86,000 cr; VB-G RAM G Act Dec 2025 replaces it — 125 days; 60:40 cost sharing; 60-day pause during agri season DAY-NRLM 2011 RD 10 cr women in 91 lakh SHGs (2025); Lakhpati Didi (1 cr achieved; target 3 cr) ; Drone Didi (15,000); Bank Sakhi PMAY-G 2016 RD 2.95 cr + 2 cr new (2024–29); ₹1.2 lakh plain / ₹1.3 lakh hilly; 60:40; Awaas+ 2024 survey PMGSY 2000 RD PMGSY-IV (2024–29): 25,000 habitations, 62,500 ","chars":16865},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/short","file":"governance-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/short-notes/governance-prelims.html","title":"Governance, Bodies &amp; Schemes — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Governance, Bodies & Schemes — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Constitutional vs statutory vs executive (the ever-green table)"},{"level":2,"text":"Bodies' tenure/appointment levers"},{"level":2,"text":"Local-programmes tracker (selected bench hits)"},{"level":2,"text":"RTI & transparency quicks"},{"level":2,"text":"Delimitation/election-line CA"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Governance, Bodies & Schemes — Prelims Revision Constitutional vs statutory vs executive (the ever-green table) Constitutional : Election Commission (Art 324) · UPSC/SPSCs (315–323) · CAG (148) · Finance Commission (280) · GST Council (279A) · AG (76)/Advocate General (165) · NCSC (338) · NCST (338A) · NCBC (338B) · Inter-State Council (263) · Zonal Councils (statutory! States Reorganisation Act 1956 — perennial trap) · Scheduled Areas machinery (5th/6th Sched; Tribes Advisory Council 244; DPCs). Statutory : NHRC (Protection of Human Rights Act 1993) · NCPCR (CPCR Act 2005) · NCW (1990 Act) · NCBC-origin (pre-2018 statutory) · Lokpal (Lokpal & Lokayuktas Act 2013) · CIC/SIC (RTI 2005) · CVC (2003) · NGT (2010) · CCI (2002 Act) · NALSA (1987 Act) · UIDAI (Aadhaar Act 2016) · UGC/AICTE (statutory respective) · TRAI · SEBI(1992) · IRDAI · RBI (statutory 1934) · ARCs (SARFAESI lineage). Exec","chars":4463},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/bullets","file":"statutory-bodies-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/bullet-points/statutory-bodies-points.html","title":"Statutory & Regulatory Bodies — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Statutory & Regulatory Bodies — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Rights bodies flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Regulator flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Executive flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Statutory & Regulatory Bodies — Bullet Points Body → act flash. Rights bodies flash NHRC → PHRA 1993 (ex-CJI chair, no armed-forces probe, 1-year bar) · SHRC mirror. CIC → RTI 2005 (≤10 ICs; 2019 amendment tenure to Centre) · Lokpal → 2013 act (PM in scope with safeguards). CVC → 2003 act (1964 Santhanam origin, supervises CBI-corruption work) · CBI → 1963 executive resolution, DSPE 1946. NCPCR → 2007 act · NCW → 1992 act · NGT → 2010 act (Delhi principal bench). Regulator flash RBI → 1934 (MPC via 2016 amendment) · SEBI → 1992 · IRDAI → 1999 · PFRDA → 2013 · TRAI → 1997 · FSSAI → FSS Act 2006 · CERC → Electricity 2003 · IBBI → IBC 2016 · CCI → Competition Act 2002. Appeal ladders: SEBI→SAT→SC · TRAI→TDSAT · IBC→NCLAT. Executive flash NITI Aayog → 1 Jan 2015 cabinet resolution (no statutory basis) · Planning Commission → 1950-2014 · NDC → 1952. Tribunal Reforms Act 2021 → 4-year tenure d","chars":1293},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/notes","file":"statutory-bodies-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/detailed-notes/statutory-bodies-detailed.html","title":"Statutory, Regulatory & Executive Bodies — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Statutory, Regulatory and Executive Bodies — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Rights & oversight commissions"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Economic regulators"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Executive/policy bodies"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Classifying Bodies — the Question Behind the Question"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Human Rights and Social Justice Commissions"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Transparency and Anti-Corruption Architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Regulatory and Sectoral Bodies"}],"text":"Statutory, Regulatory and Executive Bodies — Detailed Notes Created by acts (statutory), executive resolutions (executive), or dedicated regulatory acts. Match body → act → mandate. 1. Rights & oversight commissions NHRC (PHRA 1993) : chair ex-CJI or SC judge; quasi-judicial recommendations; 1-year limitation; can't probe armed forces; 2019 amendment: 3-year tenure, woman member added. SHRCs mirror. CIC (RTI 2005) : CIC + ≤10 ICs; second-appeal body; 2019 amendment: Centre decides tenure-salary (previously 5y fixed) — federalism critique anchor. Lokpal (2013 act, functional 2019) : chair + ≤8 (50% judicial); jurisdiction PM with safeguards; Lokayuktas in states (state act variance). CVC (2003 act; origins 1964 Santhanam) : superintendence of DSPE/CBI corruption work; Central Bureau of Investigation = executive-resolution body (1963, DSPE Act 1946 base) — classic exam pair. NCPCR/NCW (200","chars":9882},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/pyqs","file":"statutory-bodies-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/pyqs/statutory-bodies-mcqs.html","title":"Statutory, Regulatory &amp; Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Statutory, Regulatory & Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Statutory, Regulatory & Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the National Human Rights Commission: 1. Its chairperson must be a retired Chief Justice of India. 2. The chairpersons of the National Commission for Backward Classes and the National Commission for Protection of Child Rights are deemed members. 3. It can inquire into a matter more than one year after the alleged violation. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 only (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Match the appointment committee with the office: A. PM, Leader of Opposition, CJI or his nominee — 1. Central Vigilance Commissioner · B. PM, Home Minister, Leader of Opposition — 2. Director, CBI · C. PM, Leader of Opposition, a Union Cabinet Minister nominated by PM — 3. Chief Information Commissioner (a) A-2 B-1 C-3 (b) A-1 B-2 C-3 (c) A-3 B-1 C-2 (d) A-2 B-3 C","chars":3991},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/short","file":"statutory-bodies-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/governance/statutory-bodies/short-notes/statutory-bodies-notes.html","title":"Statutory, Regulatory &amp; Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Statutory, Regulatory & Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Master table"},{"level":2,"text":"Ombudsman & accountability architecture"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Statutory, Regulatory & Quasi-Judicial Bodies — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Governance. NHRC, CIC, CVC, CBI, Lokpal, NCW/NCM/NCPCR, NITI Aayog and the sectoral regulators — who created them, who heads them, what they can and cannot do. Master table Body Basis Year Head / composition Key facts NHRC Protection of Human Rights Act 1993 (amended 2006, 2019) 1993 Chair: ex-CJI or ex-SC judge (2019); 5 members + 7 ex-officio (NCM, NCSC, NCST, NCW, NCBC, NCPCR, CC Disabilities); 3 yrs/70 Recommendatory; 1-year limitation; armed forces — only Union report; Justice V. Ramasubramanian chair (Dec 2024); GANHRI accreditation deferred 2023 SHRC same — Chair ex-HC CJ or HC judge (2019) appointed by Governor (committee), removed by President CIC RTI Act 2005 2005 CIC + ≤10 ICs; term & salary by Centre ( 2019 Amdt — 3 yrs) Committee: PM, LoP, Union Minister; Heeralal Samariya CIC (Nov 2023","chars":14383},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/bullets","file":"bilateral-ties-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/bullet-points/bilateral-ties-points.html","title":"Bilateral Relations — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Bilateral Relations — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Neighbour flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Power flash"},{"level":2,"text":"West-Asia flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Indo-Pacific flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Bilateral Relations — Bullet Points Partner → anchor flash. Neighbour flash Simla 1972 → Pakistan LoC · Panchsheel 1954 → China · Galwan 2020 · LBA 2015 → Bangladesh (100th Amdt) · Ganga treaty 1996. Nepal 1950 treaty + Mahakali 1996 + Kalapani friction · Bhutan 2007 revised treaty + hydel · Sri Lanka 1987 accord + IPKF + 13A. Maldives Operation Cactus 1988 · Afghanistan post-2021 humanitarian · Myanmar FMR suspension 2024. Power flash US civil-nuclear 123 (2008) + COMCASA-BEA foundational pacts + iCET 2023 · Russia S-400 + Kudankulam + crude pivot · Japan HSR + CEPA 2011. France Rafale + logistics pact 2018 · UK Roadmap 2030 + FTA track. West-Asia flash UAE CEPA 2022 + I2U2 · Saudi council 2019 · Israel 1992 full ties · Iran Chabahar 2016/2024 + INSTC · Qatar LNG-workforce. Indo-Pacific flash SAGAR 2015 → MAHASAGAR 2025 · Quad leaders since 2021 · IMEC 2023 · IORA + IONS. Trap flash 100","chars":1255},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/notes","file":"bilateral-ties-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/detailed-notes/bilateral-ties-detailed.html","title":"India — Bilateral Relations — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"India — Bilateral Relations — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Neighbours"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Major powers"},{"level":2,"text":"3. West Asia & others"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"India — Bilateral Relations — Detailed Notes Neighbours first, then major powers, West Asia, Indo-Pacific. Match partner → treaty/year/initiative. 1. Neighbours Pakistan : 1947-48 war-Kashmir, Simla 1972 (bilateral LoC), Lahore 1999, post-Uri/Balakot (2019) freeze; Article 370 reorganisation 2019; trade suspended (2019). China : Panchsheel 1954, 1962 war, 1993-96 agreements, Wuhan-Mamallapuram informal summits, 2020 Galwan clash + disengagement phases, trade deficit squeeze, Brahmaputra hydro-project watch. Bangladesh : 1971 liberation partnership, Ganga treaty 1996, LBA 2015 (100th amendment), Teesta pending, Maitri bridge-ports, 2024 political transition watch. Nepal : 1950 Peace-Friendship treaty; Kalapani-Lipulekh, EPG report, Mahakali treaty 1996, open border-rokars. Bhutan : 1949 treaty revised 2007 (sovereignty clause), hydropower (Tala-Punatsangchhu), RuPay-Druk link. Sri Lanka :","chars":2793},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/pyqs","file":"bilateral-ties-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/pyqs/bilateral-ties-mcqs.html","title":"India&#x27;s Bilateral Relations — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"India's Bilateral Relations — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"India's Bilateral Relations — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Under the Indus Waters Treaty (1960): 1. India has unrestricted use of the waters of the Ravi, Beas and Sutlej. 2. Pakistan has rights over the Indus, Jhelum and Chenab, subject to India's limited non-consumptive uses. 3. The treaty was brokered by the United Nations. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Match the port with the country: A. Chabahar — 1. Iran · B. Sittwe — 2. Myanmar · C. Hambantota — 3. Sri Lanka · D. Duqm — 4. Oman (a) A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4 (b) A-2 B-1 C-3 D-4 (c) A-1 B-3 C-2 D-4 (d) A-4 B-2 C-3 D-1 Q3. Which of the following \"foundational\" defence agreements has India signed with the United States? 1. LEMOA 2. COMCASA 3. BECA 4. AUKUS (a) 1, 2 and 3 (b) 1 and 4 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q4. Consider the India–Bangladesh Land ","chars":2885},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/short","file":"bilateral-ties-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/bilateral-ties/short-notes/bilateral-ties-notes.html","title":"India&#x27;s Bilateral Relations — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"India's Bilateral Relations — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Neighbourhood"},{"level":2,"text":"Major powers & key partners"},{"level":2,"text":"Exercises quick list (bilateral)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"India's Bilateral Relations — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › International Relations (prelims-relevant facts). Neighbourhood, major powers, key agreements, exercises, and 2024–25 developments. Neighbourhood Country Testable facts Pakistan Simla 1972; Lahore 1999; LoC 740 km; IB; Sir Creek; IWT 1960 (World Bank; Indus, Jhelum, Chenab to Pak; Ravi, Beas, Sutlej to India; held in abeyance 23 Apr 2025 after Pahalgam; Court of Arbitration June 2025 'supplemental award' rejected by India; neutral expert Kishenganga/Ratle) ; Kartarpur corridor 2019 (suspended May 2025); Operation Sindoor 7–10 May 2025 (9 terror sites; ceasefire 10 May; Pak Op Bunyan-um-Marsoos) ; Pahalgam 22 Apr 2025 (26 killed; TRF — US FTO July 2025); trade suspended (Attari); MFN withdrawn 2019; no cricket bilateral; SAARC stalled since 2016; Shehbaz Sharif PM; Asim Munir Field Marshal (May 2025); Pak–Saudi Strate","chars":22784},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/bullets","file":"global-groups-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/bullet-points/global-groups-points.html","title":"Global Groupings — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Global Groupings — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Regional flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Strategic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Economic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Global Groupings — Bullet Points Group → number/HQ/status flash. Regional flash SAARC 1985, 8, Kathmandu · BIMSTEC 1997, 7, Dhaka, Charter 2024 · ASEAN 1967, 10, Jakarta · EAS 18. IORA 1997, 23, Indian Ocean rim · BBIN = Bhutan-Bangladesh-India-Nepal MVA pending · Mekong-Ganga = India-5 Mekong states. RCEP exit 2019 · Look East 1991 → Act East 2014 · SAFTA trade frame. Strategic flash Quad 4 revived 2017 · AUKUS 3 (2021) no-India · SCO 2001, India 2017, RATS Tashkent · I2U2 4 (India-Israel-UAE-US) 2022. G4 vs Coffee Club UNSC reform · NAM 1961 Belgrade · Five Eyes = intel 5 (no India). Economic flash G20 troika + AU 2023 · BRICS NDB Shanghai 2015 + CRA $100bn + 2024 expansion · IPEF 2022 (India 3/4 pillars). FATF India 2010 · OECD = non-member engagement · Cairns = agri-export group (India outside). Trap flash BIMSTEC members = 7 (don't add Maldives — that's SAARC). SCO RATS HQ = Tashken","chars":1108},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/notes","file":"global-groups-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/detailed-notes/global-groups-detailed.html","title":"Global Groupings & Summits — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Global Groupings and Summits — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Regional organisations"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Strategic/limited-membership"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Economic"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The United Nations System"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Economic and Financial Groupings"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Regional and Strategic Groupings"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Export Control Regimes, Environment and Miscellaneous Bodies"}],"text":"Global Groupings and Summits — Detailed Notes Regional + strategic + economic clubs: membership, secretariat, India status, latest-summit anchors. 1. Regional organisations SAARC (1985, Kathmandu HQ) : 8 (Afghanistan added 2007); stalled after 2016 Islamabad summit boycott (Uri); areas: SAPTA-SAFTA, South Asian University. BIMSTEC (1997, Dhaka secretariat) : 7 — Bay of Bengal littorals + Himalayan (Bangladesh-Bhutan-India-Myanmar-Nepal-Sri Lanka-Thailand); BIMSTEC Charter 2024; FTA talks legacy. ASEAN (1967, Jakarta) : 10; ASEAN+1/+3, East Asia Summit (18); India-ASEAN summitry, Act East (2014 from Look East 1991); RCEP — India exited Nov 2019 ; Mekong-Ganga Cooperation; IORA (1997) : 23 members, India vice-chair line; BBIN sub-regional (MVA pending Bhutan). CIS/EAEU : Russia-led economic lines; India-EAEU FTA talks. 2. Strategic/limited-membership Quad (India-US-Japan-Australia): revive","chars":10723},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/pyqs","file":"global-groups-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/pyqs/global-groups-mcqs.html","title":"Global Groupings, Reports &amp; Indices — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Global Groupings, Reports & Indices — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Global Groupings, Reports & Indices — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following countries is NOT a member of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP)? (a) Japan (b) Australia (c) India (d) New Zealand Q2. Consider the Colombo Security Conclave: 1. Its members are India, Sri Lanka, Maldives, Mauritius and Bangladesh. 2. Seychelles participates as an observer. 3. Its secretariat is located in New Delhi. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Match the report with its publisher: A. Global Hunger Index — 1. Concern Worldwide & Welthungerhilfe · B. Global Innovation Index — 2. WIPO · C. Global Gender Gap Report — 3. World Economic Forum · D. World Investment Report — 4. UNCTAD (a) A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4 (b) A-2 B-1 C-3 D-4 (c) A-1 B-3 C-2 D-4 (d) A-4 B-2 C-3 D-1 Q4. Which of the following are ","chars":2613},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/short","file":"global-groups-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/global-groups/short-notes/global-groups-notes.html","title":"Global Groupings, Reports &amp; Indices — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Global Groupings, Reports & Indices — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Membership cheat-sheet"},{"level":2,"text":"Reports → publisher (memorise)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Global Groupings, Reports & Indices — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › International Relations. \"Which country is NOT a member\" style facts: membership lists of groupings, who publishes which report/index, and India's latest ranks. Membership cheat-sheet Grouping Members (count) Non-members often used as traps G7 US, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Canada, Japan (+EU) Russia (out 2014), China, India (invitee) G20 G7 + Argentina, Australia, Brazil, China, India, Indonesia, Mexico, Russia, Saudi, South Africa, South Korea, Türkiye + EU + AU (2023) Spain (permanent guest), Nigeria, Egypt, Iran BRICS (10) Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa, Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE, Indonesia Saudi (undecided), Argentina (declined), Türkiye (partner-invited) SCO (10) China, Russia, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan, India, Pakistan, Iran (2023), Belarus (2024) Afghanistan/Mongolia","chars":12792},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/bullets","file":"multilateral-forums-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/bullet-points/multilateral-forums-points.html","title":"Multilateral Forums — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Multilateral Forums — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"UN flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Economic flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Climate flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Clubs flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Multilateral Forums — Bullet Points Forum-anchor flash. UN flash UNGA 193, one-nation-one-vote · UNSC 15 (P5 veto, 10 two-year) · India non-perm 8 terms · G4 = Brazil-Germany-Japan-India. UNHRC 47 · ECOSOC 54 · ICJ 15 judges (Bhandari 2017 win) · UN Peacekeeping — major Indian troop contributor. Economic flash IMF quota-votes (India ~8th largest) · WB = IBRD+IDA+IFC+MIGA+ICSID · WTO 1995: Bali 2013 food, MC12 2022 fisheries, MC13 2024 Abu Dhabi. India blocs → G33 (agri) + G20 developing (food security) · ADB 1966 · AIIB 2016 (Beijing) · NDB 2015 (Shanghai). Climate flash UNFCCC 1992 → Kyoto 1997 → Paris 2015 (NDC) · COP27 loss-damage · COP28 Dubai fund operational + stocktake · COP29 Baku NCQG finance. India Panchamrit 2022 → 500GW non-fossil, 45% intensity, net-zero 2070 · ISA Gurugram · CDRI 2019 · Global Biofuel Alliance 2023. Clubs flash G20 → AU admitted 2023 India presidency, IMEC ","chars":1422},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/notes","file":"multilateral-forums-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/detailed-notes/multilateral-forums-detailed.html","title":"Multilateral Forums & India — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Multilateral Forums and India — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. UN system"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Economic institutions"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Climate & energy bodies"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Newer clubs"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Multilateral Forums and India — Detailed Notes UN system + economic bodies + climate + newer clubs. Match forum → membership → India's status. 1. UN system UNGA: 193 members, one-nation-one-vote, resolutions non-binding; UNSC: 15 (P5 veto + 10 non-perm 2-year) ; India non-perm 8 times (latest 2021-22), G4 (Brazil-Germany-Japan) expansion push, Coffee Club/Uniting-for-Consensus (Italy-Pakistan) opposition. ECOSOC (54), UNHRC (47 seats, India elected), ICJ (15 judges; Dalveer Bhandari re-elected 2017), UNESCO, WHO, ILO, FAO, WIPO and ITU; UNFPA-UNICEF-UNDP funds; UN Peacekeeping: among largest troop contributors historically. Financing: UNGA budget scale; India: small funding share but among the largest troop contributors. 2. Economic institutions IMF : quota-votes; India ~2.75% quota (among the top 10); SDRs; World Bank Group : IBRD-IDA-IFC-MIGA-ICSID; WTO (1995, successor GATT) : MCs — B","chars":2749},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/pyqs","file":"multilateral-forums-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/pyqs/multilateral-forums-mcqs.html","title":"Multilateral Forums &amp; International Organisations — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Multilateral Forums & International Organisations — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Multilateral Forums & International Organisations — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Match the organisation with its headquarters: A. International Solar Alliance — 1. Gurugram · B. Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure — 2. New Delhi · C. Indian Ocean Rim Association — 3. Ebène, Mauritius · D. BIMSTEC — 4. Dhaka (a) A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4 (b) A-2 B-1 C-3 D-4 (c) A-1 B-2 C-4 D-3 (d) A-2 B-1 C-4 D-3 Q2. India is a member of which of the following export-control regimes? 1. Missile Technology Control Regime 2. Wassenaar Arrangement 3. Australia Group 4. Nuclear Suppliers Group (a) 1, 2 and 3 (b) 1 and 4 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Consider BRICS as of 2025: 1. Indonesia became a full member in January 2025. 2. Saudi Arabia has formally completed its accession as a full member. 3. India holds the BRICS chairship in ","chars":3015},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/short","file":"multilateral-forums-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/multilateral-forums/short-notes/multilateral-forums-notes.html","title":"Multilateral Forums &amp; International Organisations — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Multilateral Forums & International Organisations — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"United Nations system"},{"level":2,"text":"Bretton Woods, trade & finance"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Multilateral Forums & International Organisations — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › International Relations. UN system, Bretton Woods, WTO, regional/plurilateral forums and India's membership status — a HQ/year/head table plus 2024–25 summits. United Nations system Body HQ Est. Head (2025) India facts UN New York 24 Oct 1945 (51 founders; 193 members; South Sudan 2011 last) SG António Guterres (till Dec 2026; selection 2026) India founding member; UNGA President 2025–26 Annalena Baerbock ; UN80 reforms; UNSC reform (G4 — India, Japan, Germany, Brazil; L.69; IGN; UfC opposes); India UNSC non-permanent 8 times (last 2021–22; candidate 2028–29) UNSC New York 1945 rotating presidency P5 + 10 (2 yrs); 2025 elected: Bahrain, Colombia, DRC, Latvia, Liberia (2026–27); 1267 Committee (TRF designation blocked by China; Pahalgam statement Apr 2025); Resolution 2758 ECOSOC NY 1945 54 membe","chars":23728},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/short","file":"ir-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/international-relations/short-notes/ir-prelims.html","title":"International Relations — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"International Relations — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"UN system drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Global financial-eco bodies"},{"level":2,"text":"Groupings membership micro-drill"},{"level":2,"text":"India treaties/exports drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Organisations by purpose"},{"level":2,"text":"Map-linked routings (fresh)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"International Relations — Prelims Revision UN system drill Founding 1945 (Charter signed San Francisco June; in force 24 Oct); 6 principal organs : GA, UNSC, ECOSOC, Trusteeship Council, ICJ (The Hague), Secretariat. UNSC = 15 members : P5 (US, UK, France, Russia, China) with veto + 10 elected for 2-year terms (regional slots: Africa 3, Asia-Pacific 2, Latin America 2, WEOG 2, Eastern Europe 1). India terms: most recent 2021-22 (8th). GA resolutions non-binding (except budget/internal); UNGA 2023 themes etc; UNSC reform groups: G4 (India, Germany, Japan, Brazil), L.69 (developing bloc), Coffee Club/Uniting for Consensus (Italy, Pakistan etc). Agencies location pairing: UNESCO Paris · UNICEF NY · WHO Geneva · FAO Rome · ILO Geneva · IAEA Vienna · WMO Geneva · IMO London · ICAO Montreal · UPU Bern (oldest level) · ITU Geneva · UNIDO Vienna · WIPO Geneva · IFAD Rome. Global financial-eco bo","chars":5080},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/bullets","file":"executive-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/bullet-points/executive-points.html","title":"Executive — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Executive — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"President flash"},{"level":2,"text":"VP flash"},{"level":2,"text":"PM & CoM flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Governor flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Executive — Bullet Points Article-anchor flash. President flash 52 office · 53 executive+supreme command · 54-55 election college · 61 impeachment (2/3 both houses, constitutional violation) · 72 pardon (incl. death + court-martial). 72 pardon types → pardon, commutation, remission, respite, reprieve · Governor 161 → no death, no court-martial. 111 assent → assent / withhold / return (money: no return) · 123 ordinance (both houses not in session) · 143 SC reference · 267 contingency fund. VP flash Election → both houses incl. RS nominated · office 63-64 ex-officio RS chairman · removal → RS-initiated resolution + LS agreement. PM & CoM flash 74 aid-advice (42nd binding, 44th one reconsideration) · 75 appointment + ≤15% (91st) + collective responsibility to LS · 78 PM communication duties · 88 speak rights in either house. Deputy PM = extra-constitutional · Cabinet meaning added by 44th (","chars":1475},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/notes","file":"executive-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/detailed-notes/executive-detailed.html","title":"Executive: President, VP, PM & CoM — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Executive: President, Vice-President, PM and Council of Ministers — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. President (52-62)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Vice-President (63-71)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. PM & Council of Ministers (74-78)"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Governor snapshot (153-163)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Executive: President, Vice-President, PM and Council of Ministers — Detailed Notes Articles 52-78. Election methods, powers, pardons, PM-CoM mechanics — table-friendly. 1. President (52-62) Election (54-55) : electoral college = elected MPs + elected MLAs (NCT Delhi and Puducherry — UTs with legislatures — included); proportional representation single-transferable vote; value formulas (MLA value = pop(1971)/seats/1000). 50+ age, RS-qualified, no office-of-profit; oath by CJI (76). Impeachment (61) : either house initiates, violation-of-Constitution charge, 2/3 present-voting both houses — no quasi-process for 'treaty violation' phrasing. Powers: executive (appoints PM-CoM-governors-judges), legislative (summon-prorogue-dissolve; addresses 87; ordinances 123; assent options 111 — assent, withhold, return (non-money)), financial (money bills prior recommendation 117, contingency fund 267) ","chars":2960},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/pyqs","file":"executive-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/pyqs/executive-mcqs.html","title":"Union &amp; State Executive — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Union & State Executive — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Union & State Executive — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Who among the following participate in the election of the President of India? 1. Nominated members of the Rajya Sabha 2. Elected members of the Legislative Assembly of Puducherry 3. Members of Legislative Councils 4. Elected members of the Lok Sabha (a) 2 and 4 (b) 1, 2 and 4 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Consider the impeachment of the President: 1. The charge must be signed by at least one-fourth of the members of the House initiating it. 2. The resolution must be passed by two-thirds of the members present and voting. 3. Nominated members of Parliament can participate in the process. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. The pardoning power of the Governor under Article 161 differs from that of the President under Article 72 in that the Go","chars":4035},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/short","file":"executive-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/executive/short-notes/executive-notes.html","title":"Union &amp; State Executive — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Union & State Executive — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"President (Arts 52–62, 72–78)"},{"level":2,"text":"Vice-President (Arts 63–71)"},{"level":2,"text":"Prime Minister & Council of Ministers (Arts 74–75)"},{"level":2,"text":"Governor (Arts 153–162, 200–201, 213)"},{"level":2,"text":"Chief Minister, State CoM, Advocate General"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Union & State Executive — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Organs of Government. President, Vice-President, PM & Council of Ministers, Attorney General; Governor, CM, Advocate General. President (Arts 52–62, 72–78) Election (Art 54/55): electoral college — elected MPs + elected MLAs of states + Delhi & Puducherry (70th Amdt 1992; the J&K UT assembly is not included unless Art 54 is amended; Ladakh has no legislature) — no nominated members, no MLCs; proportional representation by single transferable vote, secret ballot ; uniformity & parity — MLA vote = state population (1971 census till 2026) ÷ (elected MLAs × 1000); MP vote = total MLA votes ÷ elected MPs (2022 value 700 ; 2017 708); quota = (valid votes ÷ 2) + 1; disputes → SC (Art 71) ; election can't be challenged on vacancies in college; nomination — 50 proposers + 50 seconders, ₹15,000 deposit; qualifications — citizen, 3","chars":14468},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/bullets","file":"judiciary-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/bullet-points/judiciary-points.html","title":"Judiciary — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Judiciary — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"SC flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Collegium flash"},{"level":2,"text":"HC flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Tribunal flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Judiciary — Bullet Points Court-anchor flash. SC flash 124 composition (CJI+33) · 124(3) quals (HC 5y / advocate 10y / jurist) · 129 court of record · 131 original federal · 136 SLP widest. 137 review · 141 binding precedent · 142 complete justice · 143 advisory · curative (2002). Removal → 124(4) special majority + Inquiry Act 1968 (3-member committee). Collegium flash First Judges 1981 → executive primacy · Second 1993 → collegium born · Third 1998 → CJI+4 · Fourth 2015 → NJAC struck. HC appointments → CJI+2 collegium; Memorandum of Procedure; transfers by President (Collegium advice). HC flash 25 HCs · 226 writs any legal right · 227 superintendence · 235 subordinate control · 1862 oldest trio. Tribunal flash 323A/B via 42nd · CAT 1985 · NGT 2010 · NCLT-NCLAT · Chandra Kumar 1997 → HC review survives. Lok Adalat award = decree (LSA 1987) · Gram Nyayalaya 2008 · Mediation Act 2023. Tra","chars":1219},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/notes","file":"judiciary-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/detailed-notes/judiciary-detailed.html","title":"Judiciary: SC, HCs & Tribunals — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Judiciary: Supreme Court, High Courts and Tribunals — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Supreme Court (124-147)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. High Courts (214-231)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Subordinate courts & tribunals"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Judiciary: Supreme Court, High Courts and Tribunals — Detailed Notes Composition, appointment (collegium), jurisdiction ladder, tribunals, PIL, landmark cases. 1. Supreme Court (124-147) CJI + 33 judges (34; strength grew 8 in 1950 → 34). Judge qualifications (124(3)) : citizen + (HC judge 5 years OR HC advocate 10 years OR distinguished jurist). Appointment: executive till 1981 → Judges-case chain: First 1981 (executive primacy) → Second 1993 (collegium, CJI primacy) → Third 1998 (CJI + 4) → NJAC (99th Amdt, 2014 act) struck 2015 (judicial independence = basic structure) . Removal (124(4)): proved misbehaviour-incapacity; special majority both houses; Judges (Inquiry) Act 1968 3-member committee; sole motion: Justice V. Ramaswami (failed vote). Post-retirement practise bar in any Indian court; salaries on charged fund. Jurisdiction : original 131 (federal: Centre-state, inter-state), wr","chars":2923},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/pyqs","file":"judiciary-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/pyqs/judiciary-mcqs.html","title":"Judiciary — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Judiciary — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Judiciary — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the qualifications for a judge of the Supreme Court: 1. A person must be at least 45 years of age. 2. A distinguished jurist in the opinion of the President is eligible. 3. Five years as a High Court judge or ten years as a High Court advocate qualifies a person. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The original and exclusive jurisdiction of the Supreme Court under Article 131 does NOT extend to: 1. Inter-state river water disputes 2. Matters referred to the Finance Commission 3. A dispute between the Union and a state over the Union's borrowing limits imposed on the state (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. With reference to writ jurisdiction: 1. Article 226 empowers High Courts to issue writs for enforcement of Fundamental Rights and","chars":3524},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/short","file":"judiciary-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/judiciary/short-notes/judiciary-notes.html","title":"Judiciary — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Judiciary — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Supreme Court (Part V ch IV, Arts 124–147)"},{"level":2,"text":"High Courts (Part VI ch V, Arts 214–231)"},{"level":2,"text":"Tribunals & other adjudicators"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Judiciary — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Organs of Government. Supreme Court, High Courts, subordinate courts, tribunals, judicial review, PIL and the collegium. Supreme Court (Part V ch IV, Arts 124–147) Strength 34 (CJI + 33; Supreme Court (Number of Judges) Amendment Act 2019; originally 1+7); appointment by President (Art 124(2)) after consultation → collegium (CJI + 4 senior-most for SC; CJI + 2 for HC; Second Judges 1993, Third 1998 ; NJAC (99th Amdt) struck 2015 — 4:1 ; MoP pending); qualifications — citizen; HC judge 5 yrs / HC advocate 10 yrs / distinguished jurist (never appointed); no minimum age; oath by President; retire 65 (HC 62) ; resignation to President; removal — Art 124(4) — \"proved misbehaviour or incapacity\"; Judges (Inquiry) Act 1968 — motion 100 LS/50 RS members; Speaker/Chairman admits; 3-member committee (SC judge, HC CJ, jurist); special majority b","chars":15402},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/bullets","file":"parliament-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/bullet-points/parliament-points.html","title":"Parliament — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Bill flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Session & motion flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Committee flash"},{"level":2,"text":"RS flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Parliament — Bullet Points Bill-and-motion flash. Bill flash Money bill → LS only, President rec, RS 14 days, no joint sitting, Speaker final (110). Financial-1 → money+other, either house · Financial-2 (117(3)) → President rec before introduction. Constitution amendment → special majority, optional state ratification, no joint sitting, assent obligatory. Joint sittings → Dowry 1961, Banking 1978, POTA 2002 (3 so far). Session & motion flash 3 sessions typical; gap ≤6 months (85) · Question Hour first hour (starred/unstarred/short-notice) · Zero Hour = after QH. No-confidence → LS only, whole CoM · censure → individual member/minister · adjournment motion → urgent public importance. Cuts → policy ₹1, token ₹100, economy = sum specified · Guillotine → all demands voted together. Committee flash PAC 22 (Opposition chair) · Estimates 30 (LS only) · Public Undertakings 22 · DRSCs 24 (1993). ","chars":1322},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/notes","file":"parliament-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/detailed-notes/parliament-detailed.html","title":"Parliament: Bills, Sessions, Procedures — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament: Bills, Sessions and Procedures — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Composition & officers"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Bill taxonomy (the exam core)"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Sessions, motions, accountability"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Parliament: Bills, Sessions and Procedures — Detailed Notes Composition, bill taxonomy, joint sitting, committees, privileges. Bill-type comparison is the eternal MCQ. 1. Composition & officers President + RS (245 max; 233 elected by MLAs + 12 nominated — arts, science, literature, social service) + LS (530 states + 13 UTs; 543 filled; 2 Anglo-Indian nominated seats ended by 104th). Term 5y (extendable during emergency, Art 83). Quorum: 1/10th. RS = permanent house; 1/3 retire biennially (6-year terms) . Officers: Speaker-Deputy Speaker (LS), Chairman = Vice-President (RS), Deputy Chairman RS. Speaker: casting vote, Tenth-Schedule decisions (Kihoto: reviewable), money-bill certificate final, presides joint sittings. Pro-tem Speaker for new-Ls oath; Lok Sabha secretariat under Speaker. 2. Bill taxonomy (the exam core) Type Origin Other house Joint sitting Ordinary (107) either full proces","chars":3283},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/pyqs","file":"parliament-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/pyqs/parliament-mcqs.html","title":"Parliament — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Parliament — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about a joint sitting of Parliament: 1. It can be summoned to resolve a deadlock over a Money Bill. 2. It is presided over by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha. 3. Only three joint sittings have been held so far. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. Which of the following bills lapse on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha? 1. A bill pending in the Rajya Sabha that has not been passed by the Lok Sabha. 2. A bill passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha. 3. A bill passed by both Houses and awaiting the President's assent. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) 1 and 2 (d) 2 and 3 Q3. Consider the Rajya Sabha: 1. Its members are elected by the elected members of state legislative assemblies by proportional representation with a single tra","chars":4085},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/short","file":"parliament-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/parliament/short-notes/parliament-notes.html","title":"Parliament — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Parliament — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Composition (Part V, Arts 79–122)"},{"level":2,"text":"Officers"},{"level":2,"text":"Sessions & devices"},{"level":2,"text":"Legislation"},{"level":2,"text":"Financial procedure"},{"level":2,"text":"Privileges, immunities, misc"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Parliament — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Polity › Organs of Government. Composition, officers, sessions, bills, budget, committees, privileges and anti-defection. Composition (Part V, Arts 79–122) Parliament = President + RS + LS (Art 79). LS : max 550 (530 states + 20 UTs; Art 81) — Anglo-Indian nomination (Art 331) ended by 104th Amdt 2020 ; current strength 543 ; term 5 yrs (dissolvable); seats frozen on 1971 census till first census after 2026 (84th); readjustment on 2001 census (87th) within states. RS : max 250 (238 elected + 12 nominated — literature, science, art, social service); current 245 (233 + 12); permanent , 1/3 retire every 2 yrs; term 6 yrs (RPA 1951, not Constitution); elected by state MLAs by proportional representation — single transferable vote , open ballot (2003); domicile requirement removed 2003 (Kuldip Nayar 2006 upheld); UP 31, MH & TN 19, WB 16, Bihar 16","chars":16345},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/short","file":"organs-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/organs-govt/short-notes/organs-prelims.html","title":"Organs of Government — Prelims Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Organs of Government — Prelims Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"President mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"Vice-President"},{"level":2,"text":"Prime Minister & Council"},{"level":2,"text":"Parliament numbers & process"},{"level":2,"text":"Supreme Court"},{"level":2,"text":"High Courts"},{"level":2,"text":"Local-bodies skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Election Commission & offshoots"},{"level":2,"text":"AG/CAG/AG-states quick"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Organs of Government — Prelims Revision President mechanics Election: elected MPs of both Houses + elected MLAs of states & UTs with legislatures (Delhi, Puducherry, J&K when leg active) ; proportional representation by single transferable vote (secret ballot). MLA vote value = State population (1971 census snapshot) ÷ elected MLAs ÷ 1000. Term: 5 yrs; eligible re-election; resignation to VP; vacancy window 6 months max fill. Impeachment (Art 61): charge by 1/4 members of either House → 14-day notice → 2/3 pass charging House → other House investigates → 2/3 removal; quasi-judicial process. Disputed-election adjudicator = SC (Art 71). Nomination power (RS 12 Art 80); ordinance Art 123 (max life 6wk+6mo; Krishna Kumar Singh re-promulgation doctrine). Vice-President Art 63–71; ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha ; removed by RS resolution (effective 14 days' notice) + LS agreement; election","chars":5122},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/pyqs","file":"polity-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/pyqs/polity-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Polity — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Polity — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Polity — Prelims PYQs Pattern-faithful MCQs. Exact-PYQ nature hedged; answers original. Q1. Which amendment substituted \"armed rebellion\" for \"internal disturbance\" in Article 352? (a) 42nd (b) 44th (c) 52nd (d) 73rd Model answer: (b). The 44th (1978) also restricted Art 356 abuse and shielded Arts 20–21. Q2. With reference to Money Bills: The Speaker's certification is final. Rajya Sabha can amend and return within 14 days. (a) 1 only (b) 2 only (c) Both (d) Neither Model answer: (a). RS can only recommend amendments within 14 days; LS may accept/reject. Q3. [PYQ-pattern] The \"procedure established by law\" clause in Article 21 was borrowed from: (a) UK (b) USA (c) Japan (d) Ireland Model answer: (c). USA's phrase is \"due process of law\" — the standard PYQ distinction. Q4. Which DPSP directs equitable distribution of material resources? (a) Art 38 (b) Art 39(b) & 39(c) (c) Art 44 (d) Art","chars":2498},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/polity-governance/short","file":"polity-prelims-revision.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/polity-governance/short-notes/polity-prelims-revision.html","title":"Polity — Prelims Rapid Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Polity — Prelims Rapid Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Constitution skeleton"},{"level":2,"text":"Preamble-42nd-44th drill"},{"level":2,"text":"FRs vs DPSPs vs FDs"},{"level":2,"text":"Union executive"},{"level":2,"text":"Parliament mechanics"},{"level":2,"text":"Judiciary grid"},{"level":2,"text":"Federal & emergency"},{"level":2,"text":"Local governance"},{"level":2,"text":"Bodies quick-pair"},{"level":2,"text":"CA edge (2024–26)"}],"text":"Polity — Prelims Rapid Revision Pair with the Mains constitution-polity + governance-administration sets. This sheet is prelims-shaped (facts > prose). Constitution skeleton Borrowed features : UK → parliamentary, rule of law, single citizenship, cabinet, bicameralism; Ireland → DPSP, RS nomination, presidential election method; USA → FRs, judicial review, impeachment, removal of SC/HC judges, VP office; Canada → federal with unitary bias, SC appointment method; Australia → Concurrent List, joint sitting; USSR → FDs, justice ideals; Germany → emergency provisions; France → Republic, liberty-equality-fraternity (added via 42nd, preamble); SA → amendment procedure + RS election; Japan → procedure established by law; Govt of India Act 1935 → federation, PSC, governor, emergency, judiciary skeleton (~250 provisions source). Preamble-42nd-44th drill 42nd (1976): SOCIALIST·SECULAR·INTEGRITY ad","chars":3852},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/bullets","file":"physics-chem-bio-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/bullet-points/physics-chem-bio-points.html","title":"Basic Science — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Basic Science — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Physics flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Chemistry flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Biology flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Basic Science — Bullet Points Anchor flash. Physics flash PSLV → solid 1st + strapons + liquid final · GSLV/LVM3 → cryogenic upper · orbits → LEO/MEO/GEO/L1. NavIC = 7 sats · GAGAN = aviation augmentation · Quantum Mission 2023 ₹6,000 cr · LIGO-India Hingoli. Semi-fabs → Dholera (Tata) + Sanand (Micron ATMP) · ITER fusion partner. Chemistry flash Green H2 → electrolysis (5 MMT 2030) · blue → reforming+CCS · PEMFC fuel cell. Solar → perovskite tandem · CCUS amines · RO desalination · PLA biodegradable. Montreal → ODS · Kigali 2016 → HFC phase-down · Ch-1 M3 water, Ch-3 LIBS sulphur. Biology flash CRISPR 2020 Nobel · prime editing next-gen · Genome India 10k. mRNA 2023 Nobel (Kariko-Weissman) · Covaxin = inactivated · Covishield = vector · NexCAR19 CAR-T indigenous. iPSC Yamanaka · bio-economy ~$130bn · BIRAC-DBT arm. Trap flash Cryogenic = GSLV/LVM3 (PSLV has none). Green ≠ blue hydrogen ","chars":1081},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/notes","file":"physics-chem-bio-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/detailed-notes/physics-chem-bio-detailed.html","title":"Basic Science: Physics, Chemistry, Biology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Basic Science: Physics, Chemistry, Biology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Physics anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Chemistry anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Biology anchors"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Basic Science: Physics, Chemistry, Biology — Detailed Notes Applied-concept anchors: physics of launches-navigation, chemistry of energy-tech, biology of genomics. Award line-items included. 1. Physics anchors Launch physics: multi-staging (PSLV = solid 1st + liquid 2nd + solid strapons + liquid 4th; GSLV/LVM3 = cryogenic upper stage); orbit set (LEO ~400 km, MEO GNSS, GEO comms, L1 halo — Aditya-L1 at 1.5 million km sunward ); RLV-LEX autonomous landing demos (2023-24). Navigation-positioning: NavIC/IRNSS (7 satellites; L5+S bands) , GAGAN (aviation SBAS with AAI); National Quantum Mission (2023; ~₹6,000 cr; qubits, secure comms, sensing) ; semiconductors: India Semiconductor Mission (Tata fab Dholera, Micron ATMP Sanand); fusion: ITER (India partner, France) ; LIGO-India (Hingoli, approved 2023); gravitational waves 2015 detection. Fundamentals: SI seven base units; light-year vs AU; C","chars":2473},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/pyqs","file":"physics-chem-bio-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/pyqs/physics-chem-bio-mcqs.html","title":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry &amp; Biology — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry & Biology — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry & Biology — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following phenomena are explained by total internal reflection? 1. Mirage on a hot road 2. Working of optical fibres 3. Blue colour of the sky 4. Brilliance of a cut diamond (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q2. Aquatic life survives in frozen lakes during winter because: (a) Ice is a good conductor of heat (b) Water has maximum density at 4 °C, so the coldest water and ice stay at the surface (c) Fish generate their own heat (d) Dissolved salts lower the freezing point to −10 °C Q3. An induction cooktop will NOT heat a vessel made of: (a) Cast iron (b) Stainless steel with a magnetic base (c) Aluminium (d) Enamelled steel Q4. Consider the following statements about nuclear reactors: 1. Heavy water acts as a modera","chars":2935},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/short","file":"physics-chem-bio-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/basic-science/physics-chem-bio/short-notes/physics-chem-bio-notes.html","title":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry &amp; Biology — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry & Biology — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Physics in daily life"},{"level":2,"text":"Chemistry in daily life"},{"level":2,"text":"Biology & human body"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Everyday Physics, Chemistry & Biology — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Science & Technology › Basic Science. NCERT-level concepts that UPSC frames as application questions (why does something happen), plus the human-body and disease facts that recur. Physics in daily life Light : refraction — twinkling of stars, apparent depth, mirage (total internal reflection + hot-air density gradient), rainbow (dispersion + TIR in raindrops; primary 42°, secondary reversed colours), blue sky (Rayleigh scattering ∝ 1/λ⁴), red sunsets (longer path), white clouds (Mie scattering), danger signals red (least scattered); optical fibre (TIR); lenses — myopia (concave; eye too long), hypermetropia (convex), presbyopia (bifocal), astigmatism (cylindrical); LASER (coherent), LED (electroluminescence; blue LED Nobel 2014); polarisation (sunglasses, 3-D films); diffraction (CD colours); interference (soap bubb","chars":36922},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/basic-science/short","file":"basic-science-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/basic-science/short-notes/basic-science-prelims.html","title":"Basic Science — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Basic Science — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Physics quicks"},{"level":2,"text":"Chemistry quicks"},{"level":2,"text":"Biology quicks"},{"level":2,"text":"Everyday-modern quicks"}],"text":"Basic Science — Prelims Drill Physics quicks Laws/statements pairing: Newton's 1st-inertia (Galileo attribution) · 2nd F=ma · 3rd action-reaction · Archimedes (buoyancy) · Bernoulli (lift principle misuse caution) · Pascal (hydraulic press) · Ohm V=IR · Faraday (induction) · Lenz (direction) · Kepler (planetary) · Titius-Bode· Doppler (ambulance pitch) · Steffan-Boltzman (radiation) · Planck-Einstein photon E=hν · photoelectric (Einstein Nobel 1921). Instrument-purpose: pyrometer (high temperatures), sphygmomanometer (BP), altimeter, anemometer (wind speed), Fathometer (ocean depth echo), odometer, tachometer (rpm), hydrometer (relative density of liquids; NOT hygrometer — humidity), lactometer (milk), audiometer, galvanometer vs ammeter vs voltmeter vs potentiometer. Units: SI base 7 (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd); derived staples (N=kg·m/s², J, W, Pa, Hz, C, V, Ω, S, F, T, Wb, H); knots (na","chars":4338},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/bullets","file":"ai-it-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/bullet-points/ai-it-points.html","title":"AI & IT — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"AI & IT — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"AI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"DPI flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Cyber flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"AI & IT — Bullet Points Mission-term flash. AI flash NITI 2018 #AIforAll · IndiaAI Mission Mar 2024 ₹10,372 cr · AIKosh datasets · Bhashini 2022. GPAI 2020 · Bletchley-Seoul-Paris summit chain · deepfake-bias vocabulary. LLM = transformer-attention; diffusion = generative; AGI vs narrow. DPI flash Aadhaar (ID) + UPI (pay) + DigiLocker (docs) + DEPA (consent) + ONDC (commerce) + UHI (health). G20 2023 → GDI + Social Impact Fund · UPI abroad → SG-UAE-FR-SL-MU · CoWIN open-sourced · MOSIP = IIIT-B. Cyber flash IT Act 2000/2008 · 66A struck 2015 (Shreya Singhal) · DPDP 2023 Board + ₹250 cr. CERT-In 6-hr (2022) · NCIIPC · I4C + 1930 · GAC 2022 · zero-trust term. Trap flash IndiaAI year = 2024 (mission), strategy = 2018 — date trap. MOSIP ≠ Aadhaar — open-source vs statutory ID. DPDP covers personal data only. ONDC = protocol/network, not a marketplace firm. 66A = speech arrests case, not data","chars":912},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/notes","file":"ai-it-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/detailed-notes/ai-it-detailed.html","title":"AI, IT & Digital Public Infrastructure — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"AI, IT and Digital Public Infrastructure — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. AI policy & missions"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Digital Public Infrastructure"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Cyber law & security"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Artificial Intelligence: the Vocabulary the Paper Tests"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Semiconductors — Why a Chip Is a Strategic Asset"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Quantum Technologies"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Digital Infrastructure, Networks and Emerging IT"}],"text":"AI, IT and Digital Public Infrastructure — Detailed Notes AI policy stack + India's DPI exports + cyber-law frame. Mission-name-number recall. 1. AI policy & missions NITI National Strategy for AI (2018; #AIforAll) → IndiaAI Mission (Mar 2024; ₹10,372 cr; components: GPU compute, innovation centre, AIKosh datasets, application development, startup financing, future-skills) ; Bhashini (2022; AI translation, 22 scheduled languages) ; responsible-AI advisories (2024 advisory reworked 2025 under DPDP-rules process); IndiaAI safety-institute line (2025). Concepts: ML-DL-LLM chain (transformers, attention), generative diffusion models, AGI-narrow distinction, deepfakes, algorithmic bias; global: GPAI (India founding member 2020) , AI-safety summits (Bletchley 2023 → Seoul 2024 → Paris 2025). 2. Digital Public Infrastructure India Stack: Aadhaar (identity) + UPI (payments) + DigiLocker-eSign (d","chars":10970},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/pyqs","file":"ai-it-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/pyqs/ai-it-mcqs.html","title":"AI, IT, Semiconductors &amp; Quantum — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"AI, IT, Semiconductors & Quantum — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"AI, IT, Semiconductors & Quantum — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about the IndiaAI Mission: 1. It was approved by the Union Cabinet in March 2024 with an outlay of over ₹10,000 crore. 2. One of its pillars is the creation of a shared compute facility of graphics processing units. 3. It is implemented by the Department of Science and Technology. Which are correct? (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The Nobel Prize in Physics 2024 was awarded for: (a) The discovery of attosecond pulses of light (b) Foundational discoveries enabling machine learning with artificial neural networks (c) Experiments with entangled photons (d) Macroscopic quantum tunnelling in electric circuits Q3. Consider the following statements about the Telecommunications Act, 2023: 1. It repeals the Ind","chars":3817},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/short","file":"ai-it-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/ai-it/short-notes/ai-it-notes.html","title":"AI, IT, Semiconductors &amp; Quantum — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"AI, IT, Semiconductors & Quantum — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Artificial intelligence"},{"level":2,"text":"IT, telecom & cyber"},{"level":2,"text":"Semiconductors"},{"level":2,"text":"Quantum"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"AI, IT, Semiconductors & Quantum — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Science & Technology › Emerging Technologies. Concepts, India's missions and laws, and the global developments that feed prelims questions. Artificial intelligence Concepts : AI ⊃ machine learning ⊃ deep learning (neural networks — Hinton/Hopfield Nobel Physics 2024; LeCun/Bengio/Hinton Turing 2018); supervised/unsupervised/reinforcement learning (Barto & Sutton Turing 2024); transformers (2017 'Attention is all you need') ; large language models — GPT-4o/GPT-5 (OpenAI Aug 2025), Gemini 2.5/3 (Google), Claude 4 (Anthropic), Llama 4 (Meta — open weights), Grok 4 (xAI), DeepSeek-R1 (China Jan 2025 — low-cost reasoning shock), Qwen (Alibaba), Kimi, Mistral (France); generative AI (text, image — DALL-E/Midjourney/Stable Diffusion; video — Sora/Veo 3; voice); hallucination ; RAG ; fine-tuning ; parameters/tokens ; agentic AI ","chars":35882},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/bullets","file":"biotech-health-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/bullet-points/biotech-health-points.html","title":"Biotech & Health — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biotech & Health — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Biotech flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Health flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Pharma flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Biotech & Health — Bullet Points Mission-anchor flash. Biotech flash Bio-E3 2024 · $130bn → $300bn 2030 · BIRAC 2012 · Genome India 10k · IBDC deposit. Casgevy 2023 first-CRISPR (abroad) · NexCAR19 India CAR-T · iNCOVACC nasal-first · CRISPR SCD trial. Health flash PM-JAY ₹5L + AAMs 1.6L · ABDM ABHA + eSanjeevani · Vay Vandana 70+ 2024. NTEP 2025 TB · AMB anaemia · U-WIN 2024 · Indradhanush · HPV 2025 · One-Health 2025. Pharma flash PLI APIs + devices · 4 parks · Jan Aushadhi PMBJP · NLEM 2022 + NPPA · CDSCO-DCGI · CTR 2019 · PvPI · NAP-AMR 2017. Trap flash Casgevy (abroad CRISPR) vs NexCAR19 (India CAR-T) — origin pair. iNCOVACC (nasal) ≠ ZyCoV-D (needle-free patch) — delivery-type pair. PM-JAY = hospitalisation; eSanjeevani = tele-OPD. NLEM list is price-capped by NPPA — regulator-role pair. Bio-E3 = biomanufacturing policy, not a vaccine scheme.","chars":860},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/notes","file":"biotech-health-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/detailed-notes/biotech-health-detailed.html","title":"Biotech & Health Technology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biotech and Health Technology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Biotech missions & economy"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Health programmes & digital health"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Pharma, devices, regulation"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. The Molecular Foundation"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Gene Editing and CRISPR"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Vaccines and Immunology"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Agricultural Biotechnology and GM Regulation"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Health Policy, Emerging Threats and Ethics"}],"text":"Biotech and Health Technology — Detailed Notes Bio-missions, gene-therapy frontier, health-programme anchors, pharma regulation. 1. Biotech missions & economy Bio-E3 policy (2024; biomanufacturing, biofoundries, Economy-Environment-Employment framing) ; bio-economy ~$130bn (2024) with $300bn-2030 target; BIRAC (2012, DBT PPP arm); Genome India (~10,000 genomes; IBDC data deposit) ; Biotech-KISAN; 25+ biotech parks. Frontier: Casgevy (2023, UK-US) = world's first CRISPR therapy (sickle cell) ; India's first CRISPR sickle-cell trial approved; NexCAR19 = indigenous CAR-T (IIT-Bombay-Tata) ; iNCOVACC = world's first nasal COVID vaccine ; Gennova mRNA platform; dengue vaccine phase-3. 2. Health programmes & digital health Ayushman Bharat: PM-JAY (₹5L/family) + Ayushman Arogya Mandirs (1.6L+) ; ABDM (2021: ABHA IDs, HPR-HFR, eSanjeevani teleconsults — world's largest govt telemedicine) ; Ayush","chars":11040},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/pyqs","file":"biotech-health-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/pyqs/biotech-health-mcqs.html","title":"Biotechnology &amp; Health Technologies — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biotechnology & Health Technologies — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Biotechnology & Health Technologies — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Consider the following statements about CRISPR-Cas9: 1. It is derived from a bacterial adaptive immune system. 2. A guide RNA directs the Cas9 enzyme to the target DNA sequence. 3. Base editing requires a double-strand break in DNA. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q2. The first genome-edited rice varieties released in India in 2025, DRR Dhan 100 (Kamala) and Pusa DST Rice 1, were developed using: (a) Transgenic insertion of Bt genes (b) Site-directed nuclease (SDN-1/SDN-2) editing without foreign DNA (c) Marker-assisted backcrossing (d) Mutation breeding with gamma rays Q3. Which of the following genetically modified crops are commercially cultivated in India as of 2025? 1. Bt cotton 2. Bt brinjal 3. GM mustard DMH-11 4. Golden rice (a)","chars":3510},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/short","file":"biotech-health-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/biotech-health/short-notes/biotech-health-notes.html","title":"Biotechnology &amp; Health Technologies — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Biotechnology & Health Technologies — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Gene editing & genomics"},{"level":2,"text":"GM crops & agri-biotech"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Biotechnology & Health Technologies — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Science & Technology › Emerging Technologies. Gene editing, genomics, GM crops, vaccines/therapeutics platforms, and India's biotech policy and institutions. Gene editing & genomics CRISPR-Cas9 (Doudna & Charpentier — Nobel Chemistry 2020; bacterial immune system; guide RNA + Cas9 nuclease → double-strand break → NHEJ (knock-out) or HDR (knock-in)); variants — Cas12, Cas13 (RNA), base editing (single letter, no cut — David Liu), prime editing , epigenome editing ; Casgevy (exa-cel; Vertex/CRISPR Tx) — first approved CRISPR therapy (UK Nov 2023; US Dec 2023) for sickle cell & β-thalassaemia; ₹~25 crore ; first personalised CRISPR (baby KJ — CPS1 deficiency, Philadelphia May 2025 — in vivo base editing) ; India: BIRSA-101 (sickle cell gene-editing therapy — CSIR-IGIB; first Indian CRISPR trial at CMC Vellore 2025); FELU","chars":16769},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/bullets","file":"defence-nuclear-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/bullet-points/defence-nuclear-points.html","title":"Defence & Nuclear — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Defence & Nuclear — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Missile flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Navy-air flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Nuclear flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Defence & Nuclear — Bullet Points System-type flash. Missile flash Agni-V ICBM-class + MIRV 2024 · K-15/K-4 SLBM · Pralay quasi-ballistic. BrahMos supersonic (PH 2022) · Nirbhay/ITCM subsonic · Akash SAM · MR-SAM Indo-Israel · QRSAM · VSHORADS. Astra BVR · Nag ATGM · Pinaka rockets · ATAGS howitzer · S-400 Russian SAM. Navy-air flash Vikrant 2022 carrier · Scorpene P-75 French · Arihant SSBN · Tejas Mk1A 83+97 · Rafale-M 2025 · iDEX startups · exports ₹21,000 cr band. Nuclear flash 3-stage → PHWR → FBR → thorium · monazite Kerala-TN · PFBR Kalpakkam critical 2024 · Kudankulam VVER · Tarapur oldest BWR · Kakrapar-4 2024. Bharat SMR 220 MW (2025) · 100 GW 2047 vision · AERB regulator · NCA-SFC chain. Trap flash Akash (SAM) / Nag (ATGM) / Astra (air-air) — type swap. BrahMos supersonic vs Nirbhay subsonic. PFBR = Bhavini breeder (not NPCIL PHWR). Vikrant = carrier; Vagir = submarine — INS-n","chars":950},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/notes","file":"defence-nuclear-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/detailed-notes/defence-nuclear-detailed.html","title":"Defence & Nuclear Technology — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Defence and Nuclear Technology — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Missile families (DRDO-led)"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Nuclear power (DAE-NPCIL-BARC-AERB)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Defence and Nuclear Technology — Detailed Notes Missile families, induction systems, nuclear-power chain, agencies. System-type-origin matching. 1. Missile families (DRDO-led) Ballistic : Agni series (Agni-V ~5,000+ km ICBM-class, canisterised; MIRV test Mar 2024 — 'Mission Divyastra' ); K-15/K-4 SLBMs (Arihant-class SSBNs) ; Pralay quasi-ballistic. Cruise : BrahMos (Indo-Russian supersonic ~Mach 2.8; land-ship-air-submarine variants; first export Philippines 2022) ; Nirbhay/ITCM subsonic line. Air-defence-strike : Akash (+NG), MR-SAM/Barak-8 (Indo-Israel), QRSAM, VSHORADS; Astra BVR air-to-air ; Nag ATGM; Pinaka rocket artillery; ATAGS howitzer; S-400 (Russian, 5 squadrons); BMD interceptors (PDV-Ashwin). Induction frame: INS Vikrant (2022; first indigenous carrier) , P-75 Scorpene (Mazagon-Naval Group; +3 approved 2025), Arihant-class SSBNs, LCA Tejas Mk1A (83+97 ordered), Rafale-M (20","chars":2199},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/pyqs","file":"defence-nuclear-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/pyqs/defence-nuclear-mcqs.html","title":"Defence, Missiles &amp; Nuclear Technology — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Defence, Missiles & Nuclear Technology — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Defence, Missiles & Nuclear Technology — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. \"Mission Divyastra\" conducted in March 2024 refers to: (a) India's first test of an anti-satellite weapon (b) The first flight test of Agni-V with multiple independently targetable re-entry vehicles (c) The maiden launch of a K-4 submarine-launched missile (d) The induction of the S-400 system Q2. Consider the following pairs of missiles and their categories: 1. Pralay — tactical quasi-ballistic missile 2. Astra — beyond-visual-range air-to-air missile 3. Rudram — anti-ship cruise missile 4. Nirbhay — subsonic land-attack cruise missile Which pairs are correctly matched? (a) 1, 2 and 4 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2, 3 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Consider the following statements about BrahMos: 1. It is a joint venture between DRDO and Russia's NPO Mashinostroyeniy","chars":3507},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/short","file":"defence-nuclear-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/defence-nuclear/short-notes/defence-nuclear-notes.html","title":"Defence, Missiles &amp; Nuclear Technology — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Defence, Missiles & Nuclear Technology — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"Missiles (DRDO unless noted)"},{"level":2,"text":"Platforms & indigenous programmes"},{"level":2,"text":"Nuclear programme & technology"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Defence, Missiles & Nuclear Technology — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Science & Technology › Emerging Technologies. Missile families and ranges, indigenous platforms, defence reforms/exports, nuclear fuel cycle and India's nuclear programme, plus the treaty positions. Missiles (DRDO unless noted) Missile Type Range / notes Agni-I SRBM 700–900 km; single-stage solid Agni-II MRBM 2,000+ km Agni-III IRBM 3,000–3,500 km Agni-IV IRBM 4,000 km Agni-V ICBM-class 5,000–5,500+ km; Mission Divyastra (Mar 2024) — first MIRV test ; canisterised; road/rail-mobile; Agni-5 bunker-buster variant reported 2025 Agni-Prime (Agni-P) MRBM 1,000–2,000 km; two-stage canisterised; rail-launched test Sept 2025 Agni-VI ICBM under development (8,000+ km; MIRV) Prithvi-II / Dhanush SRBM 350 km / ship-launched; Prithvi Defence Vehicle K-15 (Sagarika/B-05) SLBM 750 km; on INS Arihant class K-4 SLBM 3,500 km; test","chars":26563},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/short","file":"emerging-tech-prelims.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/short-notes/emerging-tech-prelims.html","title":"Emerging Tech — Prelims Drill","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Emerging Tech — Prelims Drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Missions & money anchors (2023–26 frame)"},{"level":2,"text":"AI vocabulary drill"},{"level":2,"text":"Biotech & health-tech"},{"level":2,"text":"Quantum vocab"},{"level":2,"text":"Defence & space S&T hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"IT classics refreshers (setters still ask)"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Emerging Tech — Prelims Drill Missions & money anchors (2023–26 frame) IndiaAI Mission (₹10,371 cr; Mar 2024) : compute pillar (GPU pooling — tender outcome toward ~38,000 GPUs provisioning programmes), AIKosh datasets, skilling (Data & AI Labs), application development (agri/health/logistics), startup financing, Safe & Trustworthy AI → India's AI Safety Institute (announced 2025) ; frontier-models homespun (AI4Bharat IndicTrans2; BharatGen IIT-B consortium 2024; Krutrim & Sarvam private builds; Hanooman family). National Quantum Mission (₹6,003 cr; Apr 2023) : T-Hubs at IISc + IIT Madras/Kanpur/Delhi triads; verticals: computing (superconducting, photonic, ion-trap, neutral-atom), communication (QKD free-space + fibre; network build with BSNL + army pilots), sensing/metrology, materials & devices; announced target horizon ~50–100-qubit class machines by mid-decade (verify current milest","chars":5818},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/bullets","file":"space-missions-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/bullet-points/space-missions-points.html","title":"Space Missions — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Space Missions — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Legacy flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Planetary flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Current flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Space Missions — Bullet Points Mission-year flash. Legacy flash Aryabhata 1975 · SLV-3/Rohini 1980 (Kalam) · PSLV debut 1993 · C37 = 104 sats (2017). Ch-1 2008 water · Ch-2 2019 orbiter-only · Ch-3 = 23 Aug 2023 south pole (sulphur, LIBS) · Space Day = 23 Aug. Planetary flash MOM 2013-14 Mars first-attempt (retired 2022) · Astrosat 2015 · Aditya-L1 L1 halo Jan 2024 · XPoSat 2024. Current flash Gaganyaan uncrewed-first · Axiom-4 ISS 2025 · SpaDeX docking Jan 2025 (4th) · NISAR 2025 radar. Approved → Ch-4 sample return, Venus orbiter, BAS 2035 · Kulasekarapattinam pad-2. Private → Skyroot 2022, Agnikul 2024, Pixxel · IN-SPACe + NSIL + FDI 2024. Trap flash Ch-3: launch 14 Jul vs landing 23 Aug 2023 — date swap. Aditya-L1 = L1 (sunward), not L2. Ch-3 launcher = LVM3 (not PSLV). IN-SPACe (authorise) vs NSIL (commercialise) — role swap. SpaDeX = docking demo (not rendezvous-only).","chars":887},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/notes","file":"space-missions-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/detailed-notes/space-missions-detailed.html","title":"Space Missions & ISRO — Detailed Notes","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Space Missions and ISRO — Detailed Notes"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Legacy missions"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Current & upcoming"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Orbits: the Concept Behind Every Space Question"},{"level":2,"text":"7. India's Launch Vehicle Family"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Planetary and Scientific Missions"},{"level":2,"text":"9. Application Satellites — the Ones That Pay for the Programme"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Governance, Reform and Commercial Space"}],"text":"Space Missions and ISRO — Detailed Notes Mission-year-outcome table + upcoming pipeline. Chandrayaan-3 and Aditya-L1 dominate recent asks. 1. Legacy missions Aryabhata (1975; USSR-launched) ; SLV-3 (1980; Rohini; Kalam); INSAT-IRS operational eras; PSLV first flight 1993 ; PSLV-C37 (2017): 104 satellites world record ; LVM3 = heavy-lift (Chandrayaan-3, OneWeb, Gaganyaan). Lunar arc: Chandrayaan-1 (2008; Moon Impact Probe; M3 instrument = water-molecule signatures) → Chandrayaan-2 (2019; orbiter science continues; Vikram lander lost) → Chandrayaan-3 (launched 14 Jul 2023; landed 23 Aug 2023 near lunar south pole; Vikram-Pragyan; LIBS detected sulphur; 4th nation to soft-land, 1st at south pole) ; National Space Day = 23 August . Planetary-scientific: MOM/Mangalyaan (2013; first-attempt Mars orbit; retired 2022) ; Astrosat (2015); Aditya-L1 (Sep 2023 launch; halo orbit around L1 from Jan 2","chars":10223},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/pyqs","file":"space-missions-mcqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/pyqs/space-missions-mcqs.html","title":"Space: Missions, ISRO &amp; Satellites — Practice MCQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Space: Missions, ISRO & Satellites — Practice MCQs"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Space: Missions, ISRO & Satellites — Practice MCQs 10 original prelims-pattern questions; key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. With the SpaDeX mission in January 2025, India became the fourth country to demonstrate: (a) Soft landing on the Moon (b) Autonomous in-orbit docking of two spacecraft (c) Reusable launch vehicle landing (d) Anti-satellite capability Q2. Consider the following statements about NISAR: 1. It is a joint NASA–ISRO Earth observation satellite carrying L-band and S-band synthetic aperture radars. 2. It was launched by a PSLV into a sun-synchronous orbit. 3. NASA provided the L-band radar and ISRO the S-band radar. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 1 and 3 (c) 2 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Group Captain Shubhanshu Shukla's spaceflight in June–July 2025 was: (a) The first crewed Gaganyaan mission (b) A Soyuz flight to the Russian segment of the ISS (c) The Axiom-4 commercial mission o","chars":3322},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/short","file":"space-missions-notes.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/emerging-tech/space-missions/short-notes/space-missions-notes.html","title":"Space: Missions, ISRO &amp; Satellites — Prelims Fact Card","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Space: Missions, ISRO & Satellites — Prelims Fact Card"},{"level":2,"text":"ISRO basics"},{"level":2,"text":"Traps"}],"text":"Space: Missions, ISRO & Satellites — Prelims Fact Card Prelims GS-I › Science & Technology › Emerging Technologies. ISRO's launchers, satellites, science missions, human spaceflight, policy/private sector, and the global missions that share headlines. ISRO basics ISRO (1969; Vikram Sarabhai; HQ Bengaluru; under Department of Space (1972) → PM; Space Commission ; Chairman V. Narayanan (Jan 2025; earlier S. Somanath 2022–25) ); centres — VSSC Thiruvananthapuram (launch vehicles), LPSC (liquid propulsion; Valiamala/Bengaluru), SDSC-SHAR Sriharikota (2 pads; 3rd pad approved Jan 2025 ₹3,985 cr; Kulasekarapattinam (TN) SSLV port — 2026 ), URSC Bengaluru (satellites), SAC Ahmedabad (payloads), NRSC Hyderabad (remote sensing; Bhuvan), ISTRAC (tracking), IPRC Mahendragiri (engine tests), HSFC (human spaceflight; 2019), IIRS Dehradun , NARL Gadanki , PRL Ahmedabad , IIST Thiruvananthapuram , SCL ","chars":23431},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/pyqs","file":"scitech-prelims-pyqs.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/pyqs/scitech-prelims-pyqs.html","title":"Science &amp; Tech — Prelims PYQs","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Tech — Prelims PYQs"}],"text":"Science & Tech — Prelims PYQs Q1. SpaDeX's primary purpose (Dec 2024/Jan 2025) was to demonstrate: (a) Solar-sail propulsion (b) Autonomous rendezvous & docking (c) Cryogenic deep-space staging (d) Reusable first-stage recovery Model answer: (b). Key capability for Bharatiya Antariksh Station plans. Q2. Aditya-L1 observes primarily at — (a) Earth's L2 (b) Sun-Earth L1 halo orbit (c) Lunar orbit (d) GEO Model answer: (b). 1.5 million km sunward; PELKO coronagraph & UVIT etc. Q3. The CE-20 is a: (a) Semi-cryogenic booster (b) Cryogenic upper-stage engine of LVM3 (c) Solid strap-on (d) Hypergolic thruster Model answer: (b). India's most powerful cryogenic engine; human-rating variants drive Gaganyaan. Q4. MIRV technology was validated by India via: (a) Agni-I \"Shourya\" (b) Agni-V \"Mission Divyastra\" (c) Prithvi-II (d) K-4 SLBM Model answer: (b). Tested 11 March 2024. Q5. [PYQ-pattern] India","chars":2189},{"nav":"prelims-gs1/science-tech/short","file":"scitech-prelims-revision.html","rel":"content/prelims/gs1/science-tech/short-notes/scitech-prelims-revision.html","title":"Science &amp; Tech — Prelims Rapid Revision","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Science & Tech — Prelims Rapid Revision"},{"level":2,"text":"Space ladder (must-know dates)"},{"level":2,"text":"Launch pads & centres"},{"level":2,"text":"NavIC vs GPS"},{"level":2,"text":"Missiles gestalt"},{"level":2,"text":"Nuclear & particle"},{"level":2,"text":"IT & AI"},{"level":2,"text":"Biotech"},{"level":2,"text":"Defence-dual-use quick hits"},{"level":2,"text":"Awards / factoids on rotation"}],"text":"Science & Tech — Prelims Rapid Revision Space mission-stack questions repeat; pair with Mains S&T set for institutional depth. Space ladder (must-know dates) Aryabhata 1975 (first sat, Soviet launcher); SLV-3 1980 (Rohini); ASLV; PSLV 1993→ ; GSLV (Russian cryo-debptive then indigenous CE-7.5); GSLV Mk III/LVM3 2017. Chandrayaan-1 2008 (MIP + M3 water) → MOM/Mangalyaan 2013 → Chandrayaan-2 2019 (orbiter alive; Vikram hard landing) → Chandrayaan-3 2023 (23 Aug soft landing, Shiv Shakti Point) → Aditya-L1 Sep 2023 → XPoSat Jan 2024 → SpaDeX Dec 2024 (docking Jan 2025) → NISAR Jul 2025 . Gaganyaan: TV-D1 (Oct 2023) → Vyomanauts named (Feb 2024) → Bharatiya Antariksh Station target ~2035. Space policy: IN-SPACe (2020) + Indian Space Policy 2023 + FDI liberal 2024 (auto 100% components; capped routes upstream). Launch pads & centres Sriharikota (SHAR) launch; Thumba sounding rockets; VSSC Thi","chars":3397},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/csat-mock/bullets","file":"csat-mock-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/csat-mock/bullet-points/csat-mock-points.html","title":"CSAT Mock — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Mock — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Paper flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Rounds flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Analysis flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"CSAT Mock — Bullet Points Test flash. Paper flash 80 Q · 2 hr · 200 marks · −0.83 wrong · qualify 33% (target 90-100+). Mix band → RC 25-30 · quant 30-35 · reasoning 20-25 · DI 5-8. Rounds flash R1 sitters → R2 RC blocks → R3 skips · ~90 sec/Q · skip at 2 min. Guess only after 2 eliminations · keep end buffer. Analysis flash Buckets → gap (re-learn) · trap (flash-list) · misread (scan habit) · speed (skip discipline). Same-day re-solve · weekly re-attempt · track trend. Trap flash Marathoning one puzzle — walk away. Random guessing at −0.83 — negative EV. Reading RC cold in round 3 — batch passages together. Chasing attempts over accuracy — CSAT fails on accuracy.","chars":672},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/csat-mock/notes","file":"csat-mock-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/csat-mock/detailed-notes/csat-mock-detailed.html","title":"CSAT Mock — Test Guide & Analysis Framework","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Mock — Test Guide and Analysis Framework"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Test anatomy"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Attempt strategy"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Post-test analysis (the actual marks)"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"CSAT Mock — Test Guide and Analysis Framework How to take the 30-question drill here and scale to the real 80-question paper. Qualifying paper — but only if practised. 1. Test anatomy Real Paper-II: 80 questions, 2 hours, 200 marks (2.5 each), negative 0.83 per wrong ; qualifying = 33% (66.67 marks) — but safety margin target: 90-100+ marks so unpredictability never bites. This drill (csat-mock-30q): 30 questions, 45 minutes — same negative marking; treat as a weekly pulse-check, not the full distance. Question mix in real paper (band): RC ~25-30 Q, quant ~30-35, reasoning ~20-25, DI ~5-8, decision-making emphasised less in recent years. 2. Attempt strategy Three rounds : (1) instant-solve syllogisms-percentages-averages; (2) RC passages in one sitting each (context warm); (3) return to skips with remaining time. Time discipline: ~90 sec/question average; hard-skip at 2 minutes; keep 8-1","chars":1788},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/csat-mock/pyqs","file":"csat-mock-30q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/csat-mock/pyqs/csat-mock-30q.html","title":"CSAT Mock — Paper II (30 Questions · 45 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Mock — Paper II (30 Questions · 45 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Part A — Reading Comprehension (Q1–Q10)"},{"level":2,"text":"Part B — Quantitative Aptitude (Q11–Q20)"},{"level":2,"text":"Part C — Logical Reasoning & Data Interpretation (Q21–Q30)"},{"level":2,"text":"Worked Solutions"}],"text":"CSAT Mock — Paper II (30 Questions · 45 Minutes) Qualifying paper: 33% (66/200) needed. This 30-question set mirrors the real mix — reading comprehension 10, quantitative aptitude 10, logical reasoning & data interpretation 10. Marking +2.5 / −0.83. Worked solutions follow the questions. Part A — Reading Comprehension (Q1–Q10) Passage 1. Urbanisation in India is often described as 'messy and hidden'. Messy, because a large share of urban growth takes the form of unplanned peripheral sprawl and informal settlements; hidden, because official definitions understate the extent of urbanisation. Census towns—settlements that meet the demographic criteria of a town but continue to be governed as villages—illustrate this. They receive neither the infrastructure investment of statutory towns nor the rural programmes tailored to villages, and fall through the cracks of public policy. Q1. Which one","chars":9285},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/csat-mock/short","file":"csat-mock-brief.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/csat-mock/short-notes/csat-mock-brief.html","title":"CSAT Mock — One-Page Brief","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CSAT Mock — One-Page Brief"},{"level":2,"text":"Numbers"},{"level":2,"text":"Plan"},{"level":2,"text":"After"}],"text":"CSAT Mock — One-Page Brief Print-keeper. The whole test plan in one screen. Numbers Real: 80 Q · 2 hr · 200 marks · −0.83 wrong · qualify 33% (aim 90-100+). Drill here: 30 Q · 45 min · same negative marking. Plan R1 sitters (quant-reasoning sprints) → R2 RC in blocks → R3 skips. ~90 sec/Q · hard-skip at 2 min · guess only after 2 eliminations. Last 10 min: bubbling + review only. After Tag misses: gap / trap / misread / speed → same-day re-solve → week-later re-attempt. Track trend across sets; accuracy over attempts, always. Pair with: comprehension/logical-reasoning/numeracy topic pages and csat-strategy.","chars":614},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/full-length/bullets","file":"full-length-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/full-length/bullet-points/full-length-points.html","title":"Full-Length Mock — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Full-Length Mock — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Blueprint flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Walk flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Review flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Full-Length Mock — Bullet Points Walk flash. Blueprint flash 100 Q · 2 hr · 200 marks · −0.66 wrong. Mix band → history 15-18 · polity 12-15 · geo 10-14 · env 15-18 · econ 12-15 · sci-tech 10-12. Walk flash 0-5 scan · 5-60 sitters · 60-100 50-50s · 100-115 partials · 115-120 buffer. 50-50 rule → attempt only when 2 eliminated. Review flash Buckets → lucky / wrong (gap-trap-misread-speed) / unattempted. Same-day analysis · week-later error re-solve · topic-map next queue. Bands → 110+ safe · 90-110 sharpen · under-90 content week. Trap flash Statement-bunch questions last — they eat time in round 1. Bubbling drift after Q70 — batch-check. Fresh risky guess in buffer time — forbidden. Mock without same-day analysis = half wasted.","chars":737},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/full-length/notes","file":"full-length-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/full-length/detailed-notes/full-length-detailed.html","title":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — Test Guide & Blueprint","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — Test Guide and Blueprint"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Paper blueprint"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The 2-hour walk"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Review loop"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — Test Guide and Blueprint One 100-question simulation beats five un-analysed ones. Blueprint, walk-through, review loop. 1. Paper blueprint Real GS1 prelims: 100 questions, 2 hours, 200 marks (2 each), negative 0.66 per wrong . Subject mix band (recent years): history-culture ~15-18, polity ~12-15, geography ~10-14, environment ~15-18, economy ~12-15, science-tech ~10-12, current-affairs linkage woven in — the sectional tests here mirror these six areas. This full-length (gs1-full-length-mock-01) follows the same blueprint; attempt strictly in 120 minutes with OMR-style answer-sheet discipline. 2. The 2-hour walk 0-5 min : scan the paper, tick sitters, note statement-bunch questions for round 3. 5-60 : round 1 — certain answers only (~40-50 attempts expected). 60-100 : round 2 — 50-50 eliminations; attempt when 2 options are dead. 100-115 : round 3 — remaining; atte","chars":1627},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/full-length/pyqs","file":"gs1-full-length-mock-01.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/full-length/pyqs/gs1-full-length-mock-01.html","title":"Full-Length Mock Test 01 — Prelims GS Paper I (100 Questions · 2 Hours)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Full-Length Mock Test 01 — Prelims GS Paper I (100 Questions · 2 Hours)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section A — History & Culture (Q1–Q15)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section B — Geography (Q16–Q27)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section C — Polity & Governance (Q28–Q42)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section D — Economy (Q43–Q57)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section E — Environment & Ecology (Q58–Q72)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section F — Science & Technology (Q73–Q84)"},{"level":2,"text":"Section G — Current Affairs, IR & Miscellaneous (Q85–Q100)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer Key with Explanations"},{"level":3,"text":"Scoring"}],"text":"Full-Length Mock Test 01 — Prelims GS Paper I (100 Questions · 2 Hours) Instructions. 100 questions, 200 marks, 2 hours. +2 for a correct answer, −0.66 for a wrong one, 0 for a blank. Attempt in one sitting with an OMR-style sheet; use the answer key and one-line explanations at the end only after the timer stops. Subject mix mirrors the recent UPSC pattern: History & Culture 15 · Geography 12 · Polity & Governance 15 · Economy 15 · Environment 15 · Science & Technology 12 · Current Affairs, IR & Miscellaneous 16. Section A — History & Culture (Q1–Q15) Q1. Consider the following pairs of Harappan sites and the features they are known for: Dholavira — elaborate water reservoirs and a signboard of large script signs Lothal — dockyard Kalibangan — ploughed field Rakhigarhi — largest Harappan site within present-day India How many of the pairs are correctly matched? (a) Only one (b) Only two","chars":36122},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/full-length/short","file":"full-length-brief.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/full-length/short-notes/full-length-brief.html","title":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — One-Page Brief","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — One-Page Brief"},{"level":2,"text":"Numbers"},{"level":2,"text":"Walk"},{"level":2,"text":"After"}],"text":"GS1 Full-Length Mock — One-Page Brief Print-keeper for the 2-hour simulation. Numbers 100 Q · 2 hr · 200 marks · −0.66 wrong. Mix band: env 15-18 · history 15-18 · econ 12-15 · polity 12-15 · geo 10-14 · sci-tech 10-12. Walk 0-5 scan → 5-60 sitters → 60-100 50-50s → 100-115 partials → 115-120 buffer. 50-50 rule: attempt only when 2 options eliminated. After Buckets: lucky / wrong / unattempted → same-day analysis → week-later error re-solve. Bands: 110+ safe · 90-110 sharpen elimination · under-90 content week. Before the mock: re-read the six sectional error sheets only.","chars":578},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/bullets","file":"sectional-tests-points.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/bullet-points/sectional-tests-points.html","title":"Sectional Tests — Bullet Points","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Tests — Bullet Points"},{"level":2,"text":"Map flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Bands flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Log flash"},{"level":2,"text":"Trap flash"}],"text":"Sectional Tests — Bullet Points Usage flash. Map flash 6 papers × 20 Q → history-culture · polity · geography · economy · environment · science-tech. Timing → after area revision, never cold · 1-2/week. Bands flash 60%+ → cleared, maintain · 40-60% → trap-flash + PYQ redo · under-40% → content-first rebuild. Log flash gap → notes page · trap → trap-flash · misread → scan drill · speed → skip rule. Rolling error-sheet per area → final-month revision fuel. Trap flash Cold sectional = wasted set — revise first. Ignoring negative marking in scoring — count it. Jumping to full mocks before banking 4-5 sectionals. Re-testing without redoing notes — same score loop.","chars":667},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/notes","file":"sectional-tests-detailed.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/detailed-notes/sectional-tests-detailed.html","title":"Sectional Tests — Guide & Score Bands","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Tests — Guide and Score Bands"},{"level":2,"text":"1. The six sectionals"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Cadence & score bands"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Error logging discipline"},{"level":2,"text":"MCQ hooks"}],"text":"Sectional Tests — Guide and Score Bands Six 20-question papers, one per GS1 area: build-up instruments before full mocks. 1. The six sectionals history-culture · polity · geography · economy · environment · science-tech — 20 questions each, negative-marked like the real paper (on a 20Q scale, ~40 marks with −1.33 per wrong equivalent). Each sectional sits in its area's context: take it right after finishing that area's full revision — never cold, not before first revision. 2. Cadence & score bands Cadence: 1-2 sectionals per week in the build phase; full-length mocks only after 4-5 sectionals are banked. 60%+ (12/20): area cleared — maintain with weekly flash-card recall. 40-60% (8-11/20): targeted revision — redo the trap-flash lists + area PYQs, then a fresh 20Q set. Below 40%: content-first — re-read detailed-notes of weak topics, rebuild short-notes, only then re-test. 3. Error loggi","chars":1454},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"economy-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/economy-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — Economy (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — Economy (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — Economy (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Macro concepts, banking, fiscal policy, external sector and current schemes. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Gross Value Added at basic prices equals GDP at market prices: (a) Plus product taxes minus product subsidies (b) Minus product taxes plus product subsidies (c) Plus depreciation (d) Minus net factor income from abroad Q2. The base year for India's current GDP series is: (a) 2004–05 (b) 2011–12 (c) 2017–18 (d) 2022–23 Q3. Consider the following statements about inflation measurement: 1. CPI (Combined) is compiled by the National Statistics Office. 2. WPI is compiled by the Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT. 3. Food has a weight of about 46% in CPI (Combined) under the 2012 base. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Which of the following instruments are used by the RBI to abs","chars":6299},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"environment-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/environment-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — Environment &amp; Ecology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — Environment & Ecology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — Environment & Ecology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Ecology concepts, biodiversity, climate negotiations, pollution law and institutions. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. In an ecological pyramid, which of the following is always upright? (a) Pyramid of numbers (b) Pyramid of biomass (c) Pyramid of energy (d) None of the above Q2. 'Ecotone' refers to: (a) A zone of transition between two ecosystems (b) The functional role of a species (c) A layer of vegetation in a forest (d) The total genetic variation in a population Q3. Which of the following are 'keystone species' examples commonly cited? 1. Sea otter 2. Elephant in savanna 3. Fig trees in tropical forests (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Consider the following statements about mangroves: 1. Sundarbans has the largest mangrove cover in India. 2. Gujarat has the secon","chars":6053},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"geography-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/geography-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — Geography (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — Geography (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — Geography (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Physical, Indian and world geography with map-based items. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which one of the following layers of the atmosphere contains the ozone layer and shows a temperature increase with altitude? (a) Troposphere (b) Stratosphere (c) Mesosphere (d) Thermosphere Q2. Consider the following statements: 1. The Coriolis force is maximum at the equator. 2. Winds are deflected to the right in the Northern Hemisphere. 3. Tropical cyclones do not form within about 5° of the equator. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. 'Mistral' is a: (a) Warm, dry wind of the Rockies (b) Cold, dry wind blowing down the Rhone valley to the Mediterranean (c) Hot wind of the Sahara blowing into Italy (d) Local wind of the Argentine pampas Q4. Consider the following pairs of plate boundaries an","chars":5027},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"history-culture-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/history-culture-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — History &amp; Culture (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — History & Culture (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — History & Culture (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Covers ancient, medieval, modern history and art & culture in the proportion UPSC has used since 2020. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The Bhimbetka rock shelters, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, are located in: (a) Chhattisgarh (b) Madhya Pradesh (c) Maharashtra (d) Odisha Q2. Which Harappan site has yielded evidence of a stadium-like structure and a 'signboard' of ten large script signs? (a) Mohenjo-daro (b) Harappa (c) Dholavira (d) Banawali Q3. The Vedic text that deals with rituals and is attached to the Rig Veda is: (a) Aitareya Brahmana (b) Shatapatha Brahmana (c) Gopatha Brahmana (d) Taittiriya Brahmana Q4. Consider the following statements about Jainism: 1. Mahavira was the 24th Tirthankara. 2. The first Jain Council was held at Vallabhi. 3. The Digambara–Svetambara split is traditionall","chars":5061},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"polity-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/polity-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — Polity &amp; Governance (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — Polity & Governance (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — Polity & Governance (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Constitution, institutions, recent amendments and landmark judgments. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. The idea of a Constituent Assembly for India was first put forward by: (a) M.N. Roy in 1934 (b) Jawaharlal Nehru in 1938 (c) The Cabinet Mission in 1946 (d) The Cripps Mission in 1942 Q2. Which of the following provisions came into force on 26 November 1949 itself? 1. Citizenship 2. Elections 3. Provisional Parliament 4. Fundamental Rights (a) 1, 2 and 3 (b) 2 and 4 (c) 1 and 4 (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Q3. Consider the following statements about Article 21A: 1. It was inserted by the 86th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2002. 2. It covers children aged 6 to 14 years. 3. A corresponding Fundamental Duty was added under Article 51A(k). (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q4. Under which A","chars":6934},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs","file":"science-tech-sectional-20q.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/pyqs/science-tech-sectional-20q.html","title":"Sectional Test — Science &amp; Technology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Test — Science & Technology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes)"},{"level":2,"text":"Answer key"}],"text":"Sectional Test — Science & Technology (20 Questions · 25 Minutes) +2 / −0.66 marking. Basic science, space, biotech, defence and emerging technologies. Key with one-line reasoning at the end. Q1. Which of the following is the SI unit of radioactivity? (a) Gray (b) Sievert (c) Becquerel (d) Curie Q2. Consider the following statements: 1. Sound travels faster in water than in air. 2. Ultrasound has frequencies below 20 Hz. 3. The Doppler effect is used in radar speed guns. (a) 1 and 2 (b) 2 and 3 (c) 1 and 3 (d) 1, 2 and 3 Q3. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes: (a) Scurvy (b) Pernicious anaemia (c) Rickets (d) Night blindness Q4. Consider the following pairs of diseases and pathogens: 1. Kala-azar — Leishmania 2. Nipah — bacterium 3. Chikungunya — virus 4. Sleeping sickness — Trypanosoma How many pairs are correctly matched? (a) One (b) Two (c) Three (d) Four Q5. Which one of the following lau","chars":5287},{"nav":"prelims-mocks/sectional-tests/short","file":"sectional-brief.html","rel":"content/prelims/mocks/sectional-tests/short-notes/sectional-brief.html","title":"Sectional Tests — One-Page Brief","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Sectional Tests — One-Page Brief"},{"level":2,"text":"When"},{"level":2,"text":"Bands"},{"level":2,"text":"Logging"}],"text":"Sectional Tests — One-Page Brief Six 20-question papers, one per GS1 area. When Right after finishing that area's revision — never cold. 1-2 per week; full mocks only after 4-5 sectionals banked. Bands 12+/20 → area cleared · 8-11/20 → trap-flash + PYQs redo · under-8/20 → content-first rebuild. Logging Every miss tagged: gap → notes · trap → flash-list · misread → scan drill · speed → skip rule. One rolling error-sheet per area = final-month revision fuel. Papers: history-culture · polity · geography · economy · environment · science-tech (in sectional-tests/pyqs).","chars":572},{"nav":"","file":"README.html","rel":"content/README.html","title":"content/ — Study Material Library","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"content/ — Study Material Library"},{"level":2,"text":"Conventions"}],"text":"content/ — Study Material Library Two branches mirror the exam. Every folder below corresponds to a node of the syllabus tree in assets/js/data.js , and every node has its own material (316 chapters — each a styled, print-ready .html page — plus 23 SVG diagrams). content/ ├── prelims/ │ ├── gs1/ # → paper \"prelims-gs1\" (176 files) │ │ ├── history-culture/ # 4 branches · 19 leaf topics │ │ ├── geography/ # 4 branches · 12 leaf topics │ │ ├── polity-governance/ # 4 branches · 15 leaf topics │ │ ├── economy/ # 5 branches · 12 leaf topics │ │ ├── environment-ecology/ # 3 branches · 7 leaf topics │ │ └── science-tech/ # 2 branches · 5 leaf topics │ │ └── <branch>/<leaf>/{short-notes/<leaf>-notes.html, pyqs/<leaf>-mcqs.html} │ ├── csat/ # → \"prelims-csat\": comprehension, logical-reasoning, │ │ # decision-making, numeracy (+ strategy notes, PYQ trends) │ └── mocks/ # → \"prelims-mocks\" │ ├── ful","chars":2890}];
